INSERT INTO `biology_syllabus_comprehensive` (`id`, `topic`, `sub_topic`, `note`, `keywords`, `created_at`) VALUES
(1, '1. CLASSIFICATION', 'Living and Non-living Things', 'Living organisms are distinguished by seven fundamental life processes collectively remembered as MRS GREN: Movement (change in position or place), Respiration (chemical reactions releasing energy from food), Sensitivity (detecting and responding to stimuli), Growth (permanent increase in size and complexity), Reproduction (producing offspring), Excretion (removing metabolic wastes), and Nutrition (acquiring and processing food). Non-living things do not exhibit these integrated processes. Viruses occupy a unique position, being acellular and unable to reproduce independently, yet possessing genetic material and evolving, blurring the line between living and non-living. Prions, viroids, and satellite viruses further challenge definitions of life.', 'MRS GREN, movement, respiration, sensitivity, growth, reproduction, excretion, nutrition, viruses, acellular, obligate parasites, evolution, metabolism, stimuli, homeostasis, prions, viroids, satellite viruses', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(2, '1. CLASSIFICATION', 'Kingdom Monera', 'Kingdom Monera comprises all prokaryotic organisms, including bacteria and cyanobacteria (blue-green algae). They are unicellular, lack membrane-bound organelles, and have a single circular chromosome in a nucleoid region. Their cell walls contain peptidoglycan (murein). Nutrition is diverse: autotrophic (photosynthetic cyanobacteria using chlorophyll a and phycobilins; chemosynthetic bacteria oxidizing inorganic compounds like hydrogen sulfide, ammonia, iron) or heterotrophic (saprophytic decomposers; parasitic pathogens). Reproduction is primarily asexual through binary fission, generating identical daughter cells. Genetic variation occurs via conjugation (direct transfer of plasmids through pilus), transformation (uptake of free DNA from environment), and transduction (phage-mediated DNA transfer). Archaea, once grouped with bacteria, are now recognized as a separate domain, often extremophiles (thermophiles, halophiles, methanogens) with distinct membrane lipids and cell wall composition. Ecologically, Monera are crucial as decomposers recycling nutrients; nitrogen-fixers (Rhizobium in root nodules, free-living Azotobacter, cyanobacteria Anabaena); and pathogens causing diseases like tuberculosis (Mycobacterium tuberculosis), cholera (Vibrio cholerae), typhoid (Salmonella typhi), anthrax (Bacillus anthracis), and bubonic plague (Yersinia pestis). Some produce antibiotics (Streptomyces). Biofilm formation and quorum sensing regulate gene expression in bacterial communities.', 'Monera, prokaryotes, bacteria, cyanobacteria, blue-green algae, unicellular, nucleoid, peptidoglycan, murein, autotrophic, photosynthetic, chemosynthetic, heterotrophic, saprophytic, parasitic, binary fission, conjugation, transformation, transduction, plasmid, pilus, phage, Archaea, extremophiles, thermophiles, halophiles, methanogens, decomposers, nitrogen fixation, Rhizobium, Azotobacter, Anabaena, pathogens, tuberculosis, Mycobacterium tuberculosis, cholera, Vibrio cholerae, typhoid, Salmonella typhi, anthrax, Bacillus anthracis, plague, Yersinia pestis, antibiotics, Streptomyces, biofilm, quorum sensing', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(3, '1. CLASSIFICATION', 'Kingdom Protista', 'Protista is a diverse, paraphyletic kingdom of mostly unicellular eukaryotic organisms. They possess a true nucleus, membrane-bound organelles, and complex cytoskeletal structures. Major phyla include Rhizopoda (Amoeba proteus, moving by cytoplasmic streaming and pseudopodia; phagotrophic nutrition via phagocytosis); Zoomastigina (Trypanosoma brucei causing sleeping sickness, transmitted by tsetse fly; Leishmania causing leishmaniasis; Trichomonas, Giardia causing intestinal infections); Ciliophora (Paramecium caudatum with ciliary locomotion and feeding; macronucleus for somatic functions, micronucleus for reproduction; conjugation for genetic exchange; Stentor, Vorticella); Apicomplexa (Plasmodium species causing malaria, complex life cycle with sexual reproduction in Anopheles mosquito and asexual in human liver and red blood cells; Toxoplasma gondii causing toxoplasmosis); and algae phyla including Chlorophyta (green algae: Chlamydomonas, Volvox, Spirogyra, Ulva), Rhodophyta (red algae: Porphyra, used as nori in sushi), and Phaeophyta (brown algae: Fucus, Sargassum, kelp forming massive underwater forests). Protists exhibit all three modes of nutrition: photosynthetic (algal protists contributing ~50% of global primary production), ingestive (phagotrophic protozoa), and absorptive (osmotrophic slime molds and oomycetes). Some are mixotrophic (Euglena gracilis, photosynthetic in light, heterotrophic in darkness). Oomycetes (water molds) include devastating plant pathogens like Phytophthora infestans causing Irish potato famine. This kingdom represents critical evolutionary transitions from unicellularity to multicellularity (colonial forms like Volvox) and the origin of key organelles via endosymbiosis (mitochondria from alpha-proteobacteria; chloroplasts from cyanobacteria; secondary endosymbiosis in some lineages).', 'Protista, protocista, eukaryotes, unicellular, paraphyletic, Rhizopoda, Amoeba, pseudopodia, cytoplasmic streaming, phagocytosis, Zoomastigina, Trypanosoma, sleeping sickness, tsetse fly, Leishmania, leishmaniasis, Trichomonas, Giardia, Ciliophora, Paramecium, cilia, macronucleus, micronucleus, conjugation, Stentor, Vorticella, Apicomplexa, Plasmodium, malaria, Anopheles, Toxoplasma, toxoplasmosis, Chlorophyta, Chlamydomonas, Volvox, Spirogyra, Ulva, Rhodophyta, Porphyra, Phaeophyta, Fucus, Sargassum, kelp, photosynthesis, phagocytosis, absorptive, osmotrophic, mixotrophic, Euglena, Oomycetes, water molds, Phytophthora, potato famine, colonial, multicellularity, endosymbiosis, mitochondria, chloroplasts, secondary endosymbiosis', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(4, '1. CLASSIFICATION', 'Kingdom Fungi', 'Fungi are eukaryotic, heterotrophic organisms with chitinous cell walls. They exist as unicellular yeasts or multicellular filamentous forms. The body consists of microscopic threads called hyphae, which may be septate (with cross-walls) or coenocytic (multinucleate without cross-walls). Hyphae collectively form a mycelium, the main vegetative structure. Major phyla include Zygomycota (bread mould Rhizopus stolonifer with stolons, rhizoids, and sporangiophores; sexual reproduction producing zygospores); Ascomycota (sac fungi: yeasts like Saccharomyces cerevisiae for baking and brewing; Penicillium for antibiotic production and cheese ripening; morels and truffles as edible delicacies; powdery mildews as plant pathogens; Neurospora crassa as model organism); and Basidiomycota (club fungi: mushrooms like Agaricus bisporus, bracket fungi, puffballs, rusts, smuts as plant pathogens). Nutrition is absorptive: they secrete exoenzymes (cellulases, ligninases, proteases, amylases) to break down complex organic matter externally and absorb the resulting simple compounds. Ecological roles: saprobes (decomposers recycling nutrients in ecosystems); parasites causing plant diseases (Dutch elm disease, chestnut blight, ergot of rye) and animal/human diseases (ringworm, athlete\'s foot, candidiasis, histoplasmosis); mutualists forming lichens (symbiosis with photosynthetic algae or cyanobacteria) and mycorrhizae (associations with plant roots, enhancing nutrient uptake). Lichens are pioneer species and sensitive bioindicators of air pollution. Mycorrhizae include ectomycorrhizae (sheathing roots of trees) and endomycorrhizae (arbuscular mycorrhizae penetrating root cells). Some fungi are predacious (nematode-trapping fungi with specialized hyphae).', 'Fungi, eukaryotes, heterotrophic, chitin, hyphae, septate, coenocytic, mycelium, Zygomycota, Rhizopus, bread mould, stolons, rhizoids, sporangiophores, zygospores, Ascomycota, sac fungi, yeasts, Saccharomyces, baking, brewing, Penicillium, antibiotic, cheese, morels, truffles, powdery mildew, Neurospora, Basidiomycota, club fungi, mushrooms, Agaricus, bracket fungi, puffballs, rusts, smuts, exoenzymes, cellulases, ligninases, proteases, amylases, absorptive nutrition, saprobes, decomposers, parasites, plant diseases, Dutch elm disease, chestnut blight, ergot, ringworm, athlete\'s foot, candidiasis, histoplasmosis, mutualists, lichens, mycorrhizae, ectomycorrhizae, endomycorrhizae, arbuscular, bioindicators, predacious fungi, nematode-trapping', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(5, '1. CLASSIFICATION', 'Kingdom Plantae', 'Plantae are multicellular, primarily autotrophic eukaryotes with cellulosic cell walls and chlorophyll a and b for photosynthesis. They store starch as reserve food and exhibit adaptations for terrestrial life, including cuticles (waxy layer preventing desiccation), stomata (pores for gas exchange), and vascular tissue (xylem and phloem for transport). Major divisions: Bryophyta (non-vascular: mosses like Polytrichum and Funaria, liverworts like Marchantia, hornworts) with gametophyte dominant generation, requiring water for fertilization; Filicinophyta (seedless vascular: ferns like Dryopteris and Nephrolepis, horsetails, clubmosses) with sporophyte dominant, vascular tissue, spores produced in sporangia; Coniferophyta (gymnosperms: pines, firs, spruces, cycads, Ginkgo) with naked seeds in cones, pollen grains for wind dispersal, well-developed vascular tissue; Angiospermophyta (flowering plants) with enclosed seeds in ovaries, flowers for reproduction, fruits for seed dispersal, divided into monocotyledons (one cotyledon, parallel leaf venation, scattered vascular bundles, fibrous roots, floral parts in multiples of three) and dicotyledons (two cotyledons, netted leaf venation, vascular bundles in ring, taproot, floral parts in multiples of four or five). Angiosperms dominate most terrestrial ecosystems, providing food, fiber, medicine, and habitat. Plant life cycles feature alternation of generations between haploid gametophyte and diploid sporophyte, with progressive reduction of gametophyte in evolution.', 'Plantae, multicellular, autotrophic, cellulose, chlorophyll a, chlorophyll b, photosynthesis, starch, cuticle, stomata, vascular tissue, xylem, phloem, Bryophyta, mosses, Polytrichum, Funaria, liverworts, Marchantia, hornworts, gametophyte dominant, water for fertilization, Filicinophyta, ferns, Dryopteris, Nephrolepis, horsetails, clubmosses, sporophyte dominant, spores, sporangia, Coniferophyta, gymnosperms, pines, firs, cycads, Ginkgo, naked seeds, cones, pollen, Angiospermophyta, flowering plants, enclosed seeds, ovaries, flowers, fruits, monocotyledons, monocots, one cotyledon, parallel venation, scattered vascular bundles, fibrous roots, floral parts three, dicotyledons, dicots, two cotyledons, netted venation, vascular ring, taproot, floral parts four or five, alternation of generations, gametophyte, sporophyte', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(6, '1. CLASSIFICATION', 'Kingdom Animalia', 'Animalia are multicellular, heterotrophic eukaryotes that ingest food. They are generally motile (at some life stage) and lack cell walls. Body plans exhibit increasing complexity: symmetry (radial in cnidarians, bilateral in most); germ layers (diploblastic with ectoderm and endoderm in cnidarians; triploblastic with ectoderm, mesoderm, endoderm in bilaterians); coelom (body cavity lined with mesoderm: acoelomate, pseudocoelomate, eucoelomate); segmentation (metamerism in annelids, arthropods, chordates); cephalization (concentration of sense organs and nervous tissue at anterior end). Major phyla: Porifera (sponges with choanocytes, spicules, sessile filter feeders); Cnidaria (jellyfish, corals, Hydra with cnidocytes, radial symmetry, gastrovascular cavity, two body forms: polyp and medusa); Platyhelminthes (flatworms: planaria free-living, tapeworms and flukes parasitic with complex life cycles, acoelomate); Nematoda (roundworms: Caenorhabditis elegans model organism, Ascaris parasitic, hookworms, pinworms, pseudocoelomate, complete digestive tract); Annelida (segmented worms: earthworms for soil aeration, leeches for medicine, polychaetes, eucoelomate, closed circulatory system); Mollusca (snails, slugs, clams, oysters, octopus, squid with muscular foot, visceral mass, mantle secreting shell, radula in many); Arthropoda (insects, crustaceans, spiders, scorpions, millipedes, centipedes with jointed appendages, exoskeleton of chitin, segmented body, open circulatory system, most diverse animal phylum); Echinodermata (starfish, sea urchins, sea cucumbers with pentaradial symmetry in adults, water vascular system, tube feet, endoskeleton of calcareous plates); Chordata (vertebrates: fish, amphibians, reptiles, birds, mammals plus invertebrate chordates like tunicates and lancelets with notochord, dorsal hollow nerve cord, pharyngeal slits, post-anal tail at some stage). Animals exhibit vast diversity in feeding adaptations (herbivores, carnivores, omnivores, filter feeders, parasites), locomotion (swimming, flying, running, burrowing), sensory systems, reproduction (asexual budding, sexual with various mating systems, parental care), and behavior.', 'Animalia, multicellular, heterotrophic, ingestion, motile, symmetry, radial symmetry, bilateral symmetry, germ layers, diploblastic, triploblastic, ectoderm, mesoderm, endoderm, coelom, acoelomate, pseudocoelomate, eucoelomate, segmentation, metamerism, cephalization, Porifera, sponges, choanocytes, spicules, filter feeders, Cnidaria, jellyfish, corals, Hydra, cnidocytes, gastrovascular cavity, polyp, medusa, Platyhelminthes, flatworms, planaria, tapeworms, flukes, acoelomate, Nematoda, roundworms, Caenorhabditis elegans, Ascaris, hookworms, pinworms, pseudocoelomate, Annelida, segmented worms, earthworms, leeches, polychaetes, closed circulatory system, Mollusca, snails, slugs, clams, oysters, octopus, squid, foot, visceral mass, mantle, shell, radula, Arthropoda, insects, crustaceans, spiders, scorpions, millipedes, centipedes, jointed appendages, exoskeleton, chitin, open circulatory system, Echinodermata, starfish, sea urchins, sea cucumbers, pentaradial symmetry, water vascular system, tube feet, endoskeleton, Chordata, vertebrates, fish, amphibians, reptiles, birds, mammals, notochord, dorsal hollow nerve cord, pharyngeal slits, post-anal tail, tunicates, lancelets, herbivores, carnivores, omnivores, filter feeders, parasites', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(7, '1. CLASSIFICATION', 'Differences Between Plants and Animals', 'Plants and animals differ fundamentally in structure, nutrition, and physiology. Plants are autotrophic, producing their own food through photosynthesis using chlorophyll. Animals are heterotrophic, obtaining energy by consuming other organisms. Plant cells possess rigid cellulosic cell walls providing structural support; animal cells lack cell walls, allowing flexibility and varied shapes. Plants exhibit unlimited growth throughout life via meristems; animals typically have limited, determinate growth. Plants respond slowly to stimuli via hormones (phototropism, gravitropism); animals respond rapidly via nervous and hormonal systems. Plants lack specialized sensory organs and nervous systems; animals possess complex sensory structures and nervous coordination. At cellular level, plants contain chloroplasts (for photosynthesis) and large central vacuoles (for storage, turgor); animals lack these but possess centrioles (involved in cell division) and more prominent lysosomes. Plants store carbohydrates as starch; animals store as glycogen. Plants have alternation of generations life cycle; animals do not. Plants are primarily sedentary (except some motile gametes); animals are generally motile. Excretion in plants involves storing wastes or shedding parts; animals have specialized excretory organs. Plants have open circulatory systems (if any); animals have diverse circulatory systems including closed systems.', 'plants vs animals, autotrophic, heterotrophic, cell wall, cellulose, unlimited growth, limited growth, stimuli response, hormones, nervous system, chloroplasts, central vacuole, centrioles, lysosomes, starch, glycogen, alternation of generations, sedentary, motile, excretion, circulatory system, photosynthesis, chlorophyll, meristems, phototropism, gravitropism, turgor pressure', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(8, '2. ORGANIZATION OF LIFE', 'Levels of Organization - Cell Level', 'The cell is the fundamental structural and functional unit of all living organisms, capable of independent existence and performing all life functions. In unicellular organisms like Amoeba proteus, Euglena viridis, and Paramecium caudatum, the cell itself represents the highest level of biological organization. This single cell must carry out all essential processes: metabolism (anabolic and catabolic reactions), homeostasis (maintaining internal stability), sensitivity (detecting and responding to environmental stimuli), reproduction (asexual division), and excretion (removing metabolic wastes). These organisms demonstrate that life can exist and function fully without tissue, organ, or system formation. Division of labor occurs at the subcellular level through specialized organelles: mitochondria for energy production, contractile vacuole for osmoregulation, cilia or flagella for movement, food vacuoles for digestion, and eye spots for light detection. The complexity of unicellular organisms challenges the notion that single cells are \"simple\" – they exhibit sophisticated behaviors, including chemotaxis, phototaxis, prey capture, avoidance responses, and even learning-like habituation. Cell theory, developed by Schleiden, Schwann, and Virchow, establishes that all living things are composed of cells, the cell is the basic unit of life, and all cells arise from pre-existing cells. Exceptions include skeletal muscle fibers (multinucleated syncytia), fungal hyphae (coenocytic with multiple nuclei), and viruses (acellular), which modify but do not invalidate the theory.', 'cell level, unicellular, Amoeba proteus, Euglena viridis, Paramecium caudatum, metabolism, homeostasis, sensitivity, reproduction, excretion, division of labor, organelles, mitochondria, contractile vacuole, osmoregulation, cilia, flagella, food vacuoles, eyespot, chemotaxis, phototaxis, habituation, cell theory, Schleiden, Schwann, Virchow, omnis cellula e cellula, syncytia, coenocytic, acellular, viruses', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(9, '2. ORGANIZATION OF LIFE', 'Levels of Organization - Tissue Level', 'A tissue is an integrated group of similar cells specialized to perform a common function. The tissue level represents a significant evolutionary advance beyond solitary cells, allowing for division of labor and increased efficiency. Hydra, a cnidarian, exemplifies the tissue level of organization. While lacking true organs, Hydra possesses distinct cell types organized into functional layers: an outer epidermis (protective and sensory) and inner gastrodermis (digestive). Specific cell types include epitheliomuscular cells (containing contractile fibers for body movement), interstitial cells (undifferentiated stem cells capable of regeneration and differentiation into other cell types), gland cells (secreting digestive enzymes into the gastrovascular cavity), cnidocytes (stinging cells with nematocysts for prey capture and defense), and sensory cells (detecting chemical and mechanical stimuli). Nerve cells form a simple nerve net coordinating responses. This cellular specialization and integration represent the evolutionary transition from cell-level to tissue-level organization. In more complex animals, four primary tissue types develop: epithelial (covering and lining), connective (support and connection), muscle (movement), and nervous (coordination and communication). In plants, tissues include meristematic (dividing) and permanent tissues like parenchyma (storage, photosynthesis), collenchyma (support), sclerenchyma (strength), xylem (water transport), and phloem (nutrient transport).', 'tissue level, Hydra, cnidarian, specialized cells, epitheliomuscular cells, interstitial cells, stem cells, regeneration, gland cells, digestive enzymes, gastrovascular cavity, cnidocytes, nematocysts, sensory cells, nerve net, evolutionary complexity, epithelial tissue, connective tissue, muscle tissue, nervous tissue, plant tissues, meristematic, parenchyma, collenchyma, sclerenchyma, xylem, phloem', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(10, '2. ORGANIZATION OF LIFE', 'Levels of Organization - Organ Level', 'An organ is a structure composed of different tissues integrated to perform a specific function. Organs represent a higher level of biological organization, enabling complex physiological processes. In plants, storage organs exemplify the organ level: bulbs (onion, garlic) consist of fleshy scale leaves (modified leaves) attached to a short stem, storing nutrients; rhizomes (ginger, turmeric) are underground horizontal stems with nodes, internodes, and buds, functioning in storage and vegetative propagation; tubers (potato) are swollen underground stems storing starch; corms (corm) are swollen stem bases. These organs comprise multiple tissues: parenchyma for storage, epidermis for protection, and vascular tissues for transport. In animals, the heart is a muscular organ composed of cardiac muscle tissue (for contraction), connective tissue (forming valves and providing structure), epithelial tissue (lining chambers and vessels), and nervous tissue (regulating heart rate). Other examples include the liver (metabolic organ with hepatocytes, blood vessels, bile ducts), kidneys (excretory organs with nephrons, blood vessels, collecting ducts), lungs (respiratory organs with alveoli, bronchi, blood vessels), and brain (nervous organ with neurons, glia, blood vessels). Organs often have complex three-dimensional architecture optimizing function: alveoli maximize surface area, nephrons create concentration gradients, and heart chambers coordinate blood flow.', 'organ level, bulb, onion, garlic, scale leaves, stem, rhizome, ginger, turmeric, nodes, internodes, tuber, potato, corm, storage organs, parenchyma, epidermis, vascular tissues, heart, cardiac muscle, connective tissue, epithelial tissue, nervous tissue, liver, hepatocytes, kidneys, nephrons, lungs, alveoli, brain, neurons, glia, organ structure, function optimization', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(11, '2. ORGANIZATION OF LIFE', 'Levels of Organization - System Level', 'An organ system is a group of organs working together to carry out major bodily functions. This highest level of organization enables integrated physiological responses and homeostasis. In mammals, the digestive system (mouth, esophagus, stomach, small intestine, large intestine, liver, pancreas, gall bladder) works to break down food, absorb nutrients, and eliminate waste. Each organ contributes specialized functions: mouth mechanically breaks food, stomach chemically digests proteins, small intestine absorbs nutrients, liver produces bile, pancreas secretes digestive enzymes. The respiratory system (nasal cavity, trachea, bronchi, lungs, diaphragm) facilitates gas exchange: oxygen uptake and carbon dioxide removal. The circulatory system (heart, blood vessels, blood) transports oxygen, nutrients, hormones, and wastes. The excretory system (kidneys, ureters, bladder, urethra) removes metabolic wastes and regulates water and salt balance. The nervous system (brain, spinal cord, nerves) coordinates rapid responses. The endocrine system (glands and hormones) regulates slower, longer-lasting processes. In flowering plants, the root system anchors the plant, absorbs water and minerals, and stores food. The shoot system (stem, leaves, flowers, fruits) handles photosynthesis, reproduction, and transport. Vascular tissue (xylem and phloem) connects these systems, enabling long-distance transport. This integration of systems maintains internal stability (homeostasis) through complex feedback mechanisms.', 'system level, organ system, digestive system, mouth, esophagus, stomach, small intestine, large intestine, liver, pancreas, gall bladder, mechanical digestion, chemical digestion, absorption, respiratory system, nasal cavity, trachea, bronchi, lungs, diaphragm, gas exchange, circulatory system, heart, blood vessels, blood, transport, excretory system, kidneys, ureters, bladder, urethra, osmoregulation, nervous system, brain, spinal cord, nerves, endocrine system, hormones, root system, shoot system, stem, leaves, flowers, fruits, xylem, phloem, integration, homeostasis, feedback mechanisms', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(12, '2. ORGANIZATION OF LIFE', 'Complexity in Higher Organisms', 'Higher organisms exhibit complex hierarchical organization with emergent properties not present at lower levels. Advantages include functional specialization (cells, tissues, organs dedicated to specific tasks), increased efficiency (optimized structures for particular functions), adaptability (ability to survive in diverse environments), and larger body size (reduced predation risk, broader niche exploitation). Specialization allows for division of labor, where different parts perform different functions simultaneously, increasing overall efficiency. However, complexity brings disadvantages: interdependence (failure of one part affects the whole organism), slower systemic responses (coordination requires communication), higher energy requirements (maintaining specialized structures and systems), limited regenerative capacity (specialized cells often cannot dedifferentiate), and vulnerability to systemic diseases (cancer, autoimmune disorders). The evolution of complexity required innovations like bilateral symmetry (directional movement), cephalization (centralized control), segmentation (modular design), coelom (fluid-filled cavity for organ suspension), closed circulatory systems (efficient transport), and homeostatic mechanisms (maintaining stable internal conditions despite external changes). Trade-offs between specialization and flexibility, between efficiency and robustness, shape organismal design. Understanding complexity requires systems biology approaches integrating molecular, cellular, organismal, and ecological levels.', 'complexity, higher organisms, emergent properties, advantages, specialization, efficiency, adaptability, larger body size, division of labor, disadvantages, interdependence, slower responses, energy requirements, regenerative capacity, systemic diseases, bilateral symmetry, cephalization, segmentation, coelom, closed circulatory systems, homeostasis, trade-offs, systems biology', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(13, '2. ORGANIZATION OF LIFE', 'Volume/Surface Area Ratio', 'The surface area to volume ratio (SA:V) is a critical biophysical constraint shaping organismal structure and function. As organisms increase in size, volume (which dictates metabolic demand and waste production) increases cubically (L³), while surface area (available for exchange of materials with environment) increases quadratically (L²). Consequently, SA:V ratio decreases with increasing size. This has profound implications: small organisms (bacteria, protozoa) can rely on simple diffusion for gas exchange, nutrient uptake, and waste removal because distances are short and SA:V high. Large organisms cannot; their central cells would die from oxygen deprivation and waste accumulation. Adaptations to overcome SA:V constraints include: (1) Flattened body shapes (flatworms) maintaining high SA:V; (2) Extensive branching (lungs, gills, roots) creating enormous internal surface areas; (3) Specialized exchange surfaces with large surface areas: alveoli in lungs (human lungs ~70-100 m²), lamellae in fish gills, villi and microvilli in small intestine (increasing absorptive surface ~600-fold), root hairs in plants (vastly increasing absorption area); (4) Circulatory systems (bulk transport) to move materials between exchange surfaces and deep tissues; (5) Respiratory pigments (hemoglobin) increasing oxygen transport capacity. The SA:V ratio also affects heat exchange (small animals lose heat faster, requiring higher metabolic rates), strength (support structures needed for large size), and ecological interactions. This principle explains why cells cannot grow indefinitely, why multicellularity required transport systems, and why organisms have characteristic size ranges.', 'SA:V ratio, volume/surface area ratio, diffusion limit, metabolic demand, waste production, exchange surfaces, flattened bodies, flatworms, branching, lungs, alveoli, gills, lamellae, villi, microvilli, root hairs, circulatory systems, bulk transport, respiratory pigments, hemoglobin, heat exchange, metabolic rate, support structures, cell size limitation, multicellularity, organism size', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(14, '3. FORMS OF CELL EXISTENCE', 'Single and Free-living Cells', 'Unicellular organisms represent the simplest form of life, where a single cell performs all essential functions. These organisms exhibit remarkable complexity and sophisticated behaviors. Amoeba proteus, a rhizopod, demonstrates amoeboid movement via pseudopodia (false feet) formed by cytoplasmic streaming driven by actin filament polymerization and myosin motor proteins. It captures food through phagocytosis, engulfing bacteria, algae, and other protozoa into food vacuoles where lysosomes fuse to digest prey. Osmoregulation is achieved by the contractile vacuole, which collects excess water entering by osmosis and actively expels it, preventing osmotic lysis in freshwater environments. Amoeba can encyst under unfavorable conditions. Paramecium caudatum, a ciliate, uses coordinated beating of thousands of cilia for locomotion and feeding. Cilia beat in a metachronal rhythm, creating water currents that sweep food bacteria into the oral groove and gullet. It possesses two nuclei: the macronucleus (polyploid, controls somatic functions like metabolism and protein synthesis) and the micronucleus (diploid, involved in sexual reproduction through conjugation and autogamy). During conjugation, two paramecia exchange haploid micronuclei, promoting genetic variation. Euglena viridis, a flagellate, is mixotrophic, possessing chloroplasts with pyrenoids for photosynthesis in light and can absorb organic nutrients heterotrophically in darkness. It moves via a single emergent flagellum with paraflagellar body and an eyespot (stigma) for phototaxis (movement toward optimal light). The pellicle (protein strips under cell membrane) provides flexibility. Chlamydomonas reinhardtii, a green alga, is a model organism for studying photosynthesis, flagellar motility, phototaxis, and mating behaviors. It has two anterior flagella, a cup-shaped chloroplast with pyrenoid, and an eyespot. It reproduces asexually by zoospores and sexually by isogamous fusion of gametes. These organisms demonstrate that unicellular life is not \"simple\" but highly adapted and complex.', 'single cells, free-living, unicellular, Amoeba proteus, pseudopodia, cytoplasmic streaming, actin, myosin, phagocytosis, food vacuoles, lysosomes, contractile vacuole, osmoregulation, encystment, Paramecium caudatum, cilia, metachronal rhythm, oral groove, gullet, macronucleus, micronucleus, conjugation, autogamy, genetic variation, Euglena viridis, mixotroph, chloroplasts, pyrenoids, flagellum, paraflagellar body, eyespot, stigma, phototaxis, pellicle, Chlamydomonas reinhardtii, model organism, zoospores, isogamous gametes', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(15, '3. FORMS OF CELL EXISTENCE', 'Colonial Organization', 'Colonial organisms represent an evolutionary intermediate between unicellularity and true multicellularity. In colonies, individual cells are similar but show some interdependence and primitive division of labor. Volvox is the classic example, forming hollow spherical colonies (coenobia) of 500 to 50,000 biflagellate cells embedded in a gelatinous matrix of glycoproteins. Each cell resembles a Chlamydomonas cell, with two flagella, an eyespot, and a chloroplast. Cells are interconnected by fine cytoplasmic strands (plasmodesmata) allowing communication and coordination. The colony exhibits division of labor: most cells are somatic (vegetative, responsible for motility and photosynthesis), while a few cells are specialized for reproduction (gonidia). Gonidia are larger, non-motile cells that divide asexually to form new daughter colonies inside the parent colony before release. Some Volvox species are oogamous with differentiated eggs and sperm. The colony moves as a coordinated unit, with flagella beating in a coordinated manner, and shows phototaxis. This organization represents a key step in the evolution of multicellularity, demonstrating how cell specialization and integration can arise from unicellular ancestors. Other colonial examples include Gonium (flat plate of 4-16 cells), Pandorina (16-cell colony), Eudorina (32-cell colony), and Pleodorina (with partial division of labor). Colonial theory of multicellularity proposes that multicellular organisms evolved from colonial protists through increasing specialization and interdependence.', 'colonial organization, evolutionary intermediate, unicellularity, multicellularity, Volvox, coenobia, biflagellate cells, gelatinous matrix, glycoproteins, cytoplasmic strands, plasmodesmata, communication, coordination, division of labor, somatic cells, gonidia, reproduction, asexual reproduction, daughter colonies, oogamous, eggs, sperm, phototaxis, Gonium, Pandorina, Eudorina, Pleodorina, colonial theory, evolution of multicellularity', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(16, '3. FORMS OF CELL EXISTENCE', 'Filamentous Organization', 'Filamentous organization involves cells arranged end-to-end in chains or filaments, representing another evolutionary step toward multicellularity. Spirogyra, a green alga, is a classic example, forming unbranched filaments of cylindrical cells connected end-to-end. Each cell has a distinct cell wall, with end walls (cross-walls) that may have pores allowing limited cytoplasmic continuity. The cells are not fully independent; they share a common extracellular matrix and can exchange materials through plasmodesmata. This organization allows for increased size and resource capture compared to unicellular forms. Spirogyra is distinguished by its striking spiral (helical) chloroplasts, each with several pyrenoids for starch synthesis. The central vacuole occupies most of the cell volume, with cytoplasm confined to a peripheral layer. Nucleus is suspended in the center by cytoplasmic strands. Reproduction occurs asexually by fragmentation (filament breaks into pieces, each regenerating) and sexually by conjugation. During conjugation, adjacent filaments form conjugation tubes (or papillae) between aligned cells. Protoplasts of one filament act as male gametes (amoeboid) and migrate through the tube to fuse with female gametes (non-motile) in the other filament, forming a diploid zygospore. Zygospores undergo meiosis upon germination, producing new haploid filaments. This life cycle demonstrates alternation of generations in algae. Other filamentous algae include Ulothrix (unbranched filaments), Oedogonium (with specialized reproductive structures), and Cladophora (branched filaments). Filamentous fungi (molds) also exhibit this organization.', 'filamentous organization, Spirogyra, unbranched filaments, cylindrical cells, end walls, cross-walls, pores, cytoplasmic continuity, extracellular matrix, plasmodesmata, increased size, resource capture, spiral chloroplasts, helical chloroplasts, pyrenoids, starch, central vacuole, cytoplasmic strands, asexual reproduction, fragmentation, sexual reproduction, conjugation, conjugation tubes, papillae, male gametes, amoeboid, female gametes, non-motile, zygospore, meiosis, alternation of generations, Ulothrix, Oedogonium, Cladophora, filamentous fungi, molds', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(17, '4. CELL STRUCTURE AND FUNCTIONS', 'Cell Wall', 'The cell wall is a rigid, semi-permeable layer external to the cell membrane, providing structural support, mechanical strength, and protection against osmotic lysis. Its composition varies across taxonomic groups: plants and algae primarily use cellulose microfibrils (long, unbranched β(1→4)-linked glucose chains) embedded in a matrix of hemicellulose (cross-linking glycans) and pectin (gelatinous polysaccharides rich in galacturonic acid). Fungi use chitin (β(1→4)-linked N-acetylglucosamine polymer). Bacteria use peptidoglycan (murein), a polymer of glycan chains cross-linked by short peptides (in Gram-positive bacteria, thick layer with teichoic acids; in Gram-negative bacteria, thin layer with outer membrane containing lipopolysaccharides). Archaea have diverse wall compositions (pseudopeptidoglycan, polysaccharides, proteins). The plant cell wall consists of several layers: the middle lamella (rich in pectin) cements adjacent cells together; the primary wall (relatively thin and flexible) is deposited during cell growth, allowing expansion; the secondary wall (thicker and often lignified) is deposited inside the primary wall after growth ceases, providing additional strength in specialized cells like xylem vessels and fibers. Lignin (complex aromatic polymer) impregnates the secondary wall, making it waterproof and extremely strong. Plasmodesmata are cytoplasmic channels through the walls, lined with plasma membrane and containing desmotubule (modified ER), allowing communication and transport between plant cells. Functions: maintains cell shape, resists turgor pressure, provides mechanical support for plant body, acts as barrier against pathogens, mediates cell-cell adhesion, and stores carbohydrates (hemicellulose can be mobilized).', 'cell wall, cellulose, microfibrils, β(1→4) glucose, hemicellulose, pectin, galacturonic acid, chitin, N-acetylglucosamine, peptidoglycan, murein, Gram-positive, teichoic acids, Gram-negative, outer membrane, lipopolysaccharides, Archaea, pseudopeptidoglycan, middle lamella, primary wall, secondary wall, lignified, lignin, plasmodesmata, desmotubule, structural support, mechanical strength, osmotic protection, turgor pressure, barrier, cell adhesion, carbohydrate storage', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(18, '4. CELL STRUCTURE AND FUNCTIONS', 'Cell Membrane (Plasma Membrane)', 'The plasma membrane is a selectively permeable phospholipid bilayer with embedded and peripheral proteins, best described by the fluid mosaic model proposed by Singer and Nicolson (1972). This model depicts the membrane as a dynamic structure where lipids and proteins can move laterally within the bilayer. The phospholipid bilayer consists of hydrophilic heads (phosphate groups, glycerol) facing outward and hydrophobic tails (fatty acid chains) facing inward, creating a barrier to most water-soluble molecules. Cholesterol (in animal cells) or sterols (in plants and fungi) modulate membrane fluidity and stability. Membrane proteins include integral proteins (transmembrane proteins spanning the bilayer) and peripheral proteins (attached to membrane surface). Functions include: (1) Selective permeability: regulating passage of substances via simple diffusion (small nonpolar molecules), facilitated diffusion (channel and carrier proteins), osmosis (water movement), and active transport (pumps requiring ATP); (2) Cell recognition: glycoproteins and glycolipids on outer surface form glycocalyx, enabling cell-cell recognition, immune responses, and tissue formation; (3) Cell signaling: receptor proteins bind signaling molecules (hormones, neurotransmitters), triggering intracellular responses; (4) Cell adhesion: adhesion proteins (cadherins, integrins) attach cells to each other and to extracellular matrix; (5) Enzymatic activity: some membrane proteins are enzymes catalyzing reactions; (6) Intercellular junctions: tight junctions (sealing), desmosomes (anchoring), gap junctions (communication). Membrane fluidity is essential for function, allowing protein movement, vesicle fusion, and cell division. Factors affecting fluidity: fatty acid saturation (unsaturated increase fluidity), cholesterol content (moderates fluidity), and temperature.', 'cell membrane, plasma membrane, phospholipid bilayer, fluid mosaic model, Singer and Nicolson, selectively permeable, hydrophilic heads, hydrophobic tails, cholesterol, sterols, integral proteins, transmembrane proteins, peripheral proteins, simple diffusion, facilitated diffusion, channel proteins, carrier proteins, osmosis, active transport, ATP, glycoproteins, glycolipids, glycocalyx, cell recognition, cell signaling, receptor proteins, hormones, neurotransmitters, cell adhesion, cadherins, integrins, extracellular matrix, enzymatic activity, tight junctions, desmosomes, gap junctions, membrane fluidity, fatty acid saturation, unsaturated, temperature', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(19, '4. CELL STRUCTURE AND FUNCTIONS', 'Nucleus', 'The nucleus is the control center of the eukaryotic cell, containing the genetic material (DNA) organized into chromosomes. It is enclosed by a double-membraned nuclear envelope, continuous with the endoplasmic reticulum. The outer membrane is studded with ribosomes; the inner membrane is lined by the nuclear lamina (meshwork of lamin proteins providing structural support and chromatin attachment). Nuclear pores are large protein complexes (nuclear pore complexes) spanning both membranes, regulating macromolecular traffic between nucleus and cytoplasm. Small molecules (<40 kDa) diffuse freely; larger molecules require active transport mediated by importins and exportins recognizing nuclear localization signals (NLS) and nuclear export signals (NES). The nucleolus is a dense, membrane-less region where rRNA genes are transcribed, rRNA is processed, and ribosome subunits are assembled from rRNA and ribosomal proteins imported from cytoplasm. It contains fibrillar centers (rRNA genes), dense fibrillar component (rRNA processing), and granular component (ribosome assembly). The nucleus directs cellular activities by controlling gene expression (transcription of DNA to RNA), DNA replication (during S phase of cell cycle), and RNA processing (capping, splicing, polyadenylation). Chromatin exists in two forms: euchromatin (less condensed, transcriptionally active) and heterochromatin (highly condensed, transcriptionally inactive). Chromatin consists of DNA wrapped around histone proteins (H2A, H2B, H3, H4) forming nucleosomes, further compacted into higher-order structures. During cell division, chromatin condenses into visible chromosomes, each consisting of two sister chromatids joined at centromere. Telomeres (repetitive DNA sequences at chromosome ends) protect against deterioration and shortening with each division, linked to cellular aging and senescence.', 'nucleus, nuclear envelope, double membrane, endoplasmic reticulum, nuclear lamina, lamin proteins, nuclear pores, nuclear pore complexes, importins, exportins, nuclear localization signals, NLS, nuclear export signals, NES, nucleolus, rRNA genes, rRNA processing, ribosome assembly, fibrillar centers, dense fibrillar component, granular component, gene expression, transcription, DNA replication, RNA processing, capping, splicing, polyadenylation, chromatin, euchromatin, heterochromatin, histones, H2A, H2B, H3, H4, nucleosomes, chromosomes, sister chromatids, centromere, telomeres, aging, senescence', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(20, '4. CELL STRUCTURE AND FUNCTIONS', 'Cytoplasm', 'The cytoplasm is the gel-like matrix filling the cell, composed of cytosol (aqueous fluid), organelles, and the cytoskeleton. It occupies the space between the plasma membrane and the nucleus. Cytosol is a complex solution of water (70-80%), ions (K⁺, Na⁺, Ca²⁺, Cl⁻, Mg²⁺), small molecules (ATP, amino acids, nucleotides, sugars), and macromolecules (proteins, RNA). It has a pH around 7.2 and maintains osmotic balance. The cytoplasm is not a static soup but a highly organized, crowded environment where macromolecular crowding effects influence biochemical reactions. It exhibits non-Newtonian fluid properties, transitioning between sol (fluid) and gel (semi-solid) states. The cytoplasm is the site of numerous metabolic reactions: glycolysis (first stage of respiration), protein synthesis (on free ribosomes), the pentose phosphate pathway, fatty acid synthesis, and many biosynthetic and degradative pathways. It facilitates intracellular transport through diffusion (short distances) and active transport along cytoskeletal tracks (long distances). The cytoplasm contains various inclusions: glycogen granules (energy storage in animal cells), starch grains (energy storage in plant cells), lipid droplets, pigment granules, and crystals. Cyclosis (cytoplasmic streaming) is the directed flow of cytoplasm driven by motor proteins along actin filaments, distributing nutrients, organelles, and vesicles. This is especially prominent in large plant cells like Elodea and in amoeboid movement. The cytoplasm provides the medium for biochemical reactions, enables intracellular communication, and contributes to cell shape and movement.', 'cytoplasm, cytosol, organelles, cytoskeleton, ions, K⁺, Na⁺, Ca²⁺, Cl⁻, Mg²⁺, ATP, amino acids, nucleotides, sugars, proteins, RNA, pH, osmotic balance, macromolecular crowding, sol, gel, metabolic reactions, glycolysis, protein synthesis, pentose phosphate pathway, fatty acid synthesis, intracellular transport, diffusion, active transport, inclusions, glycogen granules, starch grains, lipid droplets, pigment granules, crystals, cyclosis, cytoplasmic streaming, motor proteins, actin filaments, Elodea, amoeboid movement, biochemical reactions, intracellular communication', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(21, '4. CELL STRUCTURE AND FUNCTIONS', 'Mitochondria', 'Mitochondria are double-membraned organelles, the \"powerhouses of the cell,\" generating most cellular ATP via aerobic respiration. According to the endosymbiotic theory, they originated from free-living aerobic bacteria (likely alpha-proteobacteria) engulfed by ancestral eukaryotic cells, evolving into permanent organelles. Evidence includes: circular DNA (mtDNA, 16.6 kb in humans, encoding 37 genes), 70S ribosomes (similar to bacteria), double membrane (inner from bacterial plasma membrane, outer from host vesicle), and semi-autonomous replication. Structure: outer membrane (smooth, permeable to small molecules due to porins); inner membrane (highly folded into cristae, increasing surface area, impermeable to most ions/molecules, contains electron transport chain complexes, ATP synthase, and transport proteins); intermembrane space; matrix (enclosed by inner membrane, contains enzymes for Krebs cycle, fatty acid oxidation, mtDNA, ribosomes). Cristae morphology varies (lamellar, tubular, vesicular) reflecting metabolic demands. Functions: (1) ATP production via oxidative phosphorylation: NADH and FADH₂ from Krebs cycle donate electrons to electron transport chain, driving proton pumping, creating electrochemical gradient, powering ATP synthase (chemiosmosis); (2) Krebs cycle (citric acid cycle) in matrix; (3) Fatty acid oxidation (beta-oxidation); (4) Heme synthesis; (5) Iron-sulfur cluster assembly; (6) Apoptosis (programmed cell death) via release of cytochrome c and other factors; (7) Calcium homeostasis (uptake and release regulating cytosolic Ca²⁺); (8) Thermogenesis in brown adipose tissue (via uncoupling protein UCP1). Mitochondria are dynamic, undergoing fusion (creating networks, mixing contents) and fission (producing new mitochondria, quality control). Mitochondrial dysfunction links to numerous diseases: mitochondrial myopathies, Leigh syndrome, Parkinson\'s, Alzheimer\'s, type 2 diabetes, and aging.', 'mitochondria, powerhouse of cell, aerobic respiration, ATP, endosymbiotic theory, alpha-proteobacteria, circular DNA, mtDNA, 70S ribosomes, semi-autonomous, outer membrane, inner membrane, cristae, porins, intermembrane space, matrix, Krebs cycle, fatty acid oxidation, electron transport chain, ATP synthase, oxidative phosphorylation, chemiosmosis, NADH, FADH₂, apoptosis, cytochrome c, calcium homeostasis, thermogenesis, brown adipose tissue, uncoupling protein, UCP1, fusion, fission, mitochondrial diseases, myopathies, Leigh syndrome, Parkinson\'s, Alzheimer\'s, diabetes, aging', '2026-02-27 15:40:30');
INSERT INTO `biology_syllabus_comprehensive` (`id`, `topic`, `sub_topic`, `note`, `keywords`, `created_at`) VALUES
(22, '4. CELL STRUCTURE AND FUNCTIONS', 'Chloroplasts', 'Chloroplasts are double-membraned organelles found in plant cells and some protists (algae), responsible for photosynthesis. They belong to the plastid family, which also includes chromoplasts (pigment storage) and leucoplasts (amyloplasts for starch storage). According to endosymbiotic theory, chloroplasts originated from photosynthetic cyanobacteria engulfed by early eukaryotic cells. Evidence includes: circular DNA (cpDNA), 70S ribosomes, double membrane, and semi-autonomous replication. Structure: outer membrane (permeable); inner membrane (less permeable); intermembrane space; thylakoid membrane system (internal membrane network) forming flattened sacs (thylakoids) stacked into grana (singular: granum); stroma (fluid matrix surrounding thylakoids). Thylakoid membranes contain photosynthetic pigments (chlorophyll a, chlorophyll b, carotenoids) organized into photosystems I and II, electron transport chain components, and ATP synthase. The light-dependent reactions of photosynthesis occur on thylakoid membranes: photosystems capture light energy, exciting electrons; electron transport chain generates ATP (photophosphorylation) and reduces NADP⁺ to NADPH; water is split (photolysis) by the oxygen-evolving complex of photosystem II, releasing O₂. The light-independent reactions (Calvin cycle) occur in the stroma: CO₂ is fixed to ribulose bisphosphate (RuBP) by the enzyme rubisco (ribulose bisphosphate carboxylase/oxygenase, the most abundant protein on Earth); using ATP and NADPH, glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate (G3P) is produced, which is used to synthesize glucose and other carbohydrates. Chloroplasts also perform other functions: synthesis of amino acids, fatty acids, pigments; nitrogen and sulfur assimilation; temporary starch storage; and signaling in plant defense. Chloroplast movement within cells optimizes light absorption (avoidance response under high light).', 'chloroplasts, photosynthesis, plastids, chromoplasts, leucoplasts, amyloplasts, endosymbiotic theory, cyanobacteria, circular DNA, cpDNA, 70S ribosomes, semi-autonomous, outer membrane, inner membrane, thylakoid membrane, thylakoids, grana, stroma, photosynthetic pigments, chlorophyll a, chlorophyll b, carotenoids, photosystem I, photosystem II, electron transport chain, ATP synthase, light-dependent reactions, photophosphorylation, NADPH, photolysis, oxygen-evolving complex, light-independent reactions, Calvin cycle, RuBP, rubisco, carbon fixation, G3P, glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate, glucose, starch, amino acids, fatty acids, pigments, nitrogen assimilation, sulfur assimilation, plant defense, chloroplast movement, phototaxis', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(23, '4. CELL STRUCTURE AND FUNCTIONS', 'Endoplasmic Reticulum (ER)', 'The endoplasmic reticulum is an extensive network of interconnected membranous tubules, vesicles, and flattened sacs (cisternae), continuous with the nuclear envelope. It is the largest organelle in many eukaryotic cells, comprising more than half the total membrane. Two distinct types exist: rough ER (RER) and smooth ER (SER). Rough ER is studded with ribosomes on its cytosolic surface, giving it a \"rough\" appearance under electron microscopy. These ribosomes synthesize proteins destined for secretion, incorporation into membranes, or delivery to lysosomes. RER functions include: (1) Protein synthesis of secretory and membrane proteins; (2) Initial glycosylation (addition of oligosaccharides to asparagine residues - N-linked glycosylation); (3) Protein folding with assistance from chaperones (BiP, calnexin, calreticulin); (4) Quality control (retaining misfolded proteins for refolding or degradation); (5) Formation of transport vesicles for protein delivery to Golgi. Smooth ER lacks ribosomes and has a tubular structure. SER functions vary by cell type: (1) Lipid synthesis (phospholipids, cholesterol, steroids); (2) Detoxification of drugs and poisons in liver cells (cytochrome P450 enzymes); (3) Calcium storage and release (in muscle cells as sarcoplasmic reticulum, regulating contraction); (4) Carbohydrate metabolism (glucose-6-phosphatase in gluconeogenesis); (5) Steroid hormone synthesis (in adrenal cortex, gonads). The ER also plays roles in autophagosome formation, lipid droplet biogenesis, and ER-associated degradation (ERAD) of misfolded proteins via the proteasome. ER stress (accumulation of unfolded proteins) triggers the unfolded protein response (UPR), activating protective mechanisms.', 'endoplasmic reticulum, ER, rough ER, RER, smooth ER, SER, cisternae, ribosomes, protein synthesis, secretory proteins, membrane proteins, lysosomal proteins, glycosylation, N-linked glycosylation, chaperones, BiP, calnexin, calreticulin, protein folding, quality control, transport vesicles, lipid synthesis, phospholipids, cholesterol, steroids, detoxification, cytochrome P450, calcium storage, sarcoplasmic reticulum, muscle contraction, carbohydrate metabolism, glucose-6-phosphatase, gluconeogenesis, steroid hormone synthesis, adrenal cortex, gonads, autophagosome, lipid droplets, ERAD, ER-associated degradation, proteasome, ER stress, unfolded protein response, UPR', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(24, '4. CELL STRUCTURE AND FUNCTIONS', 'Ribosomes', 'Ribosomes are non-membranous ribonucleoprotein complexes composed of ribosomal RNA (rRNA) and ribosomal proteins. They are the sites of protein synthesis (translation). Ribosomes consist of two subunits: a large subunit and a small subunit, each assembled in the nucleolus from rRNA and proteins imported from cytoplasm. Prokaryotic ribosomes are 70S (with sedimentation coefficient Svedberg units), composed of 50S large subunit (23S rRNA, 5S rRNA, ~34 proteins) and 30S small subunit (16S rRNA, ~21 proteins). Eukaryotic ribosomes are 80S, composed of 60S large subunit (28S rRNA, 5.8S rRNA, 5S rRNA, ~49 proteins) and 40S small subunit (18S rRNA, ~33 proteins). Mitochondria and chloroplasts contain 70S ribosomes, supporting endosymbiotic theory. Ribosomes can be found free in the cytoplasm (synthesizing proteins for use within the cell) or bound to the rough ER (synthesizing proteins for secretion, membrane incorporation, or lysosomes). Polyribosomes (polysomes) are clusters of multiple ribosomes translating the same mRNA simultaneously, increasing efficiency. Ribosome structure includes three tRNA binding sites: A site (aminoacyl-tRNA enters), P site (peptidyl-tRNA holds growing polypeptide), and E site (exit site for deacylated tRNA). Peptidyl transferase activity (catalyzing peptide bond formation) resides in the large subunit rRNA, making ribosomes ribozymes (RNA enzymes). Ribosome function is targeted by many antibiotics: tetracycline (blocks A site), chloramphenicol (inhibits peptidyl transferase), erythromycin (blocks peptide exit tunnel), streptomycin (causes misreading).', 'ribosomes, rRNA, ribosomal proteins, translation, protein synthesis, large subunit, small subunit, 70S, 80S, 50S, 30S, 60S, 40S, 23S rRNA, 16S rRNA, 18S rRNA, 5.8S rRNA, 5S rRNA, mitochondria, chloroplasts, endosymbiotic theory, free ribosomes, bound ribosomes, rough ER, polyribosomes, polysomes, A site, P site, E site, peptidyl transferase, ribozymes, antibiotics, tetracycline, chloramphenicol, erythromycin, streptomycin', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(25, '4. CELL STRUCTURE AND FUNCTIONS', 'Golgi Apparatus (Golgi Body)', 'The Golgi apparatus is a stack of flattened membranous sacs (cisternae), typically 4-8 per stack in animal cells, more in plant cells. It functions in modifying, sorting, and packaging proteins and lipids received from the ER. The Golgi has distinct polarity: the cis-Golgi network (CGN) faces the ER and receives vesicles; the medial-Golgi (middle cisternae); the trans-Golgi network (TGN) faces the plasma membrane and packages materials for export. Proteins and lipids move through Golgi cisternae by cisternal maturation (cisternae progress from cis to trans) or vesicular transport (vesicles shuttle between stable cisternae). Golgi functions include: (1) Modification: further glycosylation (processing N-linked glycans, adding O-linked glycans), phosphorylation (adding phosphate groups to proteins), sulfation (adding sulfate to proteoglycans), and proteolytic cleavage; (2) Sorting: proteins are sorted based on their final destinations (secretion, plasma membrane, lysosomes, other organelles) recognized by sorting signals; (3) Packaging: materials are packaged into transport vesicles (COPII-coated for forward transport, COPI-coated for retrograde transport, clathrin-coated for lysosomal/enzyme targeting); (4) Secretion: constitutive secretion (continuous, default pathway) and regulated secretion (stored in secretory vesicles, released upon signal). The Golgi also synthesizes complex polysaccharides (pectin, hemicellulose in plant cell walls, glycosaminoglycans in animal extracellular matrix). Lysosomal enzymes are tagged with mannose-6-phosphate for targeting to lysosomes. Defects in Golgi function cause various diseases: I-cell disease (lysosomal enzyme mistargeting), congenital disorders of glycosylation.', 'Golgi apparatus, Golgi body, cisternae, cis-Golgi network, CGN, medial-Golgi, trans-Golgi network, TGN, cisternal maturation, vesicular transport, modification, glycosylation, N-linked glycans, O-linked glycans, phosphorylation, sulfation, proteoglycans, proteolytic cleavage, sorting, packaging, transport vesicles, COPII vesicles, COPI vesicles, clathrin-coated vesicles, constitutive secretion, regulated secretion, secretory vesicles, lysosomal enzymes, mannose-6-phosphate, polysaccharides, pectin, hemicellulose, glycosaminoglycans, I-cell disease, congenital disorders of glycosylation', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(26, '4. CELL STRUCTURE AND FUNCTIONS', 'Lysosomes', 'Lysosomes are membrane-bound vesicles containing a variety of hydrolytic enzymes (acid hydrolases) that function optimally at acidic pH (around 4.5-5.0). They are the cell\'s digestive system, degrading various biomolecules. Lysosomes contain over 50 different enzymes, including proteases (cathepsins), nucleases (DNases, RNases), glycosidases, lipases, phosphatases, and sulfatases. The lysosomal membrane maintains the acidic internal pH via V-type ATPase (proton pump) and contains highly glycosylated membrane proteins (LAMPs) protecting it from digestion. Lysosomes receive enzymes via two pathways: (1) mannose-6-phosphate receptor pathway (for most soluble enzymes); (2) direct transport (for membrane proteins). Lysosome functions: (1) Phagocytosis: digesting material internalized from outside (bacteria, cellular debris). Phagosomes fuse with lysosomes to form phagolysosomes. (2) Autophagy: digesting worn-out organelles and cytoplasmic components. Autophagosomes (double-membrane vesicles) engulf cytoplasmic material and fuse with lysosomes (autolysosomes). This is crucial for quality control, nutrient recycling, and survival during starvation. (3) Endocytosis: digesting material internalized via receptor-mediated endocytosis. (4) Crinophagy: digesting excess secretory vesicles. (5) Extracellular digestion: lysosomes can release enzymes outside cell (bone resorption by osteoclasts). (6) Programmed cell death (apoptosis): lysosomal enzymes can trigger apoptosis pathways. Lysosomal storage diseases result from deficiencies in specific lysosomal enzymes, leading to accumulation of undegraded substrates: Tay-Sachs disease (hexosaminidase A deficiency, GM2 ganglioside accumulation), Gaucher\'s disease (glucocerebrosidase deficiency), Niemann-Pick disease (sphingomyelinase deficiency). These diseases cause severe neurological and developmental problems.', 'lysosomes, hydrolytic enzymes, acid hydrolases, acidic pH, cathepsins, proteases, nucleases, DNases, RNases, glycosidases, lipases, phosphatases, sulfatases, V-type ATPase, proton pump, LAMPs, mannose-6-phosphate receptor, phagocytosis, phagosomes, phagolysosomes, autophagy, autophagosomes, autolysosomes, quality control, nutrient recycling, endocytosis, receptor-mediated endocytosis, crinophagy, extracellular digestion, osteoclasts, bone resorption, apoptosis, lysosomal storage diseases, Tay-Sachs disease, hexosaminidase A, GM2 ganglioside, Gaucher\'s disease, glucocerebrosidase, Niemann-Pick disease, sphingomyelinase', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(27, '4. CELL STRUCTURE AND FUNCTIONS', 'Centrosomes', 'The centrosome is the main microtubule-organizing center (MTOC) in animal cells, playing crucial roles in cell division and organization of the microtubule cytoskeleton. It typically contains a pair of centrioles arranged perpendicularly, surrounded by pericentriolar material (PCM). Centrioles are barrel-shaped structures approximately 0.5 μm long and 0.2 μm in diameter, composed of nine triplet microtubules arranged in a cylinder (9+0 pattern). Each triplet consists of A-, B-, and C-tubules. Centrioles are involved in organizing the mitotic spindle and forming basal bodies for cilia and flagella. The PCM contains γ-tubulin ring complexes (γ-TuRCs) that nucleate microtubule polymerization, anchoring microtubules with their minus ends at the centrosome and plus ends extending outward. During interphase, the centrosome organizes the microtubule network, influencing cell shape, polarity, motility, and intracellular transport. During cell division, centrosomes duplicate (semi-conservatively) in S phase, with each centriole serving as template for a new daughter centriole. In mitosis, the two centrosomes separate to opposite poles, forming the bipolar mitotic spindle that ensures accurate chromosome segregation. Defects in centrosome number or function can cause aneuploidy and contribute to cancer. Higher plants lack centrosomes with centrioles; they have MTOCs without centrioles, organizing microtubules from nuclear envelope and cortical sites. Basal bodies are modified centrioles that anchor and nucleate axoneme formation in cilia and flagella. Primary cilia (non-motile) function as cellular antennae sensing mechanical and chemical signals.', 'centrosome, MTOC, microtubule-organizing center, animal cells, centrioles, pericentriolar material, PCM, triplet microtubules, A-tubule, B-tubule, C-tubule, 9+0 pattern, γ-tubulin, γ-TuRC, microtubule nucleation, microtubule anchoring, interphase, cell division, centriole duplication, S phase, mitotic spindle, chromosome segregation, aneuploidy, cancer, plants, basal bodies, cilia, flagella, primary cilia, axoneme', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(28, '4. CELL STRUCTURE AND FUNCTIONS', 'Chromosomes', 'Chromosomes are thread-like structures consisting of DNA tightly coiled around histone proteins, forming nucleosomes. They carry genes, the units of heredity. In eukaryotes, chromosomes are linear and are only visible as distinct structures during cell division when they condense. A duplicated chromosome consists of two identical sister chromatids joined at the centromere (primary constriction). The centromere contains specialized histone H3 variant (CENP-A) and is the site of kinetochore assembly for spindle microtubule attachment. Chromosome arms extend from centromere; ends are protected by telomeres. Telomeres are repetitive DNA sequences (TTAGGG in vertebrates) bound by shelterin protein complex, preventing chromosome fusion and degradation. Telomeres shorten with each cell division due to end-replication problem; telomerase (enzyme with RNA template) can extend telomeres in stem cells and germ cells. Chromosome structure involves multiple levels of organization: (1) DNA double helix (2 nm); (2) Nucleosomes (11 nm \"beads on a string\"): DNA (~146 bp) wrapped around histone octamer (H2A, H2B, H3, H4); (3) 30 nm chromatin fiber (solenoid structure stabilized by H1 histone); (4) Loops attached to scaffold proteins; (5) Condensed metaphase chromosome. Chromatin exists in two forms: euchromatin (less condensed, transcriptionally active, gene-rich) and heterochromatin (highly condensed, transcriptionally inactive, gene-poor). Constitutive heterochromatin (permanently condensed, centromeres, telomeres); facultative heterochromatin (developmentally regulated, X-inactivation). Humans have 46 chromosomes (23 pairs): 22 pairs of autosomes and one pair of sex chromosomes (XX female, XY male). Karyotype analysis reveals chromosome number and structure abnormalities. Chromosomal abnormalities include aneuploidy (Down syndrome - trisomy 21, Turner syndrome - XO, Klinefelter syndrome - XXY), deletions (cri-du-chat syndrome), duplications, inversions, and translocations (Philadelphia chromosome in chronic myeloid leukemia).', 'chromosomes, DNA, histones, nucleosomes, genes, heredity, sister chromatids, centromere, CENP-A, kinetochore, chromosome arms, telomeres, TTAGGG, shelterin, end-replication problem, telomerase, DNA double helix, 2 nm, 11 nm fiber, beads on a string, histone octamer, 30 nm chromatin fiber, solenoid, H1 histone, scaffold proteins, condensed metaphase chromosome, euchromatin, heterochromatin, constitutive heterochromatin, facultative heterochromatin, X-inactivation, humans, 46 chromosomes, 23 pairs, autosomes, sex chromosomes, XX, XY, karyotype, aneuploidy, Down syndrome, trisomy 21, Turner syndrome, XO, Klinefelter syndrome, XXY, deletions, cri-du-chat syndrome, duplications, inversions, translocations, Philadelphia chromosome, chronic myeloid leukemia', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(29, '4. CELL STRUCTURE AND FUNCTIONS', 'Similarities and Differences: Plant vs Animal Cells', 'Both plant and animal cells are eukaryotic, sharing fundamental features: membrane-bound nucleus containing genetic material, mitochondria for energy production, endoplasmic reticulum for protein/lipid synthesis, Golgi apparatus for modification and packaging, ribosomes for protein synthesis, plasma membrane regulating transport, and cytoskeleton for structure and movement. They also share basic metabolic pathways (glycolysis, Krebs cycle) and cell division processes (mitosis). Key differences: (1) Cell wall: Plant cells have a rigid cell wall outside the plasma membrane, composed primarily of cellulose, hemicellulose, and pectin, providing structural support, maintaining cell shape, and preventing osmotic lysis. Animal cells lack cell walls, allowing greater flexibility and varied shapes. (2) Chloroplasts: Plant cells contain chloroplasts for photosynthesis (converting light energy to chemical energy). Animal cells lack chloroplasts and must obtain energy by consuming organic matter. (3) Vacuoles: Plant cells typically have a large central vacuole occupying up to 90% of cell volume, maintaining turgor pressure, storing nutrients and waste, and contributing to cell growth. Animal cells may have smaller, temporary vacuoles (phagocytic vesicles, pinocytic vesicles) but no permanent large vacuole. (4) Centrioles: Animal cells have centrioles (part of centrosome) involved in organizing mitotic spindle. Plant cells lack centrioles; spindle organizes from microtubule-organizing centers. (5) Lysosomes: Animal cells typically contain more prominent lysosomes. Plant cells have lysosome-like vacuoles performing similar digestive functions. (6) Cilia/flagella: Present in some animal cells (sperm flagella, epithelial cilia); rare in plants (only in some gametes). (7) Storage: Plants store carbohydrates as starch; animals store as glycogen. (8) Intercellular connections: Plant cells connected by plasmodesmata (cytoplasmic channels); animal cells connected by gap junctions, tight junctions, desmosomes. (9) Cytokinesis: Plant cells divide by forming cell plate; animal cells divide by cleavage furrow.', 'plant cell, animal cell, similarities, differences, cell wall, cellulose, hemicellulose, pectin, structural support, shape maintenance, osmotic lysis, chloroplasts, photosynthesis, central vacuole, turgor pressure, nutrient storage, waste storage, centrioles, mitotic spindle, MTOC, lysosomes, digestive functions, cilia, flagella, starch, glycogen, plasmodesmata, gap junctions, tight junctions, desmosomes, cytokinesis, cell plate, cleavage furrow', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(30, '5. CELL AND ITS ENVIRONMENT', 'Diffusion', 'Diffusion is the passive net movement of molecules or ions from a region of higher concentration to a region of lower concentration, down a concentration gradient. It is driven by the random kinetic motion of particles (Brownian motion) and requires no external energy input. The rate of diffusion is described by Fick\'s laws of diffusion. Fick\'s first law states that the flux (J) is proportional to the concentration gradient (dC/dx): J = -D (dC/dx), where D is the diffusion coefficient. Fick\'s second law describes how concentration changes with time. Factors influencing diffusion rate: (1) Concentration gradient steepness (steeper gradient = faster rate); (2) Temperature (higher temperature increases kinetic energy, faster rate); (3) Molecular size (smaller molecules diffuse faster); (4) Medium viscosity (higher viscosity slows diffusion); (5) Surface area (larger area increases rate); (6) Distance (shorter distance increases rate). In biological systems, diffusion is crucial for: (1) Gas exchange: O₂ and CO₂ diffuse across alveolar membranes in lungs and across capillary walls; O₂ and CO₂ diffuse across stomata and mesophyll cell walls in leaves; (2) Nutrient absorption: digested monomers (glucose, amino acids) diffuse across intestinal epithelium into blood; (3) Waste removal: metabolic wastes (CO₂, urea) diffuse from cells into blood; (4) Signaling: neurotransmitters diffuse across synaptic clefts; (5) Ion movement: ions diffuse through channels in nerve and muscle cells. Diffusion is efficient only over short distances (micrometers), explaining why organisms need circulatory systems and why cells are small.', 'diffusion, passive transport, concentration gradient, net movement, kinetic energy, Brownian motion, Fick\'s laws, Fick\'s first law, flux, diffusion coefficient, Fick\'s second law, factors affecting diffusion, gradient steepness, temperature, molecular size, medium viscosity, surface area, distance, gas exchange, O₂, CO₂, alveoli, capillaries, stomata, mesophyll, nutrient absorption, glucose, amino acids, intestinal epithelium, waste removal, urea, signaling, neurotransmitters, synaptic cleft, ion movement, ion channels, diffusion limitation, short distances', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(31, '5. CELL AND ITS ENVIRONMENT', 'Osmosis', 'Osmosis is the net movement of water molecules across a selectively permeable membrane from a region of higher water potential (higher concentration of water, lower solute concentration) to a region of lower water potential (lower water concentration, higher solute concentration). Water potential (Ψ, measured in pressure units - kilopascals kPa or megapascals MPa) is the chemical potential of water, determining the direction of water movement. Pure water at standard conditions has water potential of zero. Addition of solute makes water potential negative (more negative with higher solute concentration). Water potential (Ψ) is sum of solute potential (Ψs, osmotic potential, always negative) and pressure potential (Ψp, turgor pressure, positive in plant cells, negative in xylem). For plant cells: Ψ = Ψs + Ψp. Osmosis is driven by differences in water potential: water moves from higher (less negative) to lower (more negative) water potential. Osmotic pressure (π) is the pressure required to prevent water movement across a selectively permeable membrane; related to solute concentration by van\'t Hoff equation: π = iCRT, where i = ionization constant, C = molar concentration, R = gas constant, T = absolute temperature. In biological systems, osmosis is vital for: (1) Water absorption by root hairs from soil; (2) Stomatal opening and closing via guard cell turgor changes; (3) Cell turgor maintenance in plants; (4) Osmoregulation in freshwater organisms; (5) Reabsorption of water in kidney tubules; (6) Fluid balance in animal tissues. Terms: hypotonic (lower solute concentration outside cell), hypertonic (higher solute concentration outside), isotonic (equal solute concentration).', 'osmosis, water movement, selectively permeable membrane, water potential, Ψ, solute potential, Ψs, osmotic potential, pressure potential, Ψp, turgor pressure, kilopascals, kPa, megapascals, MPa, pure water, zero potential, negative potential, osmotic pressure, π, van\'t Hoff equation, iCRT, ionization constant, gas constant, R, absolute temperature, T, root hairs, soil water, stomata, guard cells, turgor, osmoregulation, freshwater organisms, kidney tubules, fluid balance, hypotonic, hypertonic, isotonic', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(32, '5. CELL AND ITS ENVIRONMENT', 'Osmosis Effects on Cells', 'The response of cells to osmotic conditions depends on their environment and structural features. Animal cells (lacking cell walls): (1) Hypotonic solution: lower solute concentration outside than inside. Water enters cell by osmosis, causing cell to swell and potentially burst (haemolysis in red blood cells). This is why intravenous fluids must be isotonic with blood. (2) Hypertonic solution: higher solute concentration outside than inside. Water leaves cell, causing cell to shrink and become crenated (red blood cells show characteristic scalloped appearance). (3) Isotonic solution: equal solute concentration inside and outside. No net water movement; cell maintains normal shape (e.g., 0.9% NaCl solution for mammals, 0.16 M NaCl). Plant cells (with cell walls): (1) Hypotonic solution: water enters cell by osmosis, but cell wall prevents bursting. Cell becomes turgid (firm), with protoplast pressing against cell wall. Turgor pressure (Ψp positive) balances osmotic pressure. This is normal state for healthy plant tissue, providing mechanical support. (2) Hypertonic solution: water leaves cell. Protoplast shrinks away from cell wall (plasmolysis). Initially, cell becomes flaccid (limp). As water loss continues, plasma membrane detaches from cell wall (incipient plasmolysis), then complete plasmolysis with protoplast contracted. Plasmolyzed cells cause wilting in plants. (3) Isotonic solution: no net movement, cell becomes flaccid (no turgor). Plant wilts. These principles apply to microorganisms: bacteria in hypotonic environments may undergo osmotic lysis if cell wall compromised (lysozyme treatment). Contractile vacuoles in freshwater protozoa (Paramecium, Amoeba) actively expel excess water to prevent osmotic lysis.', 'hypotonic, hypertonic, isotonic, animal cells, red blood cells, haemolysis, lysis, crenation, shrivel, intravenous fluids, 0.9% NaCl, plant cells, cell wall, turgid, turgor pressure, protoplast, mechanical support, flaccid, plasmolysis, incipient plasmolysis, complete plasmolysis, protoplast contraction, wilting, bacteria, osmotic lysis, lysozyme, contractile vacuole, Paramecium, Amoeba, osmoregulation, freshwater protozoa', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(33, '5. CELL AND ITS ENVIRONMENT', 'Active Transport', 'Active transport is the movement of molecules or ions across a membrane against their concentration gradient (from low to high concentration), requiring energy input (ATP) and specific carrier proteins (pumps). Unlike diffusion and osmosis (passive processes), active transport can accumulate substances on one side of membrane. Primary active transport directly uses ATP hydrolysis for energy. The sodium-potassium pump (Na⁺/K⁺ ATPase) is a classic example: it pumps 3 Na⁺ ions out of the cell and 2 K⁺ ions into the cell per ATP hydrolyzed, maintaining the electrochemical gradient essential for nerve impulse transmission, nutrient uptake, and osmotic balance. This pump establishes the resting membrane potential (typically -70 mV in animal cells). Other primary active transporters: calcium pump (Ca²⁺ ATPase) sequesters Ca²⁺ into ER/sarcoplasmic reticulum or pumps out of cell; proton pump (H⁺ ATPase) in plant and fungal cells, and in lysosomal membranes. Secondary active transport (coupled transport) uses energy stored in electrochemical gradients established by primary active transport. Symport (cotransport) moves two substances in same direction: Na⁺-glucose symporter (SGLT) uses Na⁺ gradient to import glucose against its gradient in intestinal and kidney cells. Antiport (exchange) moves two substances in opposite directions: Na⁺/Ca²⁺ exchanger uses Na⁺ gradient to export Ca²⁺. Active transport is crucial for: (1) Mineral ion uptake by root hairs from soil (against concentration gradient); (2) Glucose and amino acid reabsorption in kidney tubules; (3) Neurotransmitter reuptake at synapses; (4) Maintaining ion gradients for nerve and muscle function; (5) Secreting substances (H⁺ in stomach parietal cells, I⁻ in thyroid follicular cells); (6) Drug efflux pumps in bacteria and cancer cells (multidrug resistance).', 'active transport, concentration gradient, energy, ATP, carrier proteins, pumps, primary active transport, ATP hydrolysis, sodium-potassium pump, Na⁺/K⁺ ATPase, 3 Na⁺ out, 2 K⁺ in, electrochemical gradient, resting membrane potential, -70 mV, calcium pump, Ca²⁺ ATPase, sarcoplasmic reticulum, proton pump, H⁺ ATPase, lysosomes, secondary active transport, coupled transport, symport, cotransport, Na⁺-glucose symporter, SGLT, antiport, exchange, Na⁺/Ca²⁺ exchanger, mineral ion uptake, root hairs, glucose reabsorption, amino acid reabsorption, kidney tubules, neurotransmitter reuptake, ion gradients, nerve function, muscle function, secretion, stomach parietal cells, H⁺ secretion, thyroid follicular cells, I⁻ uptake, drug efflux pumps, multidrug resistance', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(34, '5. CELL AND ITS ENVIRONMENT', 'Significance of Transport Processes', 'The integrated functioning of diffusion, osmosis, and active transport is essential for cellular and organismal homeostasis. These processes collectively regulate the internal environment despite external fluctuations. Key roles: (1) Nutrient uptake: cells acquire glucose, amino acids, fatty acids, vitamins, and minerals necessary for metabolism, growth, and repair. Intestinal epithelial cells absorb digested nutrients via combination of diffusion and active transport. (2) Waste removal: metabolic wastes (CO₂, urea, ammonia) diffuse out of cells or are actively transported into blood for elimination by excretory organs. (3) Cell volume regulation: osmotic balance maintains appropriate cell size; disruptions cause swelling or shrinkage, impairing function. (4) Maintenance of cell turgor: in plants, turgor pressure from water uptake provides mechanical support and drives cell expansion. (5) Establishment of electrochemical gradients: ion gradients (Na⁺/K⁺, Ca²⁺, H⁺) power secondary active transport, generate electrical signals in neurons and muscle, and drive ATP synthesis in mitochondria. (6) pH regulation: active transport of H⁺ and HCO₃⁻ maintains intracellular and extracellular pH. (7) Signaling: transport processes generate and transmit signals (neurotransmitter release, hormone secretion, action potentials). (8) Cellular defense: multidrug resistance pumps export toxins; phagocytes use membrane transport to kill pathogens. (9) Water balance: osmoregulation in aquatic organisms prevents excessive water gain/loss. Disruptions to these transport processes underlie numerous diseases: cystic fibrosis (defective Cl⁻ channel), diabetes insipidus (ADH receptor dysfunction), cholera (toxin-induced Cl⁻ secretion causing diarrhea), digitalis toxicity (Na⁺/K⁺ ATPase inhibition), and many channelopathies.', 'transport processes, homeostasis, nutrient uptake, glucose, amino acids, fatty acids, vitamins, minerals, intestinal absorption, waste removal, CO₂, urea, ammonia, excretory organs, cell volume regulation, osmotic balance, turgor pressure, plant support, electrochemical gradients, ion gradients, secondary active transport, electrical signals, neurons, muscle, ATP synthesis, mitochondria, pH regulation, H⁺, HCO₃⁻, intracellular pH, extracellular pH, signaling, neurotransmitter release, hormone secretion, action potentials, cellular defense, multidrug resistance, phagocytes, osmoregulation, water balance, aquatic organisms, diseases, cystic fibrosis, Cl⁻ channel, diabetes insipidus, ADH, cholera, toxin, diarrhea, digitalis toxicity, Na⁺/K⁺ ATPase inhibition, channelopathies', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(35, '6. PROPERTIES OF LIVING CELL', 'Nutrition - Autotrophic (Photosynthesis)', 'Autotrophic nutrition is the process by which organisms synthesize complex organic compounds from simple inorganic sources using an external energy source. Photoautotrophs (plants, algae, cyanobacteria) use light energy; chemoautotrophs (some bacteria) use energy from oxidation of inorganic compounds. Photosynthesis converts light energy to chemical energy stored in carbohydrates. Overall equation: 6CO₂ + 6H₂O + light energy → C₆H₁₂O₆ + 6O₂. This process occurs in chloroplasts and involves two major stages: Light-dependent reactions: Occur in thylakoid membranes. Photosystems (PSII and PSI) containing chlorophyll and accessory pigments absorb light, exciting electrons. Electron transport chain (cytochrome b6f complex, plastoquinone, plastocyanin) transfers electrons, pumping protons into thylakoid lumen, creating proton gradient. Photophosphorylation: ATP synthase uses proton gradient to synthesize ATP. Two types: non-cyclic (produces ATP and NADPH, involves both photosystems, water split releasing O₂) and cyclic (produces only ATP, involves only PSI). NADP⁺ reductase reduces NADP⁺ to NADPH using electrons from PSI. Photolysis: oxygen-evolving complex of PSII splits water: 2H₂O → 4H⁺ + 4e⁻ + O₂. Light-independent reactions (Calvin cycle): Occur in stroma. CO₂ fixation: enzyme rubisco (ribulose bisphosphate carboxylase/oxygenase) combines CO₂ with ribulose bisphosphate (RuBP, 5C), producing unstable 6C intermediate that splits into two 3-phosphoglycerate (3PGA, 3C). Reduction: ATP and NADPH from light reactions convert 3PGA to glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate (G3P). Regeneration: some G3P used to synthesize glucose, fructose, starch; most regenerates RuBP using ATP. One glucose requires 6 Calvin cycle turns, 18 ATP, 12 NADPH. Photosynthesis also produces other compounds: amino acids, fatty acids, nucleotides. C4 plants (maize, sugarcane) and CAM plants (cacti, succulents) have adaptations to reduce photorespiration (rubisco oxygenase activity) in hot, dry conditions.', 'autotrophic nutrition, photosynthesis, photoautotrophs, chemoautotrophs, light energy, chemical energy, equation, 6CO₂, 6H₂O, C₆H₁₂O₆, 6O₂, chloroplasts, light-dependent reactions, thylakoid membranes, photosystems, PSII, PSI, chlorophyll, accessory pigments, electron transport chain, cytochrome b6f, plastoquinone, plastocyanin, proton gradient, photophosphorylation, ATP synthase, non-cyclic photophosphorylation, cyclic photophosphorylation, NADPH, NADP⁺ reductase, photolysis, oxygen-evolving complex, light-independent reactions, Calvin cycle, stroma, carbon fixation, rubisco, RuBP, ribulose bisphosphate, 3PGA, 3-phosphoglycerate, reduction, G3P, glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate, regeneration, glucose, fructose, starch, C4 plants, maize, sugarcane, CAM plants, cacti, succulents, photorespiration', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(36, '6. PROPERTIES OF LIVING CELL', 'Nutrition in Euglena and Chlamydomonas', 'Euglena and Chlamydomonas exhibit mixotrophic nutrition, demonstrating evolutionary plasticity and adaptation to variable environments. They are primarily photoautotrophic, containing chloroplasts for photosynthesis when light is available. Their chloroplasts contain chlorophyll a and b, similar to green plants. However, in prolonged darkness or when organic carbon sources are abundant, they can switch to heterotrophic modes. Euglena absorbs organic nutrients (amino acids, fatty acids) through its pellicle (osmotrophy) and can also ingest particles via a rudimentary phagotrophic mechanism. It can survive indefinitely in darkness if supplied with organic carbon. Chlamydomonas, when deprived of light, can utilize acetate as a carbon source (acetate flagellates). Some species can also perform phagocytosis of bacteria. This mixotrophic capability provides ecological flexibility, allowing survival in fluctuating light conditions and colonization of diverse habitats. The dual nutritional strategy highlights the evolutionary link between plant-like autotrophy and animal-like heterotrophy, suggesting that early eukaryotes may have been mixotrophic. The presence of an eyespot (stigma) in both genera allows phototaxis (movement toward optimal light) when photosynthesis is advantageous. This regulatory mechanism optimizes energy acquisition. Studies of these organisms have revealed much about chloroplast evolution, photosynthesis regulation, and the metabolic integration of autotrophic and heterotrophic pathways. They serve as model organisms for investigating the trade-offs between different nutritional modes and the evolutionary transitions to obligate autotrophy or heterotrophy.', 'Euglena, Chlamydomonas, mixotrophic, mixotroph, photoautotrophic, chloroplasts, photosynthesis, chlorophyll a, chlorophyll b, light availability, darkness, organic carbon, heterotrophic, osmotrophy, pellicle, phagotrophy, phagocytosis, acetate, acetate flagellates, ecological flexibility, fluctuating light, evolutionary link, autotrophy, heterotrophy, eyespot, stigma, phototaxis, optimal light, chloroplast evolution, photosynthesis regulation, metabolic integration, model organisms, nutritional modes, evolutionary transitions', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(37, '6. PROPERTIES OF LIVING CELL', 'Nutrition in Amoeba and Paramecium', 'Amoeba and Paramecium exhibit holozoic nutrition (animal-like nutrition involving ingestion of solid food). Their feeding mechanisms illustrate adaptations for capturing and digesting prey. Amoeba proteus feeds on bacteria, algae, and other protozoa. Feeding process: (1) Chemotaxis: detects prey via chemical signals. (2) Phagocytosis: extends pseudopodia around prey, engulfing it into a food vacuole (phagosome). Pseudopodia formation involves actin polymerization driven by Arp2/3 complex and formins. (3) Food vacuole formation: membrane encloses prey, pinches off into cytoplasm. (4) Phagosome maturation: vacuole acidifies (V-ATPase pumps H⁺), fuses with lysosomes containing hydrolytic enzymes (proteases, nucleases, glycosidases, lipases). (5) Digestion: enzymes break down macromolecules into absorbable monomers. (6) Absorption: nutrients diffuse or are transported into cytoplasm. (7) Egestion: undigested remains expelled by exocytosis (reverse phagocytosis). Paramecium caudatum uses ciliary feeding. (1) Ciliary currents: coordinated ciliary beating creates water currents directing food particles (bacteria, yeast) toward oral groove. (2) Oral groove and gullet: food particles swept into cytostome (cell mouth) and cytopharynx (gullet). (3) Food vacuole formation: at end of gullet, food vacuoles form regularly (one per minute). (4) Vacuole circulation: vacuoles follow defined path through cytoplasm (cyclosis), fusing with lysosomes for digestion. (5) Absorption and egestion: nutrients absorbed, undigested material expelled through anal pore (cytoproct). Both organisms demonstrate intracellular digestion, contrasting with extracellular digestion in higher animals. Their digestive efficiency depends on enzyme diversity, vacuole pH regulation, and vacuole-lysosome fusion. These processes are regulated by signaling pathways and cytoskeletal dynamics.', 'Amoeba, Paramecium, holozoic nutrition, phagocytosis, chemotaxis, pseudopodia, food vacuole, phagosome, actin polymerization, Arp2/3, formins, acidification, V-ATPase, lysosomes, hydrolytic enzymes, proteases, nucleases, glycosidases, lipases, digestion, absorption, egestion, exocytosis, ciliary feeding, Paramecium, cilia, ciliary beating, oral groove, gullet, cytostome, cytopharynx, food vacuole formation, cyclosis, vacuole circulation, anal pore, cytoproct, intracellular digestion, extracellular digestion, enzyme diversity, pH regulation, vacuole-lysosome fusion, signaling pathways, cytoskeletal dynamics', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(38, '6. PROPERTIES OF LIVING CELL', 'Cellular Respiration - Aerobic', 'Aerobic respiration is the complete oxidation of glucose to CO₂ and H₂O in the presence of oxygen, releasing large amounts of energy stored as ATP. Overall equation: C₆H₁₂O₆ + 6O₂ → 6CO₂ + 6H₂O + 36-38 ATP. This exergonic process (ΔG = -2870 kJ/mol) captures about 40% of energy as ATP. Stages: (1) Glycolysis (occurs in cytoplasm): Glucose (6C) is phosphorylated (using 2 ATP) and split into two glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate (G3P). G3P oxidized to pyruvate (3C), producing 4 ATP (net gain 2 ATP) and 2 NADH. Key enzymes: hexokinase, phosphofructokinase (rate-limiting), pyruvate kinase. Glycolysis occurs in all organisms, suggesting ancient origin. (2) Pyruvate oxidation (mitochondrial matrix): Pyruvate transported into mitochondria, converted to acetyl-CoA (2C) by pyruvate dehydrogenase complex, releasing CO₂ and producing 1 NADH per pyruvate (2 per glucose). (3) Krebs cycle (citric acid cycle, TCA cycle) (mitochondrial matrix): Acetyl-CoA (2C) combines with oxaloacetate (4C) to form citrate (6C). Through 8 reactions, citrate oxidized back to oxaloacetate, releasing 2 CO₂, producing 3 NADH, 1 FADH₂, and 1 ATP (per acetyl-CoA; per glucose: 2 acetyl-CoA → 6 NADH, 2 FADH₂, 2 ATP). Key enzymes: citrate synthase, isocitrate dehydrogenase, α-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase. (4) Electron transport chain (ETC) and oxidative phosphorylation (inner mitochondrial membrane): NADH and FADH₂ donate electrons to ETC complexes I-IV. Electron flow pumps protons from matrix to intermembrane space, creating proton-motive force. Complexes: I (NADH dehydrogenase), II (succinate dehydrogenase, also Krebs cycle enzyme), III (cytochrome bc1 complex), IV (cytochrome c oxidase, reduces O₂ to H₂O). Mobile carriers: ubiquinone (coenzyme Q), cytochrome c. ATP synthase (complex V) uses proton flow back to matrix to phosphorylate ADP (chemiosmosis, Peter Mitchell\'s theory). Each NADH yields ~2.5 ATP, each FADH₂ ~1.5 ATP. Oxygen is final electron acceptor, essential for ATP production.', 'aerobic respiration, glucose oxidation, CO₂, H₂O, ATP, equation, exergonic, ΔG, glycolysis, cytoplasm, glucose phosphorylation, G3P, glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate, pyruvate, hexokinase, phosphofructokinase, rate-limiting, pyruvate kinase, ancient origin, pyruvate oxidation, mitochondrial matrix, acetyl-CoA, pyruvate dehydrogenase complex, Krebs cycle, citric acid cycle, TCA cycle, oxaloacetate, citrate, isocitrate dehydrogenase, α-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase, citrate synthase, NADH, FADH₂, electron transport chain, ETC, oxidative phosphorylation, inner mitochondrial membrane, complexes I-IV, proton pumping, proton-motive force, complex I, NADH dehydrogenase, complex II, succinate dehydrogenase, complex III, cytochrome bc1, complex IV, cytochrome c oxidase, ubiquinone, coenzyme Q, cytochrome c, ATP synthase, complex V, chemiosmosis, Peter Mitchell, proton flow, oxygen, final electron acceptor', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(39, '6. PROPERTIES OF LIVING CELL', 'Cellular Respiration - Anaerobic', 'Anaerobic respiration (fermentation) is the incomplete oxidation of glucose in the absence of oxygen, yielding small amounts of ATP and organic end products. It occurs when oxygen is unavailable or when cells cannot meet energy demands aerobically (e.g., exercising muscle). Two main types: (1) Alcoholic fermentation (yeast, some bacteria): Glucose → 2 pyruvate (glycolysis) → 2 ethanol + 2 CO₂. Pyruvate decarboxylated to acetaldehyde by pyruvate decarboxylase (yeast enzyme). Acetaldehyde reduced to ethanol by alcohol dehydrogenase, regenerating NAD⁺ from NADH (essential for glycolysis to continue). Net yield: 2 ATP per glucose. Used in baking (CO₂ raises dough) and brewing (ethanol production). (2) Lactic acid fermentation (animal cells, some bacteria): Glucose → 2 pyruvate (glycolysis) → 2 lactate. Pyruvate reduced directly to lactate by lactate dehydrogenase, regenerating NAD⁺. Net yield: 2 ATP per glucose. Occurs in: (a) Skeletal muscle during vigorous exercise when O₂ supply limited; lactate accumulation contributes to muscle fatigue and soreness. (b) Some bacteria (Lactobacillus, Streptococcus) in yogurt, cheese, pickles, sauerkraut production. (c) Red blood cells (no mitochondria, rely solely on glycolysis). Other fermentation types: propionic acid fermentation (Swiss cheese), butyric acid fermentation (Clostridium), mixed acid fermentation (E. coli). Anaerobic respiration yields only 2 ATP vs 36-38 in aerobic respiration, making it much less efficient. However, it allows ATP production when oxygen scarce, enabling survival in anoxic environments (deep sediments, waterlogged soils, animal digestive tracts). Some organisms are obligate anaerobes (cannot survive with oxygen), facultative anaerobes (can switch between modes), or aerotolerant anaerobes (tolerate oxygen but don\'t use it).', 'anaerobic respiration, fermentation, glucose oxidation, absence of oxygen, ATP yield, organic end products, alcoholic fermentation, yeast, bacteria, ethanol, CO₂, pyruvate decarboxylase, acetaldehyde, alcohol dehydrogenase, NAD⁺ regeneration, baking, brewing, lactic acid fermentation, animal cells, lactate, lactate dehydrogenase, skeletal muscle, vigorous exercise, muscle fatigue, muscle soreness, Lactobacillus, Streptococcus, yogurt, cheese, pickles, sauerkraut, red blood cells, propionic acid fermentation, butyric acid fermentation, Clostridium, mixed acid fermentation, E. coli, efficiency, 2 ATP vs 36-38 ATP, anoxic environments, obligate anaerobes, facultative anaerobes, aerotolerant anaerobes', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(40, '6. PROPERTIES OF LIVING CELL', 'Role of ATP', 'Adenosine triphosphate (ATP) is the primary energy currency of all living cells, coupling exergonic and endergonic reactions. Structure: adenosine (adenine + ribose) linked to three phosphate groups. The phosphoanhydride bonds between phosphates are high-energy bonds (ΔG of hydrolysis ~ -30.5 kJ/mol for ATP → ADP + Pi; ~ -45.6 kJ/mol for ATP → AMP + PPi). ATP hydrolysis releases energy that drives cellular work. ATP is continuously regenerated from ADP and Pi during catabolic pathways (glycolysis, Krebs cycle, oxidative phosphorylation, fermentation). ATP functions: (1) Chemical work: drives biosynthesis of macromolecules (proteins from amino acids, nucleic acids from nucleotides, polysaccharides from sugars). Aminoacyl-tRNA synthetase uses ATP to charge tRNAs for protein synthesis. (2) Mechanical work: powers muscle contraction (myosin ATPase), cytoskeletal motor proteins (kinesin, dynein moving along microtubules), chromosome segregation (spindle motors), and cell motility (flagellar dynein). (3) Transport work: fuels active transport pumps (Na⁺/K⁺ ATPase, Ca²⁺ ATPase, H⁺ ATPase) moving ions against gradients. (4) Signal transduction: ATP used for protein phosphorylation by kinases, regulating enzyme activity, gene expression, and cellular responses. (5) Activation of metabolites: ATP phosphorylates glucose (hexokinase) to trap it in cells and commit to glycolysis; activates fatty acids (acyl-CoA synthetase) for oxidation or lipid synthesis. (6) Thermoregulation: uncoupling proteins (UCP1 in brown adipose tissue) dissipate proton gradient, generating heat. ATP concentration in cells is maintained around 1-10 mM, with total daily turnover matching body weight. ATP also serves as precursor for other nucleotides (GTP, UTP, CTP) used in specific reactions (GTP in protein synthesis, signal transduction; UTP in glycogen synthesis; CTP in phospholipid synthesis).', 'ATP, adenosine triphosphate, energy currency, exergonic, endergonic, structure, adenosine, adenine, ribose, phosphate groups, phosphoanhydride bonds, high-energy bonds, hydrolysis, ΔG, ADP, Pi, AMP, PPi, energy release, regeneration, glycolysis, Krebs cycle, oxidative phosphorylation, fermentation, chemical work, biosynthesis, proteins, amino acids, nucleic acids, nucleotides, polysaccharides, sugars, aminoacyl-tRNA synthetase, tRNA charging, mechanical work, muscle contraction, myosin ATPase, cytoskeleton, kinesin, dynein, microtubules, chromosome segregation, spindle motors, cell motility, flagellar dynein, transport work, active transport, Na⁺/K⁺ ATPase, Ca²⁺ ATPase, H⁺ ATPase, ion gradients, signal transduction, protein phosphorylation, kinases, enzyme regulation, gene expression, metabolite activation, hexokinase, glucose phosphorylation, acyl-CoA synthetase, fatty acid activation, thermoregulation, uncoupling proteins, UCP1, brown adipose tissue, heat generation, ATP concentration, turnover, GTP, UTP, CTP, protein synthesis, glycogen synthesis, phospholipid synthesis', '2026-02-27 15:40:30');
INSERT INTO `biology_syllabus_comprehensive` (`id`, `topic`, `sub_topic`, `note`, `keywords`, `created_at`) VALUES
(41, '6. PROPERTIES OF LIVING CELL', 'Anaerobic Respiration in Food Processing', 'Anaerobic respiration (fermentation) by microorganisms is fundamental to food production and preservation worldwide. These processes exploit microbial metabolism to transform raw ingredients, enhance flavor, improve digestibility, and extend shelf life. Alcoholic fermentation by Saccharomyces cerevisiae (brewer\'s/baker\'s yeast): (1) Bread-making: Yeast ferments sugars in dough, producing CO₂ that forms gas bubbles, causing dough to rise. Ethanol evaporates during baking. Flavor compounds (esters, aldehydes) contribute to bread aroma. Sourdough uses lactic acid bacteria with yeast for complex flavor. (2) Beer production: Malted barley provides fermentable sugars. Hops add bitterness and antimicrobial compounds. Yeast fermentation produces ethanol and CO₂. Different yeast strains (ale vs lager) and fermentation conditions yield diverse beer styles. (3) Wine production: Grapes provide sugars and flavor compounds. Wild or cultured yeasts ferment juice (must) to wine. Malolactic fermentation (lactic acid bacteria) may follow, converting malic acid to milder lactic acid. (4) Distilled spirits: Fermented mash distilled to concentrate alcohol (whiskey, vodka, rum, gin). Lactic acid fermentation by various bacteria (Lactobacillus, Lactococcus, Streptococcus, Leuconostoc): (1) Yogurt: Lactobacillus bulgaricus and Streptococcus thermophilus ferment lactose to lactic acid, coagulating milk proteins (casein), creating thick texture and tangy flavor. (2) Cheese: Lactic acid bacteria acidify milk, aiding rennet coagulation. Different bacteria contribute to specific cheese characteristics (Swiss cheese: Propionibacterium produces CO₂ holes). (3) Fermented vegetables: Sauerkraut (cabbage) and kimchi (Korean fermented vegetables) rely on natural lactic acid bacteria. Salt inhibits spoilage organisms, favors lactobacilli. (4) Pickles: Cucumbers fermented in brine by lactic acid bacteria. (5) Fermented sausages: Salami, pepperoni use lactic acid bacteria for preservation and flavor. (6) Fermented fish products: Asian fish sauces, pastes. Acetic acid fermentation (Acetobacter) converts ethanol to vinegar. Other fermentations: propionic acid (Swiss cheese), butyric acid (some cheeses), citric acid (Aspergillus niger), soy sauce (koji mold + lactic yeast fermentation), tempeh (Rhizopus mold fermentation of soybeans).', 'anaerobic respiration, fermentation, food processing, food preservation, microbial metabolism, flavor enhancement, digestibility, shelf life, alcoholic fermentation, Saccharomyces cerevisiae, yeast, bread-making, CO₂, dough rising, ethanol, baking, flavor compounds, esters, aldehydes, sourdough, lactic acid bacteria, beer production, malted barley, hops, ethanol, ale, lager, wine production, grapes, must, malolactic fermentation, distilled spirits, whiskey, vodka, rum, gin, lactic acid fermentation, Lactobacillus, Lactococcus, Streptococcus, Leuconostoc, yogurt, Lactobacillus bulgaricus, Streptococcus thermophilus, lactose, milk proteins, casein, cheese, rennet, Propionibacterium, sauerkraut, cabbage, kimchi, Korean fermented vegetables, pickles, cucumbers, brine, fermented sausages, salami, pepperoni, fermented fish, fish sauces, pastes, acetic acid fermentation, Acetobacter, vinegar, propionic acid fermentation, butyric acid fermentation, citric acid fermentation, Aspergillus niger, soy sauce, koji mold, tempeh, Rhizopus, soybeans', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(42, '6. PROPERTIES OF LIVING CELL', 'Excretion in Single-celled Organisms', 'Unicellular organisms lack specialized excretory organs but efficiently eliminate metabolic wastes through simple mechanisms adapted to their environments. These processes maintain internal homeostasis and prevent toxic accumulation. (1) Diffusion: Small, lipid-soluble molecules (CO₂, O₂, ammonia) diffuse directly across the plasma membrane down concentration gradients. CO₂ produced during respiration diffuses out; in aquatic environments, it dissolves or reacts with water. Ammonia (NH₃), the primary nitrogenous waste from protein metabolism, is highly toxic but very soluble; in aquatic environments, it diffuses rapidly and is diluted. This is efficient only in small organisms with high surface area-to-volume ratios. (2) Contractile vacuole: Found in freshwater protozoa (Amoeba, Paramecium, Euglena) and some marine/soil species. Freshwater environments are hypotonic; water constantly enters by osmosis. Contractile vacuole collects excess water and actively expels it, preventing osmotic lysis. Structure: central vacuole surrounded by radiating tubules or spongiome. Function: (a) Water enters spongiome by osmosis; (b) Tubules coalesce, transferring water to central vacuole; (c) Vacuole expands, moves to cell surface; (d) Vacuole fuses with plasma membrane, expels water (exocytosis). Cycle repeats every few seconds to minutes, regulated by osmotic conditions. In Paramecium, two contractile vacuoles operate alternately. ATP-dependent proton pumps and aquaporins facilitate water collection. (3) Exocytosis: Undigested food residues from food vacuoles (Amoeba, Paramecium) are expelled by exocytosis. (4) Passive excretion through cell surface: Some wastes (urea, certain ions) may diffuse or be transported out. (5) Encystment: Under unfavorable conditions, some protozoa form resistant cysts, accumulating wastes in dormant state. These mechanisms demonstrate how single cells maintain internal environment despite external challenges, illustrating fundamental principles of homeostasis at the cellular level.', 'excretion, single-celled organisms, unicellular, homeostasis, metabolic wastes, diffusion, CO₂, O₂, ammonia, NH₃, plasma membrane, concentration gradients, nitrogenous waste, protein metabolism, toxic, soluble, aquatic environments, surface area-to-volume ratio, contractile vacuole, freshwater protozoa, Amoeba, Paramecium, Euglena, hypotonic, osmosis, osmotic lysis, spongiome, tubules, water collection, central vacuole, exocytosis, ATP-dependent proton pumps, aquaporins, Paramecium two contractile vacuoles, exocytosis, food vacuoles, undigested food residues, passive excretion, urea, ions, encystment, resistant cysts, homeostasis, cellular level', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(43, '6. PROPERTIES OF LIVING CELL', 'Waste Products of Metabolism', 'Cellular metabolism generates diverse waste products that must be eliminated or detoxified to maintain homeostasis. Major categories: (1) Carbon dioxide (CO₂): Produced during aerobic respiration (Krebs cycle, pyruvate oxidation) and fermentation. CO₂ dissolves in water forming carbonic acid (H₂CO₃), affecting pH. Transported in blood as bicarbonate (HCO₃⁻), dissolved CO₂, and carbamino compounds. Excreted via lungs (vertebrates), gills (fish), stomata (plants), or diffusion (small organisms). (2) Water: Produced in respiration (especially electron transport chain), dehydration synthesis reactions, and as byproduct of various pathways. Excess water excreted by kidneys (animals), transpiration (plants), contractile vacuoles (protozoa). (3) Nitrogenous wastes: From protein and nucleic acid catabolism. Ammonia (NH₃): Highly toxic, very soluble. Produced by deamination of amino acids. Requires large water volumes for dilution. Excreted by most aquatic animals (fish, aquatic invertebrates). Urea: Produced in liver (ornithine cycle/urea cycle) from ammonia and CO₂. Less toxic, requires less water. Main excretory product of mammals, adult amphibians, some fish. Uric acid: Insoluble white paste/crystals. Produced from purine breakdown and amino acid metabolism. Minimal water requirement. Excreted by birds, reptiles, insects, terrestrial gastropods. Advantageous for water conservation. (4) Salts and ions: Excess Na⁺, K⁺, Cl⁻, Ca²⁺, Mg²⁺ excreted by kidneys (animals), salt glands (marine birds, reptiles), or specialized cells. (5) Bile pigments: Bilirubin from heme breakdown (senescent red blood cells). Processed by liver, excreted in bile (feces). (6) Other metabolic byproducts: Lactic acid (anaerobic respiration in muscles), urates, creatinine (muscle creatine breakdown), hippuric acid, various drugs and toxins after liver processing. In plants, waste products often stored rather than excreted: (1) Oxygen (photosynthesis byproduct) diffuses out; (2) CO₂ (respiration) diffuses out; (3) Secondary metabolites (alkaloids, tannins, resins, essential oils, latex) stored in vacuoles, cell walls, or specialized structures; (4) Calcium oxalate crystals stored in vacuoles; (5) Senescent leaves, bark, fruits shed with accumulated wastes.', 'waste products, metabolism, homeostasis, carbon dioxide, CO₂, aerobic respiration, Krebs cycle, fermentation, carbonic acid, H₂CO₃, pH, bicarbonate, HCO₃⁻, lungs, gills, stomata, diffusion, water, electron transport chain, dehydration synthesis, kidneys, transpiration, contractile vacuole, nitrogenous wastes, protein catabolism, nucleic acid catabolism, ammonia, NH₃, toxic, soluble, deamination, aquatic animals, urea, ornithine cycle, urea cycle, liver, less toxic, mammals, adult amphibians, uric acid, insoluble, purine breakdown, birds, reptiles, insects, terrestrial gastropods, water conservation, salts, ions, Na⁺, K⁺, Cl⁻, Ca²⁺, Mg²⁺, salt glands, bile pigments, bilirubin, heme breakdown, red blood cells, liver, bile, feces, lactic acid, anaerobic respiration, creatinine, muscle metabolism, hippuric acid, drugs, toxins, plant wastes, oxygen, photosynthesis, secondary metabolites, alkaloids, tannins, resins, essential oils, latex, vacuoles, cell walls, calcium oxalate crystals, senescence, leaf abscission', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(44, '6. PROPERTIES OF LIVING CELL', 'Growth - Basis of Growth', 'Growth in living organisms is a fundamental characteristic involving an irreversible increase in size, mass, and often complexity. At the cellular level, growth is based on three integrated processes: (1) Cell division (mitosis): Increases cell number (hyperplasia). Essential for development (embryo to adult), regeneration, and replacing lost cells. Regulated by cell cycle checkpoints and growth factors. (2) Cell enlargement (hypertrophy): Individual cells increase in volume through water uptake (vacuole expansion in plants), synthesis of new cytoplasm (proteins, organelles), and deposition of extracellular material (cell wall in plants, matrix in animals). (3) Cell differentiation: Cells become specialized in structure and function through differential gene expression. Develop specific characteristics (e.g., muscle cells with contractile proteins, neurons with axons/dendrites, red blood cells with hemoglobin). Differentiation is usually irreversible (except stem cells). Growth patterns: (a) Isometric growth: all parts grow at same rate (simple organisms, early embryos). (b) Allometric growth: different parts grow at different rates, producing changing proportions (most organisms; human brain grows faster than body in early development). (c) Determinate growth: growth stops at maturity (most animals, some plant organs). (d) Indeterminate growth: continues throughout life (most plants, some animals like fish, reptiles). Growth measurement: increase in dry weight (biomass accumulation), wet weight, length, volume, cell number, or specific dimensions. Growth curves typically sigmoid (S-shaped) showing: lag phase (slow initial growth), exponential/log phase (rapid growth), stationary phase (growth plateaus due to limiting factors), and sometimes decline phase. Growth regulation involves complex interplay of genetic programs, hormones (auxins, cytokinins, gibberellins in plants; growth hormone, IGF, thyroid hormone in animals), nutrition, and environmental factors (temperature, light, water availability).', 'growth, basis of growth, irreversible increase, size, mass, complexity, cell division, mitosis, hyperplasia, cell cycle checkpoints, growth factors, cell enlargement, hypertrophy, water uptake, vacuole expansion, cytoplasm synthesis, organelles, extracellular material, cell wall, matrix, cell differentiation, specialization, differential gene expression, muscle cells, neurons, red blood cells, isometric growth, allometric growth, determinate growth, indeterminate growth, growth measurement, dry weight, biomass, wet weight, length, volume, cell number, growth curves, sigmoid curve, S-shaped curve, lag phase, exponential phase, log phase, stationary phase, decline phase, limiting factors, growth regulation, genetic programs, hormones, auxins, cytokinins, gibberellins, growth hormone, IGF, thyroid hormone, nutrition, environmental factors, temperature, light, water availability', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(45, '6. PROPERTIES OF LIVING CELL', 'Aspects of Growth', 'Growth in organisms manifests in multiple measurable aspects, each providing insights into developmental processes and physiological status. (1) Increase in dry weight: Dry weight (mass after removing all water) represents true organic matter accumulation (carbohydrates, proteins, lipids, nucleic acids). Unlike wet weight (affected by water content), dry weight reflects actual growth. Measured by drying sample at 60-80°C until constant weight. Used in plant growth analysis and microbial growth curves. (2) Irreversible increase in size: Linear dimensions (height, length), volume, or area increase that cannot be reversed (unlike temporary expansion from water uptake). In plants, measured as increase in height, stem diameter, leaf area. In animals, height, body length, organ dimensions. (3) Increase in cell number: Hyperplasia - mitotic division increases cell count. Measured by cell counting (hemocytometer), DNA content assays, or indirectly by organ size increase. Particularly important in embryonic development, tissue regeneration, and tumor growth. (4) Increase in structural complexity: Development of new organs (leaves, branches), tissues, or specialized structures. In plants, node formation, leaf initiation, flower development. In animals, organogenesis, tissue differentiation. (5) Accumulation of specific components: Protein content, chlorophyll content (plants), enzyme activities, storage materials (starch, oil, glycogen). (6) Morphological changes: Changes in shape, proportions, and form as organisms develop (metamorphosis in insects/amphibians, human adolescent growth spurt). Growth is rarely uniform; different tissues/organs grow at different rates (allometry). Growth curves (plotting size vs. time) typically show characteristic patterns: (a) Simple sigmoid (most annual plants, many animals): slow start, rapid middle, slow plateau. (b) Double sigmoid (some perennial plants, humans): shows growth spurts. (c) Exponential (early embryos, microbial cultures in ideal conditions). Growth rate affected by: genetics, nutrition, hormones, environmental factors (temperature, light, water, oxygen), and stress. Understanding growth aspects is crucial in agriculture (crop yield optimization), medicine (growth disorders diagnosis), and biotechnology (optimizing microbial production).', 'aspects of growth, dry weight, organic matter accumulation, carbohydrates, proteins, lipids, nucleic acids, wet weight, water content, drying, constant weight, plant growth analysis, microbial growth curves, irreversible size increase, linear dimensions, height, length, volume, area, temporary expansion, water uptake, cell number increase, hyperplasia, mitotic division, hemocytometer, DNA content, embryonic development, tissue regeneration, tumor growth, structural complexity, new organs, leaves, branches, node formation, leaf initiation, flower development, organogenesis, tissue differentiation, component accumulation, protein content, chlorophyll content, enzyme activities, storage materials, starch, oil, glycogen, morphological changes, shape, proportions, metamorphosis, insects, amphibians, human growth spurt, allometry, growth curves, sigmoid curve, double sigmoid, growth spurts, exponential growth, early embryos, microbial cultures, growth rate factors, genetics, nutrition, hormones, environmental factors, temperature, light, water, oxygen, stress, agriculture, crop yield optimization, medicine, growth disorders, biotechnology, microbial production', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(46, '6. PROPERTIES OF LIVING CELL', 'Regions of Fastest Growth in Plants', 'Plants exhibit localized growth in specific regions called meristems, unlike animals which grow more uniformly. Meristems are tissues containing undifferentiated cells capable of continuous division. (1) Apical meristems: Located at tips of roots and shoots (buds). Responsible for primary growth - increase in length. Root apical meristem: Beneath root cap, produces cells for root elongation. Protected by root cap (secretes mucilage, protects from abrasion). Cells: promeristem (initial cells), followed by derivatives differentiating into protoderm (epidermis), ground meristem (cortex), procambium (vascular tissue). Root cap cells continuously sloughed and replaced. Shoot apical meristem: At stem tip, within apical bud. Produces leaf primordia (future leaves) and bud primordia. Arranged in tunica (outer layers, divide anticlinally) and corpus (inner mass, divide in all directions). Maintains itself while generating cells for stem elongation and leaf formation. (2) Lateral meristems: Vascular cambium and cork cambium (phellogen). Responsible for secondary growth - increase in girth (woody plants). Vascular cambium: Between xylem and phloem in stems and roots. One layer of initials divides periclinally, producing secondary xylem (wood) inward and secondary phloem outward. Annual rings form from seasonal activity. Cork cambium: In outer cortex, produces cork cells (phellem) outward and phelloderm inward. Cork cells impregnated with suberin (waterproof), forming bark. (3) Intercalary meristems: Found at bases of leaves and internodes in grasses and monocots. Allow rapid regrowth after grazing or mowing. (4) Floral meristems: Develop from shoot apical meristems after floral induction, producing flower parts. Growth rates in these regions are regulated by: (a) Hormones: auxins (promote cell elongation, vascular differentiation), cytokinins (cell division), gibberellins (stem elongation), abscisic acid (inhibits growth). (b) Environmental factors: light (photomorphogenesis), gravity (gravitropism), water availability, temperature. (c) Nutrient availability. Measuring growth: auxanometers record elongation rates; microscopic observation of cell division frequencies.', 'plant growth, meristems, undifferentiated cells, cell division, apical meristems, root tips, shoot tips, buds, primary growth, length increase, root apical meristem, root cap, mucilage, abrasion protection, promeristem, initial cells, protoderm, epidermis, ground meristem, cortex, procambium, vascular tissue, root cap cells, sloughed, shoot apical meristem, apical bud, leaf primordia, future leaves, bud primordia, tunica, anticlinal division, corpus, lateral meristems, vascular cambium, cork cambium, phellogen, secondary growth, girth increase, woody plants, periclinal division, secondary xylem, wood, secondary phloem, annual rings, seasonal activity, cork cells, phellem, phelloderm, suberin, waterproof, bark, intercalary meristems, grasses, monocots, bases of leaves, internodes, regrowth, grazing, mowing, floral meristems, floral induction, flower parts, growth regulation, hormones, auxins, cell elongation, vascular differentiation, cytokinins, cell division, gibberellins, stem elongation, abscisic acid, growth inhibition, environmental factors, light, photomorphogenesis, gravity, gravitropism, water availability, temperature, nutrient availability, growth measurement, auxanometers, elongation rates, microscopic observation, cell division frequencies', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(47, '6. PROPERTIES OF LIVING CELL', 'Growth Hormones and Auxins', 'Plant growth and development are orchestrated by a complex interplay of hormones (phytohormones) - signaling molecules produced in low concentrations that regulate physiological processes. Major classes: (1) Auxins (indole-3-acetic acid, IAA): Primary hormone controlling cell elongation. Synthesized in shoot tips, young leaves, developing seeds. Transported polarly (basipetally in shoots, acropetally in roots) via PIN efflux carriers. Effects: (a) Cell elongation: activates proton pumps (acid growth hypothesis), loosening cell walls, allowing expansion. (b) Apical dominance: inhibits growth of lateral buds, ensuring main stem dominance. (c) Root initiation: stimulates adventitious root formation (used in rooting cuttings). (d) Tropisms: asymmetric distribution causes differential growth - phototropism (toward light), gravitropism (roots down, shoots up). (e) Vascular differentiation: promotes xylem and phloem differentiation. (f) Fruit development: stimulates fruit set and growth. (g) Leaf abscission: delays leaf fall. Synthetic auxins: IBA (indolebutyric acid), NAA (naphthaleneacetic acid), 2,4-D (2,4-dichlorophenoxyacetic acid - selective herbicide). (2) Cytokinins (zeatin, kinetin): Promote cell division (cytokinesis). Synthesized in root tips, developing seeds. Effects: (a) Cell division: work with auxins in tissue culture. (b) Shoot initiation: promote shoot formation. (c) Delay senescence: \"anti-aging\" hormone. (d) Nutrient mobilization: attract nutrients to application site. (3) Gibberellins (GA1, GA3, etc.): Promote stem elongation, seed germination, fruit development. Effects: (a) Stem elongation: internode elongation (bolting in rosette plants). (b) Seed germination: mobilize stored food in cereal grains (aleurone layer produces amylase). (c) Fruit development: parthenocarpy (seedless fruit). (d) Flowering: promote flowering in long-day plants. (4) Abscisic acid (ABA): Stress hormone, promotes dormancy, stomatal closure. Effects: (a) Stomatal closure: induces guard cell ion efflux during water stress. (b) Seed dormancy: inhibits germination, maintains dormancy. (c) Stress responses: induces stress-protective proteins. (5) Ethylene (C₂H₄): Gaseous hormone, promotes fruit ripening, senescence, abscission. Effects: (a) Fruit ripening: triggers ripening processes (climacteric fruits). (b) Triple response in seedlings: inhibits stem elongation, promotes thickening, horizontal growth (when encountering obstacles). (c) Leaf and flower senescence. (d) Abscission: promotes leaf and fruit drop. (e) Stress responses: wounding, pathogen attack. (6) Brassinosteroids: Promote cell expansion, division; similar to animal steroids. (7) Jasmonates: Defense responses against herbivores, pathogens. (8) Strigolactones: Inhibit branching, promote mycorrhizal associations. Hormones interact synergistically or antagonistically (hormonal cross-talk), regulating plant development and environmental responses.', 'plant hormones, phytohormones, growth hormones, signaling molecules, physiological regulation, auxins, IAA, indole-3-acetic acid, cell elongation, shoot tips, young leaves, developing seeds, polar transport, PIN efflux carriers, acid growth hypothesis, cell wall loosening, apical dominance, lateral buds, root initiation, adventitious roots, rooting cuttings, phototropism, gravitropism, vascular differentiation, xylem, phloem, fruit development, fruit set, leaf abscission, synthetic auxins, IBA, indolebutyric acid, NAA, naphthaleneacetic acid, 2,4-D, dichlorophenoxyacetic acid, herbicide, cytokinins, zeatin, kinetin, cell division, cytokinesis, root tips, tissue culture, shoot initiation, senescence delay, anti-aging, nutrient mobilization, gibberellins, GA1, GA3, stem elongation, seed germination, fruit development, internode elongation, bolting, rosette plants, cereal grains, aleurone layer, amylase, parthenocarpy, seedless fruit, flowering, long-day plants, abscisic acid, ABA, stress hormone, dormancy, stomatal closure, guard cells, ion efflux, water stress, seed dormancy, stress-protective proteins, ethylene, C₂H₄, fruit ripening, climacteric fruits, triple response, seedling, stem inhibition, horizontal growth, leaf senescence, flower senescence, abscission, wounding, pathogen attack, brassinosteroids, cell expansion, jasmonates, defense responses, herbivores, pathogens, strigolactones, branching inhibition, mycorrhizal associations, hormonal cross-talk, synergism, antagonism', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(48, '6. PROPERTIES OF LIVING CELL', 'Growth Curvatures (Tropisms)', 'Tropisms are directional growth responses of plants to environmental stimuli, resulting in curvature toward or away from the stimulus. These responses are crucial for optimizing resource acquisition and survival. Tropisms are mediated by asymmetric distribution of auxin, causing differential cell elongation on opposite sides of the responding organ. (1) Phototropism: Growth response to light. Shoots exhibit positive phototropism (grow toward light) to maximize photosynthesis. Roots are generally negatively phototropic (grow away from light) or non-responsive. Mechanism (Cholodny-Went hypothesis): (a) Light perception by phototropin (blue light receptor, especially phot1, phot2). (b) Lateral auxin redistribution to shaded side. (c) Higher auxin concentration on shaded side stimulates greater cell elongation. (d) Shoot bends toward light. Phototropin activation triggers signaling cascades affecting PIN3 auxin efflux carrier polarization. (2) Gravitropism (geotropism): Response to gravity. Roots show positive gravitropism (grow downward) for anchorage, water, mineral absorption. Shoots show negative gravitropism (grow upward) for light access, CO₂ exchange. Mechanism: (a) Gravity perception by sedimentation of starch-filled amyloplasts (statoliths) in specialized cells (statocytes in root cap, endodermal cells in shoots). (b) Signal transduction leading to auxin redistribution: in roots, auxin accumulates on lower side, inhibiting elongation (higher concentrations inhibit root growth); in shoots, auxin accumulates on lower side, promoting elongation. (c) Differential growth causes curvature. (3) Hydrotropism: Response to water gradients. Roots are positively hydrotropic, growing toward higher moisture. Mechanism distinct from gravitropism, involves changes in root cap gene expression and auxin signaling. Important for drought avoidance. (4) Thigmotropism: Response to touch. Seen in tendrils (peas, grapes), climbing stems (morning glory). Contact stimulates localized cell elongation on opposite side, causing coiling around support. Involves rapid calcium signaling, changes in microtubule orientation, auxin redistribution. (5) Chemotropism: Response to chemicals. Pollen tubes grow toward ovules guided by chemical signals (Ca²⁺, sugars, specific proteins). Roots may grow toward nutrient patches. (6) Thermotropism: Response to temperature gradients. Roots may avoid temperature extremes. (7) Electrotropism: Response to electric fields. Tropisms often interact; roots must integrate multiple signals (gravity, water, nutrients, touch) to determine optimal growth direction. Mutants defective in tropic responses (auxin transport or signaling mutants) show impaired growth orientation.', 'tropisms, growth curvatures, directional growth, environmental stimuli, auxin asymmetric distribution, differential cell elongation, phototropism, positive phototropism, shoots, light, photosynthesis, roots, negative phototropism, Cholodny-Went hypothesis, phototropin, blue light receptor, phot1, phot2, lateral auxin redistribution, shaded side, cell elongation, PIN3, auxin efflux carrier, gravitropism, geotropism, gravity, positive gravitropism, roots, anchorage, water absorption, mineral absorption, negative gravitropism, shoots, amyloplasts, statoliths, starch-filled, sedimentation, statocytes, root cap, endodermal cells, signal transduction, hydrotropism, water gradients, positive hydrotropism, drought avoidance, thigmotropism, touch, tendrils, peas, grapes, climbing stems, morning glory, coiling, calcium signaling, microtubule orientation, chemotropism, chemicals, pollen tubes, ovules, Ca²⁺, sugars, nutrient patches, thermotropism, temperature gradients, electrotropism, electric fields, signal integration, mutants, auxin transport mutants, auxin signaling mutants', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(49, '6. PROPERTIES OF LIVING CELL', 'Development: Enlargement and Differentiation', 'Development encompasses all progressive changes an organism undergoes from zygote formation to maturity and eventual senescence. It involves three fundamental processes: cell division (proliferation), cell enlargement (growth), and cell differentiation (specialization). These processes are precisely coordinated in time and space through genetic programs and environmental signals. Cell enlargement: Increase in cell volume through water uptake, cytoplasmic synthesis, and organelle replication. In plants, vacuole expansion drives most cell enlargement (up to 90% of volume). Cell wall loosening (by expansins) allows turgor-driven expansion. Orientation of cellulose microfibrils determines direction of expansion. In animals, cell enlargement involves increased protein synthesis, cytoskeletal reorganization, and organelle proliferation. Cell differentiation: Process by which cells acquire specialized structures and functions. Driven by differential gene expression: specific sets of genes activated, others repressed. Transcription factors (master regulators) coordinate differentiation programs. Examples: (1) Xylem differentiation: cells elongate, secondary cell wall deposited with lignin, programmed cell death leaves hollow vessels for water transport. Expression of transcription factors (VND, NST) activates genes for cellulose synthase, lignin biosynthesis enzymes, and autolysis. (2) Phloem differentiation: sieve tube elements lose nucleus, retain cytoplasm, develop sieve plates; companion cells retain nucleus, support sieve elements. (3) Muscle cell differentiation: myoblasts fuse into multinucleated myotubes, express contractile proteins (myosin, actin), organize sarcomeres. MyoD family transcription factors master regulators. (4) Neuron differentiation: extend axons and dendrites, express ion channels, neurotransmitter receptors, synaptic proteins. (5) Red blood cell differentiation: enucleate, accumulate hemoglobin, lose organelles. Erythropoietin stimulates differentiation. (6) Root hair cell differentiation: trichoblasts extend tubular projections, express water channels (aquaporins), ion transporters. Developmental stages in plants: embryogenesis (pattern formation), seed germination, vegetative growth, reproductive development, senescence. In animals: embryogenesis (cleavage, gastrulation, organogenesis), growth, maturation, aging. Developmental plasticity: ability to alter development in response to environment (plants exhibit high plasticity; animals limited plasticity). Stem cells retain capacity for differentiation throughout life, enabling regeneration and repair.', 'development, enlargement, differentiation, cell division, proliferation, growth, specialization, genetic programs, environmental signals, cell enlargement, volume increase, water uptake, cytoplasmic synthesis, organelle replication, vacuole expansion, cell wall loosening, expansins, turgor-driven expansion, cellulose microfibrils, orientation, animals, protein synthesis, cytoskeletal reorganization, cell differentiation, differential gene expression, transcription factors, master regulators, xylem differentiation, elongation, secondary cell wall, lignin, programmed cell death, hollow vessels, water transport, VND, NST, cellulose synthase, lignin biosynthesis, autolysis, phloem differentiation, sieve tube elements, nucleus loss, cytoplasm, sieve plates, companion cells, muscle cell differentiation, myoblasts, myotubes, multinucleated, contractile proteins, myosin, actin, sarcomeres, MyoD, neuron differentiation, axons, dendrites, ion channels, neurotransmitter receptors, synaptic proteins, red blood cell differentiation, enucleation, hemoglobin, erythropoietin, root hair cell differentiation, trichoblasts, tubular projections, aquaporins, ion transporters, embryogenesis, pattern formation, seed germination, vegetative growth, reproductive development, senescence, cleavage, gastrulation, organogenesis, growth, maturation, aging, developmental plasticity, stem cells, regeneration, repair', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(50, '6. PROPERTIES OF LIVING CELL', 'Movement - Organelles', 'Movement in unicellular organisms is facilitated by specialized locomotory organelles, each adapted for particular environments and lifestyles. These organelles are remarkable examples of cellular engineering, converting chemical energy (ATP) into mechanical work. (1) Cilia: Short (2-10 μm), numerous (hundreds per cell), hair-like structures covering cell surface. Structure: axoneme (9+2 microtubule arrangement: nine outer doublet microtubules surrounding two central singlet microtubules). Dynein motor proteins (axonemal dynein) generate sliding between microtubules, causing bending. Ciliary beat in coordinated waves (metachronal rhythm) propelling cell (Paramecium). Cilia also create water currents for feeding. Primary cilia (non-motile, 9+0) function as sensory antennae in many cell types. (2) Flagella: Longer (up to 150 μm), fewer (1-4 per cell), whip-like. Similar 9+2 axoneme structure. Beat in undulating waves (Euglena) or planar waves (sperm). Flagellar movement can pull (tinsel type with hairs) or push (whiplash type). Bacterial flagella structurally different: filament of flagellin protein, rotates like propeller driven by basal body motor (H⁺ or Na⁺ gradient), not 9+2 arrangement. (3) Pseudopodia: Temporary projections of cytoplasm in amoeboid cells. Types: lobopodia (blunt, Amoeba), filopodia (thin, pointed), reticulopodia (net-like, foraminifera), axopodia (stiff, actin-supported). Mechanism: actin polymerization drives membrane protrusion (Arp2/3 complex nucleates branching networks, formins nucleate unbranched filaments). Myosin motors contract rear, pulling cell forward. Allows movement on surfaces and phagocytosis. (4) Gliding motility: Smooth movement without visible organelles. Apicomplexan parasites (Plasmodium) glide using actin-myosin system underneath membrane. Cyanobacteria, myxobacteria glide by secreting slime. (5) Contractile structures: Myonemes (contractile fibrils) in some protozoa (Stentor) allow rapid contraction. (6) Undulating membranes: Fin-like structures in some flagellates (Trichomonas). Organelle movement (intracellular) involves: (1) Microtubule-based transport: kinesin (moves toward plus end) and dynein (moves toward minus end) transport vesicles, organelles along microtubules. (2) Actin-based transport: myosin motors move along actin filaments (cytoplasmic streaming, vesicle transport in plants, muscle contraction).', 'movement organelles, unicellular organisms, locomotory organelles, ATP, mechanical work, cilia, short, numerous, Paramecium, axoneme, 9+2 microtubule arrangement, outer doublet microtubules, central singlet microtubules, dynein, axonemal dynein, microtubule sliding, bending, metachronal rhythm, coordinated beating, feeding, primary cilia, 9+0, sensory antennae, flagella, long, few, Euglena, sperm, undulating waves, planar waves, tinsel type, whiplash type, bacterial flagella, flagellin, filament, rotation, basal body motor, H⁺ gradient, Na⁺ gradient, pseudopodia, temporary projections, amoeboid cells, lobopodia, Amoeba, filopodia, reticulopodia, foraminifera, axopodia, actin polymerization, membrane protrusion, Arp2/3, branching networks, formins, unbranched filaments, myosin, contraction, phagocytosis, gliding motility, Plasmodium, apicomplexan parasites, actin-myosin system, cyanobacteria, myxobacteria, slime, contractile structures, myonemes, Stentor, undulating membranes, Trichomonas, intracellular movement, microtubule-based transport, kinesin, plus end, dynein, minus end, vesicle transport, organelle transport, actin-based transport, myosin, cytoplasmic streaming, plant transport, muscle contraction', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(51, '6. PROPERTIES OF LIVING CELL', 'Cyclosis (Cytoplasmic Streaming)', 'Cyclosis, also called cytoplasmic streaming, is the directed, continuous flow of cytoplasm within eukaryotic cells. This phenomenon is particularly prominent in large plant cells (Elodea, Chara, Nitella) but also occurs in animal cells, fungi, and protists. Cyclosis facilitates efficient distribution of nutrients, metabolites, organelles, and genetic information throughout the cell, overcoming limitations of slow diffusion. Mechanism: (1) Actin cytoskeleton: Bundles of actin filaments (F-actin) are aligned along the direction of streaming, with their plus ends oriented toward the direction of flow. (2) Myosin motors: Class XI myosins (in plants) or class V myosins (in animals/fungi) attached to organelles, vesicles, or ER move along actin filaments toward the plus end, dragging attached cargo. (3) Force generation: Myosin ATPase hydrolyzes ATP, undergoes conformational changes, producing directional movement (step size ~5-35 nm per ATP). (4) Bulk flow: Moving organelles drag surrounding cytosol, creating bulk flow. In large cells, multiple streams can flow in opposite directions (e.g., fountain-like patterns). In plant cells (e.g., Elodea), chloroplasts circulate along transvacuolar strands or cortical cytoplasm. Velocity varies (1-100 μm/s), fastest in Characean algae (up to 100 μm/s). Regulation: (a) Ca²⁺: high Ca²⁺ inhibits myosin activity; (b) Light: affects rate; (c) pH: optimal pH for myosin activity; (d) Phosphorylation: regulates myosin activity. Functions: (1) Nutrient distribution: delivers photosynthetic products (sucrose) from chloroplasts to rest of cell. (2) Organelle positioning: moves chloroplasts to optimal light positions (avoidance response under high light). (3) Cell growth: delivers materials to growing regions (pollen tubes, root hairs, fungal hyphae). (4) Signaling: propagates Ca²⁺ waves, electrical signals. (5) Wound response: rapid streaming changes seal wounds in giant algae. (6) Reproduction: facilitates gamete movement in some algae. Cyclosis is essential for cells too large for diffusion alone; its inhibition leads to growth defects and impaired organelle distribution.', 'cyclosis, cytoplasmic streaming, directed flow, continuous flow, eukaryotic cells, large plant cells, Elodea, Chara, Nitella, animal cells, fungi, protists, nutrient distribution, metabolite distribution, organelle distribution, genetic information, slow diffusion limitation, mechanism, actin cytoskeleton, actin filaments, F-actin, plus ends, direction of flow, myosin motors, class XI myosins, plants, class V myosins, animals, fungi, organelles, vesicles, endoplasmic reticulum, ER, myosin ATPase, ATP hydrolysis, conformational changes, directional movement, step size, bulk flow, moving organelles, cytosol, fountain-like patterns, Elodea, chloroplasts, transvacuolar strands, cortical cytoplasm, velocity, μm/s, Characean algae, regulation, Ca²⁺, calcium inhibition, light effects, pH effects, phosphorylation, myosin regulation, functions, photosynthetic products, sucrose, organelle positioning, light avoidance response, cell growth, pollen tubes, root hairs, fungal hyphae, signaling, Ca²⁺ waves, electrical signals, wound response, giant algae, reproduction, gamete movement, diffusion limitation, growth defects, impaired distribution', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(52, '6. PROPERTIES OF LIVING CELL', 'Reproduction - Asexual', 'Asexual reproduction involves a single parent producing offspring genetically identical to itself (clones). This mode of reproduction is widespread among prokaryotes, protists, fungi, plants, and some animals. It enables rapid population increase without requiring mates, advantageous in stable environments. Types: (1) Binary fission: Common in bacteria, protozoa (Amoeba, Paramecium). Parent cell divides into two equal daughter cells. Process: DNA replication → chromosome segregation → cytokinesis. In bacteria, involves FtsZ protein forming Z-ring at division site. In protozoa, mitosis followed by cytokinesis. (2) Multiple fission: Parent nucleus divides repeatedly before cytoplasm divides, producing many daughter cells simultaneously. Examples: Plasmodium (malaria parasite) in liver cells produces thousands of merozoites; some algae produce zoospores. (3) Budding: Parent cell produces outgrowth (bud) that develops into new individual, eventually detaches. Examples: yeast (Saccharomyces) bud scars visible; Hydra buds from body wall; some sponges. (4) Fragmentation: Organism breaks into pieces, each regenerating into complete individual. Examples: Spirogyra filaments fragment; planaria flatworms; starfish from arms; many plants from stem cuttings. (5) Spore formation: Specialized cells (spores) produced, often in large numbers, dispersing to form new individuals. Examples: fungi (sporangiospores in Rhizopus, conidia in Penicillium); ferns (spores in sporangia); mosses; some algae. Spores may be motile (zoospores) or non-motile. (6) Vegetative propagation in plants: New individuals from vegetative parts, not seeds. Types: (a) Runners (stolons): horizontal stems (strawberry, spider plant). (b) Rhizomes: underground stems (ginger, bamboo, iris). (c) Tubers: swollen underground stems (potato eyes are buds). (d) Bulbs: shortened stems with fleshy leaves (onion, garlic, tulip). (e) Corms: swollen stem bases (crocus, taro). (f) Suckers: shoots from roots (banana). (g) Plantlets: on leaf margins (Kalanchoe, Bryophyllum). (h) Cuttings: stem, leaf, or root pieces planted (many horticultural plants). (i) Grafting: joining scion and rootstock (fruit trees). (j) Tissue culture: micropropagation from small explants (orchids, bananas). Advantages: rapid, no mate needed, preserves favorable genotypes. Disadvantages: no genetic variation, vulnerable to environmental changes and diseases.', 'asexual reproduction, single parent, clones, identical offspring, prokaryotes, protists, fungi, plants, animals, rapid population increase, stable environments, binary fission, bacteria, Amoeba, Paramecium, DNA replication, chromosome segregation, cytokinesis, FtsZ protein, Z-ring, division site, multiple fission, Plasmodium, malaria parasite, merozoites, zoospores, budding, yeast, Saccharomyces, bud scars, Hydra, sponges, fragmentation, Spirogyra, filaments, planaria, flatworms, starfish, stem cuttings, spore formation, spores, fungi, sporangiospores, Rhizopus, conidia, Penicillium, ferns, sporangia, mosses, motile spores, zoospores, non-motile spores, vegetative propagation, plants, runners, stolons, strawberry, spider plant, rhizomes, ginger, bamboo, iris, tubers, potato, eyes, bulbs, onion, garlic, tulip, corms, crocus, taro, suckers, banana, plantlets, leaf margins, Kalanchoe, Bryophyllum, cuttings, horticultural plants, grafting, scion, rootstock, fruit trees, tissue culture, micropropagation, explants, orchids, advantages, rapid, no mate needed, genotype preservation, disadvantages, genetic variation, environmental vulnerability, disease vulnerability', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(53, '6. PROPERTIES OF LIVING CELL', 'Reproduction - Sexual', 'Sexual reproduction involves fusion of gametes from two parents, producing offspring genetically different from parents. This process generates genetic diversity, enhancing adaptation and evolutionary potential. Key features: (1) Gametogenesis: Formation of specialized haploid gametes (sperm and egg) through meiosis. Spermatogenesis: continuous production of motile sperm in testes. Oogenesis: cyclic production of large, non-motile eggs in ovaries, with nutrient-rich cytoplasm. (2) Fertilization: Fusion of haploid gametes to form diploid zygote. Can be external (in water, many fish, amphibians) or internal (in reproductive tract, reptiles, birds, mammals). (3) Meiosis: Specialized cell division reducing chromosome number from diploid (2n) to haploid (n). Occurs in gamete-producing organs. Includes one DNA replication followed by two divisions. Prophase I features crossing over (synapsis of homologous chromosomes, exchange of genetic material at chiasmata), increasing genetic variation. Metaphase I: independent assortment of homologous chromosomes further increases variation. Anaphase I: homologous chromosomes separate. Meiosis II: sister chromatids separate, producing four haploid cells. (4) Conjugation: Temporary union for genetic exchange without gamete formation. Examples: Paramecium (macronucleus degenerates, micronuclei undergo meiosis, haploid nuclei exchanged); Spirogyra (conjugation tubes form between filaments, protoplasts act as gametes). Types of sexual reproduction: (a) Isogamy: gametes morphologically similar (some algae, fungi). (b) Anisogamy: gametes differ in size (some algae). (c) Oogamy: large non-motile egg, small motile sperm (most animals, some plants). (d) Hermaphroditism: individual produces both male and female gametes (many plants, some animals like earthworms, snails). (e) Parthenogenesis: development from unfertilized egg (some insects, reptiles, plants). Advantages: genetic variation (crossing over, independent assortment, random fertilization), elimination of deleterious mutations, adaptation to changing environments. Disadvantages: requires finding mate, slower reproduction, only 50% of genes passed on. Sexual reproduction likely evolved early in eukaryotic history, with most eukaryotes capable of at least occasional sex. Its prevalence despite costs indicates strong selective advantages.', 'sexual reproduction, gametes fusion, two parents, genetic diversity, offspring variation, adaptation, evolutionary potential, gametogenesis, haploid gametes, sperm, egg, meiosis, spermatogenesis, testes, motile sperm, oogenesis, ovaries, non-motile eggs, nutrient-rich cytoplasm, fertilization, zygote, diploid, external fertilization, fish, amphibians, internal fertilization, reptiles, birds, mammals, chromosome number reduction, diploid 2n, haploid n, crossing over, prophase I, synapsis, homologous chromosomes, chiasmata, genetic material exchange, metaphase I, independent assortment, variation, anaphase I, chromosome separation, meiosis II, sister chromatids, four haploid cells, conjugation, genetic exchange, Paramecium, macronucleus, micronuclei, Spirogyra, conjugation tubes, isogamy, morphologically similar gametes, algae, fungi, anisogamy, size difference, oogamy, large egg, small sperm, hermaphroditism, male gametes, female gametes, earthworms, snails, parthenogenesis, unfertilized egg, insects, reptiles, advantages, genetic variation, crossing over, independent assortment, random fertilization, deleterious mutation elimination, changing environments, disadvantages, mate finding, slower reproduction, gene transmission, eukaryotic evolution, selective advantages', '2026-02-27 15:40:30');
INSERT INTO `biology_syllabus_comprehensive` (`id`, `topic`, `sub_topic`, `note`, `keywords`, `created_at`) VALUES
(54, '7. TISSUES AND SUPPORTING SYSTEMS', 'Skeleton in Animals - Biological Significance', 'The skeletal system is a fundamental adaptation in many animals, providing essential structural and physiological functions that enabled larger body sizes, terrestrial life, and complex behaviors. Its biological significance encompasses multiple roles: (1) Support: Provides rigid framework maintaining body shape against gravity. Without skeleton, soft tissues would collapse. In terrestrial animals, must resist gravitational forces; in aquatic, may be reduced (buoyancy supports). (2) Protection: Encases vital organs, shielding them from mechanical injury. Skull protects brain; rib cage protects heart, lungs; vertebral column protects spinal cord; pelvis protects reproductive organs. (3) Movement: Bones act as levers for muscle attachment. Joints provide fulcrums. Muscles attach via tendons, pull on bones to produce movement. Lever systems amplify force (power levers) or speed/distance (speed levers). (4) Mineral storage: Reservoir for calcium (99% body Ca²⁺) and phosphorus. Hormonal regulation (parathyroid hormone, calcitonin) maintains blood Ca²⁺ levels by depositing or releasing minerals. Essential for nerve function, muscle contraction, blood clotting. (5) Blood cell production: Hematopoiesis occurs in bone marrow. Red marrow (in flat bones, ends of long bones) produces erythrocytes, leukocytes, platelets. Yellow marrow (fat) can convert to red marrow in emergencies. (6) Energy storage: Yellow marrow stores lipids as energy reserve. (7) Sound transmission: Middle ear ossicles (hammer, anvil, stirrup) amplify sound vibrations. (8) Acid-base balance: Bone buffers blood pH by releasing or absorbing alkaline salts. (9) Endocrine functions: Bone produces osteocalcin, hormone regulating energy metabolism, glucose homeostasis, male fertility. (10) Growth and development: Provides framework for body proportions; growth plates (epiphyseal plates) determine final stature. Skeletal adaptations reflect evolutionary pressures: lightweight bird skeletons for flight; dense whale bones for buoyancy control; antlers for combat/display; hollow bones in dinosaurs. Skeletal disorders (osteoporosis, osteoarthritis, fractures) significantly impact health.', 'skeleton, biological significance, structural functions, physiological functions, larger body sizes, terrestrial life, complex behaviors, support, rigid framework, body shape, gravity, gravitational forces, buoyancy, protection, vital organs, mechanical injury, skull, brain protection, rib cage, heart protection, lung protection, vertebral column, spinal cord protection, pelvis, reproductive organs, movement, bones as levers, muscle attachment, joints, fulcrums, tendons, lever systems, power levers, speed levers, mineral storage, calcium, 99% body Ca²⁺, phosphorus, hormonal regulation, parathyroid hormone, calcitonin, blood Ca²⁺ levels, nerve function, muscle contraction, blood clotting, blood cell production, hematopoiesis, bone marrow, red marrow, flat bones, long bone ends, erythrocytes, leukocytes, platelets, yellow marrow, fat, energy storage, sound transmission, middle ear ossicles, hammer, anvil, stirrup, sound amplification, acid-base balance, blood pH buffering, alkaline salts, endocrine functions, osteocalcin, energy metabolism, glucose homeostasis, male fertility, growth and development, body proportions, growth plates, epiphyseal plates, final stature, skeletal adaptations, bird skeletons, lightweight, flight, whale bones, buoyancy control, antlers, combat, display, hollow bones, dinosaurs, osteoporosis, osteoarthritis, fractures', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(55, '7. TISSUES AND SUPPORTING SYSTEMS', 'Skeletal Materials', 'Different skeletal materials provide varying mechanical properties optimized for specific functions. Understanding these materials reveals evolutionary solutions to biomechanical challenges. (1) Bone: Composite material of organic matrix (collagen fibers, ~35%) providing tensile strength and flexibility, and inorganic mineral component (hydroxyapatite crystals, calcium phosphate, ~65%) providing compressive strength and hardness. Collagen (type I) arranged in lamellae gives bone its characteristic structure. Remodeling by osteoblasts (bone-forming) and osteoclasts (bone-resorbing) allows adaptation to stress (Wolff\'s law). Types: compact (cortical) bone - dense, outer layer; spongy (trabecular) bone - porous, inner layer with struts along stress lines. (2) Cartilage: Flexible, resilient connective tissue. Chondrocytes embedded in extracellular matrix rich in proteoglycans (chondroitin sulfate, hyaluronic acid) and collagen (type II). Avascular, nutrients diffuse through matrix. Types: hyaline cartilage (smooth, covers joint surfaces, nose, tracheal rings); elastic cartilage (with elastic fibers, external ear, epiglottis); fibrocartilage (with dense collagen, intervertebral discs, knee menisci). (3) Chitin: Tough, flexible nitrogen-containing polysaccharide (polymer of N-acetylglucosamine). Main component of arthropod exoskeletons. Arranged in microfibrils, often combined with proteins (sclerotization for hardening) and calcium carbonate (crustaceans). Provides protection, muscle attachment sites. Must be molted (ecdysis) for growth. (4) Calcium carbonate: Forms shells of mollusks (calcite or aragonite), exoskeletons of corals, tests of echinoderms. Provides rigidity, protection. (5) Silica: Forms spicules in some sponges, diatom frustules. (6) Keratin: Protein in hair, nails, claws, hooves, horns, feathers, reptile scales. (7) Dentin, enamel: Specialized materials in teeth. Material properties determine function: bone must be strong but not brittle; cartilage must be resilient, withstand compression; chitin must be lightweight yet strong. These materials represent remarkable biological composites, inspiring biomimetic materials engineering.', 'skeletal materials, mechanical properties, biomechanical challenges, bone, composite material, organic matrix, collagen fibers, tensile strength, flexibility, inorganic mineral component, hydroxyapatite crystals, calcium phosphate, compressive strength, hardness, type I collagen, lamellae, remodeling, osteoblasts, bone-forming, osteoclasts, bone-resorbing, Wolff\'s law, compact bone, cortical bone, dense, outer layer, spongy bone, trabecular bone, porous, inner layer, stress lines, cartilage, flexible, resilient connective tissue, chondrocytes, extracellular matrix, proteoglycans, chondroitin sulfate, hyaluronic acid, type II collagen, avascular, nutrient diffusion, hyaline cartilage, joint surfaces, nose, tracheal rings, elastic cartilage, elastic fibers, external ear, epiglottis, fibrocartilage, dense collagen, intervertebral discs, knee menisci, chitin, tough, flexible, nitrogen-containing polysaccharide, N-acetylglucosamine, arthropod exoskeletons, microfibrils, sclerotization, calcium carbonate, crustaceans, muscle attachment, molting, ecdysis, growth, calcium carbonate shells, mollusks, calcite, aragonite, corals, echinoderms, rigidity, protection, silica, spicules, sponges, diatom frustules, keratin, hair, nails, claws, hooves, horns, feathers, reptile scales, dentin, enamel, teeth, material properties, strength, brittleness, resilience, compression, lightweight, biomimetic materials engineering', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(56, '7. TISSUES AND SUPPORTING SYSTEMS', 'Types of Skeleton', 'Animals have evolved three main types of skeletal systems, each with distinct structural and functional characteristics adapted to different body plans and environments. (1) Exoskeleton: External skeleton covering body surface. Found in arthropods (insects, crustaceans, arachnids) - composed of chitin, often hardened with sclerotin or calcium carbonate; mollusk shells - calcium carbonate; corals - calcium carbonate; some protozoa (foraminifera) - calcium carbonate tests. Advantages: excellent protection against predators, physical trauma; provides extensive surface for muscle attachment; prevents desiccation in terrestrial arthropods. Disadvantages: limits growth - must be periodically molted (ecdysis), leaving animal vulnerable; heavy, limiting maximum size (exoskeleton weight scales with volume², strength with cross-sectional area, limiting arthropod size). Molting process: apolysis (old cuticle separation), secretion of new cuticle, ecdysis (shedding), sclerotization (hardening). (2) Endoskeleton: Internal skeleton embedded within soft tissues. Found in vertebrates (bone, cartilage) and echinoderms (calcareous plates). Advantages: grows with animal (no molting); allows larger body size; internal, not limiting body shape; can be lightweight relative to strength; provides internal support and organ suspension. Disadvantages: less protective than exoskeleton; requires more complex musculature; internal injuries can be severe. Vertebrate endoskeleton: axial (skull, vertebral column, ribs, sternum) and appendicular (limb girdles, bones). (3) Hydrostatic skeleton: Fluid-filled cavity (coelom or pseudocoelom) under pressure, surrounded by muscles. Found in soft-bodied invertebrates: annelids (earthworms), cnidarians (Hydra, jellyfish), nematodes, mollusks (except shelled). Mechanism: fluid is incompressible; circular muscle contraction reduces diameter, forcing elongation; longitudinal muscle contraction shortens body. In earthworms, peristalsis: alternating circular and longitudinal contractions propagate along body, moving through soil. In cnidarians, hydrostatic skeleton enables polyp extension, tentacle movement. Advantages: flexible, allows diverse movements; can change shape dramatically; no rigid parts to limit growth. Disadvantages: limited protection; requires continuous muscle tone; less efficient for large size. Many animals combine skeletal types: vertebrates have endoskeleton plus some exoskeletal elements (teeth, scales, antlers, shells in turtles).', 'exoskeleton, external skeleton, arthropods, insects, crustaceans, arachnids, chitin, sclerotin, calcium carbonate hardening, mollusk shells, calcium carbonate, corals, foraminifera, tests, protection, predators, physical trauma, muscle attachment, desiccation prevention, terrestrial arthropods, growth limitation, molting, ecdysis, vulnerability, heavy, size limitation, apolysis, old cuticle separation, new cuticle secretion, sclerotization, hardening, endoskeleton, internal skeleton, vertebrates, bone, cartilage, echinoderms, calcareous plates, grows with animal, larger body size, lightweight, internal support, organ suspension, less protective, complex musculature, axial skeleton, skull, vertebral column, ribs, sternum, appendicular skeleton, limb girdles, limb bones, hydrostatic skeleton, fluid-filled cavity, coelom, pseudocoelom, pressure, muscles, soft-bodied invertebrates, annelids, earthworms, cnidarians, Hydra, jellyfish, nematodes, mollusks, incompressible fluid, circular muscle contraction, diameter reduction, elongation, longitudinal muscle contraction, shortening, peristalsis, alternating contractions, soil movement, polyp extension, tentacle movement, flexibility, shape change, continuous muscle tone, size limitation, combined skeletal types, teeth, scales, antlers, shells, turtles', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(57, '7. TISSUES AND SUPPORTING SYSTEMS', 'Vertebral Column and Girdles', 'The vertebral column (spine) and girdles are central components of the vertebrate endoskeleton, providing structural support, protection, and enabling locomotion. Vertebral column: Flexible column of individual vertebrae (typically 33 in humans) separated by intervertebral discs (fibrocartilage with nucleus pulposus). Vertebrae categorized by region: (1) Cervical (neck): 7 in mammals, atlas (C1) supports skull, axis (C2) allows head rotation. (2) Thoracic (chest): 12, articulate with ribs via costal facets. (3) Lumbar (lower back): 5, large, weight-bearing. (4) Sacral: 5 fused into sacrum, articulates with pelvic girdle. (5) Caudal (tail): 4 fused into coccyx (humans); variable in tailed animals. Vertebral structure: body (centrum) weight-bearing; vertebral arch surrounds spinal cord; spinous and transverse processes for muscle attachment; intervertebral foramina for spinal nerves. Functions: (1) Support: bears body weight, maintains posture. (2) Protection: encloses and protects spinal cord within vertebral canal. (3) Movement: flexibility allows bending, twisting. (4) Shock absorption: intervertebral discs cushion. (5) Muscle attachment: processes provide leverage. Pectoral girdle (shoulder girdle): Attaches forelimbs to axial skeleton. Components: scapula (shoulder blade) - broad, flat bone with glenoid cavity for humerus articulation; clavicle (collarbone) - rod-like, braces shoulder, present in mammals, absent in many cursorial animals. Allows wide range of arm movement. Not rigidly attached to spine (only via muscles and clavicle to sternum), increasing mobility. Pelvic girdle (hip girdle): Attaches hindlimbs to axial skeleton. Components: paired hip bones (coxal bones), each formed by fusion of ilium, ischium, pubis. Articulates with sacrum at sacroiliac joints (strong, weight-bearing). Acetabulum: socket for femur head. Functions: supports body weight; transmits forces from hindlimbs to spine; encloses pelvic cavity protecting reproductive, urinary, digestive organs. Differences: pelvic girdle is more rigidly attached than pectoral, reflecting hindlimbs\' primary role in locomotion and weight support.', 'vertebral column, spine, endoskeleton, structural support, protection, locomotion, vertebrae, intervertebral discs, fibrocartilage, nucleus pulposus, cervical vertebrae, neck, 7, mammals, atlas, C1, skull support, axis, C2, head rotation, thoracic vertebrae, chest, 12, ribs, costal facets, lumbar vertebrae, lower back, 5, weight-bearing, sacral vertebrae, 5, fused, sacrum, pelvic girdle articulation, caudal vertebrae, tail, 4, fused, coccyx, vertebral structure, body, centrum, weight-bearing, vertebral arch, spinal cord, spinous processes, transverse processes, muscle attachment, intervertebral foramina, spinal nerves, functions, support, posture, spinal cord protection, vertebral canal, movement, flexibility, bending, twisting, shock absorption, intervertebral discs cushion, muscle attachment, leverage, pectoral girdle, shoulder girdle, forelimb attachment, axial skeleton, scapula, shoulder blade, glenoid cavity, humerus articulation, clavicle, collarbone, rod-like, shoulder brace, cursorial animals, arm movement, mobility, pelvic girdle, hip girdle, hindlimb attachment, hip bones, coxal bones, ilium, ischium, pubis, fusion, sacroiliac joints, weight-bearing, acetabulum, femur head, pelvic cavity protection, reproductive organs, urinary organs, digestive organs, rigid attachment, locomotion, weight support', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(58, '7. TISSUES AND SUPPORTING SYSTEMS', 'Long Bones of Appendicular Skeleton', 'Long bones are characteristic of limbs, designed for leverage, weight support, and movement. Their structure reflects biomechanical principles optimizing strength with minimal weight. Major long bones: humerus (upper arm), radius and ulna (forearm), femur (thigh), tibia and fibula (lower leg), also metacarpals, metatarsals, phalanges. Structural features: (1) Diaphysis (shaft): Thick wall of compact bone surrounding medullary cavity. Provides strength where bending stresses greatest. Curved (biomechanically optimized) to resist compressive and tensile forces. (2) Epiphyses (ends): Expanded regions of spongy (trabecular) bone covered by thin compact bone. Trabeculae align along stress lines (Wolff\'s law), providing strength while reducing weight. Articular cartilage (hyaline) covers joint surfaces, reducing friction, absorbing shock. (3) Metaphyses: Regions between diaphysis and epiphyses; contain epiphyseal (growth) plates in growing individuals. (4) Medullary cavity: Central cavity in diaphysis. In adults, contains yellow marrow (adipose tissue). In infants, red marrow extends here. (5) Periosteum: Dense fibrous membrane covering outer surface (except joints). Contains osteoprogenitor cells, blood vessels, nerves. Essential for bone growth in diameter, fracture repair, muscle attachment (via Sharpey\'s fibers). (6) Endosteum: Thin membrane lining medullary cavity, covers trabeculae. Contains osteoblasts, osteoclasts, osteoprogenitor cells. (7) Nutrient foramen: Opening for blood vessels supplying bone interior. Bone tissue types: Compact bone (cortical) - dense, organized into osteons (Haversian systems) with concentric lamellae around central canal (containing blood vessels, nerves). Spongy bone (trabecular) - porous lattice of trabeculae, spaces filled with marrow. Bone cells: Osteoblasts (bone-forming), osteocytes (mature, maintain bone), osteoclasts (bone-resorbing). Long bone proportions vary with function: longer in cursorial animals for speed; robust in burrowing for force. Bone remodeling (constant turnover) adapts to mechanical stress, repairs microdamage, regulates calcium homeostasis. Osteoporosis results from imbalance favoring resorption, causing fragility.', 'long bones, appendicular skeleton, limbs, leverage, weight support, movement, biomechanical principles, strength, minimal weight, humerus, upper arm, radius, ulna, forearm, femur, thigh, tibia, fibula, lower leg, metacarpals, metatarsals, phalanges, diaphysis, shaft, compact bone, medullary cavity, bending stresses, curved, biomechanically optimized, compressive forces, tensile forces, epiphyses, ends, spongy bone, trabecular bone, stress lines, Wolff\'s law, weight reduction, articular cartilage, hyaline cartilage, joint surfaces, friction reduction, shock absorption, metaphyses, epiphyseal plates, growth plates, yellow marrow, adipose tissue, red marrow, periosteum, fibrous membrane, osteoprogenitor cells, blood vessels, nerves, bone growth, diameter, fracture repair, muscle attachment, Sharpey\'s fibers, endosteum, thin membrane, osteoblasts, osteoclasts, osteocytes, nutrient foramen, blood supply, compact bone, cortical bone, osteons, Haversian systems, concentric lamellae, central canal, spongy bone, trabecular bone, porous lattice, trabeculae, marrow, bone-forming cells, bone-resorbing cells, cursorial animals, speed, burrowing, force, bone remodeling, constant turnover, mechanical stress adaptation, microdamage repair, calcium homeostasis, osteoporosis, resorption imbalance, fragility', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(59, '7. TISSUES AND SUPPORTING SYSTEMS', 'Mechanism of Support in Animals', 'Support and movement in animals result from integrated action of skeletal elements, muscles, and joints, operating as a biomechanical system. This system enables diverse behaviors: locomotion, feeding, manipulation, communication. (1) Bones as levers: Long bones function as levers, amplifying force or speed/movement distance. Lever systems have four components: rigid bar (bone), fulcrum (joint), effort (muscle force), load (resistance). Three lever classes in body: (a) First-class (fulcrum between effort and load): neck extension (atlanto-occipital joint). (b) Second-class (load between fulcrum and effort): standing on toes (rare). (c) Third-class (effort between fulcrum and load): most limb movements (e.g., elbow flexion) - favor speed/range over force. (2) Joints as fulcrums: Classified by movement range: (a) Synarthroses (immovable): skull sutures. (b) Amphiarthroses (slightly movable): intervertebral discs, pubic symphysis. (c) Diarthroses (freely movable, synovial joints): most limb joints. Synovial joints have articular cartilage, synovial fluid (lubrication, nutrient supply), joint capsule, ligaments. Types: hinge (elbow, knee), ball-and-socket (shoulder, hip), pivot (atlas-axis, radioulnar), condyloid (wrist), saddle (thumb), gliding (carpals, tarsals). (3) Muscles as motors: Skeletal muscles attach to bones via tendons. Muscles only contract (pull), cannot push. Therefore arranged in antagonistic pairs: agonist (prime mover) contracts, antagonist relaxes and stretches. Example elbow flexion: biceps brachii (agonist) contracts, triceps (antagonist) relaxes; extension reverses roles. Muscle architecture: parallel fibers (fast, less force), pennate fibers (slower, more force). Muscle contraction mechanism: sliding filament theory (actin and myosin filaments slide past each other). Motor units (motor neuron + muscle fibers) recruited for graded force. (4) Tendons and ligaments: Tendons (muscle to bone) transmit force; ligaments (bone to bone) stabilize joints. Both contain collagen, elastin, providing strength and elasticity. (5) Biomechanical principles: Lever mechanics (force = effort × lever arm). Torque (rotational force) = force × perpendicular distance from fulcrum. Mechanical advantage = effort arm/load arm >1 favors force, <1 favors speed. (6) Energy efficiency: Elastic energy storage in tendons (Achilles tendon in running), gravity utilization in pendulum-like limb swing. (7) Neuromuscular control: Proprioceptors (muscle spindles, Golgi tendon organs) monitor muscle length, tension, providing feedback for coordinated movement.', 'support mechanism, movement, integrated action, skeleton, muscles, joints, biomechanical system, locomotion, feeding, manipulation, communication, bones as levers, rigid bar, fulcrum, effort, muscle force, load, resistance, lever classes, first-class lever, fulcrum between effort and load, neck extension, atlanto-occipital joint, second-class lever, load between fulcrum and effort, standing on toes, third-class lever, effort between fulcrum and load, elbow flexion, speed, range, force, joints as fulcrums, synarthroses, immovable joints, skull sutures, amphiarthroses, slightly movable joints, intervertebral discs, pubic symphysis, diarthroses, freely movable joints, synovial joints, articular cartilage, synovial fluid, lubrication, nutrient supply, joint capsule, ligaments, hinge joint, elbow, knee, ball-and-socket joint, shoulder, hip, pivot joint, atlas-axis, radioulnar, condyloid joint, wrist, saddle joint, thumb, gliding joint, carpals, tarsals, muscles as motors, skeletal muscles, tendons, antagonistic pairs, agonist, prime mover, antagonist, relaxes, stretches, elbow flexion, biceps brachii, triceps, parallel fibers, fast, less force, pennate fibers, slower, more force, muscle contraction, sliding filament theory, actin, myosin, motor units, motor neuron, muscle fibers, graded force, tendons, muscle to bone, force transmission, ligaments, bone to bone, joint stabilization, collagen, elastin, strength, elasticity, biomechanical principles, lever mechanics, force = effort × lever arm, torque, rotational force, perpendicular distance, mechanical advantage, effort arm/load arm, force, speed, energy efficiency, elastic energy storage, Achilles tendon, running, gravity utilization, pendulum-like limb swing, neuromuscular control, proprioceptors, muscle spindles, Golgi tendon organs, muscle length, tension, feedback, coordinated movement', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(60, '7. TISSUES AND SUPPORTING SYSTEMS', 'Functions of Skeleton in Animals', 'The vertebrate skeleton performs multiple critical functions essential for survival, adaptation, and complex behavior. These functions reflect the skeleton\'s role as both structural framework and dynamic physiological organ. (1) Protection: Skeletal elements shield vulnerable organs from mechanical trauma. Skull: encloses brain, protects sensory organs (eyes, ears, nasal passages). Rib cage: with sternum and thoracic vertebrae, protects heart, lungs, major vessels. Vertebral column: surrounds spinal cord within vertebral canal. Pelvis: protects reproductive organs, urinary bladder, lower digestive tract. (2) Support: Provides rigid framework maintaining body shape against gravity. Axial skeleton (skull, vertebral column, ribs) supports trunk, head. Appendicular skeleton supports limbs, suspends body. Without skeleton, muscles would have no firm attachment for generating force. (3) Locomotion: Bones act as levers for muscle attachment, enabling movement. Joints provide articulation points. Limb proportions reflect locomotor adaptations: long limbs in cursorial animals (horse) for speed; robust limbs in fossorial animals (mole) for digging; modified limbs for swimming (whale flippers), flying (bird wings), grasping (primate hands). (4) Respiratory movement: Rib cage movement (by intercostal muscles, diaphragm) essential for ventilation (breathing) in mammals. Ribs attach to thoracic vertebrae, sternum, forming flexible cage that expands/contracts with respiration. (5) Mineral homeostasis: Bone serves as reservoir for calcium and phosphorus. Parathyroid hormone (PTH) stimulates osteoclast activity, releasing Ca²⁺ when blood levels low. Calcitonin (thyroid) promotes Ca²⁺ deposition. This regulation critical for nerve function, muscle contraction, blood clotting, enzyme activity. (6) Blood cell production: Hematopoiesis occurs in bone marrow. Red marrow produces erythrocytes (O₂ transport), leukocytes (immunity), platelets (clotting). Marrow also produces stem cells (mesenchymal, hematopoietic). (7) Energy storage: Yellow marrow stores lipids as energy reserve; can be mobilized during starvation. (8) Endocrine functions: Bone-derived hormone osteocalcin regulates insulin secretion, glucose homeostasis, energy expenditure, male fertility. (9) Sound transmission: Middle ear ossicles (malleus, incus, stapes) amplify sound vibrations from eardrum to oval window. (10) Acid-base balance: Bone buffers blood pH by releasing or absorbing alkaline salts (carbonates, phosphates). (11) Growth and development: Provides framework determining body proportions. Growth plates determine final stature. (12) Protection against infection: Bone marrow produces immune cells; bone itself can wall off infection (Brodie abscess). Skeletal disorders (osteoporosis, osteomalacia, Paget\'s disease, fractures) compromise these functions, causing significant morbidity.', 'skeleton functions, protection, skeletal elements, mechanical trauma, skull, brain protection, sensory organs, eyes, ears, nasal passages, rib cage, sternum, thoracic vertebrae, heart protection, lung protection, major vessels, vertebral column, spinal cord protection, vertebral canal, pelvis, reproductive organs, urinary bladder, lower digestive tract, support, rigid framework, body shape, gravity, axial skeleton, trunk support, head support, appendicular skeleton, limbs support, muscle attachment, force generation, locomotion, bones as levers, joints, articulation, limb proportions, locomotor adaptations, cursorial animals, horse, speed, fossorial animals, mole, digging, swimming, whale flippers, flying, bird wings, grasping, primate hands, respiratory movement, rib cage movement, intercostal muscles, diaphragm, ventilation, breathing, ribs, thoracic vertebrae, sternum, flexible cage, expansion, contraction, mineral homeostasis, calcium reservoir, phosphorus reservoir, parathyroid hormone, PTH, osteoclast activity, Ca²⁺ release, calcitonin, thyroid, Ca²⁺ deposition, nerve function, muscle contraction, blood clotting, enzyme activity, blood cell production, hematopoiesis, bone marrow, red marrow, erythrocytes, O₂ transport, leukocytes, immunity, platelets, clotting, stem cells, mesenchymal, hematopoietic, energy storage, yellow marrow, lipids, energy reserve, starvation, endocrine functions, osteocalcin, insulin secretion, glucose homeostasis, energy expenditure, male fertility, sound transmission, middle ear ossicles, malleus, incus, stapes, sound amplification, eardrum, oval window, acid-base balance, blood pH buffering, alkaline salts, carbonates, phosphates, growth and development, body proportions, growth plates, final stature, infection protection, immune cells, Brodie abscess, skeletal disorders, osteoporosis, osteomalacia, Paget\'s disease, fractures, morbidity', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(61, '7. TISSUES AND SUPPORTING SYSTEMS', 'Supporting Tissues in Plants', 'Plants, lacking skeletons, have evolved diverse supporting tissues providing mechanical strength, flexibility, and resilience. These tissues enable plants to grow tall, compete for light, withstand wind and rain, and recover from bending. (1) Turgid parenchyma: Most basic support in herbaceous (non-woody) plants. Living cells with thin primary walls, large vacuoles. Support through turgor pressure: water in vacuole presses against cell wall, making cells rigid. Loss of turgor causes wilting. Provides support in young stems, leaves, fruits. Also functions in storage, photosynthesis. (2) Collenchyma: Living cells with unevenly thickened primary walls (thicker at corners). Provide flexible mechanical support in young stems, petioles, leaf midribs. Walls contain pectin, hemicellulose, cellulose (no lignin). Allows organs to bend without breaking while maintaining support. Found in outer cortex of stems, along veins. Cells can elongate as organ grows. Common in dicots, especially in regions subjected to mechanical stress (wind). (3) Sclerenchyma: Dead cells at maturity with thick, lignified secondary walls. Provide exceptional strength and rigidity. Two types: (a) Fibers: long, slender cells (often with pointed ends) occurring in bundles or sheets. Found in phloem (bast fibers), xylem (wood fibers), around vascular bundles, in leaf sheaths. Examples: flax fibers (textiles), hemp, jute. (b) Sclereids (stone cells): shorter, irregular cells. Found in seed coats (pea, bean), nut shells (coconut), fruit stone (peach pit), pear fruit (gritty texture). (4) Xylem: Besides water conduction, lignified xylem vessels and tracheids provide significant mechanical support. Wood (secondary xylem) is primary support in trees and shrubs. Cell walls heavily lignified, often with additional thickenings (annular, spiral, reticulate, pitted). (5) Sclerenchyma fibers in phloem (bast fibers) add strength. Supporting tissues distributed strategically: (a) Collenchyma in periphery of young stems (just beneath epidermis) for flexible support. (b) Sclerenchyma fibers around vascular bundles (caps or sheaths) strengthening conducting tissues. (c) Xylem in center (stems) or center/periphery (roots) providing rigid core. (d) Sclereids in specific locations needing hardness (seed coats, nutshells). Mechanical properties: (a) Tensile strength: resistance to pulling (sclerenchyma fibers). (b) Compressive strength: resistance to crushing (xylem, sclereids). (c) Flexibility: ability to bend (collenchyma). (d) Elasticity: ability to return to original shape (collenchyma, turgid cells). Support strategies: annual plants rely on turgor + collenchyma; perennials add sclerenchyma, xylem; trees develop massive woody trunks (secondary growth). Environmental responses: plants increase support tissues in response to mechanical stress (thigmomorphogenesis) - wind triggers shorter, thicker stems with more sclerenchyma.', 'plant supporting tissues, mechanical strength, flexibility, resilience, grow tall, light competition, wind resistance, rain resistance, bending recovery, turgid parenchyma, herbaceous plants, non-woody plants, living cells, thin primary walls, large vacuoles, turgor pressure, water, vacuole, cell wall, rigid, wilting, young stems, leaves, fruits, storage, photosynthesis, collenchyma, living cells, unevenly thickened walls, corners thickened, flexible mechanical support, young stems, petioles, leaf midribs, pectin, hemicellulose, cellulose, no lignin, bending, outer cortex, veins, elongation, dicots, mechanical stress, wind, sclerenchyma, dead cells, lignified secondary walls, strength, rigidity, fibers, long slender cells, pointed ends, bundles, sheets, phloem, bast fibers, xylem, wood fibers, vascular bundles, leaf sheaths, flax fibers, textiles, hemp, jute, sclereids, stone cells, shorter irregular cells, seed coats, pea, bean, nut shells, coconut, fruit stone, peach pit, pear fruit, gritty texture, xylem, water conduction, lignified vessels, tracheids, wood, secondary xylem, trees, shrubs, cell walls, lignified, thickenings, annular, spiral, reticulate, pitted, bast fibers, supporting tissue distribution, periphery, young stems, just beneath epidermis, flexible support, sclerenchyma fibers around vascular bundles, caps, sheaths, conducting tissues, xylem center, stems, roots, rigid core, sclereids, specific locations, hardness, seed coats, nutshells, mechanical properties, tensile strength, resistance to pulling, sclerenchyma fibers, compressive strength, resistance to crushing, xylem, sclereids, flexibility, ability to bend, collenchyma, elasticity, return to original shape, turgid cells, support strategies, annual plants, turgor, collenchyma, perennials, sclerenchyma, xylem, trees, woody trunks, secondary growth, environmental responses, thigmomorphogenesis, mechanical stress, wind, shorter thicker stems, more sclerenchyma', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(62, '7. TISSUES AND SUPPORTING SYSTEMS', 'Functions of Supporting Tissues in Plants', 'Plant supporting tissues perform specialized functions essential for survival, growth, and reproduction in terrestrial environments. These functions reflect adaptations to mechanical stresses and architectural requirements. (1) Strength: Resistance to mechanical forces. (a) Tensile strength: Sclerenchyma fibers provide high resistance to pulling forces (e.g., from wind, gravity). Fibers oriented along stress lines (vertical in stems, horizontal in roots). (b) Compressive strength: Lignified xylem and sclereids resist crushing forces (e.g., weight of canopy, fruit load). Wood (secondary xylem) provides massive compressive strength allowing trees to reach great heights. (c) Bending strength: Combination of tissues resists breaking under bending loads (wind, snow). (2) Rigidity: Maintaining upright posture against gravity. Essential for light competition (taller plants get more light), reproductive success (flowers displayed for pollinators), and efficient gas exchange (leaves positioned optimally). Lignified tissues (xylem, sclerenchyma) provide permanent rigidity; turgor provides adjustable rigidity. (3) Flexibility: Ability to bend under stress without breaking. Collenchyma in young, growing organs allows them to bend with wind, rain, animal contact, then return to original position. This prevents damage while accommodating growth. (4) Resilience: Capacity to recover from deformation. Turgid parenchyma enables plants to recover from wilting when water becomes available; collenchyma returns to shape after bending; elastic cell walls (containing elastin-like proteins) contribute. (5) Protection: Hard tissues (sclereids in seed coats, nutshells) protect embryos from physical damage, predation. Sclerenchyma fibers in bark protect against herbivores, pathogens. (6) Conducting tissue support: Sclerenchyma fibers around vascular bundles protect delicate phloem and xylem, preventing collapse under tension/compression. (7) Anchorage: Roots with lignified stele provide firm anchorage in soil, resisting uprooting by wind. (8) Growth accommodation: Collenchyma in elongation zones allows continued support during rapid growth; can stretch as cells elongate. (9) Environmental adaptation: Plants adjust support tissue development in response to mechanical stimuli (thigmomorphogenesis). Wind-exposed plants develop more sclerenchyma, shorter thicker stems. (10) Economic importance: Fibers used for textiles (flax, hemp, jute), cordage, paper; wood for construction, fuel; cork for insulation. Supporting tissue failure causes lodging (crop stems bending/breaking), windthrow (trees uprooted), stem breakage, significant economic losses in agriculture, forestry.', 'plant tissue functions, strength, mechanical forces, tensile strength, sclerenchyma fibers, pulling forces, wind, gravity, stress lines, vertical in stems, horizontal in roots, compressive strength, lignified xylem, sclereids, crushing forces, canopy weight, fruit load, wood, secondary xylem, tree height, bending strength, breaking resistance, wind loads, snow loads, rigidity, upright posture, light competition, taller plants, more light, reproductive success, flowers displayed, pollinators, gas exchange, leaves positioned, lignified tissues, xylem, sclerenchyma, permanent rigidity, turgor, adjustable rigidity, flexibility, bending under stress, collenchyma, young growing organs, wind bending, rain bending, animal contact, return to original position, damage prevention, growth accommodation, resilience, deformation recovery, turgid parenchyma, wilting recovery, water availability, elastic cell walls, elastin-like proteins, protection, hard tissues, sclereids, seed coats, nutshells, embryo protection, physical damage, predation, sclerenchyma fibers, bark, herbivore protection, pathogen protection, conducting tissue support, vascular bundles, delicate phloem, xylem collapse prevention, tension, compression, anchorage, roots, lignified stele, soil anchorage, uprooting resistance, growth accommodation, elongation zones, rapid growth, stretch, environmental adaptation, thigmomorphogenesis, mechanical stimuli, wind-exposed plants, more sclerenchyma, shorter thicker stems, economic importance, fibers, textiles, flax, hemp, jute, cordage, paper, wood, construction, fuel, cork, insulation, supporting tissue failure, lodging, crop stems bending, crop stems breaking, windthrow, trees uprooted, stem breakage, economic losses, agriculture, forestry', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(63, '8. TRANSPORT SYSTEM', 'Need for Transport - Surface Area/Volume Ratio', 'The evolution of specialized transport systems in larger organisms is a direct consequence of the surface area to volume (SA:V) ratio constraint. As organisms increase in size, their volume (which dictates metabolic demand and waste production) grows cubically (L³), while their surface area (through which exchange with the environment occurs) grows quadratically (L²). This results in a rapidly decreasing SA:V ratio with increasing size. For a sphere, SA:V = 3/r, halving as radius doubles. Implications: (1) Diffusion limitation: Small organisms (bacteria, protozoa) with high SA:V can rely on simple diffusion for all exchanges. Oxygen diffuses sufficient distance, wastes diffuse out. (2) Inefficiency in larger organisms: As size increases, central cells become too far from surface for diffusion to meet metabolic needs. Oxygen would be depleted before reaching center; wastes would accumulate. (3) Need for transport systems: Larger organisms evolved specialized circulatory systems to overcome diffusion limitations. These systems: (a) Bulk transport: Move fluids (blood, hemolymph, sap) containing oxygen, nutrients, hormones to all cells. (b) Increase effective exchange: Create extensive internal surfaces (lungs, gills, roots) with large areas. (c) Maintain concentration gradients: Circulatory flow constantly replenishes oxygen, removes wastes. (4) Organismal adaptations: (a) Flat body shape (flatworms) maintains high SA:V, no circulatory system. (b) Thin body plan (tapeworms) allows diffusion. (c) Specialized exchange organs: lungs (alveoli ~70-100 m² human), gills (lamellae greatly increase area), intestines (villi, microvilli increase absorption area ~600-fold). (5) Cellular consequences: Cells must be small (maintain adequate SA:V for their own exchanges) or have specialized shapes (microvilli increase SA). (6) Metabolic rate correlation: Animals with higher metabolic rates (endotherms) require more extensive circulatory systems, higher cardiac output. (7) Evolutionary pattern: Increasing body size in evolution correlated with development of more complex circulatory systems. SA:V ratio explains why no organism can grow indefinitely without structural and transport adaptations.', 'need for transport, SA:V ratio, surface area to volume ratio, metabolic demand, waste production, diffusion limitation, small organisms, bacteria, protozoa, simple diffusion, oxygen diffusion, waste diffusion, larger organisms, central cells, oxygen depletion, waste accumulation, transport systems, circulatory systems, bulk transport, blood, hemolymph, sap, oxygen, nutrients, hormones, internal surfaces, lungs, gills, roots, concentration gradients, organismal adaptations, flat body shape, flatworms, no circulatory system, thin body plan, tapeworms, specialized exchange organs, alveoli, 70-100 m², human, lamellae, gill area, intestines, villi, microvilli, 600-fold, absorption area, cellular consequences, small cells, specialized shapes, metabolic rate, endotherms, cardiac output, evolutionary pattern, body size, complex circulatory systems, structural adaptations, transport adaptations', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(64, '8. TRANSPORT SYSTEM', 'Need for Transport - Distance', 'In addition to SA:V constraints, the sheer distance between exchange surfaces and internal cells necessitates transport systems. Diffusion is efficient only over very short distances (micrometers). The time for diffusion increases with the square of distance (t ∝ d²). For a molecule to diffuse 1 μm takes ~0.5 ms; to diffuse 1 mm takes ~500 s (over 8 minutes); to diffuse 1 cm would take ~14 hours. Implications for organismal design: (1) Microscopic organisms: Bacteria (1-5 μm) can rely entirely on diffusion; all parts within diffusion distance. (2) Small multicellular organisms: Flatworms (~1 mm thick) near diffusion limit; can survive without circulatory system because all cells within ~1 mm of surface. (3) Larger organisms: Most cells far from exchange surfaces. Human cells ~50-100 μm from nearest capillary, but capillary network brings blood within diffusion distance. Capillaries separated by ~50-100 μm ensuring all cells served. (4) Distance challenges: (a) Oxygen delivery: From lungs to toes ~1.5 m in humans - impossible by diffusion. (b) Nutrient transport: From intestine to brain ~1 m. (c) Hormone distribution: From gland to target organs throughout body. (d) Waste removal: From tissues to excretory organs. (5) Transport system solutions: (a) Circulatory system: Rapid bulk flow (human blood circulates in ~1 minute) overcomes distance. (b) Vascular networks: Branching vessels bring exchange surfaces close to all cells. (c) Pressure gradients: Heart generates pressure driving flow. (d) Respiratory pigments: Hemoglobin increases oxygen-carrying capacity 70-fold, reducing required flow rate. (6) Plant challenges: Water and minerals from roots to leaves up to 100+ m (tallest trees). Solution: transpiration-cohesion-tension mechanism pulls water through xylem. (7) Evolutionary innovations: (a) Closed circulatory systems (vertebrates, annelids) enable high-pressure, directed flow. (b) Counter-current exchange (fish gills, bird legs) maximizes efficiency. (c) Portal systems (hepatic portal, hypothalamic-pituitary) connect specific organs. Distance constraints fundamentally shaped organismal architecture, limiting size without transport systems and driving evolution of circulatory complexity.', 'need for transport, distance, exchange surfaces, internal cells, transport systems, diffusion, diffusion distance, micrometers, diffusion time, t ∝ d², 1 μm diffusion, 0.5 ms, 1 mm diffusion, 500 s, 8 minutes, 1 cm diffusion, 14 hours, organismal design, microscopic organisms, bacteria, 1-5 μm, diffusion reliance, small multicellular organisms, flatworms, 1 mm thick, diffusion limit, no circulatory system, larger organisms, cells far from surface, human cells, 50-100 μm, capillary, capillary network, blood, diffusion distance, capillary separation, oxygen delivery, lungs to toes, 1.5 m, impossible by diffusion, nutrient transport, intestine to brain, 1 m, hormone distribution, gland to target organs, waste removal, tissues to excretory organs, transport system solutions, circulatory system, rapid bulk flow, human blood circulation, 1 minute, distance overcoming, vascular networks, branching vessels, exchange surfaces, pressure gradients, heart pressure, flow, respiratory pigments, hemoglobin, oxygen-carrying capacity, 70-fold, reduced flow rate, plant challenges, water transport, roots to leaves, 100+ m, tallest trees, transpiration-cohesion-tension mechanism, xylem, evolutionary innovations, closed circulatory systems, vertebrates, annelids, high-pressure, directed flow, counter-current exchange, fish gills, bird legs, portal systems, hepatic portal, hypothalamic-pituitary, distance constraints, organismal architecture, size limitation, circulatory complexity evolution', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(65, '8. TRANSPORT SYSTEM', 'Media of Transport', 'Living organisms use diverse media for internal transport, each adapted to specific physiological needs and evolutionary histories. (1) Intracellular transport: Cytoplasm serves as transport medium within cells. Diffusion moves small molecules, ions over short distances. Cyclosis (cytoplasmic streaming) in large cells (plant cells, some protozoa) uses actin-myosin to circulate cytoplasm, distributing organelles, nutrients. (2) Plant transport media: (a) Cell sap: Fluid in vacuoles and xylem/phloem. Xylem sap: dilute solution of water, minerals, hormones; transported under tension. Phloem sap: concentrated solution of sucrose (10-30%), amino acids, hormones, RNA; transported under pressure. (b) Latex: Milky fluid in specialized cells (laticifers) of some plants (rubber tree, papaya, opium poppy). Contains various compounds: rubber, alkaloids, proteins, resins. Functions in defense (wound sealing, toxin delivery), possibly nutrient transport. (3) Invertebrate transport media: (a) Hemolymph: Fluid in open circulatory systems (arthropods, most molluscs). Composition similar to interstitial fluid; contains hemocyanin (copper-based respiratory pigment in some) or hemoglobin (in some insects). Functions in nutrient distribution, waste removal, immune defense, hydraulic support (e.g., spider leg extension). Not confined to vessels; bathes tissues directly. (b) Coelomic fluid: In animals with hydrostatic skeletons (annelids, nematodes), fluid in body cavity provides transport, support. (4) Vertebrate transport media: (a) Blood: Specialized connective tissue confined to vessels. Composition: plasma (~55%) - water, proteins (albumins, globulins, fibrinogen), nutrients, hormones, wastes; formed elements (~45%) - erythrocytes (RBCs, O₂/CO₂ transport), leukocytes (WBCs, immunity), thrombocytes/platelets (clotting). Respiratory pigments: hemoglobin (in RBCs) greatly increases O₂ capacity. (b) Lymph: Interstitial fluid entering lymphatic vessels. Composition similar to plasma but less protein. Transports fats (chyle) from intestine, returns interstitial fluid to blood, immune surveillance. (5) Comparative aspects: Transport media composition reflects organismal requirements: (a) Osmotic balance: Freshwater vs marine organisms regulate solute content. (b) Oxygen transport: Presence/absence of respiratory pigments; pigment type (hemoglobin, hemocyanin, chlorocruorin, hemerythrin). (c) Waste handling: Ammonia (aquatic) vs urea (mammals) vs uric acid (birds, reptiles) in blood. (d) Pressure: Open systems (low pressure) vs closed systems (high pressure). (6) Transport media diseases: Anemias (low RBC/hemoglobin), leukemias (WBC cancers), hemoglobinopathies (sickle cell), hemophilia (clotting disorders).', 'media of transport, intracellular transport, cytoplasm, diffusion, small molecules, ions, short distances, cyclosis, cytoplasmic streaming, large cells, plant cells, protozoa, actin-myosin, organelle distribution, nutrient distribution, plant transport media, cell sap, vacuoles, xylem, phloem, xylem sap, dilute solution, water, minerals, hormones, tension, phloem sap, concentrated solution, sucrose, 10-30%, amino acids, RNA, pressure, latex, milky fluid, laticifers, rubber tree, papaya, opium poppy, rubber, alkaloids, proteins, resins, defense, wound sealing, toxin delivery, invertebrate transport media, hemolymph, open circulatory systems, arthropods, molluscs, interstitial fluid, hemocyanin, copper-based, respiratory pigment, hemoglobin, insects, nutrient distribution, waste removal, immune defense, hydraulic support, spider leg extension, tissues bathed, coelomic fluid, hydrostatic skeletons, annelids, nematodes, body cavity, vertebrate transport media, blood, specialized connective tissue, vessels, plasma, 55%, water, proteins, albumins, globulins, fibrinogen, nutrients, hormones, wastes, formed elements, 45%, erythrocytes, RBCs, O₂ transport, CO₂ transport, leukocytes, WBCs, immunity, thrombocytes, platelets, clotting, hemoglobin, O₂ capacity, lymph, interstitial fluid, lymphatic vessels, chyle, fats, intestine, interstitial fluid return, immune surveillance, comparative aspects, transport media composition, organismal requirements, osmotic balance, freshwater organisms, marine organisms, solute regulation, oxygen transport, respiratory pigments, hemoglobin, hemocyanin, chlorocruorin, hemerythrin, waste handling, ammonia, aquatic organisms, urea, mammals, uric acid, birds, reptiles, pressure, open systems, low pressure, closed systems, high pressure, transport media diseases, anemias, low RBC, low hemoglobin, leukemias, WBC cancers, hemoglobinopathies, sickle cell, hemophilia, clotting disorders', '2026-02-27 15:40:30');
INSERT INTO `biology_syllabus_comprehensive` (`id`, `topic`, `sub_topic`, `note`, `keywords`, `created_at`) VALUES
(66, '8. TRANSPORT SYSTEM', 'Transport in Animals - Open Circulatory System', 'Open circulatory systems are found in arthropods (insects, crustaceans, spiders) and most molluscs (snails, clams, except cephalopods). These systems represent an evolutionarily successful design, particularly for smaller animals with lower metabolic demands. Structure: (1) Heart: Tubular or chambered, often with ostia (valved openings). Located dorsally. Pericardial sinus surrounds heart. (2) Hemolymph: Circulatory fluid, analogous to blood but not confined to vessels. Contains hemocytes (immune cells), nutrients, hormones. Respiratory pigment (hemocyanin in many crustaceans, some insects; hemoglobin in some insects) may be dissolved in hemolymph (not in cells). (3) Vessels: Short arteries from heart open into sinuses. No continuous capillary network. (4) Sinuses (lacunae): Open spaces in tissues where hemolymph bathes cells directly. (5) Ostia: Openings allowing hemolymph re-entry into heart. Circulation: (1) Heart contracts (peristaltic waves), pumping hemolymph through arteries into sinuses. (2) Hemolymph percolates through sinuses, bathing tissues, exchanging gases, nutrients, wastes. (3) Hemolymph slowly returns to pericardial sinus, enters heart through ostia during diastole (heart relaxation). (4) Valves in ostia prevent backflow. (5) Rate: Low pressure, slow flow (compared to closed systems). Advantages: (1) Simple structure: Less complex than closed systems, easier to develop/evolve. (2) Lower energy cost: Heart works against lower resistance, less energy for circulation. (3) Hydraulic functions: Hemolymph pressure aids molting (ecdysis) in arthropods, wing expansion in insects, leg extension in spiders. (4) Immune surveillance: Hemocytes can access all tissues directly. (5) Sufficient for small size, low metabolic rate animals. Disadvantages: (1) Inefficient delivery: Slow flow, cannot meet high O₂ demands. (2) Limited pressure: Cannot support large body size. (3) Poor regulation: Cannot direct flow preferentially to active tissues. (4) Mixing: No separation of oxygenated/deoxygenated hemolymph. Adaptations: (1) Insects supplement with tracheal system (direct O₂ delivery to tissues), circulatory system mainly for nutrients, hormones. (2) Active insects have accessory pumps (wing hearts) to move hemolymph into appendages. (3) Some molluscs (cephalopods) evolved closed systems independently. Open systems illustrate trade-offs: simplicity vs efficiency, adequate for many organisms but limiting for large, active animals.', 'open circulatory system, arthropods, insects, crustaceans, spiders, molluscs, snails, clams, evolutionarily successful, smaller animals, lower metabolic demands, structure, heart, tubular, chambered, ostia, valved openings, dorsal, pericardial sinus, hemolymph, circulatory fluid, not confined to vessels, hemocytes, immune cells, nutrients, hormones, respiratory pigment, hemocyanin, crustaceans, insects, hemoglobin, dissolved, vessels, short arteries, sinuses, lacunae, open spaces, tissues bathed, ostia, re-entry, circulation, heart contraction, peristaltic waves, arteries, sinuses, percolates, bathing tissues, gas exchange, nutrient exchange, waste exchange, slow return, pericardial sinus, diastole, heart relaxation, valves, backflow prevention, low pressure, slow flow, advantages, simple structure, less complex, easier to evolve, lower energy cost, lower resistance, hydraulic functions, hemolymph pressure, molting, ecdysis, wing expansion, insects, leg extension, spiders, immune surveillance, hemocytes access, sufficient for small size, low metabolic rate, disadvantages, inefficient delivery, slow flow, cannot meet high O₂ demands, limited pressure, cannot support large body size, poor regulation, cannot direct flow, active tissues, mixing, no separation, oxygenated, deoxygenated, adaptations, insects, tracheal system, direct O₂ delivery, nutrients, hormones, accessory pumps, wing hearts, appendages, cephalopods, closed systems independently, trade-offs, simplicity vs efficiency, organism limitations', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(67, '8. TRANSPORT SYSTEM', 'Transport in Animals - Closed Circulatory System', 'Closed circulatory systems, found in annelids (earthworms) and all vertebrates, confine blood within a continuous network of vessels. This design enables higher pressure, directed flow, and precise regulation, supporting larger body size, higher metabolic rates, and complex activities. Structure: (1) Heart: Muscular pump(s) creating pressure. Vertebrate hearts: 2-chambered (fish), 3-chambered (amphibians, most reptiles), 4-chambered (crocodilians, birds, mammals). (2) Arteries: Thick-walled, muscular, elastic vessels carrying blood away from heart. Branch into arterioles. (3) Capillaries: Microscopic, thin-walled (one cell thick) vessels forming networks (capillary beds). Site of all exchange with tissues. (4) Venules: Collect blood from capillaries, merge into veins. (5) Veins: Thin-walled, less muscular, often with valves returning blood to heart. (6) Blood: Specialized connective tissue with plasma, formed elements (erythrocytes, leukocytes, platelets). Circulation: (1) Pumping: Heart generates high pressure (systolic ~120 mmHg human). (2) Arterial flow: Rapid, pulsatile flow in arteries. (3) Capillary exchange: Slow flow in capillaries allows diffusion time. (4) Venous return: Low pressure, aided by skeletal muscle pumps, respiratory pump, valves preventing backflow. (5) Regulation: Vasoconstriction/vasodilation of arterioles directs flow to active tissues. Types: (a) Single circulation (fish): Heart → gills → body → heart. Blood passes two capillary beds (gills, systemic) with pressure drop. (b) Double circulation (most vertebrates): Separate pulmonary (lung) and systemic circuits. Heart pumps blood to lungs then returns to heart before systemic circulation. Maintains higher pressure for systemic delivery. (c) Incomplete double circulation (amphibians, most reptiles): Some mixing of oxygenated/deoxygenated blood in ventricle. (d) Complete double circulation (birds, mammals): Septum separates ventricles, no mixing. Advantages: (1) High pressure: Enables rapid flow, supports large body size, gravity overcoming (giraffe). (2) Directed flow: Can regulate distribution to active organs. (3) Efficient delivery: Maintains steep concentration gradients for exchange. (4) Separation of circuits: Allows different pressures for pulmonary (low) and systemic (high) circuits (mammals, birds). (5) Precise regulation: Neural, hormonal control of vessel diameter, heart rate. (6) Large body size support: Essential for animals > few cm. (7) High metabolic rate support: Meets O₂ demands of endotherms. Disadvantages: (1) Complex structure: Requires more energy to develop, maintain. (2) Higher energy cost: Heart works against higher resistance. (3) Vulnerability: Vessel damage (hemorrhage) serious. (4) Disease susceptibility: Atherosclerosis, hypertension. Closed systems enabled vertebrate dominance, allowing active lifestyles, large size, colonization of diverse habitats.', 'closed circulatory system, annelids, earthworms, vertebrates, continuous network of vessels, higher pressure, directed flow, precise regulation, larger body size, higher metabolic rates, complex activities, structure, heart, muscular pump, 2-chambered heart, fish, 3-chambered heart, amphibians, reptiles, 4-chambered heart, crocodilians, birds, mammals, arteries, thick-walled, muscular, elastic, away from heart, arterioles, capillaries, microscopic, thin-walled, one cell thick, capillary beds, exchange site, venules, collect blood, veins, thin-walled, less muscular, valves, return blood, blood, specialized connective tissue, plasma, formed elements, erythrocytes, leukocytes, platelets, circulation, pumping, high pressure, systolic 120 mmHg, human, arterial flow, rapid, pulsatile, capillary exchange, slow flow, diffusion time, venous return, low pressure, skeletal muscle pumps, respiratory pump, valves, backflow prevention, regulation, vasoconstriction, vasodilation, arterioles, directed flow, active tissues, single circulation, fish, heart, gills, body, two capillary beds, gills, systemic, pressure drop, double circulation, most vertebrates, pulmonary circuit, systemic circuit, lung, heart pumping, higher pressure, systemic delivery, incomplete double circulation, amphibians, reptiles, mixing, oxygenated, deoxygenated, ventricle, complete double circulation, birds, mammals, septum, ventricles, no mixing, advantages, high pressure, rapid flow, large body size support, gravity overcoming, giraffe, directed flow, regulate distribution, active organs, efficient delivery, steep concentration gradients, exchange, separation of circuits, different pressures, pulmonary low, systemic high, precise regulation, neural control, hormonal control, vessel diameter, heart rate, large body size support, essential, high metabolic rate support, O₂ demands, endotherms, disadvantages, complex structure, more energy to develop, maintain, higher energy cost, higher resistance, vulnerability, vessel damage, hemorrhage, disease susceptibility, atherosclerosis, hypertension, vertebrate dominance, active lifestyles, large size, habitat colonization', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(68, '8. TRANSPORT SYSTEM', 'Structure of the Heart', 'The mammalian heart is a four-chambered muscular pump ensuring efficient double circulation, separating oxygenated and deoxygenated blood. Its structure reflects millions of years of evolutionary refinement for optimal function. External features: Cone-shaped, about size of fist (250-350 g humans). Located in mediastinum (between lungs), within pericardial sac (fibrous pericardium with serous fluid reducing friction). Walls: three layers - epicardium (outer), myocardium (thick muscular middle), endocardium (inner endothelial lining). Chambers: (1) Right atrium: Receives deoxygenated blood from body via superior and inferior venae cavae. Thin-walled, low pressure. (2) Right ventricle: Pumps deoxygenated blood to lungs via pulmonary artery. Moderately thick walls (less than left). (3) Left atrium: Receives oxygenated blood from lungs via pulmonary veins. Thin-walled. (4) Left ventricle: Pumps oxygenated blood to body via aorta. Thickest walls (generates high pressure ~120 mmHg). Valves: Ensure one-way flow. (1) Atrioventricular valves: Between atria and ventricles. Tricuspid (right) - three leaflets; Bicuspid (mitral, left) - two leaflets. Chordae tendineae (tendinous cords) attach to papillary muscles, preventing valve inversion during ventricular contraction. (2) Semilunar valves: Between ventricles and arteries. Pulmonary valve (right ventricle → pulmonary artery); Aortic valve (left ventricle → aorta). Each has three pocket-like cusps; prevent backflow into ventricles. Septum: Muscular wall dividing right and left sides, preventing mixing of oxygenated/deoxygenated blood. Coronary circulation: Heart muscle supplied by coronary arteries (branches from aorta just above aortic valve). Coronary veins collect into coronary sinus draining to right atrium. Blockage causes myocardial infarction (heart attack). Conducting system: (1) Sinoatrial (SA) node: Pacemaker in right atrium, initiates impulse (~70/min). (2) Atrioventricular (AV) node: In interatrial septum, delays impulse allowing atrial contraction before ventricular. (3) Bundle of His: Conducts impulse to ventricles via interventricular septum. (4) Purkinje fibers: Distribute impulse through ventricular myocardium. Cardiac cycle: Systole (contraction) - ventricles contract, AV valves close (lub), semilunar valves open, blood ejected. Diastole (relaxation) - ventricles relax, semilunar valves close (dub), AV valves open, ventricles fill. Heart sounds: \"lub\" (AV valves closing), \"dub\" (semilunar valves closing). Cardiac output = stroke volume × heart rate (~5 L/min human). Regulation: Autonomic nervous system (sympathetic increases rate/force, parasympathetic decreases), hormones (adrenaline).', 'heart structure, mammalian heart, four-chambered, double circulation, oxygenated blood, deoxygenated blood, evolutionary refinement, external features, cone-shaped, fist size, 250-350 g, humans, mediastinum, between lungs, pericardial sac, fibrous pericardium, serous fluid, friction reduction, walls, epicardium, outer, myocardium, thick muscular, endocardium, inner endothelial lining, chambers, right atrium, deoxygenated blood, superior vena cava, inferior vena cava, thin-walled, low pressure, right ventricle, pumps, lungs, pulmonary artery, moderately thick walls, left atrium, oxygenated blood, pulmonary veins, left ventricle, pumps, body, aorta, thickest walls, high pressure, 120 mmHg, valves, one-way flow, atrioventricular valves, tricuspid valve, right, three leaflets, bicuspid valve, mitral valve, left, two leaflets, chordae tendineae, tendinous cords, papillary muscles, valve inversion prevention, ventricular contraction, semilunar valves, pulmonary valve, right ventricle to pulmonary artery, aortic valve, left ventricle to aorta, pocket-like cusps, backflow prevention, septum, muscular wall, right side, left side, mixing prevention, coronary circulation, coronary arteries, aorta branches, coronary veins, coronary sinus, right atrium, blockage, myocardial infarction, heart attack, conducting system, sinoatrial node, SA node, pacemaker, right atrium, impulse initiation, 70/min, atrioventricular node, AV node, interatrial septum, delay, atrial contraction, bundle of His, interventricular septum, Purkinje fibers, ventricular myocardium, cardiac cycle, systole, contraction, AV valves close, lub, semilunar valves open, blood ejected, diastole, relaxation, semilunar valves close, dub, AV valves open, ventricles fill, heart sounds, lub, AV valves closing, dub, semilunar valves closing, cardiac output, stroke volume, heart rate, 5 L/min, human, regulation, autonomic nervous system, sympathetic, increases rate, increases force, parasympathetic, decreases, hormones, adrenaline', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(69, '8. TRANSPORT SYSTEM', 'Arteries, Veins, and Capillaries', 'Blood vessels form a hierarchical network optimizing transport, exchange, and return. Each vessel type has structural adaptations for its specific function. Arteries: Carry blood away from heart under high pressure. Structure: (1) Tunica intima: Inner endothelium (simple squamous) on basement membrane. (2) Tunica media: Thick layer of smooth muscle, elastic fibers (elastin), collagen. Vasoconstriction/vasodilation regulated by autonomic nerves, hormones. (3) Tunica adventitia: Outer connective tissue with collagen, elastic fibers, vasa vasorum (vessels supplying wall). Types: (a) Elastic arteries (aorta, pulmonary trunk) - large diameter, more elastic fibers, dampen pressure fluctuations (Windkessel effect). (b) Muscular arteries (most named arteries) - more smooth muscle, regulate distribution. (c) Arterioles - smallest arteries, diameter 10-100 μm, major resistance vessels, regulate capillary flow. Veins: Carry blood toward heart under low pressure. Structure: (1) Thinner walls, less muscle, less elastic tissue than arteries. (2) Larger lumen diameter. (3) Valves (in limbs) - folds of intima preventing backflow, essential for venous return against gravity. (4) Tunica adventitia relatively thickest layer. Types: (a) Venules - collect from capillaries, 10-100 μm. (b) Medium veins - with valves. (c) Large veins (venae cavae) - without valves, thick adventitia. Venous return mechanisms: (a) Skeletal muscle pump: contracting muscles compress veins, pushing blood toward heart. (b) Respiratory pump: inspiration decreases thoracic pressure, increases abdominal pressure, promoting flow. (c) Cardiac suction: atrial diastole creates negative pressure. (d) Venoconstriction: sympathetic stimulation reduces venous capacity, increasing return. Capillaries: Microscopic vessels (5-10 μm diameter) where exchange occurs. Structure: Single layer of endothelial cells on basement membrane. Pericytes (contractile cells) surround, regulate permeability. Types: (1) Continuous capillaries: Most common (muscle, skin, lung). Endothelial cells with tight junctions; small molecules pass through intercellular clefts. (2) Fenestrated capillaries: Kidneys, intestines, endocrine glands. Endothelial cells have pores (fenestrations, 50-100 nm) for rapid exchange. (3) Sinusoidal capillaries (discontinuous): Liver, spleen, bone marrow. Large gaps, incomplete basement membrane, allow large molecules, cells passage. Capillary beds: Networks of 10-100 capillaries. Metarterioles connect arteriole to venule; thoroughfare channels allow bypass. Precapillary sphincters regulate flow into true capillaries. Exchange mechanisms: (1) Diffusion: O₂, CO₂, small molecules down concentration gradients. (2) Filtration: Pressure-driven fluid movement (Starling forces). (3) Transcytosis: Vesicular transport for larger molecules. Capillary density correlates with tissue metabolic rate (high in muscle, brain; low in cartilage, tendons).', 'arteries, carry blood away from heart, high pressure, tunica intima, inner endothelium, simple squamous, basement membrane, tunica media, thick layer, smooth muscle, elastic fibers, elastin, collagen, vasoconstriction, vasodilation, autonomic nerves, hormones, tunica adventitia, outer connective tissue, vasa vasorum, elastic arteries, aorta, pulmonary trunk, large diameter, more elastic fibers, Windkessel effect, pressure dampening, muscular arteries, named arteries, more smooth muscle, regulate distribution, arterioles, smallest arteries, 10-100 μm, resistance vessels, regulate capillary flow, veins, carry blood toward heart, low pressure, thinner walls, less muscle, less elastic, larger lumen diameter, valves, limbs, intima folds, backflow prevention, venous return, gravity, venules, 10-100 μm, medium veins, valves, large veins, venae cavae, no valves, thick adventitia, venous return mechanisms, skeletal muscle pump, contracting muscles, compress veins, respiratory pump, inspiration, decreased thoracic pressure, increased abdominal pressure, cardiac suction, atrial diastole, negative pressure, venoconstriction, sympathetic stimulation, venous capacity reduction, capillaries, microscopic vessels, 5-10 μm, exchange site, endothelial cells, single layer, basement membrane, pericytes, contractile cells, permeability regulation, continuous capillaries, most common, muscle, skin, lung, tight junctions, intercellular clefts, fenestrated capillaries, kidneys, intestines, endocrine glands, fenestrations, pores, 50-100 nm, rapid exchange, sinusoidal capillaries, discontinuous, liver, spleen, bone marrow, large gaps, incomplete basement membrane, large molecules, cells passage, capillary beds, networks, 10-100 capillaries, metarterioles, thoroughfare channels, precapillary sphincters, flow regulation, true capillaries, exchange mechanisms, diffusion, O₂, CO₂, concentration gradients, filtration, pressure-driven, Starling forces, transcytosis, vesicular transport, larger molecules, capillary density, tissue metabolic rate, high in muscle, brain, low in cartilage, tendons', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(70, '8. TRANSPORT SYSTEM', 'Composition of Blood', 'Blood is a specialized connective tissue composed of cellular elements suspended in plasma. Its complex composition reflects diverse functions: transport, regulation, protection. Plasma (~55% of blood volume): Pale yellow fluid (90% water) containing: (1) Plasma proteins (7-8%): (a) Albumins (60%): osmotic pressure maintenance, transport of fatty acids, hormones, drugs. (b) Globulins (35%): α- and β-globulins transport lipids, fat-soluble vitamins, metals; γ-globulins (immunoglobulins, antibodies) - immune function. (c) Fibrinogen (4%): blood clotting precursor; converted to fibrin during coagulation. (2) Nutrients: Glucose, amino acids, fatty acids, glycerol, vitamins. (3) Electrolytes: Na⁺, K⁺, Ca²⁺, Mg²⁺, Cl⁻, HCO₃⁻, PO₄³⁻. (4) Gases: O₂ (mostly bound to hemoglobin), CO₂ (as bicarbonate, dissolved), N₂. (5) Waste products: Urea, uric acid, creatinine, bilirubin. (6) Hormones, enzymes. Formed elements (~45% of blood volume): (1) Erythrocytes (red blood cells, RBCs): 4.5-5.5 million/μL (men), 4-5 million/μL (women). Biconcave discs (7-8 μm diameter) maximizing surface area/volume for gas exchange. Lack nucleus, mitochondria, ribosomes (mammals) - cannot divide, limited lifespan (~120 days). Contain hemoglobin (iron-containing protein) for O₂ and CO₂ transport. Produced in bone marrow (erythropoiesis) stimulated by erythropoietin (kidney). Removed by spleen, liver (macrophages). (2) Leukocytes (white blood cells, WBCs): 4,000-11,000/μL. Nucleated, immune functions. Types: (a) Granulocytes (cytoplasmic granules): Neutrophils (50-70%) - phagocytes, first responders to bacterial infection; Eosinophils (1-4%) - combat parasites, allergic responses; Basophils (<1%) - release histamine in allergic reactions. (b) Agranulocytes: Lymphocytes (20-40%) - B cells (antibody production), T cells (cell-mediated immunity), NK cells (innate immunity); Monocytes (2-8%) - differentiate into macrophages, dendritic cells (phagocytosis, antigen presentation). (3) Platelets (thrombocytes): 150,000-400,000/μL. Cell fragments from megakaryocytes in bone marrow. Contain granules with clotting factors. Essential for hemostasis (blood clot formation). Adhere to damaged vessel, aggregate, release factors activating coagulation cascade. Hematocrit: Percentage of blood volume occupied by RBCs (45% average). Plasma composition reflects body status; abnormalities indicate disease (e.g., high glucose in diabetes, high urea in kidney failure).', 'blood composition, specialized connective tissue, cellular elements, plasma, 55% blood volume, pale yellow fluid, 90% water, plasma proteins, 7-8%, albumins, 60%, osmotic pressure maintenance, fatty acid transport, hormone transport, drug transport, globulins, 35%, α-globulins, β-globulins, lipid transport, fat-soluble vitamins, metal transport, γ-globulins, immunoglobulins, antibodies, immune function, fibrinogen, 4%, blood clotting precursor, fibrin, coagulation, nutrients, glucose, amino acids, fatty acids, glycerol, vitamins, electrolytes, Na⁺, K⁺, Ca²⁺, Mg²⁺, Cl⁻, HCO₃⁻, PO₄³⁻, gases, O₂, hemoglobin, CO₂, bicarbonate, dissolved, N₂, waste products, urea, uric acid, creatinine, bilirubin, hormones, enzymes, formed elements, 45% blood volume, erythrocytes, red blood cells, RBCs, 4.5-5.5 million/μL, men, 4-5 million/μL, women, biconcave discs, 7-8 μm, surface area maximization, gas exchange, no nucleus, no mitochondria, no ribosomes, mammals, limited lifespan, 120 days, hemoglobin, iron-containing protein, O₂ transport, CO₂ transport, bone marrow, erythropoiesis, erythropoietin, kidney, spleen removal, liver removal, macrophages, leukocytes, white blood cells, WBCs, 4,000-11,000/μL, nucleated, immune functions, granulocytes, cytoplasmic granules, neutrophils, 50-70%, phagocytes, first responders, bacterial infection, eosinophils, 1-4%, parasites, allergic responses, basophils, <1%, histamine, allergic reactions, agranulocytes, lymphocytes, 20-40%, B cells, antibody production, T cells, cell-mediated immunity, NK cells, innate immunity, monocytes, 2-8%, macrophages, dendritic cells, phagocytosis, antigen presentation, platelets, thrombocytes, 150,000-400,000/μL, cell fragments, megakaryocytes, bone marrow, granules, clotting factors, hemostasis, blood clot formation, adhere, damaged vessel, aggregate, coagulation cascade, hematocrit, percentage, RBCs, 45% average, plasma composition, body status, disease indicators, high glucose, diabetes, high urea, kidney failure', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(71, '8. TRANSPORT SYSTEM', 'Functions of Blood', 'Blood performs a remarkable array of functions essential for homeostasis, defense, and communication. These functions reflect blood\'s unique composition and circulatory distribution. (1) Transport: (a) Respiratory gases: O₂ from lungs to tissues (bound to hemoglobin in RBCs); CO₂ from tissues to lungs (as bicarbonate, dissolved, bound to hemoglobin). (b) Nutrients: Absorbed digestion products (glucose, amino acids, fatty acids, vitamins, minerals) from intestine to all cells. (c) Metabolic wastes: Urea, uric acid, creatinine from cells to kidneys for excretion. (d) Hormones: From endocrine glands to target organs (endocrine signaling). (e) Heat: From deep tissues (muscles, liver) to skin for dissipation; equalizes body temperature. (2) Regulation: (a) pH balance: Buffer systems (bicarbonate/carbonic acid, phosphate, proteins) maintain blood pH 7.35-7.45. Hemoglobin also buffers. (b) Fluid-electrolyte balance: Plasma proteins (albumins) maintain osmotic pressure, preventing excessive fluid loss from capillaries. (c) Temperature regulation: Vasodilation increases heat loss; vasoconstriction conserves heat. Blood distributes heat throughout body. (3) Protection: (a) Immune defense: Leukocytes (neutrophils, macrophages phagocytose pathogens; lymphocytes produce antibodies, kill infected cells). Complement system proteins lyse microbes. (b) Hemostasis: Platelets adhere to damaged vessels, form plug; coagulation cascade produces fibrin clot preventing blood loss. (c) Inflammation: Blood delivers immune cells, factors to injury sites; causes redness, heat, swelling. (d) Transport of antibodies: From sites of production to sites of infection. (e) Wound healing: Delivers platelets, growth factors, clotting factors. (4) Communication: (a) Hormone transport: Endocrine system uses blood as communication network. (b) Cytokine signaling: Immune cells communicate via blood-borne cytokines. (5) Homeostatic monitoring: Blood carries indicators of body status (glucose, O₂, CO₂, pH) detected by sensors (brain, carotid bodies, kidneys), triggering corrective responses. (6) Detoxification: Blood transports toxins to liver for processing. (7) Thermoregulation: Adjusts heat distribution, skin blood flow. Blood functions are integrated; disruption causes disease: anemia (O₂ transport deficient), leukopenia (infection susceptibility), hemophilia (clotting deficient), acidosis/alkalosis (pH imbalance), edema (fluid balance disrupted).', 'blood functions, transport, respiratory gases, O₂ transport, lungs to tissues, hemoglobin, RBCs, CO₂ transport, tissues to lungs, bicarbonate, dissolved, bound to hemoglobin, nutrients transport, digestion products, glucose, amino acids, fatty acids, vitamins, minerals, intestine to cells, metabolic wastes transport, urea, uric acid, creatinine, cells to kidneys, excretion, hormones transport, endocrine glands to target organs, endocrine signaling, heat transport, deep tissues, muscles, liver, skin dissipation, body temperature equalization, regulation, pH balance, buffer systems, bicarbonate, carbonic acid, phosphate, proteins, blood pH 7.35-7.45, hemoglobin buffer, fluid-electrolyte balance, plasma proteins, albumins, osmotic pressure, fluid loss prevention, capillaries, temperature regulation, vasodilation, heat loss, vasoconstriction, heat conservation, heat distribution, protection, immune defense, leukocytes, neutrophils, phagocytosis, pathogens, macrophages, lymphocytes, antibodies, infected cells, complement system, proteins, microbe lysis, hemostasis, platelets, adhere, damaged vessels, plug formation, coagulation cascade, fibrin clot, blood loss prevention, inflammation, immune cells delivery, injury sites, redness, heat, swelling, antibodies transport, production sites, infection sites, wound healing, growth factors, clotting factors, communication, hormone transport, endocrine system, communication network, cytokine signaling, immune cells, blood-borne cytokines, homeostatic monitoring, body status indicators, glucose, O₂, CO₂, pH, sensors, brain, carotid bodies, kidneys, corrective responses, detoxification, toxin transport, liver processing, thermoregulation, heat distribution, skin blood flow, integration, disruption, disease, anemia, O₂ transport deficient, leukopenia, infection susceptibility, hemophilia, clotting deficient, acidosis, pH imbalance, alkalosis, edema, fluid balance disrupted', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(72, '8. TRANSPORT SYSTEM', 'Lymph and Lymphatic System', 'The lymphatic system is a network of vessels, nodes, and organs complementing the circulatory system. It plays crucial roles in fluid balance, fat absorption, and immune defense. Lymph: Interstitial fluid that enters lymphatic capillaries. Composition similar to plasma but with less protein (due to blood capillary retention). Contains lymphocytes, antigen-presenting cells, antibodies, fats (as chyle after meals). Lymphatic vessels: (1) Lymphatic capillaries: Blind-ended, thin-walled vessels in almost all tissues. Overlapping endothelial cells act as one-way valves, allowing fluid entry but preventing backflow. Anchored by anchoring filaments, they open when interstitial pressure increases. (2) Collecting vessels: Have valves, pass through lymph nodes. (3) Lymphatic trunks: Jugular, subclavian, bronchomediastinal, intestinal, lumbar trunks. (4) Lymphatic ducts: Right lymphatic duct (drains right upper body) and thoracic duct (drains rest of body) empty into subclavian veins. Lymph nodes: Bean-shaped structures (1-25 mm) along lymphatic vessels. Cortex contains B-cell follicles, paracortex contains T-cells, medulla contains macrophages, plasma cells. Functions: (1) Filter lymph: Macrophages phagocytose pathogens, debris. (2) Immune surveillance: Dendritic cells present antigens to lymphocytes; lymphocytes encounter antigens, proliferate. (3) Antibody production: Plasma cells secrete antibodies. Lymphatic organs: (1) Spleen: Filters blood (not lymph). Removes old RBCs, recycles iron. Stores platelets, monocytes. Immune responses to blood-borne pathogens. (2) Thymus: Site of T-cell maturation (in children; involutes after puberty). (3) Tonsils: Trap pathogens from air, food. (4) Peyer\'s patches (intestine): Monitor intestinal bacteria. (5) Appendix: Contains lymphoid tissue. Functions of lymphatic system: (1) Fluid balance: Returns ~3 L/day of interstitial fluid and plasma proteins to blood, preventing edema. (2) Fat absorption: Intestinal lacteals (lymphatic capillaries in villi) absorb dietary fats (as chylomicrons) → lymph (chyle) → blood. (3) Immune defense: Lymph nodes filter pathogens; lymphocytes circulate, survey for antigens. (4) Waste removal: Removes cellular debris, foreign material. Lymph flow: Driven by (a) interstitial fluid pressure, (b) rhythmic contractions of lymphatic vessels (lymphangions), (c) skeletal muscle pump, (d) respiratory movements, (e) arterial pulsations. Disorders: Lymphedema (fluid accumulation from blockage, e.g., filariasis, cancer surgery), lymphadenopathy (swollen nodes in infection), lymphoma (lymphatic cancer).', 'lymph, lymphatic system, interstitial fluid, lymphatic capillaries, fluid balance, fat absorption, immune defense, lymph composition, plasma, less protein, lymphocytes, antigen-presenting cells, antibodies, fats, chyle, lymphatic vessels, lymphatic capillaries, blind-ended, thin-walled, overlapping endothelial cells, one-way valves, anchoring filaments, interstitial pressure, collecting vessels, valves, lymph nodes, lymphatic trunks, jugular trunk, subclavian trunk, bronchomediastinal trunk, intestinal trunk, lumbar trunk, lymphatic ducts, right lymphatic duct, right upper body, thoracic duct, rest of body, subclavian veins, lymph nodes, bean-shaped, 1-25 mm, cortex, B-cell follicles, paracortex, T-cells, medulla, macrophages, plasma cells, filter lymph, phagocytose, pathogens, debris, immune surveillance, dendritic cells, antigen presentation, lymphocyte proliferation, antibody production, plasma cells, lymphatic organs, spleen, filter blood, remove old RBCs, iron recycling, platelet storage, monocyte storage, blood-borne pathogens, thymus, T-cell maturation, children, involutes, puberty, tonsils, trap pathogens, air, food, Peyer\'s patches, intestine, intestinal bacteria, appendix, lymphoid tissue, lymphatic functions, fluid balance, 3 L/day, interstitial fluid return, plasma proteins, edema prevention, fat absorption, intestinal lacteals, villi, dietary fats, chylomicrons, chyle, blood, immune defense, lymph nodes filter pathogens, lymphocyte circulation, antigen survey, waste removal, cellular debris, foreign material, lymph flow, interstitial fluid pressure, rhythmic contractions, lymphatic vessels, lymphangions, skeletal muscle pump, respiratory movements, arterial pulsations, disorders, lymphedema, fluid accumulation, blockage, filariasis, cancer surgery, lymphadenopathy, swollen nodes, infection, lymphoma, lymphatic cancer', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(73, '8. TRANSPORT SYSTEM', 'Transport in Plants - Uptake of Water and Minerals', 'Plants absorb water and minerals from soil through root systems, transporting them to all parts via xylem. This process involves multiple steps and regulatory mechanisms. Root structure for uptake: Root hairs: Epidermal cell extensions (0.1-1.5 mm long) massively increasing surface area (up to 40% of root surface). Each root hair lives few days, continuously replaced. Zone of maturation: Region behind root tip where root hairs develop, cells differentiate. Water uptake: (1) Mechanism: Osmosis - water moves from soil (higher water potential) into root hair cells (lower water potential due to solute concentration). Water potential gradient maintained by continuous transpiration. (2) Pathways through root: (a) Apoplast pathway: Water moves through cell walls, intercellular spaces without crossing membranes. Fast, but blocked at endodermis by Casparian strip. (b) Symplast pathway: Water enters cytoplasm via plasma membrane (through aquaporins, water channels), moves through plasmodesmata between cells. Slower but allows control. (3) Endodermis and Casparian strip: Endodermis is single layer surrounding stele (vascular cylinder). Casparian strip: suberin and lignin band in radial and transverse walls, blocking apoplast pathway. Forces water and solutes into symplast, allowing selective uptake control. Mineral uptake: (1) Mechanisms: (a) Passive diffusion: Some ions enter along electrochemical gradient. (b) Facilitated diffusion: Channel proteins allow specific ions (K⁺). (c) Active transport: Most minerals against concentration gradient, requiring ATP and carrier proteins (H⁺-ATPase pumps create proton gradient, driving secondary active transport). (2) Selectivity: Root cells selectively absorb essential minerals, exclude toxins. (3) Mycorrhizal associations: Fungal hyphae extend root absorption zone, provide minerals (especially P), receive carbohydrates. Transport to xylem: After endodermis, water and minerals enter xylem vessels (dead, hollow cells with lignified walls). Loading into xylem: active secretion of ions into xylem lowers water potential, drawing water (root pressure). Xylem transport driven primarily by transpiration pull. Factors affecting uptake: (1) Soil factors: Water availability, aeration, temperature, pH, mineral concentration. (2) Plant factors: Root surface area, transpiration rate, metabolic activity (for active transport). (3) Environmental: Light (affects transpiration), humidity, wind.', 'plant transport, water uptake, mineral uptake, root hairs, epidermal cell extensions, 0.1-1.5 mm, surface area increase, 40%, zone of maturation, root tip, cell differentiation, water uptake mechanism, osmosis, soil water potential, root hair water potential, solute concentration, transpiration, water potential gradient, pathways through root, apoplast pathway, cell walls, intercellular spaces, no membrane crossing, fast, endodermis, Casparian strip, symplast pathway, cytoplasm, plasma membrane, aquaporins, water channels, plasmodesmata, slower, control, endodermis, single layer, stele, vascular cylinder, Casparian strip, suberin, lignin band, radial walls, transverse walls, apoplast blockage, symplast, selective uptake, mineral uptake mechanisms, passive diffusion, electrochemical gradient, facilitated diffusion, channel proteins, K⁺, active transport, concentration gradient, ATP, carrier proteins, H⁺-ATPase pumps, proton gradient, secondary active transport, selectivity, essential minerals, toxins exclusion, mycorrhizal associations, fungal hyphae, absorption zone extension, phosphorus, carbohydrates, transport to xylem, xylem vessels, dead cells, hollow, lignified walls, loading into xylem, active secretion, ions, water potential lowering, root pressure, xylem transport, transpiration pull, factors affecting uptake, soil factors, water availability, aeration, temperature, pH, mineral concentration, plant factors, root surface area, transpiration rate, metabolic activity, active transport, environmental factors, light, transpiration, humidity, wind', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(74, '8. TRANSPORT SYSTEM', 'Movement of Water and Minerals through Xylem', 'The ascent of water and dissolved minerals from roots to leaves occurs through xylem, primarily driven by transpiration. This process is explained by the cohesion-tension theory, one of the most elegant concepts in plant physiology. Cohesion-tension theory (Dixon and Joly, 1894): (1) Transpiration pull: Water evaporates from mesophyll cell walls into air spaces, then out through stomata. This creates negative pressure (tension) at leaf surface. (2) Cohesion: Water molecules are cohesive due to hydrogen bonding (strong intermolecular forces). This allows water columns to withstand tension without breaking (theoretical tensile strength up to 30 MPa). (3) Adhesion: Water molecules adhere to hydrophilic xylem vessel walls, helping maintain continuous columns. (4) Tension transmission: Tension from leaves transmitted down continuous water columns in xylem to roots. (5) Water uptake: Tension at root surface pulls water from soil into roots. Evidence supporting theory: (a) Xylem sap under tension can be measured with pressure bomb. (b) Water columns break if air enters (cavitation), stopping flow. (c) Transpiration rate correlates with flow rate. (d) Dye experiments show continuous columns. Root pressure: Positive pressure generated by active secretion of ions into xylem in roots (especially at night when transpiration low). Causes guttation (water droplets on leaf margins) in some plants. Contributes to water movement but insufficient for tall trees. Xylem structure adaptations: (1) Vessels (angiosperms): Stacked dead cells with perforated end walls (perforation plates), forming continuous tubes. Walls have pits (thin areas) allowing lateral water movement. Thickened with lignin (rings, spirals, reticulations) providing strength while allowing flexibility. (2) Tracheids (gymnosperms, ferns): Elongated cells with tapered ends, water moves through pits (no perforations). Slower flow but safer (cavitation contained). (3) Pits: Areas without secondary wall where primary wall allows water movement between adjacent cells. Bordered pits have overarching secondary wall, torus (thickening) that can block pit (aspiration) preventing air spread. Cavitation and embolism: Under high tension or freeze-thaw, air bubbles can enter, breaking water column (cavitation). Embolism (air bubble) blocks vessel. Plants: (a) Refill vessels via root pressure (at night). (b) Produce new xylem each year (secondary growth). (c) Have pit structures preventing air spread (torus in bordered pits). Measuring xylem flow: Thermoelectric methods, dye tracers, MRI, isotope tracking. Maximum flow speeds: 1-10 m/h (angiosperms), 0.5-2 m/h (gymnosperms). Tallest trees (redwoods, 115 m) rely on cohesion-tension, approaching physical limits.', 'xylem transport, water movement, mineral movement, ascent of water, cohesion-tension theory, Dixon and Joly, 1894, transpiration pull, water evaporation, mesophyll cell walls, air spaces, stomata, negative pressure, tension, cohesion, water molecules, hydrogen bonding, intermolecular forces, water columns, tensile strength, 30 MPa, adhesion, hydrophilic xylem vessel walls, continuous columns, tension transmission, leaves to roots, water uptake, roots, soil, pressure bomb, cavitation, air entry, flow stop, transpiration rate, flow rate correlation, dye experiments, root pressure, positive pressure, active secretion, ions, xylem, night, low transpiration, guttation, water droplets, leaf margins, tall trees, xylem structure adaptations, vessels, angiosperms, stacked dead cells, perforated end walls, perforation plates, continuous tubes, pits, thin areas, lateral water movement, thickened lignin, rings, spirals, reticulations, strength, flexibility, tracheids, gymnosperms, ferns, elongated cells, tapered ends, pit movement, no perforations, slower flow, safer, cavitation containment, pits, secondary wall, primary wall, bordered pits, overarching secondary wall, torus, thickening, pit blockage, aspiration, air spread prevention, cavitation, embolism, high tension, freeze-thaw, air bubbles, water column break, vessel block, refill vessels, root pressure, night, new xylem production, secondary growth, pit structures, torus, bordered pits, measuring xylem flow, thermoelectric methods, dye tracers, MRI, isotope tracking, flow speeds, 1-10 m/h, angiosperms, 0.5-2 m/h, gymnosperms, tallest trees, redwoods, 115 m, physical limits', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(75, '8. TRANSPORT SYSTEM', 'Translocation - Movement of Organic Materials', 'Translocation is the transport of organic nutrients (mainly sucrose, but also amino acids, hormones, RNA, proteins) in the phloem from sources to sinks. This process is fundamental to distributing photosynthetic products throughout the plant. Sources: Sites of production or mobilization: (1) Mature leaves (photosynthesis). (2) Storage organs during mobilization (germinating seeds, sprouting tubers). Sinks: Sites of utilization or storage: (1) Growing regions: root and shoot tips, developing leaves. (2) Storage organs: developing seeds, fruits, tubers, bulbs. (3) Reproductive structures: flowers, fruits. Phloem structure: Sieve tube elements (STEs): Living but enucleate at maturity (lack nucleus, ribosomes, vacuole). Retain plasma membrane, some mitochondria, plastids, smooth ER. Connected end-to-end by sieve plates (porous walls) allowing flow. P-protein (phloem protein) may seal damage. Companion cells: Adjacent, nucleated cells connected to STEs by numerous plasmodesmata. Provide metabolic support (protein synthesis, ATP production). Load sugars into STEs. Types: ordinary companion cells, transfer cells (with wall ingrowths for increased membrane area), intermediary cells (with many plasmodesmata). Pressure flow hypothesis (Mass flow hypothesis): Proposed by Ernst Münch (1930). Mechanism: (1) Phloem loading: Sucrose actively loaded into sieve tubes at source (against concentration gradient). Requires ATP and sucrose-H⁺ symporters (driven by proton gradient from H⁺-ATPase). (2) Osmotic water entry: High sucrose concentration lowers water potential; water enters from adjacent xylem by osmosis. (3) Pressure increase: Hydrostatic pressure builds in source phloem. (4) Pressure gradient: Pressure drives bulk flow of phloem sap from source (high pressure) to sink (low pressure). (5) Phloem unloading: At sink, sucrose actively or passively unloaded, decreasing concentration. (6) Water exit: Water follows osmotically, reducing pressure, returning to xylem. (7) Recycling: Xylem transports water back to leaves (transpiration stream). Evidence supporting hypothesis: (a) Aphids feeding on phloem exude sap under pressure. (b) Phloem sap flows from cut stem. (c) Pressure gradients measurable. (d) Metabolic inhibitors stop translocation (active loading required). (e) Phloem sap composition consistent (10-30% sucrose). Rates: 0.5-2 m/h. Translocation velocity varies with species, time of day, sink demand. Phloem sap contains: sucrose (main transport form), amino acids, amides, ureides (in some legumes), hormones (auxin, cytokinins, ABA), RNAs (including mRNA, siRNA), proteins (including P-proteins, chaperones). ATP also present (millimolar concentrations). Regulation: (1) Source activity: Photosynthesis rate. (2) Sink demand: Growing organs produce signals (possibly sucrose itself, hormones). (3) Hormones: Auxin, cytokinins affect unloading. (4) Diurnal rhythm: Day/night cycles affect source availability. Phloem blockage: Wounding triggers P-protein and callose (β-1,3 glucan) deposition at sieve plates, sealing damage. Some herbicides (glyphosate) disrupt phloem transport.', 'translocation, organic nutrients transport, phloem, sucrose, amino acids, hormones, RNA, proteins, sources, production sites, mobilization sites, mature leaves, photosynthesis, storage organs, germinating seeds, sprouting tubers, sinks, utilization sites, storage sites, growing regions, root tips, shoot tips, developing leaves, storage organs, developing seeds, fruits, tubers, bulbs, reproductive structures, flowers, fruits, phloem structure, sieve tube elements, STEs, living, enucleate, no nucleus, no ribosomes, no vacuole, plasma membrane, mitochondria, plastids, smooth ER, sieve plates, porous walls, flow, P-protein, phloem protein, seal damage, companion cells, adjacent, nucleated, plasmodesmata, metabolic support, protein synthesis, ATP production, sugar loading, ordinary companion cells, transfer cells, wall ingrowths, membrane area, intermediary cells, pressure flow hypothesis, mass flow hypothesis, Ernst Münch, 1930, phloem loading, active loading, sieve tubes, source, concentration gradient, ATP, sucrose-H⁺ symporters, proton gradient, H⁺-ATPase, osmotic water entry, sucrose concentration, water potential, xylem, osmosis, hydrostatic pressure, source phloem, pressure gradient, bulk flow, phloem sap, source to sink, high pressure to low pressure, phloem unloading, sink, active unloading, passive unloading, sucrose concentration decrease, water exit, water return to xylem, recycling, transpiration stream, evidence, aphids feeding, exude sap, pressure, cut stem sap flow, pressure gradients, measurable, metabolic inhibitors, translocation stop, active loading required, phloem sap composition, 10-30% sucrose, rates, 0.5-2 m/h, translocation velocity, species variation, time of day, sink demand, phloem sap contents, sucrose, amino acids, amides, ureides, legumes, hormones, auxin, cytokinins, ABA, RNAs, mRNA, siRNA, proteins, P-proteins, chaperones, ATP, millimolar concentrations, regulation, source activity, photosynthesis rate, sink demand, growing organs signals, sucrose itself, hormones, auxin, cytokinins, unloading effect, diurnal rhythm, day/night cycles, source availability, phloem blockage, wounding, P-protein, callose, β-1,3 glucan, sieve plates deposition, damage sealing, herbicides, glyphosate, phloem transport disruption', '2026-02-27 15:40:30');
INSERT INTO `biology_syllabus_comprehensive` (`id`, `topic`, `sub_topic`, `note`, `keywords`, `created_at`) VALUES
(76, '8. TRANSPORT SYSTEM', 'Ringing Experiment', 'The ringing (girdling) experiment is a classic demonstration of phloem function, elegantly revealing that phloem, not xylem, transports organic nutrients. This simple experiment has been used for centuries and remains a powerful teaching tool. Procedure: (1) Select a woody stem (tree branch) with leaves above and below. (2) Remove a complete ring of bark (including phloem, cortex, epidermis) around the circumference, about 1-2 cm wide. (3) Xylem (wood) remains intact. (4) Observe over weeks to months. Results: (1) Bark above the ring swells. (2) Tissues above ring remain healthy. (3) Tissues below ring may eventually die or show nutrient deficiency. (4) Ringed area does not heal (if bark completely removed). (5) Tree may survive if enough leaves above ring support it. Interpretations: (1) Swelling above ring: Organic nutrients (sucrose, amino acids) synthesized in leaves are translocated downward but cannot pass the ring because phloem is severed. Accumulation causes swelling. (2) Health above ring: Xylem intact, so water and minerals continue upward from roots. (3) Decline below ring: Roots deprived of organic nutrients from leaves; cannot sustain growth, may die. (4) Xylem function: Water transport continues (proved by dye uptake, no wilting initially). Critical conclusions: (1) Phloem transports organic nutrients (translocation). (2) Direction of phloem transport is from source (leaves) to sink (roots). (3) Xylem transports water and minerals (unaffected by ringing). (4) Phloem is essential for root survival. Variations and applications: (1) Time course: Swelling appears within weeks; root death may take months (depending on stored reserves). (2) Double ringing: Two rings with segment between - demonstrates that phloem transport is bidirectional? Actually, phloem transport in a single sieve tube is unidirectional, but different tubes can transport opposite directions. (3) Partial ringing: Leaving a strip of bark shows reduced effects, demonstrating phloem cross-sectional area matters. (4) Seasonal ringing: In temperate trees, ringing after leaves mature has different effects than before. Practical applications: (1) Fruit tree management: Ringing branches can increase fruit yield by diverting carbohydrates to fruits (accumulation above ring). (2) Killing unwanted trees: Ringing (girdling) kills tree by starving roots. (3) Studying source-sink relationships: Quantifying accumulation rates reveals transport rates. Modern refinements: (1) Using radioisotopes (¹⁴C-labeled CO₂) to track photosynthate movement precisely. (2) Aphid stylectomy: Cutting aphid stylets inserted into phloem collects sap for analysis. (3) Nuclear magnetic resonance (NMR) imaging visualizes phloem flow non-invasively. Limitations: (1) Only works on dicots with distinct bark; monocots (with scattered vascular bundles) can\'t be ringed effectively. (2) Wound responses (callose deposition) may affect results. (3) Doesn\'t reveal molecular mechanisms of loading/unloading. Historical significance: Used by Italian scientist Marcello Malpighi (1675) and English horticulturalist Stephen Hales (1727). Remains one of biology\'s most elegant, conceptually simple experiments.', 'ringing experiment, girdling experiment, phloem function demonstration, organic nutrient transport, xylem, phloem, teaching tool, procedure, woody stem, tree branch, leaves above, leaves below, bark removal, phloem, cortex, epidermis, complete ring, 1-2 cm wide, xylem intact, observation, weeks to months, results, bark above ring swells, tissues above ring healthy, tissues below ring die, nutrient deficiency, ringed area no healing, tree survival, interpretations, swelling above ring, organic nutrients, sucrose, amino acids, leaves, downward translocation, ring, phloem severed, accumulation, health above ring, xylem intact, water upward, minerals upward, decline below ring, roots, organic nutrients deprivation, growth stop, death, xylem function, water transport, dye uptake, no wilting, critical conclusions, phloem transports organic nutrients, translocation, phloem transport direction, source to sink, leaves to roots, xylem transports water, minerals, unaffected, phloem essential, root survival, variations, time course, swelling weeks, root death months, stored reserves, double ringing, two rings, segment between, phloem transport bidirectional, single sieve tube unidirectional, different tubes opposite directions, partial ringing, bark strip, reduced effects, cross-sectional area, seasonal ringing, temperate trees, leaves mature, fruit tree management, ringing branches, fruit yield increase, carbohydrate diversion, fruits, accumulation above ring, killing unwanted trees, girdling, root starvation, source-sink relationships, accumulation rates, transport rates, modern refinements, radioisotopes, ¹⁴C-labeled CO₂, photosynthate movement, aphid stylectomy, aphid stylets, phloem sap collection, nuclear magnetic resonance, NMR imaging, phloem flow, non-invasive, limitations, dicots only, distinct bark, monocots, scattered vascular bundles, no ringing, wound responses, callose deposition, loading/unloading mechanisms not revealed, historical significance, Marcello Malpighi, 1675, Stephen Hales, 1727, elegant experiment, conceptually simple', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(77, '8. TRANSPORT SYSTEM', 'Transpiration', 'Transpiration is the loss of water vapor from aerial plant parts, primarily through stomata. This seemingly wasteful process is an unavoidable consequence of gas exchange for photosynthesis, but plants have evolved to harness it for beneficial functions. Types of transpiration: (1) Stomatal transpiration (90-95%): Through stomata, microscopic pores in epidermis, especially leaves. Stomata open for CO₂ uptake, inevitably lose water vapor. (2) Cuticular transpiration (5-10%): Through cuticle (waxy layer covering epidermis). Rate depends on cuticle thickness; young leaves (thin cuticle) lose more, mature leaves less. (3) Lenticular transpiration (minimal): Through lenticels (loosely packed cells in bark, periderm). Significant in some woody stems. Mechanism: (1) Water evaporates from mesophyll cell walls into intercellular air spaces. (2) Water vapor diffuses through stomatal pore (driven by concentration gradient). (3) Gradient maintained by lower humidity outside (atmospheric) and transpiration stream. Driving forces: (1) Water potential gradient: From soil (high) → roots → xylem → leaves → atmosphere (very low, often -100 MPa). (2) Vapor pressure deficit: Difference between saturated water vapor pressure inside leaf and actual water vapor pressure of air. Magnitude of transpiration: Varies with species, environment. Average rates: 0.5-2.5 g water/dm² leaf area/hour. A single maize plant may lose 200 L water/growing season; a large oak tree up to 400 L/day. Benefits of transpiration: (1) Water and mineral uptake: Transpiration pull drives ascent of sap (cohesion-tension theory). Without transpiration, water movement in tall plants would be severely limited. (2) Cooling: Evaporative cooling can lower leaf temperature 10-15°C below air temperature, preventing protein denaturation, enzyme inactivation. (3) Maintains turgor: Continuous water flow keeps cells turgid, essential for growth, stomatal function, mechanical support. (4) Nutrient distribution: Delivers minerals to all parts (though xylem flow distributes minerals anyway). Costs of transpiration: (1) Water loss: Can exceed water uptake, causing water deficit, wilting, growth reduction. (2) Stomatal closure: To conserve water, plants close stomata, but this limits CO₂ uptake, reducing photosynthesis. (3) Cavitation risk: High tension can cause xylem cavitation (air embolism), blocking water transport. (4) Energy cost: Producing and maintaining extensive root systems to absorb water. Regulation: Plants balance photosynthesis vs water loss by controlling stomatal aperture. Guard cells respond to: (1) Light: Stomata open in light (for photosynthesis). (2) CO₂ concentration: Low CO₂ (due to photosynthesis) promotes opening. (3) Water status: Water deficit triggers abscisic acid (ABA) production, causing stomatal closure. (4) Humidity: Low humidity increases transpiration, may cause closure. (5) Temperature: High temperature may cause opening (for cooling) or closure (to conserve water). Adaptations to reduce transpiration: (1) Xerophytes (desert plants): Thick cuticle, sunken stomata, reduced leaf area (spines), CAM metabolism (stomata open at night), succulent tissues (water storage). (2) Hydrophytes (water plants): Stomata on upper surface (if floating), no cuticle. (3) Mesophytes (moderate environments): Moderate adaptations.', 'transpiration, water vapor loss, aerial plant parts, stomata, gas exchange, photosynthesis, beneficial functions, types of transpiration, stomatal transpiration, 90-95%, stomata, microscopic pores, epidermis, leaves, CO₂ uptake, water loss, cuticular transpiration, 5-10%, cuticle, waxy layer, cuticle thickness, young leaves, thin cuticle, mature leaves, lenticular transpiration, minimal, lenticels, loosely packed cells, bark, periderm, woody stems, mechanism, water evaporation, mesophyll cell walls, intercellular air spaces, water vapor diffusion, stomatal pore, concentration gradient, lower humidity, atmosphere, transpiration stream, driving forces, water potential gradient, soil, high, roots, xylem, leaves, atmosphere, very low, -100 MPa, vapor pressure deficit, saturated water vapor pressure, inside leaf, actual water vapor pressure, air, magnitude, species variation, environment, average rates, 0.5-2.5 g water/dm² leaf area/hour, maize plant, 200 L water/growing season, oak tree, 400 L/day, benefits, water uptake, mineral uptake, transpiration pull, ascent of sap, cohesion-tension theory, tall plants, cooling, evaporative cooling, leaf temperature, 10-15°C below air, protein denaturation prevention, enzyme inactivation prevention, turgor maintenance, continuous water flow, cell turgor, growth, stomatal function, mechanical support, nutrient distribution, mineral delivery, costs, water loss, water deficit, wilting, growth reduction, stomatal closure, water conservation, CO₂ uptake limitation, photosynthesis reduction, cavitation risk, high tension, xylem cavitation, air embolism, water transport block, energy cost, extensive root systems, water absorption, regulation, photosynthesis vs water loss balance, stomatal aperture, guard cells, light response, stomata open in light, CO₂ concentration response, low CO₂, photosynthesis, opening, water status response, water deficit, abscisic acid, ABA, stomatal closure, humidity response, low humidity, increased transpiration, closure, temperature response, high temperature, opening for cooling, closure for water conservation, adaptations, xerophytes, desert plants, thick cuticle, sunken stomata, reduced leaf area, spines, CAM metabolism, stomata open at night, succulent tissues, water storage, hydrophytes, water plants, stomata on upper surface, floating, no cuticle, mesophytes, moderate environments, moderate adaptations', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(78, '8. TRANSPORT SYSTEM', 'Factors Affecting Transpiration', 'Transpiration rate is influenced by multiple environmental and plant factors, reflecting the physical principles of evaporation and diffusion, as well as plant adaptations. Understanding these factors is crucial for predicting plant water needs, crop irrigation scheduling, and understanding plant distribution. Environmental factors: (1) Light intensity: Primary driver. Light causes stomatal opening (for photosynthesis). In darkness, stomata close (most plants). Higher light intensity increases stomatal aperture, transpiration. Some plants (CAM) open stomata at night, reverse pattern. (2) Temperature: Increases kinetic energy of water molecules, raising evaporation rate. For every 10°C increase, evaporation rate approximately doubles (van\'t Hoff rule). High temperature may also increase vapor pressure deficit. (3) Humidity: Relative humidity (RH) of air inversely affects transpiration. Low RH (dry air) increases water potential gradient between leaf and air, increasing transpiration. High RH (humid air) reduces gradient, decreasing transpiration. Absolute humidity also matters. (4) Wind speed: Initially increases transpiration by removing water vapor from leaf boundary layer (thin layer of still air adjacent to leaf surface), maintaining steep concentration gradient. Very high wind may cause stomatal closure (as protective response to avoid excessive water loss). (5) Atmospheric pressure: Lower pressure (high altitude) slightly increases transpiration (increased diffusion rate). (6) CO₂ concentration: Low CO₂ (during photosynthesis) promotes stomatal opening; high CO₂ (pollution, climate change) can cause partial closure, reducing transpiration. (7) Available water: Soil water deficit triggers ABA production, stomatal closure, reducing transpiration. Plant factors: (1) Leaf surface area: Greater leaf area = more transpiration. Plants adjust leaf area (deciduous drop leaves in dry seasons). (2) Stomatal density: Number of stomata per unit leaf area (typically 100-400/mm²). Higher density increases potential transpiration. (3) Stomatal distribution: Hypostomatous (stomata only on lower surface) reduces transpiration compared to amphistomatous (both surfaces). (4) Cuticle thickness: Thicker cuticle reduces cuticular transpiration. Xerophytes have very thick cuticles. (5) Leaf orientation: Vertical leaves (many grasses) receive less intense midday sun, reducing transpiration. (6) Leaf hairs (trichomes): Increase boundary layer thickness, reduce transpiration (many xerophytes). (7) Stomatal crypts: Sunken stomata in pits (many xerophytes) trap humid air, reducing transpiration. (8) Leaf rolling: Some grasses roll leaves in drought, reducing exposed surface area. (9) Rooting depth: Deeper roots access more water, allowing continued transpiration during surface drought. Interactions: Factors interact complexly. Example: High temperature + low humidity + high wind = extremely high transpiration, rapid wilting. Plants integrate multiple signals to optimize stomatal aperture. Measurement: Transpiration rate measured with porometer (measures stomatal conductance), lysimeter (weighs plant water loss), or gas exchange systems. Stomatal conductance (g, mol/m²/s) often reported. Crop water use: Understanding factors enables irrigation scheduling (replacing water lost). Transpiration ratio: Amount water transpired per unit dry matter produced (typically 200-1000:1). C4 plants (maize, sugarcane) have lower ratios (more water-efficient) than C3 plants (wheat, rice).', 'transpiration factors, environmental factors, plant factors, evaporation, diffusion, plant adaptations, crop irrigation, plant distribution, light intensity, primary driver, stomatal opening, photosynthesis, darkness, stomatal closure, CAM plants, stomatal aperture, temperature, kinetic energy, water molecules, evaporation rate, van\'t Hoff rule, 10°C increase, evaporation doubles, vapor pressure deficit, humidity, relative humidity, RH, inverse relationship, low RH, dry air, water potential gradient, leaf to air, high RH, humid air, gradient reduction, wind speed, leaf boundary layer, still air, concentration gradient, stomatal closure, protective response, excessive water loss, atmospheric pressure, high altitude, increased diffusion, CO₂ concentration, low CO₂, stomatal opening, high CO₂, pollution, climate change, partial closure, available water, soil water deficit, ABA, abscisic acid, stomatal closure, plant factors, leaf surface area, greater leaf area, more transpiration, deciduous, leaf drop, dry seasons, stomatal density, stomata per unit area, 100-400/mm², higher density, potential transpiration, stomatal distribution, hypostomatous, stomata lower surface, transpiration reduction, amphistomatous, both surfaces, cuticle thickness, cuticular transpiration reduction, xerophytes, thick cuticles, leaf orientation, vertical leaves, grasses, midday sun, transpiration reduction, leaf hairs, trichomes, boundary layer thickness, transpiration reduction, stomatal crypts, sunken stomata, pits, xerophytes, trapped humid air, leaf rolling, grasses, drought, surface area reduction, rooting depth, deeper roots, water access, continued transpiration, surface drought, interactions, complex interactions, high temperature, low humidity, high wind, extremely high transpiration, rapid wilting, signal integration, stomatal aperture optimization, measurement, porometer, stomatal conductance, lysimeter, plant weight loss, gas exchange systems, stomatal conductance, g, mol/m²/s, crop water use, irrigation scheduling, water replacement, transpiration ratio, water transpired per unit dry matter, 200-1000:1, C4 plants, maize, sugarcane, lower ratios, water-efficient, C3 plants, wheat, rice', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(79, '8. TRANSPORT SYSTEM', 'Measuring Transpiration Rate', 'Transpiration rate can be measured directly or indirectly using several methods, each with advantages and limitations. These measurements are essential for understanding plant water relations, crop water requirements, and ecosystem water balance. (1) Potometer (transpirometer): Measures water uptake by a cut shoot as a proxy for transpiration loss. Types: (a) Bubble potometer: Simple glass tube with air bubble. As plant transpires, water column advances; distance bubble moves per time gives rate. (b) Weight potometer: Measures weight loss of entire apparatus. (c) Ganong\'s potometer: Reservoir with graduated tube. Advantages: Simple, inexpensive, classroom use. Limitations: Measures uptake, not transpiration directly; includes water for growth; cut shoot not whole plant; wound responses may affect. (2) Weight loss method (gravimetric): Weigh a potted plant (with soil surface sealed to prevent evaporation) at intervals. Weight decrease due to transpirational water loss. Can measure whole plants over days. Expressed as g H₂O/plant/day or g H₂O/m² leaf area/hour. Advantages: Measures whole plant, realistic conditions. Limitations: Requires sealed pot (gas exchange affected), measures total loss (transpiration + minimal cuticular), cannot distinguish short-term changes. (3) Lysimeter: Large container (buried in field) with soil and plant, weighed continuously (load cells). Measures evapotranspiration (ET) - water loss from soil evaporation + plant transpiration. Essential for irrigation scheduling, hydrological studies. Expressed as mm/day. (4) Gas exchange systems (IRGA - Infrared Gas Analyzer): Measures water vapor flux from leaves in cuvette. Calculates transpiration rate from humidity difference between incoming and outgoing air, flow rate, leaf area. Also measures photosynthesis, stomatal conductance simultaneously. Advantages: Real-time, precise, simultaneous measurements, leaf-level analysis. Limitations: Expensive, requires expertise, encloses leaf (microclimate altered). (5) Porometer: Measures stomatal conductance (inverse of resistance) by humidity sensor. Portable, quick field measurements. Calculates transpiration if leaf temperature, air humidity known. (6) Heat balance methods: (a) Heat pulse velocity: Measures sap flow in stems (heat tracer, temperature sensors). Calculates transpiration from sap flow (assuming steady state). (b) Stem heat balance: Heater around stem, measures heat dissipated by sap flow. Advantages: Whole plant, continuous, field use. Limitations: Calibration, assumes steady flow. (7) Stable isotopes: Deuterium or oxygen-18 enrichment in water can trace water sources, transpiration rates in ecosystems. (8) Remote sensing: Satellite data (thermal infrared) estimates evapotranspiration over large areas (important for climate, agriculture). Units: Transpiration rate typically expressed as: mmol H₂O/m²/s (leaf level), g H₂O/dm²/hour (classical), mm/day (ecosystem, crop water use). Typical rates: C3 plants: 1-10 mmol/m²/s; C4 plants: lower; CAM plants: very low at night, near zero day. Factors affecting measurements: Time of day (diurnal variation), leaf age, position, environmental conditions. Standardization: Usually measure at midday, well-watered, full sun for comparison. Importance: Essential for: (1) Crop irrigation scheduling. (2) Understanding drought tolerance. (3) Modeling ecosystem water balance. (4) Climate change impacts on water resources. (5) Selecting water-efficient crop varieties.', 'transpiration measurement, potometer, transpirometer, water uptake measurement, cut shoot, transpiration proxy, bubble potometer, simple glass tube, air bubble, water column advance, distance per time, rate calculation, weight potometer, weight loss, Ganong\'s potometer, reservoir, graduated tube, advantages, simple, inexpensive, classroom use, limitations, uptake measurement not direct, water for growth, cut shoot, not whole plant, wound responses, weight loss method, gravimetric, potted plant, soil surface sealed, evaporation prevention, weight decrease, transpirational water loss, whole plants, days, g H₂O/plant/day, g H₂O/m² leaf area/hour, realistic conditions, sealed pot, gas exchange affected, total loss, cuticular, short-term changes not distinguished, lysimeter, large container, buried in field, soil, plant, continuous weighing, load cells, evapotranspiration, ET, soil evaporation, plant transpiration, irrigation scheduling, hydrological studies, mm/day, gas exchange systems, IRGA, infrared gas analyzer, water vapor flux, leaves, cuvette, humidity difference, incoming air, outgoing air, flow rate, leaf area, photosynthesis, stomatal conductance, simultaneous measurements, real-time, precise, leaf-level analysis, expensive, expertise required, microclimate altered, porometer, stomatal conductance, inverse of resistance, humidity sensor, portable, quick field measurements, leaf temperature, air humidity, heat balance methods, heat pulse velocity, sap flow, stems, heat tracer, temperature sensors, steady state assumption, stem heat balance, heater around stem, heat dissipated, sap flow, whole plant, continuous, field use, calibration, steady flow assumption, stable isotopes, deuterium, oxygen-18, water sources tracing, transpiration rates, ecosystems, remote sensing, satellite data, thermal infrared, evapotranspiration estimates, large areas, climate, agriculture, units, mmol H₂O/m²/s, leaf level, g H₂O/dm²/hour, classical, mm/day, ecosystem, crop water use, typical rates, C3 plants, 1-10 mmol/m²/s, C4 plants, lower, CAM plants, very low night, near zero day, measurement factors, time of day, diurnal variation, leaf age, leaf position, environmental conditions, standardization, midday, well-watered, full sun, comparison, importance, crop irrigation scheduling, drought tolerance understanding, ecosystem water balance modeling, climate change impacts, water resources, water-efficient crop varieties selection', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(80, '9. RESPIRATORY SYSTEM', 'Characteristics of Respiratory Surfaces', 'Efficient gas exchange requires respiratory surfaces to possess certain characteristics. They must have a large surface area to allow for sufficient gas exchange to meet metabolic demands. The surface must be very thin (typically one cell thick) to minimize the diffusion distance for gases. It must be moist to allow gases to dissolve before diffusing across. The surface must be in contact with the respiratory medium (air or water) and have a mechanism for ventilation to maintain a steep concentration gradient. Finally, it is often richly supplied with blood vessels (capillaries) to transport gases to and from the body cells.', 'respiratory surfaces, characteristics, large surface area, thin, one cell thick, moist, ventilation, concentration gradient, rich blood supply, capillaries, efficient gas exchange', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(81, '9. RESPIRATORY SYSTEM', 'Respiratory Organs in Insects', 'Insects have a tracheal system for gas exchange. This consists of a network of air-filled tubes called tracheae that branch throughout the body. Air enters the system through openings on the body surface called spiracles. The tracheae branch into finer tubes called tracheoles, which extend to individual cells. Gases are exchanged directly between the tracheoles and the cells by diffusion, without the involvement of a circulatory system. Ventilation may be passive or aided by body movements.', 'insect respiration, tracheal system, tracheae, spiracles, tracheoles, direct diffusion, air-filled tubes, ventilation, body movements, no blood involvement', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(82, '9. RESPIRATORY SYSTEM', 'Respiratory Organs in Fish (Tilapia)', 'Fish like Tilapia use gills for aquatic respiration. They have four pairs of gills located on either side of the head, each protected by a bony operculum. Each gill is composed of numerous filaments, which are further subdivided into lamellae. This branching creates an enormous surface area for gas exchange. Water enters the mouth, is pumped over the gills, and exits through the operculum. Blood flows through the capillaries in the lamellae in the opposite direction to the flow of water (counter-current flow). This maintains a steep concentration gradient for O₂ and CO₂ along the entire length of the lamellae, maximizing the efficiency of oxygen uptake, often extracting over 80% of the dissolved oxygen.', 'fish respiration, gills, operculum, filaments, lamellae, large surface area, water flow, counter-current flow, concentration gradient, efficient oxygen uptake, Tilapia', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(83, '9. RESPIRATORY SYSTEM', 'Respiratory Organs in Mammals (Rat)', 'Mammals like rats have lungs for terrestrial respiration. Air enters through the nostrils, passes through the pharynx, larynx, and trachea. The trachea divides into two bronchi, one entering each lung. Inside the lungs, the bronchi branch repeatedly into smaller bronchioles. Bronchioles terminate in clusters of tiny, thin-walled air sacs called alveoli. Alveoli are the primary sites of gas exchange, providing an enormous surface area (about 70-100 m² in humans) and a very short diffusion distance. They are surrounded by dense networks of capillaries. Ventilation is achieved by the action of the diaphragm and intercostal muscles, which change the volume and pressure of the thoracic cavity.', 'mammal respiration, lungs, nostrils, pharynx, larynx, trachea, bronchi, bronchioles, alveoli, gas exchange, large surface area, short diffusion distance, capillaries, ventilation, diaphragm, intercostal muscles, thoracic cavity', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(84, '9. RESPIRATORY SYSTEM', 'Respiratory Movements', 'Breathing (pulmonary ventilation) involves two phases. Inspiration (inhalation) is an active process: the diaphragm contracts and flattens, and the external intercostal muscles contract, pulling the rib cage up and out. This increases the volume of the thoracic cavity, decreasing the pressure inside (below atmospheric pressure), and air rushes into the lungs. Expiration (exhalation) is normally a passive process: the diaphragm and external intercostal muscles relax. The elastic recoil of the lungs and chest wall decreases thoracic volume, increasing pressure, and air is pushed out. Forced expiration involves contraction of internal intercostal and abdominal muscles.', 'respiratory movements, breathing, inspiration, inhalation, active process, diaphragm contracts, flattens, external intercostal muscles contract, rib cage up and out, thoracic volume increases, pressure decreases, air in, expiration, exhalation, passive process, relaxation, elastic recoil, thoracic volume decreases, pressure increases, air out, forced expiration', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(85, '9. RESPIRATORY SYSTEM', 'Mechanisms of Stomatal Opening and Closing', 'Stomata are microscopic pores in the leaf epidermis, each surrounded by two guard cells. Their opening and closing regulate gas exchange (CO₂ in, O₂ out) and water loss (transpiration). The mechanism is driven by changes in turgor pressure in the guard cells. When light is present, guard cells actively take up K⁺ ions from surrounding epidermal cells. This decreases their water potential, causing water to enter by osmosis. The guard cells become turgid, and their unevenly thickened walls (thicker on the inside) cause them to bow outward, opening the stoma. In darkness or under water stress, K⁺ ions are pumped out, water leaves by osmosis, the guard cells become flaccid, and the stoma closes. This process is also influenced by CO₂ concentration and abscisic acid (ABA) during drought.', 'stomata, guard cells, opening mechanism, closing mechanism, turgor pressure, light, K⁺ ions, active uptake, water potential, osmosis, turgid, unevenly thickened walls, stoma opens, flaccid, stoma closes, CO₂ concentration, abscisic acid, ABA, drought response', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(86, '10. EXCRETORY SYSTEMS', 'Excretory Organs in Mammals', 'Mammals possess a complex excretory system to maintain homeostasis. The kidneys are the primary excretory organs, filtering blood to remove metabolic wastes (mainly urea), excess salts, and water, producing urine. Ureters are muscular tubes that propel urine from each kidney to the urinary bladder via peristalsis. The urinary bladder is a distensible, muscular sac that stores urine temporarily. The urethra is a tube that carries urine from the bladder to the exterior during urination (micturition). The skin also plays an excretory role through sweat glands, secreting sweat containing water, salts, and a small amount of urea. The lungs excrete CO₂ and water vapor.', 'mammal excretory organs, kidneys, blood filtration, urine, urea, excess salts, ureters, peristalsis, urinary bladder, urine storage, urethra, micturition, skin, sweat glands, sweat, water, salts, lungs, CO₂, water vapor', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(87, '10. EXCRETORY SYSTEMS', 'Excretion in Plants', 'Plants lack specialized excretory organs. Most metabolic wastes are recycled or stored in non-toxic forms. Gaseous wastes from photosynthesis (O₂) and respiration (CO₂) are simply diffused out through stomata and lenticels. Water can be lost through transpiration or guttation. Solid and liquid wastes, often secondary metabolites, are stored in cell vacuoles (e.g., alkaloids like caffeine, tannins), in the cell wall (e.g., gums, resins, crystals of calcium oxalate), or in parts that will be shed, such as old leaves, bark, or fallen fruits. Latex, produced by some plants (e.g., rubber tree, pawpaw), can contain and excrete various wastes and defensive compounds.', 'plant excretion, no excretory organs, O₂, CO₂, diffusion, stomata, lenticels, water loss, transpiration, guttation, secondary metabolites, storage, vacuoles, alkaloids, caffeine, tannins, cell wall, gums, resins, calcium oxalate crystals, shedding leaves, bark, latex', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(88, '10. EXCRETORY SYSTEMS', 'Excretory Products of Plants', 'The range of excretory products in plants is diverse. Primary metabolic wastes include water, CO₂, and O₂. Secondary metabolites, often considered excretory, include a vast array of compounds: alkaloids (caffeine, nicotine, morphine), which often have defensive roles; tannins, which deter herbivores; gums and resins, which seal wounds; latex, which contains various compounds; organic acids; and essential oils (menthol, eucalyptus oil), which can deter herbivores and pathogens.', 'plant excretory products, water, CO₂, O₂, secondary metabolites, alkaloids, caffeine, nicotine, morphine, defense, tannins, herbivore deterrent, gums, resins, wound sealing, latex, organic acids, essential oils, menthol, eucalyptus oil', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(89, '10. EXCRETORY SYSTEMS', 'Kidney Function', 'The functional unit of the kidney is the nephron. Kidney function involves three main processes. (1) Filtration: Blood pressure forces small molecules (water, urea, glucose, amino acids, salts) from the glomerulus (a knot of capillaries) into Bowman\'s capsule, forming the glomerular filtrate. Blood cells and large proteins are retained. (2) Reabsorption: As the filtrate flows through the renal tubule (proximal convoluted tubule, loop of Henle, distal convoluted tubule), useful substances like glucose, amino acids, and most water and salts are selectively reabsorbed back into the blood capillaries. (3) Secretion: Some additional waste products (e.g., certain drugs, excess K⁺, H⁺ ions) are actively secreted from the blood into the tubule to be eliminated in urine.', 'kidney function, nephron, filtration, glomerulus, Bowman\'s capsule, blood pressure, glomerular filtrate, water, urea, glucose, amino acids, salts, reabsorption, renal tubule, proximal convoluted tubule, loop of Henle, distal convoluted tubule, useful substances, blood capillaries, secretion, waste products, drugs, K⁺, H⁺ ions, urine', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(90, '10. EXCRETORY SYSTEMS', 'Conditions Affecting Kidney Function', 'The rate of urine production and composition is regulated to maintain homeostasis. High water content in the blood (over-hydration) decreases secretion of Anti-Diuretic Hormone (ADH), resulting in more dilute urine. Dehydration or high salt intake increases ADH secretion, leading to more concentrated urine. Aldosterone regulates sodium reabsorption, affecting blood volume and pressure. Diseases like diabetes mellitus (high blood glucose) can overwhelm the kidney\'s reabsorption capacity, leading to glucose in the urine (glucosuria). Hypertension (high blood pressure) can damage the delicate glomeruli.', 'kidney function factors, homeostasis, water balance, ADH, anti-diuretic hormone, over-hydration, dilute urine, dehydration, concentrated urine, aldosterone, sodium reabsorption, blood pressure, diabetes mellitus, glucose in urine, glucosuria, hypertension, glomerular damage', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(91, '10. EXCRETORY SYSTEMS', 'Excretory Products', 'The main nitrogenous excretory products in animals are ammonia, urea, and uric acid. Ammonia (NH₃) is highly toxic and very soluble, requiring large amounts of water for excretion (common in bony fish). Urea is produced in the liver by the ornithine cycle from ammonia and CO₂. It is less toxic than ammonia and requires less water for excretion, making it the main excretory product of mammals. Uric acid is a white paste or powder, insoluble in water, and requires very little water for excretion, an adaptation for water conservation in birds, reptiles, and insects. Other excretory products include creatinine (from muscle metabolism), bilirubin (from hemoglobin breakdown), and various salts.', 'excretory products, nitrogenous waste, ammonia, NH₃, toxic, soluble, water needed, bony fish, urea, ornithine cycle, liver, less toxic, mammals, uric acid, insoluble, water conservation, birds, reptiles, insects, creatinine, bilirubin, salts', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(92, '11. LIVER', 'Functions of the Liver', 'The liver is the largest internal organ and a metabolic powerhouse, performing over 500 vital functions. Its key roles include: (1) Carbohydrate metabolism: It regulates blood glucose levels by storing glucose as glycogen (glycogenesis) and breaking down glycogen (glycogenolysis) or synthesizing glucose from non-carbohydrates (gluconeogenesis). (2) Protein metabolism: It performs deamination (removal of the amino group from amino acids) and converts the resulting toxic ammonia into urea (urea cycle). It also synthesizes most plasma proteins (e.g., albumins, clotting factors). (3) Lipid metabolism: It oxidizes fatty acids for energy, synthesizes cholesterol and lipoproteins, and converts excess carbohydrates and proteins into fats for storage. (4) Detoxification: It chemically alters (detoxifies) drugs, alcohol, and other poisons, making them less harmful and easier to excrete. (5) Storage: It stores vitamins (A, D, B₁₂, K) and iron. (6) Bile production: It produces bile, an alkaline fluid containing bile salts (for fat emulsification) and bile pigments (bilirubin, a waste product).', 'liver functions, carbohydrate metabolism, glucose regulation, glycogen, glycogenesis, glycogenolysis, gluconeogenesis, protein metabolism, deamination, ammonia, urea, urea cycle, plasma proteins, albumins, clotting factors, lipid metabolism, fatty acid oxidation, cholesterol, lipoproteins, detoxification, drugs, alcohol, storage, vitamins, A, D, B₁₂, K, iron, bile production, bile salts, bilirubin, fat emulsification', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(93, '11. LIVER', 'Position of Liver', 'Located in the upper right abdominal cavity, beneath the diaphragm. It is related to the gall bladder (which stores bile), the bile duct (which carries bile to the duodenum), the pancreas, the stomach, and the duodenum. Its strategic position allows it to process nutrients absorbed from the digestive tract before they enter the general circulation via the hepatic portal vein.', 'liver position, upper right abdominal cavity, diaphragm, gall bladder, bile storage, bile duct, bile transport, duodenum, pancreas, stomach, hepatic portal vein, nutrient processing', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(94, '12. SKIN', 'Structure of Mammalian Skin', 'The skin is the largest organ, consisting of three layers. The epidermis is the outer, stratified squamous epithelium. Its deepest layer, the stratum germinativum, contains dividing cells and melanocytes (producing melanin for UV protection). Cells are pushed upward, filling with keratin and dying, forming the tough, waterproof stratum corneum. The dermis is the inner layer of connective tissue containing blood vessels, nerves, hair follicles, sweat glands (thermoregulation, excretion), and sebaceous glands (oily sebum for waterproofing and lubrication). The hypodermis (subcutaneous layer) contains fat for insulation and energy storage.', 'skin structure, epidermis, dermis, hypodermis, stratified squamous epithelium, stratum germinativum, melanocytes, melanin, UV protection, stratum corneum, keratin, waterproof, connective tissue, blood vessels, nerves, hair follicles, sweat glands, thermoregulation, excretion, sebaceous glands, sebum, hypodermis, subcutaneous, fat, insulation', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(95, '12. SKIN', 'Functions of Skin', 'Protection: physical barrier against pathogens, chemicals, and physical injury; melanin protects against UV radiation. Sensation: contains receptors for touch, pressure, pain, and temperature. Temperature regulation: sweating (evaporative cooling) and vasodilation/vasoconstriction of dermal blood vessels control heat loss. Excretion: sweat removes small amounts of water, salts, and urea. Vitamin D synthesis: UV light converts a cholesterol derivative to vitamin D in the skin.', 'skin functions, protection, physical barrier, melanin, UV protection, sensation, touch receptors, pressure receptors, pain receptors, temperature receptors, thermoregulation, sweating, evaporative cooling, vasodilation, vasoconstriction, heat loss, excretion, sweat, water, salts, urea, vitamin D synthesis, UV light, cholesterol', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(96, '13. HORMONAL COORDINATION', 'Endocrine Glands and Hormones', 'Endocrine glands are ductless glands secreting hormones directly into the blood. Pituitary: \"master gland\" producing growth hormone (GH), TSH, ACTH, FSH, LH, ADH, oxytocin. Thyroid: thyroxine (regulates metabolic rate). Parathyroid: parathyroid hormone (increases blood Ca²⁺). Adrenal: adrenaline (fight-or-flight response), cortisol (stress, metabolism). Pancreas: insulin (lowers blood glucose), glucagon (raises blood glucose). Gonads: testes (testosterone), ovaries (estrogen, progesterone).', 'endocrine glands, hormones, pituitary, master gland, growth hormone, GH, TSH, ACTH, FSH, LH, ADH, oxytocin, thyroid, thyroxine, metabolism, parathyroid, parathyroid hormone, Ca²⁺ balance, adrenal, adrenaline, stress, cortisol, pancreas, insulin, blood glucose, glucagon, gonads, testes, testosterone, ovaries, estrogen, progesterone', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(97, '13. HORMONAL COORDINATION', 'Effects of Hormone Imbalance', 'Over-secretion: GH excess in childhood causes gigantism; in adulthood causes acromegaly. Thyroid over-secretion (hyperthyroidism) causes weight loss, rapid pulse, exophthalmos. Under-secretion: GH deficiency causes dwarfism. Thyroid under-secretion (hypothyroidism) in infancy causes cretinism (mental retardation, stunted growth); in adulthood causes myxedema. Insulin deficiency causes diabetes mellitus (high blood glucose).', 'hormone imbalance, over-secretion, gigantism, acromegaly, GH, hyperthyroidism, weight loss, rapid pulse, exophthalmos, under-secretion, dwarfism, hypothyroidism, cretinism, myxedema, diabetes mellitus, insulin deficiency, blood glucose', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(98, '13. HORMONAL COORDINATION', 'Metamorphosis in Toad', 'Thyroxine controls metamorphosis from tadpole to adult frog. Tadpole hatches from egg, possessing gills, tail, and no legs. Under thyroxine influence, metamorphosis occurs: legs develop, tail resorbed, lungs develop, gills disappear, and digestive system adapts from herbivorous to carnivorous diet. This process demonstrates hormonal control of development.', 'metamorphosis, toad, frog, thyroxine, tadpole, adult, egg, gills, tail, legs develop, tail resorbed, lungs develop, herbivorous, carnivorous, hormonal control, development', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(99, '13. HORMONAL COORDINATION', 'Plant Hormones (Auxins)', 'Auxins (e.g., IAA) are produced in shoot tips. Effects: cell elongation; apical dominance (suppressing lateral bud growth); phototropism and gravitropism (asymmetric distribution); root initiation; fruit development; leaf abscission (fall). Applications: rooting powder (synthetic auxins stimulate root growth on cuttings); selective weed killers (2,4-D) causing dicot death; fruit ripening and prevention of fruit drop.', 'plant hormones, auxins, IAA, shoot tips, cell elongation, apical dominance, lateral buds, phototropism, gravitropism, root initiation, fruit development, leaf fall, abscission, rooting powder, weed killers, 2,4-D, fruit ripening, applications', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(100, '14. NERVOUS COORDINATION', 'Components of Central Nervous System', 'Central nervous system (CNS) consists of brain and spinal cord. Brain: located in cranium, controls voluntary and involuntary actions, consciousness, thought, memory, and coordination. Spinal cord: extends from brain stem down vertebral column, conducts impulses between brain and peripheral nerves, and controls reflex actions independently of the brain.', 'central nervous system, CNS, brain, cranium, voluntary actions, involuntary actions, consciousness, thought, memory, coordination, spinal cord, vertebral column, impulse conduction, reflexes, sensory input, motor output', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(101, '14. NERVOUS COORDINATION', 'Parts of the Brain and Functions', 'Cerebrum: largest part, divided into hemispheres; responsible for conscious thought, memory, intelligence, voluntary movement, speech, and sensory interpretation. Cerebellum: coordinates movement, balance, and posture. Medulla oblongata: controls vital reflexes (heartbeat, breathing, blood pressure, vomiting, swallowing). Hypothalamus: maintains homeostasis (temperature, hunger, thirst, sleep), links nervous and endocrine systems (controls pituitary).', 'brain parts, cerebrum, cerebral hemispheres, conscious thought, memory, intelligence, voluntary movement, speech, sensory interpretation, cerebellum, coordination, balance, posture, medulla oblongata, vital reflexes, heartbeat, breathing, blood pressure, vomiting, swallowing, hypothalamus, homeostasis, temperature, hunger, thirst, sleep, pituitary control, neuroendocrine', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(102, '14. NERVOUS COORDINATION', 'Structure of Spinal Cord', 'Central H-shaped gray matter (contains neuron cell bodies, dendrites, unmyelinated axons) surrounded by white matter (myelinated axon tracts). Dorsal root: carries sensory neurons (afferent) into spinal cord. Ventral root: carries motor neurons (efferent) out of spinal cord. Central canal: contains cerebrospinal fluid, continuous with brain ventricles.', 'spinal cord structure, gray matter, cell bodies, dendrites, unmyelinated axons, white matter, myelinated axons, axon tracts, dorsal root, sensory neurons, afferent, ventral root, motor neurons, efferent, central canal, cerebrospinal fluid', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(103, '14. NERVOUS COORDINATION', 'Peripheral Nervous System', 'Peripheral nervous system (PNS) connects CNS to limbs and organs. Somatic nervous system: voluntary control of skeletal muscles. Autonomic nervous system: involuntary control of internal organs. Sympathetic division: \"fight or flight\" (increases heart rate, dilates pupils, inhibits digestion). Parasympathetic division: \"rest and digest\" (slows heart, stimulates digestion).', 'peripheral nervous system, PNS, somatic nervous system, voluntary control, skeletal muscles, autonomic nervous system, involuntary control, internal organs, sympathetic, fight or flight, heart rate increase, pupil dilation, digestion inhibition, parasympathetic, rest and digest, heart rate slowing, digestion stimulation', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(104, '14. NERVOUS COORDINATION', 'Structure of a Neurone', 'Cell body (soma): contains nucleus and organelles. Dendrites: branched projections receiving impulses from other neurons or receptors. Axon: single long fiber conducting impulses away from cell body. Myelin sheath: lipid-rich insulating layer (Schwann cells in PNS) speeding impulse conduction (saltatory conduction). Nodes of Ranvier: gaps between Schwann cells where impulses jump. Synaptic terminals: ends of axon releasing neurotransmitters.', 'neurone structure, cell body, soma, nucleus, dendrites, receive impulses, axon, conduct impulses, myelin sheath, Schwann cells, insulation, saltatory conduction, nodes of Ranvier, synaptic terminals, neurotransmitters, synapse', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(105, '14. NERVOUS COORDINATION', 'Classification of Neurones', 'Sensory neurons (afferent): carry impulses from receptors (sense organs, skin) to CNS. Motor neurons (efferent): carry impulses from CNS to effectors (muscles, glands). Interneurons (relay neurons): connect sensory and motor neurons within CNS; involved in reflexes and higher functions.', 'neurone classification, sensory neurons, afferent, receptors, CNS, motor neurons, efferent, effectors, muscles, glands, interneurons, relay neurons, association neurons, connect, reflexes', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(106, '14. NERVOUS COORDINATION', 'Reflex Actions', 'Reflex actions: automatic, rapid, involuntary responses to stimuli, protecting the body. Examples: blinking (eye protection), knee jerk (stretch reflex), withdrawal from hot object (pain avoidance). Reflex arc: receptor → sensory neuron → interneuron (often in spinal cord) → motor neuron → effector (muscle contracts or gland secretes). This bypasses the brain for speed.', 'reflex actions, automatic, rapid, involuntary, blinking, knee jerk, withdrawal, hot object, reflex arc, receptor, sensory neuron, interneuron, spinal cord, motor neuron, effector, muscle contraction, gland secretion, protective, speed', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(107, '15. SENSE ORGANS', 'Structure of the Eye', 'Outer layer: sclera (tough, white, protective) and cornea (transparent, refracts light). Middle layer: choroid (blood vessels, nourishes eye); iris (colored, contains muscles controlling pupil size); ciliary body (contains muscles for lens focusing). Inner layer: retina (contains photoreceptors: rods for dim light, cones for color and detail). Lens: transparent, biconvex, focuses light onto retina. Vitreous humor: jelly-like fluid maintaining eye shape. Optic nerve: carries nerve impulses from retina to brain. Blind spot: area where optic nerve exits, no photoreceptors.', 'eye structure, sclera, cornea, choroid, blood vessels, iris, pupil, ciliary body, focusing, retina, rods, dim light, cones, color vision, lens, light focusing, vitreous humor, shape maintenance, optic nerve, impulses, brain, blind spot, photoreceptors', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(108, '15. SENSE ORGANS', 'Function of the Eye', 'Light enters through cornea, passes through pupil (size regulated by iris). Lens focuses light onto retina, producing an inverted, real image. Rods and cones convert light energy into nerve impulses (phototransduction). Impulse transmitted via optic nerve to brain\'s visual cortex. Brain interprets image, correcting inversion and processing color, form, and motion.', 'eye function, light entry, cornea, pupil, iris regulation, lens focusing, retina, inverted image, real image, rods, cones, phototransduction, nerve impulses, optic nerve, transmission, brain, visual cortex, interpretation, color processing, form perception, motion detection', '2026-02-27 15:40:30');
INSERT INTO `biology_syllabus_comprehensive` (`id`, `topic`, `sub_topic`, `note`, `keywords`, `created_at`) VALUES
(109, '15. SENSE ORGANS', 'Eye Defects and Corrections', 'Myopia (nearsightedness): eyeball too long or lens too strong; image focuses in front of retina; corrected with concave (diverging) lenses. Hypermetropia (farsightedness): eyeball too short or lens too weak; image focuses behind retina; corrected with convex (converging) lenses. Astigmatism: irregular cornea curvature; corrected with cylindrical lenses. Presbyopia: age-related loss of lens elasticity; corrected with bifocals. Cataracts: lens opacity; corrected surgically (lens replacement). Glaucoma: increased intraocular pressure damaging optic nerve; treated with medication or surgery.', 'eye defects, myopia, nearsightedness, concave lenses, diverging lenses, hypermetropia, farsightedness, convex lenses, converging lenses, astigmatism, cylindrical lenses, irregular cornea, presbyopia, lens elasticity, bifocals, cataracts, lens opacity, surgery, lens replacement, glaucoma, intraocular pressure, optic nerve damage, medication', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(110, '15. SENSE ORGANS', 'Structure of the Ear', 'Outer ear: pinna (collects sound waves), ear canal (directs sound to eardrum). Middle ear: eardrum (tympanic membrane) vibrates; ossicles (hammer, anvil, stirrup) amplify and transmit vibrations to oval window. Eustachian tube equalizes pressure. Inner ear: cochlea (fluid-filled, contains hair cells for hearing); semicircular canals (fluid-filled, contain hair cells for balance/detecting head movement); vestibular apparatus (utricle and saccule) detect head position and linear acceleration.', 'ear structure, outer ear, pinna, ear canal, middle ear, eardrum, tympanic membrane, ossicles, hammer, malleus, anvil, incus, stirrup, stapes, amplify, oval window, Eustachian tube, pressure equalization, inner ear, cochlea, hearing, hair cells, semicircular canals, balance, head movement, vestibular apparatus, utricle, saccule, head position, linear acceleration', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(111, '15. SENSE ORGANS', 'Function of the Ear', 'Hearing: sound waves → eardrum vibrates → ossicles amplify → oval window vibrates → fluid in cochlea moves → hair cells bend → generate nerve impulses → auditory nerve → brain interprets as sound. Balance: semicircular canals detect rotational head movements; utricle and saccule detect linear acceleration and head tilt (due to otoliths bending hair cells).', 'ear function, hearing, sound waves, eardrum vibration, ossicles amplify, oval window, cochlea fluid, hair cells, nerve impulses, auditory nerve, brain, balance, semicircular canals, rotational movement, utricle, saccule, linear acceleration, head tilt, otoliths', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(112, '16. REPRODUCTIVE SYSTEM OF MAMMALS', 'Male Reproductive System', 'Testes: suspended in scrotum (outside body for lower temperature); produce sperm (spermatogenesis) and testosterone. Epididymis: site of sperm maturation and storage. Vas deferens: muscular tube carrying sperm from epididymis to urethra during ejaculation. Accessory glands: seminal vesicles, prostate gland, Cowper\'s glands produce seminal fluid (provides nutrients, protects sperm, aids motility) forming semen. Urethra: carries semen and urine (at different times). Penis: deposits sperm into female reproductive tract.', 'male reproductive system, testes, scrotum, temperature regulation, sperm production, spermatogenesis, testosterone, epididymis, sperm maturation, sperm storage, vas deferens, sperm transport, ejaculation, seminal vesicles, prostate gland, Cowper\'s glands, seminal fluid, semen, nutrients, sperm protection, motility, urethra, semen transport, urine transport, penis, sperm deposition', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(113, '16. REPRODUCTIVE SYSTEM OF MAMMALS', 'Female Reproductive System', 'Ovaries: produce eggs (ova, oogenesis) and hormones (estrogen, progesterone). Fallopian tubes (oviducts): capture released egg; site of fertilization. Uterus: muscular organ where embryo implants and develops; lining (endometrium) thickens cyclically. Cervix: narrow opening between uterus and vagina. Vagina: receives penis during intercourse; serves as birth canal; outlet for menstrual flow.', 'female reproductive system, ovaries, egg production, ova, oogenesis, estrogen, progesterone, fallopian tubes, oviducts, egg capture, fertilization site, uterus, embryo implantation, embryo development, endometrium, menstrual cycle, cervix, uterus opening, vagina, receives penis, birth canal, menstrual flow', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(114, '16. REPRODUCTIVE SYSTEM OF MAMMALS', 'Structure of Gametes', 'Sperm: highly specialized for motility and fertilization. Head: contains nucleus (haploid genetic material) and acrosome (cap with enzymes to penetrate egg). Midpiece: packed with mitochondria providing ATP for tail movement. Tail (flagellum): propels sperm. Ovum (egg): large, non-motile cell. Contains nucleus (haploid), abundant cytoplasm with yolk (nutrients for early embryo), surrounded by protective layers: zona pellucida (glycoprotein layer) and corona radiata (follicle cells).', 'gamete structure, sperm, head, nucleus, haploid, acrosome, enzymes, egg penetration, midpiece, mitochondria, ATP, energy, tail, flagellum, movement, ovum, egg, large cell, non-motile, cytoplasm, yolk, nutrients, zona pellucida, glycoprotein, corona radiata, follicle cells, protective layers', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(115, '16. REPRODUCTIVE SYSTEM OF MAMMALS', 'Fertilization and Embryo Development', 'Sperm swim to fallopian tube; acrosome releases enzymes to penetrate corona radiata and zona pellucida. Sperm and egg plasma membranes fuse; sperm nucleus enters egg cytoplasm. Cortical reaction prevents polyspermy (multiple sperm). Nuclei fuse (syngamy) forming diploid zygote. Zygote undergoes rapid cell divisions (cleavage) → morula (solid ball of cells) → blastocyst (hollow ball with inner cell mass). Blastocyst implants in uterine endometrium. Embryo develops (gastrulation, organogenesis) → fetus. Placenta forms from embryonic and maternal tissues for nutrient/gas/waste exchange.', 'fertilization, embryo development, sperm, egg, fallopian tube, acrosome, enzymes, corona radiata, zona pellucida, penetration, membrane fusion, cortical reaction, polyspermy prevention, syngamy, nuclei fusion, zygote, diploid, cleavage, cell division, morula, blastocyst, inner cell mass, implantation, uterus, endometrium, embryo, gastrulation, organogenesis, fetus, placenta, nutrient exchange, gas exchange, waste exchange', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(116, '16. REPRODUCTIVE SYSTEM OF MAMMALS', 'Birth (Parturition)', 'Near end of gestation, fetal hormones trigger maternal oxytocin release. Oxytocin stimulates uterine contractions. Positive feedback loop: contractions stimulate more oxytocin release, intensifying contractions. Cervix dilates; amniotic sac ruptures (\"water breaks\"). Strong contractions expel baby through vagina. Umbilical cord is clamped and cut. Further contractions expel placenta (afterbirth).', 'birth, parturition, gestation, fetal hormones, oxytocin, uterine contractions, positive feedback, cervix dilation, amniotic sac rupture, water breaks, baby expulsion, vagina, umbilical cord, clamping, placenta, afterbirth', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(117, '16. REPRODUCTIVE SYSTEM OF MAMMALS', 'Birth Control Methods', 'Natural methods: rhythm (abstinence during fertile period); withdrawal. Barrier methods: condoms (male, female), diaphragm, cervical cap (prevent sperm reaching egg). Hormonal methods: pills (oral contraceptives), implants, injections (prevent ovulation, thicken cervical mucus). Intrauterine devices (IUDs): prevent implantation. Surgical methods: vasectomy (male sterilization: cuts vas deferens); tubal ligation (female sterilization: ties/cuts fallopian tubes). Emergency contraception: \"morning-after pill\" (high-dose hormones post-coitus).', 'birth control, contraception, natural methods, rhythm method, fertility awareness, withdrawal, barrier methods, condoms, male condom, female condom, diaphragm, cervical cap, hormonal methods, pills, oral contraceptives, implants, injections, ovulation prevention, cervical mucus thickening, intrauterine devices, IUDs, implantation prevention, surgical methods, vasectomy, male sterilization, tubal ligation, female sterilization, emergency contraception, morning-after pill', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(118, '17. METAMORPHOSIS IN INSECTS', 'Complete Metamorphosis', 'Complete metamorphosis (holometabolism) involves four distinct stages: egg → larva → pupa → adult. Larva (e.g., caterpillar, grub, maggot) differs radically from adult in form, habitat, and diet (e.g., caterpillar feeds on leaves; butterfly feeds on nectar). Pupal stage is a non-feeding, often immobile stage where larval tissues are broken down and adult structures form (histolysis and histogenesis). Examples: butterfly, housefly, beetle, mosquito, bee.', 'complete metamorphosis, holometabolism, egg, larva, caterpillar, grub, maggot, pupa, chrysalis, cocoon, adult, butterfly, housefly, beetle, mosquito, bee, different forms, different habitats, different diets, histolysis, histogenesis, tissue reorganization', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(119, '17. METAMORPHOSIS IN INSECTS', 'Incomplete Metamorphosis', 'Incomplete metamorphosis (hemimetabolism) involves three stages: egg → nymph → adult. Nymph resembles a smaller version of the adult (similar form, habitat, and diet) but lacks wings and functional reproductive organs. It undergoes several molts (instars), progressively developing wing buds and external genitalia until reaching adulthood. Examples: cockroach, grasshopper, cricket, true bugs.', 'incomplete metamorphosis, hemimetabolism, egg, nymph, instar, molting, adult, cockroach, grasshopper, cricket, true bugs, similar form, similar habitat, similar diet, wing buds, gradual development', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(120, '17. METAMORPHOSIS IN INSECTS', 'Life History of Butterfly', 'Eggs laid on host plant (specific for caterpillar food). Larva (caterpillar) hatches, feeds voraciously on leaves, grows, molts several times. Pupation: caterpillar forms a chrysalis (pupa) attached to substrate. Inside chrysalis, complete reorganization occurs. Adult butterfly emerges, expands and dries wings, then flies to feed on nectar and reproduce. Complete metamorphosis.', 'butterfly life history, eggs, host plant, larva, caterpillar, feeds, grows, molts, pupation, chrysalis, pupa, metamorphosis, reorganization, adult butterfly, emerges, wings expand, nectar feeding, reproduction, complete metamorphosis', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(121, '17. METAMORPHOSIS IN INSECTS', 'Life History of Cockroach', 'Eggs laid in protective capsule (ootheca) deposited or carried by female. Nymphs hatch from ootheca, resembling small, wingless adults. They undergo a series of molts (6-12 instars over 3-12 months depending on species and conditions), gradually developing wings and reproductive organs. Final molt produces fully winged, sexually mature adult. Incomplete metamorphosis.', 'cockroach life history, eggs, ootheca, egg case, nymphs, hatch, resemble adults, wingless, molts, instars, gradual development, winged adult, sexually mature, incomplete metamorphosis', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(122, '18. REPRODUCTION IN ANIMALS (COMPARISON)', 'Reproduction in Fish', 'Most fish have external fertilization. Females lay large numbers of eggs (spawn) in water; males release sperm over them simultaneously. Eggs are often small with little yolk; development is external. Parental care is generally absent or minimal (e.g., nest guarding in some species). Examples: Tilapia, salmon, cod.', 'fish reproduction, external fertilization, eggs, spawn, water, sperm release, large numbers, small eggs, little yolk, external development, minimal parental care, nest guarding, Tilapia, salmon, cod', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(123, '18. REPRODUCTION IN ANIMALS (COMPARISON)', 'Reproduction in Amphibians', 'Typically external fertilization in water. Females lay eggs (often in jelly masses) in water; males release sperm over them. Eggs have moderate yolk; tadpoles hatch and undergo metamorphosis to adult. Large numbers of eggs produced; no parental care (except some species). Example: frog, toad, newt.', 'amphibian reproduction, external fertilization, water, eggs, jelly masses, sperm release, moderate yolk, tadpoles, hatch, metamorphosis, frog, toad, newt, large numbers, no parental care', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(124, '18. REPRODUCTION IN ANIMALS (COMPARISON)', 'Reproduction in Reptiles', 'Internal fertilization. Males have copulatory organs. Females lay amniotic eggs on land. Amniotic egg has leathery or calcareous shell and extraembryonic membranes (amnion, chorion, allantois, yolk sac) protecting embryo and providing for gas exchange and waste storage in terrestrial environment. Embryo develops within egg, nourished by yolk. Fewer eggs laid than fish/amphibians; some species exhibit parental care (e.g., guarding nest). Examples: lizard, snake, turtle, crocodile.', 'reptile reproduction, internal fertilization, copulatory organs, amniotic eggs, land, leathery shell, calcareous shell, extraembryonic membranes, amnion, chorion, allantois, yolk sac, embryo protection, gas exchange, waste storage, terrestrial adaptation, yolk nutrition, fewer eggs, some parental care, lizard, snake, turtle, crocodile', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(125, '18. REPRODUCTION IN ANIMALS (COMPARISON)', 'Reproduction in Birds', 'Internal fertilization. Females lay amniotic eggs with hard calcareous shells. Eggs are rich in yolk. Incubation (sitting on eggs) provides warmth for embryonic development. Young hatch as chicks: precocial (relatively mature, leave nest soon, e.g., chickens) or altricial (helpless, require feeding, e.g., pigeons). Few eggs laid; extensive parental care (feeding, protection).', 'bird reproduction, internal fertilization, amniotic eggs, hard calcareous shell, rich yolk, incubation, warmth, embryonic development, chicks hatch, precocial, leave nest soon, chickens, altricial, helpless, require feeding, pigeons, few eggs, extensive parental care, feeding, protection', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(126, '18. REPRODUCTION IN ANIMALS (COMPARISON)', 'Reproduction in Mammals', 'Internal fertilization. Most are placental mammals: embryo develops internally in uterus, nourished by placenta (allows longer gestation, more developed young). Live birth (viviparity). After birth, young fed milk from mammary glands (lactation). Very few offspring (usually 1- few per pregnancy); extensive parental care (feeding, protection, teaching). Examples: monotremes (egg-laying: platypus), marsupials (born very immature, develop in pouch: kangaroo), placentals (most mammals: human, dog, cat).', 'mammal reproduction, internal fertilization, placental mammals, embryo development, uterus, placenta, nourishment, gestation, live birth, viviparity, mammary glands, milk, lactation, few offspring, extensive parental care, monotremes, egg-laying, platypus, marsupials, pouch, kangaroo, placentals, human, dog, cat', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(127, '19. REPRODUCTION IN FLOWERING PLANTS', 'Structure of a Flower', 'A flower is the reproductive structure of angiosperms. Sepals: outermost whorl, usually green, protect bud. Petals: often brightly colored, attract pollinators. Stamens (male parts): anther (produces pollen grains containing male gametes) and filament (stalk supporting anther). Carpels (female parts): stigma (sticky surface receives pollen), style (connects stigma to ovary), ovary (contains ovules, each containing female gametophyte with egg cell).', 'flower structure, sepals, protect bud, petals, attract pollinators, stamens, male, anther, pollen production, pollen grains, male gametes, filament, carpels, female, stigma, pollen reception, style, ovary, ovules, female gametophyte, egg cell', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(128, '19. REPRODUCTION IN FLOWERING PLANTS', 'Insect-pollinated Flowers', 'Features adapted to attract insects and ensure pollen transfer: large, brightly colored petals; scent (attracts insects from distance); nectar (food reward, encourages visits); sticky or spiny pollen grains (adhere to insect\'s body); stigma inside flower (contacts insect\'s body). Examples: hibiscus, rose, sunflower, orchids.', 'insect-pollinated flowers, entomophilous, large petals, brightly colored, scent, fragrance, nectar, food reward, sticky pollen, spiny pollen, stigma inside, insect contact, hibiscus, rose, sunflower, orchids, adaptations', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(129, '19. REPRODUCTION IN FLOWERING PLANTS', 'Wind-pollinated Flowers', 'Features adapted for airborne pollen dispersal: small, dull, inconspicuous petals (no need to attract pollinators); no scent or nectar; large, feathery stigma (traps airborne pollen); anthers exposed and loosely attached (pollen easily released); enormous quantities of light, smooth pollen grains (carried easily by wind). Examples: grasses, maize, wheat, oats, many trees.', 'wind-pollinated flowers, anemophilous, small petals, dull petals, inconspicuous, no scent, no nectar, large feathery stigma, exposed anthers, loose anthers, enormous pollen quantities, light pollen, smooth pollen, grasses, maize, corn, wheat, oats, trees, adaptations', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(130, '19. REPRODUCTION IN FLOWERING PLANTS', 'Pollination', 'Pollination: transfer of pollen from anther to stigma. Self-pollination: pollen from same flower or same plant (occurs in some plants; ensures reproduction when pollinators absent, but reduces genetic diversity). Cross-pollination: pollen from different plant of same species (promotes genetic diversity, stronger offspring). Agents: wind, insects, birds (ornithophily), bats (chiropterophily), water (hydrophily).', 'pollination, pollen transfer, anther, stigma, self-pollination, same flower, same plant, genetic diversity reduction, cross-pollination, different plant, genetic diversity, stronger offspring, agents, wind, insects, birds, ornithophily, bats, chiropterophily, water, hydrophily', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(131, '19. REPRODUCTION IN FLOWERING PLANTS', 'Fertilization in Plants', 'After pollination, pollen grain germinates on stigma, producing pollen tube that grows down style to ovule. Tube enters ovule through micropyle. Two male gametes (sperm) travel down tube. One fuses with egg cell → forms zygote (2n, develops into embryo). Other fuses with two polar nuclei in central cell → forms triploid (3n) endosperm (nutritive tissue for developing embryo). This double fertilization is unique to angiosperms. Ovule develops into seed; ovary develops into fruit.', 'plant fertilization, pollen germination, pollen tube, style, ovule, micropyle, male gametes, sperm, egg cell, zygote, diploid, embryo, polar nuclei, central cell, endosperm, triploid, nutritive tissue, double fertilization, angiosperms, ovule, seed, ovary, fruit', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(132, '19. REPRODUCTION IN FLOWERING PLANTS', 'Placentation Types', 'Placentation: arrangement of ovules within ovary. Axile: ovules attached to central axis in partitioned ovary (hibiscus, tomato, orange). Marginal: ovules along margin of simple ovary (pea, bean). Parietal: ovules attached to ovary wall (mustard, watermelon). Free central: ovules on central column without partitions (Dianthus, primrose). Basal: single ovule at base of ovary (sunflower, marigold).', 'placentation types, axile placentation, ovules, central axis, partitions, hibiscus, tomato, orange, marginal placentation, ovary margin, pea, bean, parietal placentation, ovary wall, mustard, watermelon, free central placentation, central column, no partitions, Dianthus, primrose, basal placentation, single ovule, base, sunflower, marigold', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(133, '20. FRUITS AND DISPERSAL', 'Classification of Fruits', 'Fruits are classified based on development and structure. Simple fruits: develop from single ovary of one flower. Dry fruits: pericarp (fruit wall) dry at maturity. Dehiscent: split open to release seeds (legume, capsule). Indehiscent: do not split open (achene, caryopsis/grain, nut). Fleshy fruits: pericarp fleshy at maturity. Drupe: fleshy with stony endocarp (stone fruit: mango, coconut, peach). Berry: entire pericarp fleshy (tomato, orange, grape). Pome: fleshy tissue derived from receptacle (apple, pear). Aggregate fruits: develop from multiple ovaries of single flower (strawberry, raspberry). Multiple fruits: develop from multiple flowers fused together (pineapple, breadfruit).', 'fruit classification, simple fruits, dry fruits, dehiscent fruits, split open, legume, pod, capsule, indehiscent fruits, achene, caryopsis, grain, nut, fleshy fruits, drupe, stone fruit, endocarp, mango, coconut, peach, berry, tomato, orange, grape, pome, receptacle, apple, pear, aggregate fruits, multiple ovaries, strawberry, raspberry, multiple fruits, multiple flowers, pineapple, breadfruit', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(134, '20. FRUITS AND DISPERSAL', 'Structure of Fruits', 'Leguminous fruit (bean, pea): pod formed from single carpel, seeds attached along ventral suture; splits along both sutures when dry. Orange (hesperidium): modified berry with leathery rind containing oil glands; juicy interior divided into segments; seeds within segments. Maize (caryopsis): dry indehiscent fruit; seed coat fused to pericarp (fruit wall); single seed. Tomato (berry): fleshy fruit with soft pericarp; many seeds embedded in juicy pulp; develops from ovary.', 'fruit structure, leguminous fruit, pod, bean, pea, single carpel, seeds, ventral suture, splitting, dry fruit, orange, hesperidium, berry, leathery rind, oil glands, juicy segments, seeds, maize, caryopsis, grain, dry indehiscent, seed coat, pericarp, fused, single seed, tomato, berry, fleshy, soft pericarp, many seeds, juicy pulp, ovary', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(135, '20. FRUITS AND DISPERSAL', 'Seed Dispersal Agents', 'Wind dispersal: light seeds with wings (combretum, maple) or hairs (cotton, dandelion). Water dispersal: floating seeds with air spaces (coconut) or waterproof coats. Animal dispersal: external (hooked or sticky fruits attach to fur/feathers: bidens, desmodium, burrs); internal (fleshy fruits eaten, seeds pass through gut unharmed: birds, mammals). Explosive mechanism: pods dry and suddenly split, forcibly ejecting seeds (crotalaria, bean, okra).', 'seed dispersal, wind dispersal, light seeds, wings, combretum, maple, hairs, cotton, dandelion, pappus, water dispersal, floating seeds, air spaces, coconut, waterproof coat, animal dispersal, external, hooked fruits, sticky fruits, attach to fur, feathers, bidens, desmodium, burrs, internal, fleshy fruits, eaten, seeds pass through gut, unharmed, birds, mammals, explosive mechanism, pods dry, sudden split, forcibly eject seeds, crotalaria, bean, okra', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(136, '20. FRUITS AND DISPERSAL', 'Fruit and Seed Differences', 'Fruit develops from ovary (sometimes including other flower parts) after fertilization, contains seed(s). Function: protects seeds and aids in their dispersal. Seed develops from fertilized ovule, contains embryo (young plant) and stored food (endosperm or cotyledons), surrounded by seed coat (testa). Some structures resemble fruits but are not: strawberry (aggregate fruit from multiple ovaries); apple (pome, fleshy part from receptacle); fig (multiple fruit).', 'fruit and seed differences, fruit, ovary, develops after fertilization, contains seeds, protects seeds, aids dispersal, seed, ovule, fertilized, contains embryo, young plant, stored food, endosperm, cotyledons, seed coat, testa, aggregate fruit, strawberry, multiple ovaries, pome, apple, receptacle, multiple fruit, fig', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(137, '21. PLANT NUTRITION', 'Photosynthesis - Process', 'Photosynthesis: conversion of light energy to chemical energy (glucose) in chloroplasts. Light-dependent reactions occur in thylakoid membranes: chlorophyll absorbs light, exciting electrons; electron transport chain produces ATP (photophosphorylation) and reduces NADP⁺ to NADPH; water is split (photolysis), releasing O₂. Light-independent reactions (Calvin cycle) occur in stroma: CO₂ fixed to ribulose bisphosphate (RuBP) by enzyme rubisco; using ATP and NADPH, glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate (G3P) is produced, which is used to synthesize glucose and other carbohydrates.', 'photosynthesis process, light energy, chemical energy, glucose, chloroplasts, light-dependent reactions, thylakoid membranes, chlorophyll, light absorption, excited electrons, electron transport chain, ATP, photophosphorylation, NADPH, NADP⁺ reduction, photolysis, water splitting, oxygen release, light-independent reactions, Calvin cycle, stroma, carbon fixation, RuBP, ribulose bisphosphate, rubisco, enzyme, G3P, glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate, carbohydrate synthesis', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(138, '21. PLANT NUTRITION', 'Photosynthesis Equation', '6CO₂ + 6H₂O + light energy → C₆H₁₂O₆ + 6O₂. Carbon dioxide from air enters through stomata. Water from soil absorbed by roots. Light energy from sun captured by chlorophyll. Glucose used for energy (respiration), converted to starch for storage, or used to synthesize other organic compounds (cellulose, proteins, lipids). Oxygen released as byproduct.', 'photosynthesis equation, 6CO₂, 6H₂O, light energy, C₆H₁₂O₆, glucose, 6O₂, carbon dioxide, air, stomata, water, soil, roots, light, sun, chlorophyll, glucose uses, energy, respiration, starch storage, cellulose, proteins, lipids, oxygen byproduct', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(139, '21. PLANT NUTRITION', 'Materials for Photosynthesis', 'Carbon dioxide: enters leaf through stomata by diffusion. Water: absorbed by root hairs, transported via xylem to mesophyll cells. Light: absorbed by chlorophyll and accessory pigments (carotenoids) in chloroplasts. Chlorophyll: pigment in thylakoid membranes, essential for capturing light energy. Suitable temperature: enzymes (e.g., rubisco) function optimally within specific range (typically 25-30°C for most plants).', 'photosynthesis materials, carbon dioxide, stomata, diffusion, water, root hairs, xylem transport, mesophyll cells, light, chlorophyll, accessory pigments, carotenoids, chloroplasts, thylakoid membranes, light energy capture, suitable temperature, enzymes, rubisco, optimal temperature', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(140, '21. PLANT NUTRITION', 'Evidence of Photosynthesis', 'Starch test: leaf boiled in ethanol (removes chlorophyll), tested with iodine solution → blue-black color indicates starch presence, proving photosynthesis occurred. Oxygen production: aquatic plants (Elodea) illuminated produce bubbles; gas collected can be tested - relights glowing splint (oxygen supports combustion). CO₂ uptake: can be measured using infrared gas analyzer; plants in light remove CO₂ from surrounding air.', 'photosynthesis evidence, starch test, leaf, ethanol, chlorophyll removal, iodine test, blue-black color, starch, oxygen production, aquatic plants, Elodea, bubbles, gas collection, glowing splint test, supports combustion, CO₂ uptake, infrared gas analyzer, light, CO₂ removal', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(141, '21. PLANT NUTRITION', 'Mineral Requirements - Macro-nutrients', 'Macro-nutrients required in relatively large amounts. Carbon, hydrogen, oxygen: from air (CO₂) and water. Nitrogen: component of proteins, nucleic acids, chlorophyll. Phosphorus: component of ATP, DNA, RNA, phospholipids. Potassium: enzyme activator, osmotic regulation, stomatal function. Magnesium: central atom in chlorophyll molecule. Calcium: cell wall structure (calcium pectate), cell signaling. Sulfur: component of some amino acids (cysteine, methionine), proteins.', 'macro-nutrients, carbon, hydrogen, oxygen, nitrogen, proteins, nucleic acids, chlorophyll, phosphorus, ATP, DNA, RNA, phospholipids, potassium, enzyme activator, osmotic regulation, stomatal function, magnesium, chlorophyll central atom, calcium, cell wall, calcium pectate, cell signaling, sulfur, amino acids, cysteine, methionine, proteins', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(142, '21. PLANT NUTRITION', 'Mineral Requirements - Micro-nutrients', 'Micro-nutrients required in trace amounts. Iron: chlorophyll synthesis, electron transport chain components. Manganese: water splitting in photosystem II, enzyme activator. Zinc: enzyme activator (e.g., in auxin synthesis). Copper: component of some enzymes (e.g., in electron transport). Boron: involved in cell wall formation, membrane integrity. Molybdenum: component of enzymes in nitrogen metabolism (nitrogenase, nitrate reductase). Deficiencies cause characteristic symptoms (chlorosis, stunted growth, necrosis).', 'micro-nutrients, iron, chlorophyll synthesis, electron transport, manganese, water splitting, photosystem II, enzyme activator, zinc, auxin synthesis, copper, enzyme component, boron, cell wall formation, membrane integrity, molybdenum, nitrogen metabolism, nitrogenase, nitrate reductase, deficiency symptoms, chlorosis, stunted growth, necrosis', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(143, '22. ANIMAL NUTRITION', 'Food Substances - Classes', 'Carbohydrates: sugars (glucose, fructose) and starch; primary energy source. Proteins: amino acids; essential for growth, repair, enzyme and hormone synthesis. Lipids: fats and oils; concentrated energy source, insulation, cell membrane structure (phospholipids), hormone precursors. Vitamins: organic compounds required in small amounts for various metabolic functions (e.g., vitamin A for vision, B complex as coenzymes, C for collagen synthesis, D for calcium absorption). Minerals: inorganic elements (calcium for bones, iron for hemoglobin, iodine for thyroxine). Water: universal solvent, transport medium, temperature regulation, metabolic reactions.', 'food classes, carbohydrates, sugars, glucose, fructose, starch, energy source, proteins, amino acids, growth, repair, enzymes, hormones, lipids, fats, oils, concentrated energy, insulation, cell membrane, phospholipids, hormones precursors, vitamins, vitamin A, vision, B complex, coenzymes, vitamin C, collagen, vitamin D, calcium absorption, minerals, calcium, bones, iron, hemoglobin, iodine, thyroxine, water, solvent, transport, temperature regulation, metabolism', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(144, '22. ANIMAL NUTRITION', 'Balanced Diet', 'A balanced diet contains all nutrients in correct proportions to meet body\'s requirements. Proportions vary with age (children need more protein for growth), sex (women need more iron), activity level (athletes need more carbohydrates), health status. Malnutrition includes undernutrition (deficiency diseases: scurvy - vitamin C deficiency, rickets - vitamin D/calcium deficiency) and overnutrition (obesity, type 2 diabetes, cardiovascular disease).', 'balanced diet, nutrients, correct proportions, age, children, protein, growth, sex, women, iron, activity level, athletes, carbohydrates, health status, malnutrition, undernutrition, deficiency diseases, scurvy, vitamin C deficiency, rickets, vitamin D deficiency, calcium deficiency, overnutrition, obesity, type 2 diabetes, cardiovascular disease', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(145, '22. ANIMAL NUTRITION', 'Food Tests', 'Starch: add iodine solution → blue-black color (positive). Reducing sugar (e.g., glucose, maltose): add Benedict\'s solution, heat in water bath → color change from blue to green/yellow/brick-red precipitate (positive). Protein (biuret test): add sodium hydroxide (NaOH), then copper sulfate (CuSO₄) → violet color (positive). Fat (ethanol emulsion test): shake with ethanol, add to water → white emulsion (positive). Oil (grease spot test): place on paper → translucent spot (positive).', 'food tests, starch test, iodine solution, blue-black, reducing sugar test, Benedict\'s solution, heat, water bath, color change, green, yellow, brick-red precipitate, protein test, biuret test, sodium hydroxide, NaOH, copper sulfate, CuSO₄, violet, fat test, ethanol emulsion test, ethanol, water, white emulsion, oil test, grease spot test, translucent spot', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(146, '22. ANIMAL NUTRITION', 'Digestive Enzymes', 'Amylase (produced in salivary glands and pancreas): acts in mouth and small intestine; converts starch to maltose. Protease (pepsin in stomach, trypsin in pancreas): acts in stomach (pepsin, acidic pH) and small intestine (trypsin, alkaline pH); converts proteins to peptides to amino acids. Lipase (pancreatic): acts in small intestine; converts lipids to fatty acids and glycerol. Maltase (intestinal wall): acts in small intestine; converts maltose to glucose.', 'digestive enzymes, amylase, salivary glands, pancreas, mouth, small intestine, starch, maltose, protease, pepsin, stomach, acidic pH, trypsin, alkaline pH, proteins, peptides, amino acids, lipase, pancreatic, lipids, fatty acids, glycerol, maltase, intestinal wall, maltose, glucose', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(147, '22. ANIMAL NUTRITION', 'Characteristics of Enzymes', 'All enzymes are proteins (except some catalytic RNA). Highly specific: each enzyme catalyzes one type of reaction or acts on one specific substrate. Catalytic: speed up reactions without being consumed. Remain unchanged at end of reaction. Affected by temperature: rate increases with temperature to optimum (~37°C for human enzymes), then denatures (active site shape changes) at high temperatures. Affected by pH: each enzyme has optimal pH (pepsin pH 2, amylase pH 7, trypsin pH 8). Affected by concentration (enzyme and substrate).', 'enzyme characteristics, proteins, specific, substrate specificity, catalytic, not consumed, temperature effect, optimum temperature, 37°C, denaturation, high temperature, active site, pH effect, optimal pH, pepsin pH 2, amylase pH 7, trypsin pH 8, concentration effect, enzyme concentration, substrate concentration, reaction rate', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(148, '22. ANIMAL NUTRITION', 'Modes of Nutrition', 'Autotrophic: organisms synthesize own food (plants via photosynthesis, some bacteria via chemosynthesis). Heterotrophic: organisms obtain food from other sources. Holozoic: ingest and digest complex food (animals, protozoa like Amoeba). Parasitic: live on or in host, obtaining nutrients at host\'s expense (tapeworm, ticks). Symbiotic (mutualistic): both organisms benefit (lichens - fungus + alga; Rhizobium bacteria in root nodules). Saprophytic (saprobic): feed on dead organic matter (fungi, many bacteria).', 'modes of nutrition, autotrophic, photosynthesis, chemosynthesis, heterotrophic, holozoic, ingestion, digestion, animals, Amoeba, parasitic, host, tapeworm, ticks, symbiotic, mutualistic, both benefit, lichens, fungus, alga, Rhizobium, root nodules, nitrogen fixation, saprophytic, saprobic, dead organic matter, fungi, bacteria, decomposers', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(149, '22. ANIMAL NUTRITION', 'Alimentary System in Mammals', 'Alimentary canal (gastrointestinal tract): continuous tube from mouth to anus. Mouth: teeth (mechanical digestion), tongue, salivary glands (saliva with amylase). Pharynx and esophagus: peristalsis moves food to stomach. Stomach: gastric glands secrete HCl (kills bacteria, activates pepsin) and pepsinogen (converted to pepsin, digests proteins). Small intestine: duodenum (receives bile from liver, pancreatic juice), ileum (absorption via villi and microvilli). Large intestine (colon): water absorption, feces formation. Rectum: stores feces. Anus: eliminates feces.', 'alimentary system, mammals, mouth, teeth, mechanical digestion, tongue, salivary glands, saliva, amylase, pharynx, esophagus, peristalsis, stomach, gastric glands, HCl, hydrochloric acid, kills bacteria, activates pepsin, pepsinogen, pepsin, protein digestion, small intestine, duodenum, bile, liver, pancreatic juice, ileum, absorption, villi, microvilli, large intestine, colon, water absorption, feces formation, rectum, feces storage, anus, elimination', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(150, '22. ANIMAL NUTRITION', 'Dental Formula', 'Dental formula expresses number and types of teeth in one half of jaw (upper/lower). Human adult (permanent): I 2/2, C 1/1, P 2/2, M 3/3 = total 32 teeth (I=incisors, C=canines, P=premolars, M=molars). Human deciduous (milk teeth): I 2/2, C 1/1, M 2/2 = 20 teeth. Herbivores (e.g., cow): incisors reduced/absent in upper jaw, diastema (gap), complex molars for grinding plant material. Carnivores (e.g., dog): prominent canines for tearing flesh, carnassial teeth (specialized premolars/molars) for shearing. Dental care: brushing, flossing, limiting sugar prevents tooth decay (dental caries).', 'dental formula, teeth, incisors, canines, premolars, molars, I, C, P, M, human adult, permanent teeth, 32 teeth, I 2/2, C 1/1, P 2/2, M 3/3, human deciduous, milk teeth, 20 teeth, I 2/2, C 1/1, M 2/2, herbivores, cow, incisors reduced, diastema, gap, complex molars, grinding, carnivores, dog, prominent canines, tearing flesh, carnassial teeth, shearing, dental care, brushing, flossing, limiting sugar, tooth decay, dental caries', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(151, '23. BASIC ECOLOGICAL CONCEPTS', 'Ecosystem Components', 'An ecosystem is a community of living organisms (biotic) interacting with their non-living (abiotic) environment. The term was coined by Arthur Tansley (1935). Ecosystems range from small (pond, rotting log) to large (forest, ocean). Components: (1) Biotic components: All living organisms classified by trophic roles. (a) Producers (autotrophs): Organisms that synthesize organic compounds from inorganic sources. Photoautotrophs (plants, algae, cyanobacteria) use light energy; chemoautotrophs (some bacteria) use chemical energy from inorganic compounds. (b) Consumers (heterotrophs): Organisms that obtain energy by feeding on others. Primary consumers (herbivores) eat producers; secondary consumers (carnivores) eat herbivores; tertiary consumers (top carnivores) eat other carnivores; omnivores eat both plants and animals; detritivores feed on dead organic matter. (c) Decomposers (saprotrophs): Bacteria and fungi that break down dead organic matter, releasing nutrients for recycling. (2) Abiotic components: Physical and chemical factors. (a) Climatic factors: Light, temperature, rainfall, humidity, wind. (b) Edaphic factors: Soil type, pH, mineral composition, texture, moisture. (c) Topographic factors: Altitude, slope, aspect. (d) Aquatic factors: Salinity, turbidity, dissolved oxygen, pH, current. Energy flow: Energy enters ecosystems as sunlight, captured by producers via photosynthesis, transferred through food chains, and eventually lost as heat (second law of thermodynamics). Nutrient cycling: Elements (C, N, P, etc.) are recycled between biotic and abiotic components through biogeochemical cycles.', 'ecosystem, Arthur Tansley, biotic components, abiotic components, producers, autotrophs, photoautotrophs, chemoautotrophs, consumers, heterotrophs, primary consumers, herbivores, secondary consumers, carnivores, tertiary consumers, top carnivores, omnivores, detritivores, decomposers, saprotrophs, bacteria, fungi, climatic factors, light, temperature, rainfall, humidity, wind, edaphic factors, soil type, pH, mineral composition, texture, moisture, topographic factors, altitude, slope, aspect, aquatic factors, salinity, turbidity, dissolved oxygen, pH, current, energy flow, sunlight, photosynthesis, food chains, heat loss, second law of thermodynamics, nutrient cycling, biogeochemical cycles', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(152, '23. BASIC ECOLOGICAL CONCEPTS', 'Biotic Components', 'Biotic components are the living parts of an ecosystem, classified by their trophic (feeding) relationships. (1) Producers (autotrophs): (a) Photoautotrophs: Plants, algae, cyanobacteria use chlorophyll to capture light energy, converting CO₂ and H₂O to carbohydrates (photosynthesis). They form the base of almost all food chains. (b) Chemoautotrophs: Bacteria that oxidize inorganic compounds (hydrogen sulfide, ammonia, iron) to obtain energy, fixing CO₂. Found in deep-sea hydrothermal vents, hot springs. (2) Consumers (heterotrophs): Organisms that cannot produce their own food. (a) Primary consumers (herbivores): Feed directly on producers. Examples: cows, grasshoppers, zooplankton. (b) Secondary consumers: Feed on herbivores. Examples: cats that eat mice, birds that eat insects. (c) Tertiary consumers: Feed on secondary consumers. Examples: hawks, lions, sharks. (d) Quaternary consumers: Top predators at highest trophic level. (e) Omnivores: Feed on both plants and animals. Examples: humans, bears, rats. (f) Detritivores: Feed on dead organic matter (detritus). Examples: earthworms, millipedes, dung beetles. (3) Decomposers (saprotrophs): Break down dead organic matter externally by secreting enzymes, then absorb nutrients. Examples: bacteria, fungi. They are essential for nutrient recycling. Trophic structure: The feeding relationships determine energy flow and nutrient cycling. Biomass and energy decrease at each trophic level (about 10% transfer efficiency).', 'biotic components, trophic relationships, producers, autotrophs, photoautotrophs, plants, algae, cyanobacteria, chlorophyll, photosynthesis, chemoautotrophs, bacteria, hydrogen sulfide oxidation, ammonia oxidation, iron oxidation, hydrothermal vents, consumers, heterotrophs, primary consumers, herbivores, cows, grasshoppers, zooplankton, secondary consumers, carnivores, cats, birds, tertiary consumers, hawks, lions, sharks, quaternary consumers, top predators, omnivores, humans, bears, rats, detritivores, dead organic matter, detritus, earthworms, millipedes, dung beetles, decomposers, saprotrophs, enzymes, bacteria, fungi, nutrient recycling, trophic structure, energy flow, biomass decrease, 10% transfer efficiency', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(153, '23. BASIC ECOLOGICAL CONCEPTS', 'Abiotic Components', 'Abiotic components are the non-living physical and chemical factors that influence ecosystem structure and function. They determine which organisms can survive in a given habitat. (1) Climatic factors: (a) Light: Intensity affects photosynthesis; duration (photoperiod) influences flowering, migration, reproduction; quality (wavelength) affects aquatic plant distribution (light penetrates water selectively). (b) Temperature: Affects metabolic rates (enzyme activity), distribution of species (each species has optimal temperature range). Extreme temperatures limit survival. (c) Rainfall (precipitation): Determines biome type (forest, grassland, desert). Affects water availability for organisms. (d) Humidity: Affects transpiration rates in plants, water loss in animals. (e) Wind: Affects transpiration, pollination (wind-pollinated plants), seed dispersal, erosion. (2) Edaphic (soil) factors: (a) Soil type: Sandy, loamy, clayey - affects water retention, aeration, root penetration. (b) pH: Affects nutrient availability (most plants prefer pH 6-7), microbial activity. (c) Mineral composition: Availability of essential elements (N, P, K, Ca, Mg, Fe). (d) Texture: Proportion of sand, silt, clay particles. (e) Moisture: Water content in soil. (f) Organic matter (humus): Improves soil structure, water retention, nutrient supply. (3) Topographic factors: (a) Altitude: Temperature decreases with altitude (~1°C per 100 m), affects species distribution. (b) Slope angle: Affects drainage, erosion, solar exposure. (c) Aspect (direction slope faces): South-facing slopes (northern hemisphere) receive more sunlight, warmer and drier than north-facing. (4) Aquatic factors: (a) Salinity: Salt concentration - freshwater (<0.5 ppt), brackish (0.5-30 ppt), marine (30-50 ppt), hypersaline (>50 ppt). (b) Turbidity: Water clarity - affects light penetration, photosynthesis. (c) Dissolved oxygen: Essential for aquatic life; affected by temperature (cold water holds more O₂), pollution. (d) pH: Affects aquatic organisms; acid rain lowers pH, harming life. (e) Water current: Affects oxygen supply, nutrient distribution, organism attachment. (f) Depth: Affects light penetration, pressure, temperature.', 'abiotic components, non-living factors, physical factors, chemical factors, ecosystem structure, habitat, climatic factors, light intensity, photosynthesis, photoperiod, flowering, migration, reproduction, light quality, wavelength, temperature, metabolic rates, enzyme activity, species distribution, optimal temperature range, rainfall, precipitation, biome type, forest, grassland, desert, water availability, humidity, transpiration rates, water loss, wind, transpiration, pollination, wind-pollinated plants, seed dispersal, erosion, edaphic factors, soil factors, soil type, sandy soil, loamy soil, clayey soil, water retention, aeration, root penetration, pH, nutrient availability, microbial activity, mineral composition, nitrogen, phosphorus, potassium, calcium, magnesium, iron, soil texture, sand, silt, clay, soil moisture, organic matter, humus, topographic factors, altitude, temperature decrease, species distribution, slope angle, drainage, erosion, solar exposure, aspect, south-facing slopes, sunlight, aquatic factors, salinity, ppt, freshwater, brackish, marine, hypersaline, turbidity, water clarity, light penetration, dissolved oxygen, temperature, pollution, pH, acid rain, water current, oxygen supply, nutrient distribution, organism attachment, depth, light penetration, pressure', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(154, '23. BASIC ECOLOGICAL CONCEPTS', 'Ecological Factors', 'Ecological factors are environmental conditions that influence the distribution, abundance, and behavior of organisms. These factors interact complexly to shape ecosystems. (1) Temperature: (a) Affects enzyme reaction rates (Q₁₀ effect - rate doubles for every 10°C increase within range). (b) Determines metabolic rates (ectotherms vs endotherms). (c) Limits species distribution (coral reefs require warm water >18°C; arctic species adapted to cold). (d) Influences behavior (hibernation, migration, aestivation). (2) Light: (a) Essential for photosynthesis (light compensation point, light saturation point). (b) Affects plant morphology (etiolation in darkness, phototropism). (c) Regulates flowering (photoperiodism: short-day plants, long-day plants, day-neutral plants). (d) Influences animal behavior (diurnal vs nocturnal activity, migration timing). (3) Water: (a) Limits terrestrial life in deserts, determines biome distribution. (b) Affects plant adaptations (xerophytes, hydrophytes, mesophytes). (c) Influences animal adaptations (water conservation in desert animals, osmoregulation in aquatic animals). (4) pH: (a) Affects nutrient availability in soil (optimal pH 6-7 for most plants). (b) Influences enzyme activity (each enzyme has optimal pH). (c) Determines species composition in aquatic ecosystems (acid rain damage). (5) Salinity: (a) Affects osmoregulation in aquatic organisms (stenohaline vs euryhaline). (b) Determines distribution of plants (halophytes adapted to saline soils). (6) Nutrients: (a) Limit primary productivity (nitrogen, phosphorus often limiting). (b) Excess nutrients cause eutrophication. (7) Competition: Intraspecific (same species) and interspecific (different species) for limited resources. (8) Predation: Regulates prey populations, drives coevolution (predator-prey arms race). (9) Disease: Can cause population crashes, regulate population size. (10) Oxygen: Essential for aerobic respiration; low oxygen (hypoxia) limits aquatic life.', 'ecological factors, environmental conditions, distribution, abundance, behavior, temperature, enzyme reaction rates, Q₁₀ effect, metabolic rates, ectotherms, endotherms, species distribution, coral reefs, arctic species, hibernation, migration, aestivation, light, photosynthesis, light compensation point, light saturation point, plant morphology, etiolation, phototropism, flowering regulation, photoperiodism, short-day plants, long-day plants, day-neutral plants, animal behavior, diurnal, nocturnal, water, terrestrial life, deserts, biome distribution, xerophytes, hydrophytes, mesophytes, animal adaptations, water conservation, osmoregulation, pH, nutrient availability, enzyme activity, aquatic ecosystems, acid rain, salinity, osmoregulation, stenohaline, euryhaline, halophytes, nutrients, primary productivity, nitrogen limiting, phosphorus limiting, eutrophication, competition, intraspecific competition, interspecific competition, limited resources, predation, prey populations, coevolution, predator-prey arms race, disease, population crashes, population regulation, oxygen, aerobic respiration, hypoxia, aquatic life', '2026-02-27 15:40:30');
INSERT INTO `biology_syllabus_comprehensive` (`id`, `topic`, `sub_topic`, `note`, `keywords`, `created_at`) VALUES
(155, '23. BASIC ECOLOGICAL CONCEPTS', 'Measuring Ecological Factors', 'Ecologists use various instruments and methods to measure abiotic factors. (1) Temperature: (a) Mercury or alcohol thermometer: Simple, direct reading. (b) Thermocouple: Electrical device for remote or continuous monitoring. (c) Maximum-minimum thermometer: Records extremes over time. (d) Infrared thermometer: Non-contact measurement of surface temperature. (2) Humidity: (a) Hygrometer: Measures relative humidity. (b) Wet and dry bulb hygrometer (psychrometer): Uses evaporation cooling difference. (c) Hair hygrometer: Human hair length changes with humidity. (d) Electronic humidity sensor: Capacitance or resistance change. (3) Wind speed: (a) Anemometer: Cup or vane type measures wind speed. (b) Wind vane: Indicates wind direction. (4) Rainfall: (a) Rain gauge: Standard cylindrical collector measures precipitation depth. (b) Tipping bucket rain gauge: Records rainfall electronically. (5) Light intensity: (a) Light meter (lux meter): Measures illuminance. (b) Quantum sensor: Measures photosynthetically active radiation (PAR, 400-700 nm). (c) Photometer: Measures light intensity in specific wavelengths. (6) pH: (a) pH meter: Electronic device with glass electrode. (b) pH paper (litmus, universal indicator): Color change indicates pH. (7) Soil moisture: (a) Gravimetric method: Weigh soil sample, dry at 105°C, reweigh; moisture % = (wet-dry)/dry × 100. (b) Tensiometer: Measures soil water tension. (c) Electrical resistance blocks: Resistance changes with moisture. (d) Time domain reflectometry (TDR): Measures dielectric constant. (8) Dissolved oxygen: (a) Winkler titration: Chemical method for water samples. (b) Dissolved oxygen meter: Electrochemical sensor. (9) Salinity: (a) Refractometer: Measures salinity by light refraction. (b) Conductivity meter: Electrical conductivity correlates with salinity. (10) Turbidity: (a) Secchi disk: Measures water clarity (depth of visibility). (b) Turbidity meter: Measures light scattering. Proper measurement requires calibration, replication, and standardized techniques.', 'measuring ecological factors, temperature measurement, mercury thermometer, alcohol thermometer, thermocouple, maximum-minimum thermometer, infrared thermometer, humidity measurement, hygrometer, wet and dry bulb hygrometer, psychrometer, hair hygrometer, electronic humidity sensor, capacitance, resistance, wind speed measurement, anemometer, cup anemometer, vane anemometer, wind vane, wind direction, rainfall measurement, rain gauge, tipping bucket rain gauge, light intensity measurement, light meter, lux meter, quantum sensor, photosynthetically active radiation, PAR, photometer, pH measurement, pH meter, glass electrode, pH paper, litmus paper, universal indicator, soil moisture measurement, gravimetric method, drying at 105°C, tensiometer, electrical resistance blocks, time domain reflectometry, TDR, dissolved oxygen measurement, Winkler titration, dissolved oxygen meter, salinity measurement, refractometer, conductivity meter, turbidity measurement, Secchi disk, turbidity meter, light scattering, calibration, replication, standardized techniques', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(156, '23. BASIC ECOLOGICAL CONCEPTS', 'Food Chains and Food Webs', 'Food chains and food webs describe feeding relationships and energy flow in ecosystems. (1) Food chain: Linear sequence showing who eats whom. Arrows indicate energy transfer direction (eaten by). Example: grass → grasshopper → frog → snake → hawk. Each organism occupies a trophic level: producers (T1), primary consumers (T2), secondary consumers (T3), tertiary consumers (T4). Two main types: (a) Grazing food chain: Starts with living plants (producers). Most common in terrestrial ecosystems. (b) Detritus food chain: Starts with dead organic matter (detritus). Important in decomposition and nutrient cycling. Example: leaf litter → earthworm → bird. (2) Food web: Interconnected network of multiple food chains. More realistic representation of ecosystem feeding relationships. Complexity increases stability (more connections = more alternative pathways if one species declines). (3) Ecological efficiency: Only about 10% of energy transfers from one trophic level to the next (Lindeman\'s law of trophic efficiency). Rest lost as heat (respiration), undigested material (feces), not consumed. This limits number of trophic levels (usually 4-5). (4) Trophic cascades: Removal of top predator affects lower levels. Example: Yellowstone wolves reduce elk, allowing plant regeneration. (5) Keystone species: Species with disproportionate effect on ecosystem relative to biomass. Often top predators. (6) Bioaccumulation and biomagnification: Persistent toxins (DDT, mercury) accumulate in organisms and concentrate at higher trophic levels, harming top predators.', 'food chains, food webs, feeding relationships, energy flow, linear sequence, grass, grasshopper, frog, snake, hawk, trophic levels, producers, T1, primary consumers, T2, secondary consumers, T3, tertiary consumers, T4, grazing food chain, living plants, terrestrial ecosystems, detritus food chain, dead organic matter, detritus, decomposition, nutrient cycling, leaf litter, earthworm, bird, interconnected network, multiple food chains, realistic representation, ecosystem complexity, stability, alternative pathways, ecological efficiency, 10% energy transfer, Lindeman\'s law, trophic efficiency, heat loss, respiration, undigested material, feces, not consumed, limited trophic levels, 4-5 levels, trophic cascades, top predator removal, Yellowstone wolves, elk reduction, plant regeneration, keystone species, disproportionate effect, biomass, top predators, bioaccumulation, biomagnification, persistent toxins, DDT, mercury, concentrate, higher trophic levels, top predators', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(157, '23. BASIC ECOLOGICAL CONCEPTS', 'Trophic Levels', 'Trophic levels are the feeding positions in a food chain or web. (1) Producers (T1): Autotrophs that convert solar energy to chemical energy. Includes plants, algae, cyanobacteria. They form the foundation of all ecosystems. (2) Primary consumers (T2): Herbivores that eat producers. Examples: insects, grazing mammals, zooplankton. (3) Secondary consumers (T3): Carnivores that eat herbivores. Examples: spiders, small fish, frogs. (4) Tertiary consumers (T4): Carnivores that eat other carnivores. Examples: hawks, lions, sharks. (5) Quaternary consumers (T5): Top predators at highest level. Examples: orcas, eagles, polar bears. (6) Decomposers: Not usually assigned a trophic level; they break down all levels. Energy transfer: Energy decreases at each level. (a) Only about 10% of energy at one level is transferred to the next (Lindeman\'s efficiency). (b) Energy lost as heat (respiration), not all biomass consumed, not all consumed material digested. (c) This explains why food chains are short (rarely >5 levels). Biomass pyramids: Total mass of organisms decreases at higher trophic levels (except inverted pyramids in some aquatic systems where phytoplankton reproduce rapidly). Number pyramids: Number of individuals decreases at higher levels (except with parasites or trees). Energy pyramids: Always upright, most accurate representation.', 'trophic levels, feeding positions, food chain, food web, producers, T1, autotrophs, solar energy, chemical energy, plants, algae, cyanobacteria, ecosystem foundation, primary consumers, T2, herbivores, insects, grazing mammals, zooplankton, secondary consumers, T3, carnivores, spiders, small fish, frogs, tertiary consumers, T4, carnivores, hawks, lions, sharks, quaternary consumers, T5, top predators, orcas, eagles, polar bears, decomposers, break down all levels, energy transfer, 10% energy transfer, Lindeman\'s efficiency, heat loss, respiration, not all biomass consumed, not all digested, short food chains, rarely >5 levels, biomass pyramids, total mass decreases, inverted pyramids, aquatic systems, phytoplankton, number pyramids, number of individuals decreases, parasites, trees, energy pyramids, always upright, most accurate representation', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(158, '23. BASIC ECOLOGICAL CONCEPTS', 'Ecological Pyramids', 'Ecological pyramids graphically represent trophic structure. Three types: (1) Pyramid of numbers: Number of individuals at each trophic level. (a) Usually upright: many producers support fewer herbivores, even fewer carnivores. (b) Inverted in some ecosystems: one tree (producer) supports many insects (herbivores). Parasitic food chains also inverted (one host supports many parasites). (2) Pyramid of biomass: Total mass (dry weight) of organisms at each trophic level at a point in time (standing crop). (a) Usually upright in terrestrial ecosystems (grassland, forest). (b) Inverted in some aquatic ecosystems (phytoplankton reproduce rapidly, have low standing biomass but high productivity). (3) Pyramid of energy: Energy flow (rate of production) at each trophic level per unit time (e.g., kJ/m²/year). (a) Always upright because energy decreases at each level (thermodynamics). (b) Most informative because it accounts for productivity, not just standing stock. (c) Shows why there cannot be many trophic levels (energy loss limits length). Construction: Measure number, biomass, or energy at each level. Limitations: (a) Numbers pyramid ignores size differences (one tree vs many insects). (b) Biomass pyramid doesn\'t account for productivity (phytoplankton reproduce fast despite low biomass). (c) Energy pyramid requires measuring production rates, which is difficult.', 'ecological pyramids, trophic structure, pyramid of numbers, number of individuals, upright, many producers, fewer herbivores, fewer carnivores, inverted, one tree, many insects, parasitic food chains, one host, many parasites, pyramid of biomass, total mass, dry weight, standing crop, terrestrial ecosystems, grassland, forest, aquatic ecosystems, phytoplankton, low standing biomass, high productivity, pyramid of energy, energy flow, rate of production, per unit time, kJ/m²/year, always upright, thermodynamics, most informative, productivity, standing stock, limited trophic levels, construction, measure number, measure biomass, measure energy, limitations, ignores size differences, one tree vs many insects, doesn\'t account for productivity, phytoplankton reproduce fast, energy pyramid difficult, production rates measurement', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(159, '23. BASIC ECOLOGICAL CONCEPTS', 'Decomposition', 'Decomposition is the breakdown of dead organic matter (detritus) by decomposers, releasing nutrients for reuse by producers. Essential for nutrient cycling. (1) Process: (a) Fragmentation: Detritivores (earthworms, millipedes, termites) break dead matter into smaller pieces, increasing surface area. (b) Leaching: Water-soluble compounds (sugars, amino acids) dissolve and are lost. (c) Catabolism: Decomposers (bacteria, fungi) secrete enzymes that break down complex organic compounds (cellulose, lignin, proteins, lipids) into simpler inorganic substances (CO₂, H₂O, NH₃, minerals). (d) Humification: Resistant organic material forms humus (dark, amorphous, colloidal) that improves soil structure. (e) Mineralization: Nutrients (N, P, K, Ca, Mg) released in inorganic forms available to plants. (2) Decomposers: (a) Micro-decomposers: Bacteria, fungi (primary decomposers). (b) Macro-decomposers (detritivores): Earthworms, millipedes, woodlice, dung beetles, termites. (3) Factors affecting decomposition rate: (a) Temperature: Higher temperature increases enzyme activity (optimum 25-35°C). (b) Moisture: Decomposers need water; too dry stops activity, too wet limits oxygen. (c) Oxygen: Aerobic decomposition faster than anaerobic. (d) Substrate quality: High lignin, cellulose content slows decomposition (wood decomposes slowly). High nitrogen content speeds it (young leaves decompose faster). (e) pH: Most decomposers prefer neutral pH; acidic conditions (bogs) slow decomposition (preserve organic matter as peat). (4) Products: (a) Gaseous: CO₂ (from respiration), CH₄ (methane from anaerobic decomposition), N₂, H₂S. (b) Heat energy released. (c) Humus: Improves soil water holding capacity, aeration, nutrient retention. (d) Inorganic nutrients: NH₄⁺, NO₃⁻, PO₄³⁻, etc. (5) Ecological importance: (a) Nutrient recycling - returns elements to soil for plant uptake. (b) Soil formation - builds organic matter. (c) Waste removal - cleans environment.', 'decomposition, dead organic matter, detritus, decomposers, nutrient cycling, fragmentation, detritivores, earthworms, millipedes, termites, smaller pieces, surface area increase, leaching, water-soluble compounds, sugars, amino acids, catabolism, bacteria, fungi, enzymes, complex organic compounds, cellulose, lignin, proteins, lipids, simpler inorganic substances, CO₂, H₂O, NH₃, minerals, humification, resistant organic material, humus, colloidal, soil structure, mineralization, nutrients released, N, P, K, Ca, Mg, inorganic forms, plant available, micro-decomposers, bacteria, fungi, primary decomposers, macro-decomposers, detritivores, woodlice, dung beetles, factors affecting decomposition, temperature, enzyme activity, optimum 25-35°C, moisture, water needed, too dry stops, too wet limits oxygen, oxygen, aerobic decomposition, anaerobic decomposition, substrate quality, lignin content, cellulose content, wood slow decomposition, nitrogen content, young leaves fast decomposition, pH, neutral pH, acidic conditions, bogs, peat preservation, products, gaseous products, CO₂, respiration, CH₄, methane, anaerobic decomposition, N₂, H₂S, heat energy, humus, water holding capacity, aeration, nutrient retention, inorganic nutrients, NH₄⁺, NO₃⁻, PO₄³⁻, ecological importance, nutrient recycling, soil formation, organic matter building, waste removal, environment cleaning', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(160, '24. ECOLOGICAL MANAGEMENT', 'Biological Associations', 'Biological associations are interactions between different species in a community. Classified by effects on each species (+, beneficial; -, harmful; 0, neutral). (1) Parasitism (+/-): One organism (parasite) benefits at expense of host. (a) Ectoparasites: Live on host surface (ticks, lice, fleas). (b) Endoparasites: Live inside host (tapeworms, roundworms, Plasmodium). (c) Brood parasitism: Cuckoo lays eggs in other bird\'s nest. Host harmed (reduced fitness), parasite benefits. Adaptations: Parasites have specialized attachment organs (hooks, suckers), high reproductive capacity, complex life cycles often involving multiple hosts. (2) Mutualism (+/+): Both species benefit. (a) Obligate mutualism: Both species depend on each other for survival (lichens - fungus provides structure, alga provides food via photosynthesis). (b) Facultative mutualism: Beneficial but not essential (mycorrhizae - fungi enhance plant nutrient uptake, plant provides carbohydrates). (c) Pollination mutualism: Insects/ birds get nectar, plants get pollen transfer. (d) Seed dispersal mutualism: Animals get fruit, plants get seeds dispersed. (e) Nitrogen-fixing mutualism: Rhizobium bacteria in root nodules of legumes provide fixed nitrogen, plant provides carbohydrates and shelter. (f) Cleaning mutualism: Cleaner fish remove parasites from larger fish. (3) Commensalism (+/0): One species benefits, other unaffected. (a) Epiphytes (orchids, mosses) grow on trees for support, not harming tree. (b) Barnacles attached to whales get transport to feeding areas, whale unaffected. (c) Cattle egrets follow grazing animals, catching insects disturbed by them. (4) Amensalism (-/0): One species harmed, other unaffected. Example: Large tree shades smaller plants, inhibiting growth. (5) Competition (-/-): Both species harmed by interaction for limited resources. (a) Interspecific competition: Between different species (resource partitioning reduces competition). (b) Intraspecific competition: Between same species (regulates population size). (6) Predation (+/-): One organism (predator) kills and eats another (prey). Drives coevolution (predator adaptations for capture, prey adaptations for escape). (7) Saprophytism: Organisms feed on dead organic matter (decomposers, detritivores). Beneficial for ecosystem nutrient cycling.', 'biological associations, species interactions, community, parasite, host, ectoparasites, ticks, lice, fleas, endoparasites, tapeworms, roundworms, Plasmodium, brood parasitism, cuckoo, fitness reduced, parasite benefits, attachment organs, hooks, suckers, high reproductive capacity, complex life cycles, multiple hosts, mutualism, both benefit, obligate mutualism, lichens, fungus, alga, photosynthesis, facultative mutualism, mycorrhizae, fungi, nutrient uptake, carbohydrates, pollination mutualism, insects, birds, nectar, pollen transfer, seed dispersal mutualism, animals, fruit, seeds dispersed, nitrogen-fixing mutualism, Rhizobium, root nodules, legumes, fixed nitrogen, cleaning mutualism, cleaner fish, parasites removed, commensalism, one benefits, other unaffected, epiphytes, orchids, mosses, trees, support, barnacles, whales, transport, feeding areas, cattle egrets, grazing animals, insects disturbed, amensalism, one harmed, other unaffected, large tree shades, smaller plants, inhibited growth, competition, both harmed, limited resources, interspecific competition, different species, resource partitioning, intraspecific competition, same species, population regulation, predation, predator kills prey, coevolution, predator adaptations, prey adaptations, capture, escape, saprophytism, dead organic matter, decomposers, detritivores, nutrient cycling', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(161, '24. ECOLOGICAL MANAGEMENT', 'Pollution - Air', 'Air pollution is contamination of the atmosphere by harmful substances (pollutants) that cause damage to the environment and human health. (1) Major pollutants: (a) Carbon monoxide (CO): Colorless, odorless gas from incomplete combustion of fossil fuels (vehicles, industry). Binds hemoglobin 200× stronger than O₂, reduces oxygen delivery, causes headaches, dizziness, death at high levels. (b) Sulfur dioxide (SO₂): From burning coal and oil (power plants, industrial processes). Causes acid rain (H₂SO₄), respiratory problems, damages plants, corrodes buildings. (c) Nitrogen oxides (NO, NO₂, collectively NOₓ): From high-temperature combustion (vehicles, power plants). Contribute to acid rain (HNO₃), photochemical smog, ground-level ozone formation, respiratory irritation. (d) Particulate matter (PM): Tiny solid or liquid particles suspended in air. PM₁₀ (coarse, <10 μm), PM₂.₅ (fine, <2.5 μm). Sources: combustion, dust, industry. Penetrate deep into lungs, cause cardiovascular and respiratory disease, cancer. (e) Volatile organic compounds (VOCs): From solvents, paints, gasoline evaporation. Contribute to ozone formation. (f) Ozone (O₃): Beneficial in stratosphere (UV protection), harmful at ground level (respiratory irritant, damages plants). Formed by reaction of NOₓ and VOCs in sunlight. (g) Lead (Pb): From leaded gasoline (now phased out in many countries), industrial sources. Affects nervous system, especially harmful to children. (h) Greenhouse gases: CO₂, CH₄, N₂O, CFCs. Cause global warming and climate change. (2) Sources: (a) Stationary sources: Power plants, factories, refineries. (b) Mobile sources: Vehicles, aircraft, ships. (c) Natural sources: Volcanoes (SO₂, ash), forest fires (CO, PM), dust storms. (3) Effects: (a) Human health: Respiratory diseases (asthma, bronchitis), cardiovascular disease, cancer, premature death. (b) Environmental damage: Acid rain damages forests, acidifies lakes, kills aquatic life, corrodes buildings (limestone, marble). (c) Global warming: Greenhouse gases trap heat, causing climate change. (d) Ozone depletion: CFCs destroy stratospheric ozone, increasing UV radiation. (e) Smog: Reduces visibility, harms health. (f) Crop damage: Ozone reduces crop yields. (4) Control measures: (a) Cleaner fuels: Low-sulfur coal, natural gas, renewable energy. (b) Emission controls: Catalytic converters (vehicles), scrubbers (power plants), electrostatic precipitators (remove particulates). (c) Regulations: Clean Air Acts, emission standards. (d) Alternative energy: Solar, wind, hydro, nuclear. (e) Energy efficiency: Reduce consumption. (f) Public transport: Reduce vehicle emissions. (g) Reforestation: Trees absorb CO₂.', 'air pollution, atmosphere, pollutants, environmental damage, human health, carbon monoxide, CO, colorless, odorless, incomplete combustion, fossil fuels, vehicles, industry, hemoglobin binding, oxygen delivery, headaches, dizziness, death, sulfur dioxide, SO₂, burning coal, oil, power plants, acid rain, H₂SO₄, respiratory problems, plant damage, building corrosion, nitrogen oxides, NOₓ, high-temperature combustion, photochemical smog, ground-level ozone, respiratory irritation, particulate matter, PM, PM₁₀, PM₂.₅, combustion, dust, lung penetration, cardiovascular disease, cancer, volatile organic compounds, VOCs, solvents, paints, gasoline evaporation, ozone formation, ozone, O₃, stratosphere, UV protection, ground-level harmful, respiratory irritant, plant damage, lead, Pb, leaded gasoline, nervous system, children, greenhouse gases, CO₂, CH₄, N₂O, CFCs, global warming, climate change, stationary sources, power plants, factories, refineries, mobile sources, vehicles, aircraft, ships, natural sources, volcanoes, forest fires, dust storms, respiratory diseases, asthma, bronchitis, cardiovascular disease, premature death, acid rain, forests, lake acidification, aquatic life, building corrosion, limestone, marble, ozone depletion, UV radiation, smog, visibility, crop damage, cleaner fuels, low-sulfur coal, natural gas, renewable energy, emission controls, catalytic converters, scrubbers, electrostatic precipitators, regulations, Clean Air Acts, emission standards, alternative energy, solar, wind, hydro, nuclear, energy efficiency, public transport, reforestation, CO₂ absorption', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(162, '24. ECOLOGICAL MANAGEMENT', 'Pollution - Water and Soil', 'Water pollution is contamination of water bodies (rivers, lakes, oceans, groundwater) by harmful substances. Soil pollution is contamination of soil that reduces fertility and poses health risks. (1) Major pollutants: (a) Sewage and pathogens: Untreated sewage introduces bacteria (E. coli, Salmonella), viruses, parasites, causing waterborne diseases (cholera, typhoid, dysentery). Organic matter depletes oxygen (eutrophication). (b) Industrial waste: Heavy metals (mercury, lead, cadmium, chromium), toxic chemicals (PCBs, dioxins), acids, cyanides. Bioaccumulate in food chains. (c) Agricultural runoff: Fertilizers (nitrates, phosphates) cause eutrophication; pesticides (DDT, organophosphates) harm non-target organisms, bioaccumulate. (d) Oil spills: From tankers, pipelines, offshore drilling. Coats animals (birds, mammals), kills aquatic life, contaminates beaches. (e) Detergents and surfactants: From households, industry. Cause foaming, toxic to aquatic life. (f) Plastics: Non-biodegradable, harm wildlife (ingestion, entanglement), break down into microplastics. (g) Thermal pollution: Heated water from power plants reduces dissolved oxygen, harms aquatic life. (h) Radioactive waste: From nuclear power, medical, research. Long-lived, carcinogenic. (2) Sources: (a) Point sources: Discharge from specific location (factory pipe, sewage outfall). (b) Non-point sources: Diffuse runoff (agricultural fields, urban streets, construction sites). (3) Effects: (a) Eutrophication: Excess nutrients (N, P) cause algal blooms. Algae die, decomposed by bacteria using O₂, creating dead zones (hypoxia) where aquatic life cannot survive. (b) Bioaccumulation: Toxins accumulate in organisms. (c) Biomagnification: Toxins concentrate at higher trophic levels (DDT in birds of prey causes eggshell thinning). (d) Drinking water contamination: Causes health problems. (e) Loss of biodiversity: Species die or migrate. (f) Soil degradation: Reduced fertility, erosion, desertification. (g) Food contamination: Toxins enter food chain. (4) Control measures: (a) Wastewater treatment: Primary (settling), secondary (biological), tertiary (nutrient removal). (b) Industrial pretreatment: Remove toxins before discharge. (c) Sustainable agriculture: Reduce fertilizer/pesticide use, buffer strips, integrated pest management. (d) Proper hazardous waste disposal. (e) Oil spill prevention and response. (f) Regulations: Clean Water Acts, Safe Drinking Water Acts. (g) Soil remediation: Bioremediation (use microbes), phytoremediation (use plants). (h) Reduce, reuse, recycle plastics.', 'water pollution, soil pollution, contamination, water bodies, rivers, lakes, oceans, groundwater, soil fertility, health risks, sewage, pathogens, bacteria, E. coli, Salmonella, viruses, parasites, waterborne diseases, cholera, typhoid, dysentery, organic matter, oxygen depletion, eutrophication, industrial waste, heavy metals, mercury, lead, cadmium, chromium, toxic chemicals, PCBs, dioxins, acids, cyanides, bioaccumulation, agricultural runoff, fertilizers, nitrates, phosphates, pesticides, DDT, organophosphates, non-target organisms, oil spills, tankers, pipelines, offshore drilling, coats animals, birds, mammals, aquatic life, beaches, detergents, surfactants, foaming, plastics, non-biodegradable, wildlife ingestion, entanglement, microplastics, thermal pollution, heated water, power plants, dissolved oxygen, radioactive waste, nuclear power, medical waste, research waste, long-lived, carcinogenic, point sources, factory pipe, sewage outfall, non-point sources, diffuse runoff, agricultural fields, urban streets, construction sites, algal blooms, algae die, bacteria decompose, oxygen used, dead zones, hypoxia, bioaccumulation, biomagnification, DDT, birds of prey, eggshell thinning, drinking water contamination, health problems, biodiversity loss, species die, species migrate, soil degradation, reduced fertility, erosion, desertification, food contamination, food chain, wastewater treatment, primary treatment, settling, secondary treatment, biological, tertiary treatment, nutrient removal, industrial pretreatment, sustainable agriculture, reduce fertilizer, reduce pesticide, buffer strips, integrated pest management, hazardous waste disposal, oil spill prevention, oil spill response, regulations, Clean Water Acts, Safe Drinking Water Acts, soil remediation, bioremediation, microbes, phytoremediation, plants, reduce, reuse, recycle, plastics', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(163, '24. ECOLOGICAL MANAGEMENT', 'Noise Pollution', 'Noise pollution is excessive, displeasing human-made sound that disrupts the environment and harms human health and wildlife. (1) Sources: (a) Transportation: Road traffic (cars, trucks, motorcycles), aircraft (airports, flight paths), railways (trains, subways). (b) Industrial: Factories, construction sites, machinery. (c) Recreational: Loud music (concerts, clubs, personal devices), sporting events, fireworks. (d) Domestic: Generators, air conditioners, appliances, pets. (e) Social: Neighbors, parties, public gatherings. (2) Measurement: Decibels (dB) - logarithmic scale. 0 dB = threshold of hearing, 30 dB = whisper, 60 dB = normal conversation, 80 dB = heavy traffic, 100 dB = jackhammer, 120 dB = threshold of pain (rock concert), 140 dB = jet engine at takeoff. Prolonged exposure >85 dB causes hearing damage. (3) Effects on human health: (a) Hearing loss: Temporary threshold shift, permanent noise-induced hearing loss (damage to hair cells in cochlea). (b) Cardiovascular effects: Increased blood pressure, heart rate, stress hormones (cortisol, adrenaline), risk of hypertension, heart disease. (c) Sleep disturbance: Difficulty falling asleep, awakening, reduced sleep quality, fatigue. (d) Cognitive effects: Impaired concentration, learning, memory (especially in children). (e) Mental health: Irritability, anxiety, depression, stress. (f) Communication interference: Difficulty understanding speech, leading to social isolation. (4) Effects on wildlife: (a) Behavioral changes: Avoidance of noisy areas, altered migration patterns, disrupted breeding. (b) Communication interference: Masking of animal calls (birds, whales) used for mating, warning, foraging. (c) Physiological stress: Increased stress hormones, reduced reproductive success. (d) Hearing damage: Similar to humans. (5) Control measures: (a) Source reduction: Quieter machinery, electric vehicles, noise barriers on highways, soundproofing. (b) Planning: Zoning to separate residential areas from noise sources, building codes with sound insulation. (c) Regulations: Noise limits, quiet hours, enforcement. (d) Personal protection: Earplugs, earmuffs for high-noise occupations. (e) Urban design: Green spaces absorb sound, berms, vegetation buffers. (f) Public awareness: Education about noise effects.', 'noise pollution, excessive sound, human-made sound, environment disruption, human health, wildlife, sources, transportation, road traffic, cars, trucks, motorcycles, aircraft, airports, flight paths, railways, trains, subways, industrial, factories, construction sites, machinery, recreational, loud music, concerts, clubs, personal devices, sporting events, fireworks, domestic, generators, air conditioners, appliances, pets, social, neighbors, parties, public gatherings, measurement, decibels, dB, logarithmic scale, threshold of hearing, 0 dB, whisper, 30 dB, normal conversation, 60 dB, heavy traffic, 80 dB, jackhammer, 100 dB, threshold of pain, 120 dB, rock concert, jet engine takeoff, 140 dB, prolonged exposure, >85 dB, hearing damage, effects on human health, hearing loss, temporary threshold shift, permanent noise-induced hearing loss, hair cells, cochlea, cardiovascular effects, increased blood pressure, heart rate, stress hormones, cortisol, adrenaline, hypertension, heart disease, sleep disturbance, difficulty falling asleep, awakening, reduced sleep quality, fatigue, cognitive effects, impaired concentration, learning, memory, children, mental health, irritability, anxiety, depression, stress, communication interference, difficulty understanding speech, social isolation, effects on wildlife, behavioral changes, avoidance of noisy areas, altered migration patterns, disrupted breeding, communication interference, masking animal calls, birds, whales, mating calls, warning calls, foraging calls, physiological stress, increased stress hormones, reduced reproductive success, hearing damage, control measures, source reduction, quieter machinery, electric vehicles, noise barriers, highways, soundproofing, planning, zoning, separate residential areas, building codes, sound insulation, regulations, noise limits, quiet hours, enforcement, personal protection, earplugs, earmuffs, high-noise occupations, urban design, green spaces, sound absorption, berms, vegetation buffers, public awareness, education', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(164, '24. ECOLOGICAL MANAGEMENT', 'Ecological Succession', 'Ecological succession is the gradual, directional change in species composition of a community over time. Two main types: (1) Primary succession: Occurs on newly formed, barren substrate with no soil. Examples: bare rock (after volcanic eruption, glacial retreat), sand dunes, lava flows. Stages: (a) Pioneer stage: Lichens and mosses colonize bare rock. They secrete acids that weather rock, start soil formation. When they die, organic matter accumulates. (b) Intermediate stage: Small grasses and herbs establish in thin soil. Their roots further break rock, add organic matter. (c) Shrub stage: Shrubs and small trees establish as soil deepens. (d) Climax community: Stable community (e.g., forest) in equilibrium with climate. (2) Secondary succession: Occurs on existing soil after disturbance removes vegetation but soil remains. Examples: abandoned farmland, cleared forest, burned area, flooded land. Stages: (a) Weed stage: Fast-growing annual plants (weeds) colonize first. (b) Grass stage: Perennial grasses establish. (c) Shrub stage: Shrubs invade. (d) Tree stage: Tree seedlings grow, eventually form forest. (e) Climax community: Stable community. Secondary succession faster than primary because soil already present. (3) Mechanisms: (a) Facilitation: Early species make environment more suitable for later species (e.g., lichens create soil). (b) Inhibition: Early species make environment less suitable for others (e.g., shade from shrubs inhibits grass growth). (c) Tolerance: Later species tolerate conditions created by earlier species. (4) Characteristics: (a) Species diversity increases through succession, peaks at climax. (b) Biomass increases. (c) Food webs become more complex. (d) Nutrient cycling becomes more efficient. (e) Stability increases. (5) Climax community: Stable, self-perpetuating final stage in equilibrium with climate. Characteristics: (a) Species composition relatively constant (dynamic equilibrium). (b) High species diversity. (c) Complex food webs. (d) Balanced energy flow (energy captured = energy used). (e) Efficient nutrient cycling. (f) Well-developed soil. Remains until major disturbance (fire, flood, human activity).', 'ecological succession, gradual change, directional change, species composition, community, primary succession, newly formed substrate, no soil, bare rock, volcanic eruption, glacial retreat, sand dunes, lava flows, pioneer stage, lichens, mosses, colonize, secrete acids, weather rock, soil formation, organic matter accumulation, intermediate stage, grasses, herbs, thin soil, roots break rock, shrub stage, shrubs, small trees, soil deepens, climax community, stable community, forest, climate equilibrium, secondary succession, existing soil, disturbance, vegetation removed, abandoned farmland, cleared forest, burned area, flooded land, weed stage, fast-growing annual plants, weeds, grass stage, perennial grasses, shrub stage, shrubs, tree stage, tree seedlings, climax community, faster succession, soil present, mechanisms, facilitation, early species make suitable, later species, lichens create soil, inhibition, early species make less suitable, shade from shrubs, grass growth inhibition, tolerance, later species tolerate conditions, characteristics, species diversity increases, peaks at climax, biomass increases, food webs complex, nutrient cycling efficient, stability increases, climax community, stable, self-perpetuating, final stage, dynamic equilibrium, high species diversity, complex food webs, balanced energy flow, energy captured equals energy used, efficient nutrient cycling, well-developed soil, major disturbance, fire, flood, human activity', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(165, '24. ECOLOGICAL MANAGEMENT', 'Climax Community', 'The climax community is the final, stable, self-perpetuating stage of ecological succession. It represents the community in equilibrium with prevailing environmental conditions (climate, soil, etc.). (1) Characteristics: (a) Stability: Species composition remains relatively constant over time (dynamic equilibrium). Minor fluctuations occur but overall structure persists. (b) Self-perpetuation: Community reproduces and maintains itself without external input. (c) High species diversity: Maximum biodiversity for that region. (d) Complex food webs: Multiple interconnected trophic levels. (e) Efficient nutrient cycling: Nutrients recycled within system, minimal losses. (f) Balanced energy flow: Energy captured by producers roughly equals energy used by respiration and lost as heat. (g) Well-developed soil: Mature soil profile with distinct horizons. (h) Biomass: Maximum biomass for that ecosystem (steady state). (2) Types based on climate: (a) Climatic climax: Determined by regional climate. Example: Tropical rainforest in wet tropics, temperate deciduous forest in mid-latitudes, taiga in cold regions, grassland in semi-arid regions, desert in arid regions. (b) Edaphic climax: Determined by soil factors (different from regional climate). Example: Pine forest on sandy soil within deciduous forest region. (c) Fire climax: Maintained by periodic fire (fire-adapted species). Example: Pine barrens, savanna. (d) Biotic climax: Maintained by grazing or other biotic factors. (3) Duration: Climax communities can persist indefinitely if undisturbed. However, climate change can shift climax type over long time scales. (4) Disturbance: Major disturbances (fire, flood, hurricane, human activity) reset succession to earlier stages. (5) Poly climax theory: Multiple climax types possible in a region due to local variations (soil, topography, fire). (6) Monoclimax theory (Clements): Only one true climax determined by climate. Other communities are subclimax stages.', 'climax community, final stage, stable, self-perpetuating, ecological succession, equilibrium, environmental conditions, climate, soil, characteristics, stability, species composition constant, dynamic equilibrium, minor fluctuations, overall structure persists, self-perpetuation, reproduces, maintains itself, no external input, high species diversity, maximum biodiversity, complex food webs, multiple trophic levels, efficient nutrient cycling, nutrients recycled, minimal losses, balanced energy flow, energy captured by producers, energy used by respiration, heat loss, well-developed soil, mature soil profile, distinct horizons, biomass, maximum biomass, steady state, types based on climate, climatic climax, regional climate, tropical rainforest, wet tropics, temperate deciduous forest, mid-latitudes, taiga, cold regions, grassland, semi-arid regions, desert, arid regions, edaphic climax, soil factors, pine forest, sandy soil, fire climax, periodic fire, fire-adapted species, pine barrens, savanna, biotic climax, grazing, biotic factors, duration, persist indefinitely, undisturbed, climate change, shift climax type, long time scales, disturbance, major disturbance, fire, flood, hurricane, human activity, reset succession, earlier stages, poly climax theory, multiple climax types, local variations, soil, topography, fire, monoclimax theory, Clements, one true climax, climate, subclimax stages', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(166, '24. ECOLOGICAL MANAGEMENT', 'Factors Affecting Population', 'Population dynamics are influenced by various factors that determine size, growth rate, and distribution. (1) Natality (birth rate): Number of births per unit time. Affected by: (a) Reproductive rate: Number of offspring per individual. (b) Frequency of reproduction: How often individuals breed. (c) Age at first reproduction. (d) Proportion of population of reproductive age. (2) Mortality (death rate): Number of deaths per unit time. Affected by: (a) Predation. (b) Disease. (c) Competition for resources. (d) Natural disasters. (e) Environmental stress (temperature extremes, drought). (f) Old age. (3) Immigration: Individuals moving into population. (4) Emigration: Individuals leaving population. Population change = (Natality + Immigration) - (Mortality + Emigration). (5) Environmental factors: (a) Food supply: Determines carrying capacity. Shortage reduces reproduction, increases mortality. (b) Predation: Regulates prey populations. Predator-prey cycles (Lotka-Volterra model). (c) Competition: Intraspecific (same species) for resources leads to density-dependent regulation. Interspecific (different species) can lead to competitive exclusion or resource partitioning. (d) Disease: Spreads faster in dense populations (density-dependent). Can cause population crashes. (e) Natural disasters: Floods, fires, droughts, earthquakes (density-independent). (f) Weather and climate: Temperature extremes, rainfall patterns. (6) Density-dependent factors: Effect increases with population density (competition, predation, disease). (7) Density-independent factors: Effect independent of density (natural disasters, weather extremes). (8) Carrying capacity (K): Maximum population size environment can sustain indefinitely. Logistic growth model: dN/dt = rN(1 - N/K). (9) Population regulation: Negative feedback loops maintain population around carrying capacity.', 'population factors, population dynamics, natality, birth rate, births per unit time, reproductive rate, offspring per individual, frequency of reproduction, age at first reproduction, proportion reproductive age, mortality, death rate, deaths per unit time, predation, disease, competition, natural disasters, environmental stress, temperature extremes, drought, old age, immigration, individuals moving in, emigration, individuals leaving, population change, natality plus immigration, minus mortality plus emigration, environmental factors, food supply, carrying capacity, shortage reduces reproduction, increases mortality, predation, prey populations, predator-prey cycles, Lotka-Volterra model, competition, intraspecific competition, same species, density-dependent regulation, interspecific competition, different species, competitive exclusion, resource partitioning, disease, spreads faster dense populations, density-dependent, population crashes, natural disasters, floods, fires, droughts, earthquakes, density-independent, weather, climate, temperature extremes, rainfall patterns, density-dependent factors, effect increases with density, competition, predation, disease, density-independent factors, effect independent of density, natural disasters, weather extremes, carrying capacity, K, maximum population size, environment sustain indefinitely, logistic growth model, dN/dt = rN(1 - N/K), population regulation, negative feedback loops', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(167, '24. ECOLOGICAL MANAGEMENT', 'Food Preservation Methods', 'Food preservation extends shelf life by preventing microbial growth, enzymatic reactions, and chemical changes. (1) Principles: (a) Prevent microbial growth by creating unfavorable conditions (low temperature, low water activity, low pH, anaerobic conditions). (b) Kill microorganisms (heat, radiation). (c) Slow enzymatic reactions (low temperature, low water). (2) Methods: (a) Drying (dehydration): Removes water, inhibiting microbial growth. Sun-drying (traditional), oven-drying, freeze-drying (lyophilization). Examples: dried fish, meat (biltong), fruits (raisins, dates), vegetables, grains. (b) Salting: High salt concentration causes plasmolysis (water loss) in microbial cells, inhibiting growth. Examples: salted fish, salted meat, pickling salt. (c) Smoking: Combines drying with antimicrobial chemicals from smoke (formaldehyde, phenols, creosote). Used for fish, meat. (d) Refrigeration (4°C): Slows microbial growth and enzyme activity. Short-term preservation. (e) Freezing (-18°C or lower): Stops microbial growth, greatly slows enzymes. Long-term preservation. Examples: frozen vegetables, meat, fish. (f) Canning (bottling): Food heated in sealed containers to kill microorganisms (sterilization). Airtight seal prevents recontamination. Examples: canned vegetables, fruits, fish, meat. (g) Pasteurization: Brief heating (72°C for 15 seconds) kills pathogens, reduces spoilage organisms. Does not sterilize. Examples: milk, juice, beer. (h) Fermentation: Microorganisms (bacteria, yeast) produce acids, alcohol, creating unfavorable environment for spoilage organisms. Examples: yogurt (lactic acid bacteria), cheese, sauerkraut, kimchi, pickles, bread, beer, wine. (i) Pickling: Using acid (vinegar) or anaerobic fermentation to preserve. Examples: pickled cucumbers, onions, eggs. (j) Sugar preservation: High sugar concentration (jams, jellies) reduces water activity. (k) Irradiation: Gamma rays or electron beams kill microbes and insects. Used for spices, some fruits, meat. (l) Modified atmosphere packaging: Replace air with CO₂ or N₂ to slow spoilage. (m) Chemical preservatives: Salt, sugar, vinegar, citric acid, benzoates, sorbates, nitrites (cured meats). (3) Biological basis: (a) Microorganisms need water, nutrients, suitable pH, temperature, oxygen. Removing or altering these inhibits them. (b) Enzymes (in food and microbes) cause spoilage; low temperature or heat inactivates them.', 'food preservation, shelf life extension, microbial growth prevention, enzymatic reactions, chemical changes, principles, prevent microbial growth, unfavorable conditions, low temperature, low water activity, low pH, anaerobic conditions, kill microorganisms, heat, radiation, slow enzymatic reactions, low temperature, low water, drying, dehydration, water removal, sun-drying, oven-drying, freeze-drying, lyophilization, dried fish, biltong, raisins, dates, salting, high salt concentration, plasmolysis, water loss, microbial cells, salted fish, salted meat, pickling salt, smoking, antimicrobial chemicals, formaldehyde, phenols, creosote, smoked fish, smoked meat, refrigeration, 4°C, slows microbial growth, enzyme activity, short-term preservation, freezing, -18°C, stops microbial growth, slows enzymes, long-term preservation, frozen vegetables, frozen meat, frozen fish, canning, bottling, heated in sealed containers, sterilization, airtight seal, recontamination prevention, canned vegetables, canned fruits, canned fish, canned meat, pasteurization, brief heating, 72°C, 15 seconds, kill pathogens, reduce spoilage organisms, milk, juice, beer, fermentation, microorganisms, bacteria, yeast, acids, alcohol, unfavorable environment, yogurt, lactic acid bacteria, cheese, sauerkraut, kimchi, pickles, bread, beer, wine, pickling, acid, vinegar, anaerobic fermentation, pickled cucumbers, pickled onions, pickled eggs, sugar preservation, high sugar concentration, jams, jellies, water activity reduction, irradiation, gamma rays, electron beams, kill microbes, kill insects, spices, meat, modified atmosphere packaging, replace air, CO₂, N₂, slow spoilage, chemical preservatives, salt, sugar, vinegar, citric acid, benzoates, sorbates, nitrites, cured meats, biological basis, microorganisms need water, nutrients, suitable pH, temperature, oxygen, enzymes cause spoilage, low temperature inactivates, heat inactivates', '2026-02-27 15:40:30');
INSERT INTO `biology_syllabus_comprehensive` (`id`, `topic`, `sub_topic`, `note`, `keywords`, `created_at`) VALUES
(168, '24. ECOLOGICAL MANAGEMENT', 'Pest Control', 'Pest control manages organisms (insects, weeds, pathogens, rodents) that damage crops, transmit diseases, or cause economic loss. (1) Types of pests: (a) Insects: Locusts, aphids, boll weevils, mosquitoes, termites. (b) Weeds: Unwanted plants competing with crops. (c) Plant pathogens: Fungi, bacteria, viruses causing plant diseases. (d) Vertebrates: Rodents (rats, mice), birds, bats. (2) Methods: (a) Physical control: Traps (sticky traps, pheromone traps, light traps), barriers (nets, fences, collars), manual removal (hand-pulling weeds, crushing insects), tillage, flooding, heat treatment (soil solarization). (b) Chemical control: Pesticides (insecticides, herbicides, fungicides, rodenticides). Advantages: Effective, fast-acting, easy to apply. Disadvantages: Resistance evolves (pests become immune), non-target effects (kill beneficial insects, birds, fish), environmental pollution (water, soil), bioaccumulation in food chains, human health risks. (c) Biological control: Use natural enemies (predators, parasites, pathogens). Advantages: Specific (target only pest), sustainable (self-perpetuating), no pollution, resistance less likely. Disadvantages: Slower, unpredictable, may become pest itself (if not specific). Examples: Ladybugs eat aphids; Bacillus thuringiensis (Bt) bacteria kill caterpillars; parasitic wasps lay eggs in pest insects; mycorrhizae protect plants from pathogens. (d) Cultural control: Agricultural practices that reduce pests. Crop rotation (prevents pest buildup), intercropping (diversity confuses pests), resistant crop varieties (bred for resistance), sanitation (remove crop residues), timing (plant early to avoid pest). (e) Genetic control: Sterile insect technique (release sterile males to mate, reducing population). Genetically modified crops (Bt corn, cotton produce insecticidal protein). (f) Integrated Pest Management (IPM): Combines multiple methods for effective, environmentally sensitive control. Principles: Monitor pest populations, use economic thresholds (only act when damage exceeds cost), prefer biological and cultural methods, use chemicals only when necessary, rotate methods to prevent resistance.', 'pest control, manage organisms, insects, weeds, pathogens, rodents, crop damage, disease transmission, economic loss, pests, insects, locusts, aphids, boll weevils, mosquitoes, termites, weeds, unwanted plants, crop competition, plant pathogens, fungi, bacteria, viruses, plant diseases, vertebrates, rodents, rats, mice, birds, bats, physical control, traps, sticky traps, pheromone traps, light traps, barriers, nets, fences, collars, manual removal, hand-pulling weeds, crushing insects, tillage, flooding, heat treatment, soil solarization, chemical control, pesticides, insecticides, herbicides, fungicides, rodenticides, advantages, effective, fast-acting, easy to apply, disadvantages, resistance evolves, pests immune, non-target effects, kill beneficial insects, birds, fish, environmental pollution, water, soil, bioaccumulation, food chains, human health risks, biological control, natural enemies, predators, parasites, pathogens, advantages, specific, target only pest, sustainable, self-perpetuating, no pollution, resistance less likely, disadvantages, slower, unpredictable, may become pest, not specific, examples, ladybugs eat aphids, Bacillus thuringiensis, Bt, bacteria kill caterpillars, parasitic wasps, lay eggs in pest insects, mycorrhizae protect plants, pathogens, cultural control, agricultural practices, reduce pests, crop rotation, prevent pest buildup, intercropping, diversity confuses pests, resistant crop varieties, bred for resistance, sanitation, remove crop residues, timing, plant early avoid pest, genetic control, sterile insect technique, release sterile males, mate, reduce population, genetically modified crops, Bt corn, Bt cotton, insecticidal protein, integrated pest management, IPM, combine multiple methods, environmentally sensitive, monitor pest populations, economic thresholds, only act when damage exceeds cost, prefer biological methods, prefer cultural methods, use chemicals only when necessary, rotate methods, prevent resistance', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(169, '25. MICROORGANISMS AND HEALTH', 'Carriers of Microorganisms', 'Carriers (vectors) are organisms that transmit pathogens without being affected themselves. They play crucial roles in disease transmission. (1) Housefly (Musca domestica): (a) Mechanism: Carries pathogens on feet, mouthparts, body hairs; regurgitates on food; defecates on surfaces. (b) Diseases transmitted: Typhoid (Salmonella typhi), cholera (Vibrio cholerae), dysentery (Shigella, Entamoeba histolytica), gastroenteritis (E. coli), trachoma (Chlamydia trachomatis). (c) Control: Sanitation (cover food, dispose waste), insecticides, fly screens. (2) Mosquito: (a) Anopheles: Transmits malaria (Plasmodium). Female Anopheles requires blood meal for egg development. Plasmodium completes part of life cycle in mosquito, transmitted through saliva. (b) Aedes: Transmits yellow fever, dengue, chikungunya, Zika virus. Day-biting mosquitoes. (c) Culex: Transmits West Nile virus, filariasis. (3) Tsetse fly (Glossina): Transmits sleeping sickness (African trypanosomiasis) caused by Trypanosoma brucei. Found only in Africa. (4) Rat flea (Xenopsylla cheopis): Transmits plague (Yersinia pestis). Fleas acquire bacteria from infected rats, transmit to humans through bite. (5) Lice: Transmit typhus (Rickettsia prowazekii), relapsing fever (Borrelia recurrentis). (6) Ticks: Transmit Lyme disease (Borrelia burgdorferi), Rocky Mountain spotted fever (Rickettsia rickettsii). (7) Triatomine bugs (kissing bugs): Transmit Chagas disease (Trypanosoma cruzi). (8) Water: Not a carrier but vehicle for waterborne diseases (cholera, typhoid) through contaminated drinking water. (9) Food: Vehicle for foodborne diseases (salmonellosis, botulism). Control measures: (a) Vector control: Insecticides, bed nets (mosquitoes), draining breeding sites. (b) Sanitation: Clean water, proper waste disposal, food hygiene. (c) Personal protection: Repellents, protective clothing. (d) Vaccination: Where available (yellow fever).', 'microorganism carriers, vectors, transmit pathogens, unaffected, disease transmission, housefly, Musca domestica, pathogens on feet, mouthparts, body hairs, regurgitates on food, defecates on surfaces, typhoid, Salmonella typhi, cholera, Vibrio cholerae, dysentery, Shigella, Entamoeba histolytica, gastroenteritis, E. coli, trachoma, Chlamydia trachomatis, control, sanitation, cover food, dispose waste, insecticides, fly screens, mosquito, Anopheles, malaria, Plasmodium, blood meal, egg development, life cycle, transmitted through saliva, Aedes, yellow fever, dengue, chikungunya, Zika virus, day-biting, Culex, West Nile virus, filariasis, tsetse fly, Glossina, sleeping sickness, African trypanosomiasis, Trypanosoma brucei, Africa, rat flea, Xenopsylla cheopis, plague, Yersinia pestis, infected rats, bite, lice, typhus, Rickettsia prowazekii, relapsing fever, Borrelia recurrentis, ticks, Lyme disease, Borrelia burgdorferi, Rocky Mountain spotted fever, Rickettsia rickettsii, triatomine bugs, kissing bugs, Chagas disease, Trypanosoma cruzi, water, vehicle, waterborne diseases, contaminated drinking water, food, vehicle, foodborne diseases, salmonellosis, botulism, control measures, vector control, insecticides, bed nets, draining breeding sites, sanitation, clean water, proper waste disposal, food hygiene, personal protection, repellents, protective clothing, vaccination, yellow fever', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(170, '25. MICROORGANISMS AND HEALTH', 'Beneficial Effects of Microorganisms', 'Microorganisms play essential beneficial roles in nature, industry, medicine, and human life. (1) In nature: (a) Decomposition: Bacteria and fungi break down dead organic matter, releasing nutrients (C, N, P) for reuse by plants. Essential for nutrient cycling. (b) Nitrogen fixation: Rhizobium bacteria in root nodules of legumes convert atmospheric N₂ to ammonia (NH₃), usable by plants. Free-living bacteria (Azotobacter, Clostridium) and cyanobacteria (Anabaena) also fix nitrogen. (c) Nutrient cycling: Bacteria mediate carbon (decomposition), nitrogen (fixation, nitrification, denitrification), sulfur, phosphorus cycles. (d) Symbiotic relationships: Mycorrhizal fungi enhance plant nutrient uptake; gut microbes in animals aid digestion (cellulose breakdown in ruminants, termites). (2) In medicine: (a) Antibiotics: Produced by microorganisms. Penicillin from Penicillium fungus; streptomycin, tetracycline from Streptomyces bacteria; cephalosporins from Cephalosporium fungus. (b) Vaccines: Some vaccines produced using microorganisms (e.g., BCG vaccine for tuberculosis uses attenuated Mycobacterium bovis). (c) Probiotics: Beneficial bacteria (Lactobacillus, Bifidobacterium) in yogurt, supplements promote gut health. (d) Medical research: E. coli, yeast used as model organisms for genetics, molecular biology. (e) Insulin production: Genetically modified E. coli produce human insulin for diabetes treatment. (f) Vitamin production: Bacteria in gut produce vitamin K, B vitamins. (3) In industry: (a) Food production: Fermentation - yeast (Saccharomyces) for bread, beer, wine; lactic acid bacteria (Lactobacillus) for yogurt, cheese, pickles, sauerkraut; Acetobacter for vinegar; molds for soy sauce, tempeh. (b) Dairy industry: Cheese (bacteria and molds), yogurt. (c) Baking industry: Yeast leavens bread. (d) Alcoholic beverages: Yeast ferments sugars to ethanol. (e) Industrial chemicals: Acetone, butanol produced by bacterial fermentation. (f) Enzymes: Microbial enzymes (proteases, lipases, amylases) used in detergents, food processing, textiles. (g) Waste treatment: Microbes in sewage treatment plants break down organic waste. (h) Bioremediation: Microbes used to clean up oil spills, toxic waste. (i) Mining: Bacteria (Thiobacillus) leach metals from ores (bioleaching). (j) Biofuels: Yeast produces ethanol; bacteria produce methane (biogas).', 'beneficial microorganisms, nature roles, decomposition, bacteria, fungi, break down dead organic matter, nutrient release, C, N, P, nutrient cycling, nitrogen fixation, Rhizobium, root nodules, legumes, atmospheric N₂, ammonia, NH₃, plant usable, free-living bacteria, Azotobacter, Clostridium, cyanobacteria, Anabaena, carbon cycle, nitrogen cycle, nitrification, denitrification, sulfur cycle, phosphorus cycle, symbiotic relationships, mycorrhizal fungi, plant nutrient uptake, gut microbes, animal digestion, cellulose breakdown, ruminants, termites, medicine, antibiotics, penicillin, Penicillium, streptomycin, tetracycline, Streptomyces, cephalosporins, Cephalosporium, vaccines, BCG vaccine, tuberculosis, attenuated Mycobacterium bovis, probiotics, Lactobacillus, Bifidobacterium, yogurt, supplements, gut health, medical research, E. coli, yeast, model organisms, genetics, molecular biology, insulin production, genetically modified E. coli, human insulin, diabetes, vitamin production, gut bacteria, vitamin K, B vitamins, industry, food production, fermentation, Saccharomyces, yeast, bread, beer, wine, lactic acid bacteria, Lactobacillus, cheese, pickles, sauerkraut, Acetobacter, vinegar, molds, soy sauce, tempeh, dairy industry, cheese, yogurt, baking industry, yeast leavens bread, alcoholic beverages, ethanol production, industrial chemicals, acetone, butanol, fermentation, microbial enzymes, proteases, lipases, amylases, detergents, food processing, textiles, waste treatment, sewage treatment, organic waste breakdown, bioremediation, oil spills cleanup, toxic waste cleanup, mining, Thiobacillus, bioleaching, metals from ores, biofuels, ethanol, yeast, methane, biogas, bacteria', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(171, '25. MICROORGANISMS AND HEALTH', 'Harmful Effects of Microorganisms', 'Microorganisms cause diseases in humans, animals, and plants, and spoil food. (1) Human diseases: (a) Bacterial diseases: Cholera (Vibrio cholerae - contaminated water/food, severe diarrhea, dehydration), typhoid (Salmonella typhi - contaminated food/water, fever, intestinal perforation), tuberculosis (Mycobacterium tuberculosis - airborne, lung infection), pneumonia (Streptococcus pneumoniae - airborne, lung infection), tetanus (Clostridium tetani - soil contamination of wounds, muscle spasms), diphtheria (Corynebacterium diphtheriae - airborne, throat infection, toxin), whooping cough (Bordetella pertussis - airborne, severe cough), meningitis (Neisseria meningitidis - airborne, brain/spinal cord inflammation), gonorrhea (Neisseria gonorrhoeae - sexual contact, genital infection), syphilis (Treponema pallidum - sexual contact, multi-stage), leprosy (Mycobacterium leprae - close contact, skin/nerve damage). (b) Viral diseases: Measles (paramyxovirus - airborne, rash, fever), mumps (paramyxovirus - airborne, swollen salivary glands), rubella (togavirus - airborne, rash), chickenpox (varicella-zoster virus - airborne, blister rash), influenza (orthomyxovirus - airborne, respiratory infection), common cold (rhinovirus - airborne, respiratory), hepatitis A, B, C (liver infection), HIV/AIDS (retrovirus - blood, sexual contact, immune deficiency), Ebola (filovirus - body fluids, hemorrhagic fever), COVID-19 (coronavirus - airborne, respiratory). (c) Protozoan diseases: Malaria (Plasmodium - mosquito vector, fever, chills), amoebic dysentery (Entamoeba histolytica - contaminated water, bloody diarrhea), sleeping sickness (Trypanosoma brucei - tsetse fly vector, neurological), leishmaniasis (Leishmania - sandfly vector, skin/organ damage), toxoplasmosis (Toxoplasma gondii - cat feces, undercooked meat, fetal damage). (d) Fungal diseases: Ringworm (Tinea - contact, skin lesions), athlete\'s foot (Tinea pedis - moist surfaces, foot), candidiasis (Candida albicans - opportunistic, thrush, vaginal yeast), histoplasmosis (Histoplasma capsulatum - airborne, lung infection). (2) Food spoilage: Bacteria and fungi grow on food, causing off-flavors, odors, texture changes, making food unpalatable and potentially toxic. (3) Food poisoning: (a) Intoxication: Bacteria produce toxins in food before consumption. Staphylococcus aureus (enterotoxin), Clostridium botulinum (botulism toxin - fatal paralysis), Bacillus cereus (emetic toxin). (b) Infection: Pathogens ingested, multiply in body. Salmonella, Campylobacter, E. coli O157:H7, Listeria. (4) Plant diseases: (a) Bacterial: Blights, wilts, rots. (b) Fungal: Rusts, smuts, mildews, wilts (e.g., potato blight caused Irish famine). (c) Viral: Mosaic diseases, leaf curl. (5) Economic impact: Crop losses, livestock diseases, healthcare costs, lost productivity.', 'harmful microorganisms, human diseases, bacterial diseases, cholera, Vibrio cholerae, contaminated water, contaminated food, severe diarrhea, dehydration, typhoid, Salmonella typhi, fever, intestinal perforation, tuberculosis, Mycobacterium tuberculosis, airborne, lung infection, pneumonia, Streptococcus pneumoniae, tetanus, Clostridium tetani, soil contamination, wounds, muscle spasms, diphtheria, Corynebacterium diphtheriae, throat infection, toxin, whooping cough, Bordetella pertussis, severe cough, meningitis, Neisseria meningitidis, brain inflammation, spinal cord inflammation, gonorrhea, Neisseria gonorrhoeae, sexual contact, genital infection, syphilis, Treponema pallidum, multi-stage, leprosy, Mycobacterium leprae, close contact, skin damage, nerve damage, viral diseases, measles, paramyxovirus, rash, fever, mumps, swollen salivary glands, rubella, togavirus, chickenpox, varicella-zoster virus, blister rash, influenza, orthomyxovirus, respiratory infection, common cold, rhinovirus, hepatitis A, hepatitis B, hepatitis C, liver infection, HIV, AIDS, retrovirus, blood, sexual contact, immune deficiency, Ebola, filovirus, body fluids, hemorrhagic fever, COVID-19, coronavirus, airborne, respiratory, protozoan diseases, malaria, Plasmodium, mosquito vector, fever, chills, amoebic dysentery, Entamoeba histolytica, contaminated water, bloody diarrhea, sleeping sickness, Trypanosoma brucei, tsetse fly vector, neurological, leishmaniasis, Leishmania, sandfly vector, skin damage, organ damage, toxoplasmosis, Toxoplasma gondii, cat feces, undercooked meat, fetal damage, fungal diseases, ringworm, Tinea, contact, skin lesions, athlete\'s foot, Tinea pedis, moist surfaces, candidiasis, Candida albicans, opportunistic, thrush, vaginal yeast, histoplasmosis, Histoplasma capsulatum, airborne, lung infection, food spoilage, bacteria, fungi, off-flavors, odors, texture changes, unpalatable, toxic, food poisoning, intoxication, toxins in food, Staphylococcus aureus, enterotoxin, Clostridium botulinum, botulism toxin, fatal paralysis, Bacillus cereus, emetic toxin, infection, pathogens ingested, multiply in body, Salmonella, Campylobacter, E. coli O157:H7, Listeria, plant diseases, bacterial blights, bacterial wilts, bacterial rots, fungal rusts, fungal smuts, mildews, wilts, potato blight, Irish famine, viral mosaic diseases, leaf curl, economic impact, crop losses, livestock diseases, healthcare costs, lost productivity', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(172, '25. MICROORGANISMS AND HEALTH', 'Controlling Harmful Microorganisms', 'Various methods control or destroy harmful microorganisms, essential for public health, food safety, and infection control. (1) Physical methods: (a) High temperature: Sterilization (autoclave: 121°C, 15 psi, 15-20 minutes) kills all microorganisms including spores. Pasteurization (72°C, 15 seconds) kills pathogens, reduces spoilage organisms (milk). Boiling (100°C) kills most vegetative cells. (b) Low temperature: Refrigeration (4°C) slows microbial growth. Freezing (-18°C) stops growth but doesn\'t kill. (c) Radiation: UV light (germicidal lamps) used for surface sterilization, air disinfection. Gamma radiation (cobalt-60) used for medical equipment, food irradiation (spices, some meats). (d) Filtration: Removes microorganisms from liquids or air (membrane filters, HEPA filters). (e) Drying (desiccation): Removes water needed for growth. (f) Osmotic pressure: High salt or sugar concentrations cause plasmolysis (water loss). (2) Chemical methods: (a) Antiseptics: Safe for living tissue. Examples: alcohol (70% ethanol, isopropanol), iodine (tincture, povidone-iodine), hydrogen peroxide, chlorhexidine. Used for skin disinfection before surgery, hand sanitizers. (b) Disinfectants: Kill microbes on inanimate surfaces (not safe for living tissue). Examples: bleach (sodium hypochlorite), formaldehyde, glutaraldehyde, quaternary ammonium compounds, phenolics. Used for surfaces, instruments. (c) Sterilants: Destroy all microbes including spores. Examples: ethylene oxide gas (for heat-sensitive equipment), hydrogen peroxide plasma, peracetic acid. (d) Preservatives: Added to foods to prevent spoilage. Examples: salt, sugar, vinegar, citric acid, sodium benzoate, potassium sorbate, sulfites. (3) Antibiotics: Chemicals produced by microorganisms (or synthetic) that kill or inhibit bacteria. (a) Bactericidal: Kill bacteria (penicillins, cephalosporins, aminoglycosides). (b) Bacteriostatic: Inhibit growth (tetracyclines, sulfonamides). (c) Spectrum: Broad-spectrum (affect many bacteria types), narrow-spectrum (specific). (d) Antibiotic resistance: Bacteria evolve resistance through mutation, gene transfer (plasmids). Overuse and misuse accelerate resistance. (e) Antibiotic stewardship: Using antibiotics only when necessary, completing courses, preventing resistance. (4) Antifungals: Treat fungal infections (e.g., clotrimazole for athlete\'s foot, amphotericin B for systemic infections). (5) Antivirals: Treat viral infections (e.g., acyclovir for herpes, oseltamivir for influenza, antiretrovirals for HIV). (6) Public health measures: (a) Sanitation: Clean water, proper sewage treatment, food hygiene. (b) Hand washing: Single most effective infection control measure. (c) Vaccination: Prevents many viral and bacterial diseases. (d) Quarantine: Isolating infected individuals. (e) Personal protective equipment: Masks, gloves, gowns. (f) Surveillance: Monitoring disease outbreaks.', 'controlling harmful microorganisms, physical methods, high temperature, sterilization, autoclave, 121°C, 15 psi, 15-20 minutes, kills all microorganisms, spores, pasteurization, 72°C, 15 seconds, kills pathogens, reduces spoilage organisms, milk, boiling, 100°C, kills most vegetative cells, low temperature, refrigeration, 4°C, slows microbial growth, freezing, -18°C, stops growth, doesn\'t kill, radiation, UV light, germicidal lamps, surface sterilization, air disinfection, gamma radiation, cobalt-60, medical equipment, food irradiation, spices, meat, filtration, removes microorganisms, liquids, air, membrane filters, HEPA filters, drying, desiccation, removes water, osmotic pressure, high salt concentration, high sugar concentration, plasmolysis, water loss, chemical methods, antiseptics, safe for living tissue, alcohol, 70% ethanol, isopropanol, iodine, tincture, povidone-iodine, hydrogen peroxide, chlorhexidine, skin disinfection, surgery, hand sanitizers, disinfectants, kill microbes on inanimate surfaces, not safe for living tissue, bleach, sodium hypochlorite, formaldehyde, glutaraldehyde, quaternary ammonium compounds, phenolics, surfaces, instruments, sterilants, destroy all microbes including spores, ethylene oxide gas, heat-sensitive equipment, hydrogen peroxide plasma, peracetic acid, preservatives, added to foods, prevent spoilage, salt, sugar, vinegar, citric acid, sodium benzoate, potassium sorbate, sulfites, antibiotics, chemicals produced by microorganisms, kill bacteria, inhibit bacteria, bactericidal, kill bacteria, penicillins, cephalosporins, aminoglycosides, bacteriostatic, inhibit growth, tetracyclines, sulfonamides, broad-spectrum, affect many bacteria types, narrow-spectrum, specific, antibiotic resistance, bacteria evolve resistance, mutation, gene transfer, plasmids, overuse, misuse, accelerate resistance, antibiotic stewardship, using only when necessary, completing courses, prevent resistance, antifungals, treat fungal infections, clotrimazole, athlete\'s foot, amphotericin B, systemic infections, antivirals, treat viral infections, acyclovir, herpes, oseltamivir, influenza, antiretrovirals, HIV, public health measures, sanitation, clean water, proper sewage treatment, food hygiene, hand washing, most effective infection control, vaccination, prevents viral diseases, prevents bacterial diseases, quarantine, isolating infected individuals, personal protective equipment, masks, gloves, gowns, surveillance, monitoring disease outbreaks', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(173, '25. MICROORGANISMS AND HEALTH', 'Immunization', 'Immunization is the process of making an individual immune to an infectious disease, typically through vaccination. It is one of the most effective public health interventions. (1) Active immunity: Body produces its own antibodies in response to antigen exposure. Long-lasting (years to lifetime). (a) Natural active immunity: Exposure to pathogen through infection. Body develops memory cells. Example: Recovering from chickenpox provides lifelong immunity. (b) Artificial active immunity: Vaccination with weakened/killed pathogens, inactivated toxins (toxoids), or antigen fragments. Examples: MMR vaccine (live attenuated measles, mumps, rubella); polio vaccine (inactivated or oral live attenuated); DTaP (diphtheria and tetanus toxoids, acellular pertussis); hepatitis B vaccine (recombinant antigen). (2) Passive immunity: Individual receives pre-formed antibodies from another source. Immediate protection but short-lived (weeks to months). No memory cells formed. (a) Natural passive immunity: Maternal antibodies passed to fetus through placenta (IgG) or to infant through breast milk (IgA). Protects newborn during first months. (b) Artificial passive immunity: Injection of antibodies (immunoglobulins, antiserum). Used for immediate protection after exposure (rabies, tetanus, hepatitis B) or for immunocompromised individuals. Examples: Rabies immune globulin, tetanus antitoxin. (3) Herd immunity (community immunity): When high proportion of population is immune (through vaccination or previous infection), pathogen transmission is interrupted, protecting vulnerable individuals who cannot be vaccinated (infants, immunocompromised, elderly). Threshold varies by disease (measles ~95%, polio ~80%). (4) Vaccination schedule: Recommended timing for vaccines to provide optimal protection. Childhood vaccines: BCG (tuberculosis) at birth; hepatitis B at birth, 1, 6 months; DTaP, polio, Hib at 2,4,6 months; MMR at 12-15 months; varicella at 12-18 months; boosters throughout childhood. (5) Types of vaccines: (a) Live attenuated: Weakened live pathogen (MMR, chickenpox, oral polio). Strong immune response, but risk for immunocompromised. (b) Inactivated (killed): Whole killed pathogen (injected polio, hepatitis A). Safer but may need boosters. (c) Toxoid: Inactivated toxin (diphtheria, tetanus). (d) Subunit/recombinant: Antigen fragments (hepatitis B, HPV). (e) mRNA: Genetic material encoding antigen (COVID-19 vaccines). (f) Viral vector: Modified virus carrying antigen gene (Ebola, some COVID-19). (6) Importance of immunization: (a) Prevents disease: Smallpox eradicated, polio nearly eradicated. (b) Saves lives: Millions of deaths prevented annually. (c) Cost-effective: Prevention cheaper than treatment. (d) Protects communities: Herd immunity protects vulnerable.', 'immunization, immune to infectious disease, vaccination, public health interventions, active immunity, body produces antibodies, antigen exposure, long-lasting, years to lifetime, natural active immunity, exposure to pathogen, infection, memory cells, chickenpox, lifelong immunity, artificial active immunity, vaccination, weakened pathogens, killed pathogens, inactivated toxins, toxoids, antigen fragments, MMR vaccine, live attenuated measles, live attenuated mumps, live attenuated rubella, polio vaccine, inactivated polio, oral polio, live attenuated, DTaP, diphtheria toxoid, tetanus toxoid, acellular pertussis, hepatitis B vaccine, recombinant antigen, passive immunity, pre-formed antibodies, immediate protection, short-lived, weeks to months, no memory cells, natural passive immunity, maternal antibodies, placenta, IgG, breast milk, IgA, protects newborn, artificial passive immunity, injection of antibodies, immunoglobulins, antiserum, immediate protection after exposure, rabies, tetanus, hepatitis B, immunocompromised, rabies immune globulin, tetanus antitoxin, herd immunity, community immunity, high proportion immune, pathogen transmission interrupted, protects vulnerable, cannot be vaccinated, infants, immunocompromised, elderly, threshold, measles ~95%, polio ~80%, vaccination schedule, recommended timing, optimal protection, childhood vaccines, BCG, tuberculosis, at birth, hepatitis B, birth, 1 month, 6 months, DTaP, polio, Hib, 2 months, 4 months, 6 months, MMR, 12-15 months, varicella, 12-18 months, boosters, types of vaccines, live attenuated vaccines, weakened live pathogen, MMR, chickenpox, oral polio, strong immune response, risk for immunocompromised, inactivated vaccines, killed pathogen, injected polio, hepatitis A, safer, need boosters, toxoid vaccines, inactivated toxin, diphtheria, tetanus, subunit vaccines, recombinant vaccines, antigen fragments, hepatitis B, HPV, mRNA vaccines, genetic material encoding antigen, COVID-19 vaccines, viral vector vaccines, modified virus carrying antigen gene, Ebola vaccines, COVID-19 vaccines, importance of immunization, prevents disease, smallpox eradicated, polio nearly eradicated, saves lives, millions of deaths prevented, cost-effective, prevention cheaper than treatment, protects communities, herd immunity protects vulnerable', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(174, '26. CONSERVATION OF NATURAL RESOURCES', 'Resources to Conserve', 'Natural resources are materials or substances occurring in nature that are valuable to humans. Conservation is sustainable management to prevent depletion and maintain ecosystem services. (1) Soil: (a) Importance: Supports plant growth (food, fiber), filters water, stores carbon, provides habitat for organisms. (b) Threats: Erosion (water, wind), deforestation, overgrazing, intensive agriculture (loss of organic matter, salinization, contamination). (c) Conservation: Contour plowing, terracing, crop rotation, cover crops, no-till farming, windbreaks, reforestation, organic matter addition (compost, manure). (2) Water: (a) Importance: Drinking, sanitation, agriculture, industry, ecosystem health. (b) Threats: Overuse (depleting aquifers, rivers drying), pollution (industrial, agricultural, sewage), climate change (altered rainfall patterns). (c) Conservation: Reduce consumption (efficient irrigation, low-flow fixtures), rainwater harvesting, wastewater treatment, protect watersheds, prevent pollution, recharge groundwater. (3) Wildlife: (a) Importance: Biodiversity, ecosystem stability, genetic resources, aesthetic, cultural value. (b) Threats: Habitat loss (deforestation, urbanization), poaching (ivory, rhino horn, bushmeat), overexploitation (fishing, hunting), pollution, climate change, invasive species. (c) Conservation: Protected areas (national parks, game reserves, wildlife sanctuaries), wildlife corridors, anti-poaching laws, captive breeding, reintroduction programs, CITES (Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species). (4) Forest: (a) Importance: Timber, fuel, non-timber products, watershed protection, carbon sequestration, climate regulation, habitat, recreation. (b) Threats: Deforestation (logging, agriculture clearance, mining), forest degradation, fire, pests, climate change. (c) Conservation: Sustainable logging (selective cutting, reduced-impact logging), reforestation, afforestation, protected areas, community forestry, certification (FSC - Forest Stewardship Council). (5) Minerals: (a) Importance: Metals, fuels, construction materials, industrial raw materials. (b) Threats: Depletion (finite resources), environmental damage from mining (habitat destruction, pollution). (c) Conservation: Reduce consumption (efficiency, substitution), recycling, reuse, responsible mining practices (rehabilitation, pollution control). (6) Air: (a) Importance: Breathable air, climate regulation. (b) Threats: Pollution (vehicle emissions, industry, burning), greenhouse gases (climate change). (c) Conservation: Reduce emissions (clean energy, public transport, industrial controls), protect ozone layer (phase out CFCs).', 'conservation, natural resources, sustainable management, prevent depletion, ecosystem services, soil conservation, soil importance, plant growth, food, fiber, water filtration, carbon storage, habitat, soil threats, erosion, water erosion, wind erosion, deforestation, overgrazing, intensive agriculture, organic matter loss, salinization, contamination, soil conservation methods, contour plowing, terracing, crop rotation, cover crops, no-till farming, windbreaks, reforestation, organic matter addition, compost, manure, water conservation, water importance, drinking, sanitation, agriculture, industry, ecosystem health, water threats, overuse, depleting aquifers, rivers drying, pollution, industrial pollution, agricultural pollution, sewage pollution, climate change, altered rainfall patterns, water conservation methods, reduce consumption, efficient irrigation, low-flow fixtures, rainwater harvesting, wastewater treatment, protect watersheds, prevent pollution, recharge groundwater, wildlife conservation, wildlife importance, biodiversity, ecosystem stability, genetic resources, aesthetic value, cultural value, wildlife threats, habitat loss, deforestation, urbanization, poaching, ivory, rhino horn, bushmeat, overexploitation, fishing, hunting, pollution, climate change, invasive species, wildlife conservation methods, protected areas, national parks, game reserves, wildlife sanctuaries, wildlife corridors, anti-poaching laws, captive breeding, reintroduction programs, CITES, Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species, forest conservation, forest importance, timber, fuel, non-timber products, watershed protection, carbon sequestration, climate regulation, habitat, recreation, forest threats, deforestation, logging, agriculture clearance, mining, forest degradation, fire, pests, climate change, forest conservation methods, sustainable logging, selective cutting, reduced-impact logging, reforestation, afforestation, protected areas, community forestry, FSC certification, Forest Stewardship Council, mineral conservation, mineral importance, metals, fuels, construction materials, industrial raw materials, mineral threats, depletion, finite resources, environmental damage, mining, habitat destruction, pollution, mineral conservation methods, reduce consumption, efficiency, substitution, recycling, reuse, responsible mining practices, rehabilitation, pollution control, air conservation, air importance, breathable air, climate regulation, air threats, pollution, vehicle emissions, industry emissions, burning, greenhouse gases, climate change, air conservation methods, reduce emissions, clean energy, public transport, industrial controls, protect ozone layer, phase out CFCs', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(175, '26. CONSERVATION OF NATURAL RESOURCES', 'Ways of Ensuring Conservation', 'Effective conservation requires coordinated efforts at individual, community, national, and international levels. (1) Agencies responsible for conservation: (a) Government ministries: Environment, forestry, agriculture, wildlife, water resources - develop policies, enforce laws, manage protected areas. (b) Environmental protection agencies (EPA): Monitor pollution, enforce regulations, conduct environmental impact assessments. (c) Forestry departments: Manage forests, control logging, reforestation programs. (d) Wildlife departments: Manage protected areas, anti-poaching, wildlife research. (e) Water resources authorities: Manage water allocation, water quality monitoring. (f) International organizations: UNEP (United Nations Environment Programme), IUCN (International Union for Conservation of Nature), WWF (World Wildlife Fund), FAO (Food and Agriculture Organization) - coordinate international efforts, provide technical assistance, funding. (g) Non-governmental organizations (NGOs): Conservation International, Greenpeace, local conservation groups - advocacy, education, on-the-ground projects. (2) Conservation education: (a) Awareness campaigns: Media (TV, radio, social media), posters, workshops, seminars. (b) School programs: Environmental education in curriculum, eco-clubs, field trips to protected areas. (c) Community outreach: Engage local communities in conservation, demonstrate benefits (ecotourism revenue, sustainable resource use). (d) Training programs: For park rangers, foresters, extension workers. (e) Public information: Websites, brochures, visitor centers in parks. (3) Conservation laws and policies: (a) Protected areas legislation: Establish national parks, game reserves, forest reserves, marine protected areas. Regulate activities within them. (b) Hunting regulations: Seasons, licenses, bag limits, protected species. (c) Fishing regulations: Quotas, size limits, closed seasons, gear restrictions. (d) Pollution controls: Emission standards, water quality standards, waste disposal regulations. (e) Environmental impact assessment (EIA): Required for major development projects. (f) Endangered species protection: Laws against poaching, trade (CITES). (g) International agreements: Convention on Biological Diversity, Ramsar Convention (wetlands), CITES, Paris Agreement (climate change). (4) Benefits of conservation: (a) Sustainable resource use: Resources available for future generations. (b) Biodiversity preservation: Protects species and ecosystems. (c) Climate regulation: Forests store carbon, regulate local climate. (d) Water regulation: Forests and wetlands regulate water flow, reduce flooding. (e) Economic benefits: Tourism, sustainable harvesting, ecosystem services (pollination, water purification). (f) Cultural and aesthetic value: Recreation, spiritual value, heritage. (g) Scientific value: Genetic resources for medicine, research.', 'conservation agencies, government ministries, environment ministry, forestry ministry, agriculture ministry, wildlife ministry, water resources ministry, develop policies, enforce laws, manage protected areas, environmental protection agencies, EPA, monitor pollution, enforce regulations, environmental impact assessments, forestry departments, manage forests, control logging, reforestation programs, wildlife departments, manage protected areas, anti-poaching, wildlife research, water resources authorities, manage water allocation, water quality monitoring, international organizations, UNEP, United Nations Environment Programme, IUCN, International Union for Conservation of Nature, WWF, World Wildlife Fund, FAO, Food and Agriculture Organization, coordinate international efforts, technical assistance, funding, non-governmental organizations, NGOs, Conservation International, Greenpeace, local conservation groups, advocacy, education, on-the-ground projects, conservation education, awareness campaigns, media, TV, radio, social media, posters, workshops, seminars, school programs, environmental education, curriculum, eco-clubs, field trips, protected areas, community outreach, engage local communities, demonstrate benefits, ecotourism revenue, sustainable resource use, training programs, park rangers, foresters, extension workers, public information, websites, brochures, visitor centers, conservation laws, policies, protected areas legislation, national parks, game reserves, forest reserves, marine protected areas, regulate activities, hunting regulations, seasons, licenses, bag limits, protected species, fishing regulations, quotas, size limits, closed seasons, gear restrictions, pollution controls, emission standards, water quality standards, waste disposal regulations, environmental impact assessment, EIA, major development projects, endangered species protection, laws against poaching, trade, CITES, Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species, international agreements, Convention on Biological Diversity, Ramsar Convention, wetlands, Paris Agreement, climate change, benefits of conservation, sustainable resource use, resources for future generations, biodiversity preservation, protect species, protect ecosystems, climate regulation, forests store carbon, regulate local climate, water regulation, forests regulate water flow, wetlands regulate water flow, reduce flooding, economic benefits, tourism, sustainable harvesting, ecosystem services, pollination, water purification, cultural value, aesthetic value, recreation, spiritual value, heritage, scientific value, genetic resources, medicine, research', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(176, '26. CONSERVATION OF NATURAL RESOURCES', 'Problems of Conservation', 'Conservation faces numerous challenges that hinder effective protection of natural resources. (1) Economic development vs. conservation conflict: (a) Development projects: Dams flood forests, mines destroy habitats, roads fragment ecosystems, urban expansion consumes land. (b) Economic pressure: Short-term economic gains often prioritized over long-term sustainability. (c) Poverty: Poor communities may overexploit resources for survival (slash-and-burn agriculture, poaching). (2) Overpopulation: Growing human population increases demand for resources (food, water, fuel, land), putting pressure on ecosystems. (3) Poaching: Illegal hunting of endangered species for valuable products: ivory (elephants), rhino horn, bushmeat (apes, antelopes), skins (tigers, leopards), traditional medicine (tiger bones, bear bile). Driven by poverty, organized crime, high prices. (4) Overgrazing: Livestock (cattle, goats, sheep) exceeding carrying capacity destroys vegetation, causes soil erosion, desertification. Common in semi-arid regions. (5) Deforestation: Clearing forests for agriculture (cattle ranching, soy, palm oil), logging (legal and illegal), mining, fuelwood. Causes habitat loss, biodiversity decline, climate change, soil erosion. (6) Pollution: (a) Air pollution: Acid rain damages forests; greenhouse gases cause climate change. (b) Water pollution: Industrial waste, agricultural runoff, sewage harm aquatic life, contaminate drinking water. (c) Soil pollution: Industrial waste, pesticides, oil spills reduce fertility, contaminate food chain. (7) Climate change: Alters habitats, shifts species ranges, increases extreme events (droughts, floods, fires), threatens ecosystems (coral bleaching, polar ice melt). (8) Inadequate funding: Conservation programs underfunded; protected areas lack staff, equipment, infrastructure. (9) Weak enforcement: Laws exist but poorly enforced due to corruption, lack of resources, political will. (10) Lack of awareness: Communities may not understand importance of conservation or sustainable practices. (11) Invasive species: Non-native species outcompete native species, alter ecosystems. Introduced accidentally or intentionally. (12) Political instability: War, conflict disrupt conservation efforts, increase poaching, destroy protected areas. (13) Corruption: Officials bribed to ignore illegal logging, poaching, land encroachment.', 'conservation problems, challenges, economic development conflict, development projects, dams flood forests, mines destroy habitats, roads fragment ecosystems, urban expansion consumes land, economic pressure, short-term economic gains, long-term sustainability, poverty, poor communities overexploit resources, slash-and-burn agriculture, poaching, overpopulation, growing human population, increased resource demand, food demand, water demand, fuel demand, land demand, pressure on ecosystems, poaching, illegal hunting, endangered species, ivory, elephants, rhino horn, bushmeat, apes, antelopes, skins, tigers, leopards, traditional medicine, tiger bones, bear bile, organized crime, high prices, overgrazing, livestock, cattle, goats, sheep, exceeding carrying capacity, vegetation destruction, soil erosion, desertification, semi-arid regions, deforestation, clearing forests, agriculture, cattle ranching, soy, palm oil, logging, legal logging, illegal logging, mining, fuelwood, habitat loss, biodiversity decline, climate change, soil erosion, pollution, air pollution, acid rain damages forests, greenhouse gases cause climate change, water pollution, industrial waste, agricultural runoff, sewage, harm aquatic life, contaminate drinking water, soil pollution, industrial waste, pesticides, oil spills, reduce fertility, contaminate food chain, climate change, alters habitats, shifts species ranges, extreme events, droughts, floods, fires, threatens ecosystems, coral bleaching, polar ice melt, inadequate funding, conservation programs underfunded, protected areas lack staff, lack equipment, lack infrastructure, weak enforcement, laws exist, poorly enforced, corruption, lack of resources, lack of political will, lack of awareness, communities not understand, conservation importance, sustainable practices, invasive species, non-native species, outcompete native species, alter ecosystems, introduced accidentally, introduced intentionally, political instability, war, conflict disrupt conservation, increase poaching, destroy protected areas, corruption, officials bribed, ignore illegal logging, ignore poaching, ignore land encroachment', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(177, '27. VARIATION IN POPULATION', 'Morphological Variations', 'Morphological variation refers to differences in the physical appearance and structure of individuals within a species. These variations arise from genetic differences, environmental influences, or their interaction. (1) Types of morphological variation: (a) Continuous variation: Individuals show a range of phenotypes with no distinct categories. Examples: height, weight, skin color, leaf size. Caused by many genes (polygenic) and environmental factors. Shows normal distribution (bell curve). (b) Discontinuous variation: Individuals fall into distinct categories with no intermediates. Examples: blood group (A, B, AB, O), ability to roll tongue, attached/free earlobes, fingerprint patterns (arches, loops, whorls). Caused by one or few genes, little environmental influence. (2) Examples of morphological variations: (a) Size: Height, weight, body proportions vary among individuals due to genetics and nutrition. (b) Skin color: Determined by melanin amount (polygenic, environmental UV exposure). (c) Hair color/texture: Black, brown, blond, red; straight, wavy, curly. (d) Eye color: Brown, blue, green, hazel - determined by melanin in iris. (e) Facial features: Nose shape, lip thickness, eye shape, chin prominence. (f) Fingerprints: Arches, loops, whorls, compounds - genetically determined, unique to each individual (forensic identification). (g) Color patterns in animals: Cow coat patterns (Holstein black-and-white patches), goat colors, rabbit fur colors. (h) Color patterns in plants: Maize cob color (yellow, white, red, purple), leaf variegation, flower color variations. (3) Importance of morphological variation: (a) Adaptation: Variation allows populations to adapt to changing environments (natural selection). (b) Taxonomy: Used to classify species. (c) Medicine: Understanding normal variation helps diagnose abnormalities. (d) Forensics: Fingerprints, DNA used for identification. (e) Agriculture: Breeders select desirable traits (size, color, yield). (4) Causes: (a) Genetic factors: Different alleles, gene combinations. (b) Environmental factors: Nutrition, climate, light, soil (plants). (c) Gene-environment interaction.', 'morphological variation, physical appearance differences, structure differences, individuals, species, continuous variation, range of phenotypes, no distinct categories, height, weight, skin color, leaf size, polygenic, many genes, environmental factors, normal distribution, bell curve, discontinuous variation, distinct categories, no intermediates, blood group, A, B, AB, O, tongue rolling ability, attached earlobes, free earlobes, fingerprint patterns, arches, loops, whorls, compounds, one gene, few genes, little environmental influence, size variation, height variation, weight variation, body proportions, genetics, nutrition, skin color, melanin, polygenic, UV exposure, hair color, black, brown, blond, red, hair texture, straight, wavy, curly, eye color, brown, blue, green, hazel, melanin in iris, facial features, nose shape, lip thickness, eye shape, chin prominence, fingerprints, arches, loops, whorls, compounds, genetically determined, unique, forensic identification, color patterns in animals, cow coat patterns, Holstein, black-and-white patches, goat colors, rabbit fur colors, color patterns in plants, maize cob color, yellow, white, red, purple, leaf variegation, flower color variations, importance of variation, adaptation, populations adapt, changing environments, natural selection, taxonomy, classify species, medicine, normal variation, diagnose abnormalities, forensics, fingerprints, DNA, identification, agriculture, breeders select desirable traits, size, color, yield, causes, genetic factors, different alleles, gene combinations, environmental factors, nutrition, climate, light, soil, gene-environment interaction', '2026-02-27 15:40:30');
INSERT INTO `biology_syllabus_comprehensive` (`id`, `topic`, `sub_topic`, `note`, `keywords`, `created_at`) VALUES
(178, '27. VARIATION IN POPULATION', 'Physiological Variations', 'Physiological variation refers to differences in the functioning of body systems and metabolic processes among individuals. These variations are often not visible externally but affect how individuals respond to stimuli, metabolize substances, or resist disease. (1) Examples of physiological variations: (a) Ability to roll tongue: About 65-80% of people can roll their tongue into a U-shape. Controlled by a dominant gene (Tongue-rolling is dominant, non-rolling recessive). (b) Ability to taste phenylthiocarbamide (PTC): PTC tastes bitter to some people (tasters), others cannot taste it (non-tasters). Controlled by a single gene (TAS2R38). Taster allele (T) dominant, non-taster (t) recessive. About 70% of people are tasters. Related to ability to taste other bitter compounds (cruciferous vegetables). (c) Blood groups (ABO system): Determined by multiple alleles (IA, IB, i). IA and IB are codominant, i is recessive. Four phenotypes: A (IAIA or IAi), B (IBIB or IBi), AB (IAIB), O (ii). Distribution varies by population. Importance for blood transfusions (compatibility), disease susceptibility (e.g., O more resistant to severe malaria?). (d) Rhesus (Rh) factor: Presence (Rh+) or absence (Rh-) of D antigen on red blood cells. Rh+ dominant, Rh- recessive. Important in pregnancy (Rh- mother with Rh+ fetus can cause hemolytic disease). (e) Sickle cell trait: Heterozygous carriers (HbAS) have some protection against malaria, while homozygous (HbSS) have sickle cell anemia. Balanced polymorphism in malaria regions. (f) Enzyme variations: Lactase persistence (ability to digest lactose in adulthood) common in populations with history of dairying; alcohol dehydrogenase variants affect alcohol metabolism. (g) Metabolic rate: Basal metabolic rate (BMR) varies among individuals, affecting energy needs, weight management. (h) Drug metabolism: Genetic variations in liver enzymes (cytochrome P450) affect how individuals metabolize drugs (efficacy, side effects). (2) Importance of physiological variations: (a) Medicine: Pharmacogenomics tailors drug treatments based on genetic variation. Blood typing essential for transfusions. (b) Anthropology: Population studies of genetic markers reveal human migration, evolution. (c) Forensics: DNA profiling uses physiological markers. (d) Nutrition: Understanding lactose intolerance, other food sensitivities. (e) Disease susceptibility: Genetic variations affect risk for many diseases (diabetes, cancer, heart disease). (3) Causes: (a) Genetic differences (alleles, gene variants). (b) Environmental influences (diet, training, altitude).', 'physiological variation, body functioning differences, metabolic process differences, not externally visible, stimuli response, substance metabolism, disease resistance, tongue rolling ability, 65-80% people, U-shape, dominant gene, tongue-rolling dominant, non-rolling recessive, PTC tasting, phenylthiocarbamide, bitter taste, tasters, non-tasters, single gene, TAS2R38, taster allele T dominant, non-taster t recessive, 70% tasters, bitter compounds, cruciferous vegetables, blood groups, ABO system, multiple alleles, IA, IB, i, codominant, recessive, phenotypes, A, B, AB, O, population distribution, blood transfusions compatibility, disease susceptibility, malaria resistance, Rhesus factor, Rh factor, D antigen, red blood cells, Rh+ dominant, Rh- recessive, pregnancy, hemolytic disease, Rh- mother, Rh+ fetus, sickle cell trait, heterozygous carriers, HbAS, malaria protection, homozygous, HbSS, sickle cell anemia, balanced polymorphism, malaria regions, enzyme variations, lactase persistence, lactose digestion, adulthood, dairying history, alcohol dehydrogenase variants, alcohol metabolism, metabolic rate, basal metabolic rate, BMR, energy needs, weight management, drug metabolism, genetic variations, liver enzymes, cytochrome P450, drug efficacy, drug side effects, pharmacogenomics, importance of physiological variations, medicine, tailored drug treatments, blood typing, transfusions, anthropology, population studies, genetic markers, human migration, human evolution, forensics, DNA profiling, nutrition, lactose intolerance, food sensitivities, disease susceptibility, genetic variations, diabetes, cancer, heart disease, causes, genetic differences, alleles, gene variants, environmental influences, diet, training, altitude', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(179, '27. VARIATION IN POPULATION', 'Continuous and Discontinuous Variation', 'Variation in populations can be classified into two main types based on how traits are distributed and inherited. (1) Continuous variation: (a) Characteristics: Traits show a range of values with no distinct categories. Individuals vary gradually between extremes. Distribution often follows a normal (bell-shaped) curve. (b) Examples: Height, weight, skin color, intelligence, blood pressure, leaf size in plants, milk yield in cattle. (c) Causes: Polygenic inheritance (many genes contribute to the trait, each with small effect). Environmental factors also influence expression (nutrition affects height, sun exposure affects skin color). (d) Measurement: Quantitative traits measured numerically. Mean, median, mode, range, standard deviation used to describe. (e) Graph: Histogram or frequency polygon shows smooth curve. (2) Discontinuous variation: (a) Characteristics: Traits fall into distinct categories with no intermediates. Individuals clearly belong to one group or another. (b) Examples: Blood group (A, B, AB, O), ability to roll tongue, attached/free earlobes, presence of dimples, gender (in most species), seed color in Mendel\'s peas (yellow or green). (c) Causes: Single gene or few genes with major effects. Little environmental influence (genotype determines phenotype). (d) Measurement: Qualitative traits described by categories. Frequencies of each category counted. (e) Graph: Bar chart or pie chart shows discrete categories. (3) Genetic basis: (a) Continuous variation: Multiple genes (polygenes) each contribute small additive effects. Example: Height influenced by many genes each adding a few mm. (b) Discontinuous variation: Single gene with two or more alleles. Example: ABO blood group determined by one gene with three alleles. (4) Environmental influence: (a) Continuous variation: Strongly influenced by environment (nutrition, climate, exercise). (b) Discontinuous variation: Little environmental influence; determined primarily by genotype. (5) Evolutionary significance: (a) Continuous variation provides raw material for natural selection (gradual change). (b) Discontinuous variation can lead to rapid evolutionary change when environment favors one discrete form. (6) Examples in human populations: (a) Continuous: Height, weight, IQ, blood pressure. (b) Discontinuous: Blood type, sex, ability to taste PTC, presence of freckles, widow\'s peak.', 'continuous variation, range of values, no distinct categories, gradual variation, normal distribution, bell curve, height, weight, skin color, intelligence, blood pressure, leaf size, milk yield, polygenic inheritance, many genes, small effect, environmental factors, nutrition, sun exposure, quantitative traits, numerical measurement, mean, median, mode, range, standard deviation, histogram, frequency polygon, smooth curve, discontinuous variation, distinct categories, no intermediates, clearly belong to one group, blood group, A, B, AB, O, tongue rolling, attached earlobes, free earlobes, dimples, gender, seed color, Mendel\'s peas, yellow, green, single gene, few genes, major effects, little environmental influence, genotype determines phenotype, qualitative traits, categories, frequencies, bar chart, pie chart, discrete categories, genetic basis, multiple genes, polygenes, additive effects, height genes, each adding few mm, single gene, two alleles, three alleles, environmental influence, strong environmental influence, nutrition, climate, exercise, little environmental influence, determined by genotype, evolutionary significance, raw material for natural selection, gradual change, rapid evolutionary change, environment favors one discrete form, examples in human populations, continuous, height, weight, IQ, blood pressure, discontinuous, blood type, sex, PTC tasting ability, freckles, widow\'s peak', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(180, '28. BIOLOGY OF HEREDITY', 'Genetic Terminologies', 'Genetics is the study of heredity and variation. Understanding basic terminology is essential. (1) Gene: A segment of DNA that codes for a specific protein or functional RNA. The basic unit of heredity. Located at a specific position (locus) on a chromosome. (2) Allele: Alternative forms of a gene. For example, the gene for flower color in peas has two alleles: purple (P) and white (p). Alleles arise through mutation. (3) Genotype: The genetic makeup of an individual; the combination of alleles present. Examples: PP (homozygous dominant), Pp (heterozygous), pp (homozygous recessive). (4) Phenotype: The observable characteristics of an individual, resulting from interaction of genotype with environment. Example: Purple flowers (from PP or Pp genotype). (5) Dominant allele: An allele that is expressed in the phenotype when present in either one or two copies. Masks the effect of the recessive allele in heterozygotes. Represented by capital letter (P). (6) Recessive allele: An allele that is only expressed in the phenotype when present in two copies (homozygous). Masked by dominant allele in heterozygotes. Represented by lowercase letter (p). (7) Homozygous: Having two identical alleles for a particular gene. Homozygous dominant (PP), homozygous recessive (pp). (8) Heterozygous: Having two different alleles for a particular gene (Pp). Also called hybrid. (9) Locus (plural loci): The specific position of a gene on a chromosome. (10) Test cross: Crossing an individual of unknown genotype (showing dominant phenotype) with a homozygous recessive individual to determine the unknown genotype. If any offspring show recessive trait, unknown parent was heterozygous. (11) Back cross: Crossing an F₁ individual with one of its parents (or genetically equivalent individual). (12) Monohybrid cross: A cross involving one trait (e.g., flower color). (13) Dihybrid cross: A cross involving two traits (e.g., seed color and seed shape). (14) Punnett square: A diagram used to predict the genotypes and phenotypes of offspring from a genetic cross. (15) Pedigree: A family tree showing inheritance of a trait over generations.', 'genetics, heredity, variation, gene, segment of DNA, codes for protein, functional RNA, unit of heredity, locus, loci, chromosome, allele, alternative forms, gene for flower color, purple allele, white allele, mutation, genotype, genetic makeup, combination of alleles, homozygous dominant, PP, heterozygous, Pp, homozygous recessive, pp, phenotype, observable characteristics, genotype-environment interaction, purple flowers, dominant allele, expressed phenotype, one copy, two copies, masks recessive, heterozygotes, capital letter, recessive allele, expressed only two copies, homozygous, masked by dominant, lowercase letter, homozygous, identical alleles, homozygous dominant, homozygous recessive, heterozygous, different alleles, hybrid, test cross, unknown genotype, dominant phenotype, homozygous recessive, determine unknown genotype, offspring show recessive trait, unknown parent heterozygous, back cross, F₁ individual, one parent, genetically equivalent, monohybrid cross, one trait, flower color, dihybrid cross, two traits, seed color, seed shape, Punnett square, diagram, predict genotypes, predict phenotypes, offspring, pedigree, family tree, inheritance over generations', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(181, '28. BIOLOGY OF HEREDITY', 'Mendel\'s Experiments', 'Gregor Mendel (1822-1884), an Austrian monk, conducted experiments with pea plants (Pisum sativum) that laid the foundation of modern genetics. (1) Reasons for choosing pea plants: (a) Easy to grow, short generation time. (b) Many distinct, easily observable traits (seed color, seed shape, flower color, pod color, pod shape, flower position, stem height). (c) Can self-pollinate or be cross-pollinated artificially. (d) Produces many offspring. (2) Monohybrid cross (one trait): Example: Flower color. (a) P generation (parental): Purebred purple flowers (PP) × purebred white flowers (pp). (b) F₁ generation (first filial): All plants had purple flowers (Pp). This showed purple is dominant over white. (c) F₁ self-pollinated: Pp × Pp. (d) F₂ generation: 3/4 purple, 1/4 white (phenotypic ratio 3:1). Genotypic ratio: 1 PP : 2 Pp : 1 pp. (e) Conclusion: Law of segregation - alleles separate during gamete formation, each gamete receives one allele. (3) Dihybrid cross (two traits): Example: Seed color (yellow Y dominant over green y) and seed shape (round R dominant over wrinkled r). (a) P generation: Yellow round (YYRR) × green wrinkled (yyrr). (b) F₁ generation: All yellow round (YyRr). (c) F₁ self-pollinated: YyRr × YyRr. (d) F₂ generation: 9 yellow round : 3 yellow wrinkled : 3 green round : 1 green wrinkled (9:3:3:1 ratio). (e) Conclusion: Law of independent assortment - alleles of different genes assort independently during gamete formation (for genes on different chromosomes). (4) Test cross: To determine if a purple-flowered plant was homozygous (PP) or heterozygous (Pp), Mendel crossed it with homozygous recessive (pp). If any white offspring appear, the purple parent was heterozygous. (5) Mendel\'s success factors: (a) Chose traits with clear alternatives. (b) Kept careful records, used large sample sizes. (c) Followed one or two traits at a time. (d) Used mathematics to analyze results.', 'Mendel, Gregor Mendel, pea plants, Pisum sativum, foundation of genetics, easy to grow, short generation time, distinct traits, observable traits, seed color, seed shape, flower color, pod color, pod shape, flower position, stem height, self-pollinate, cross-pollinate, many offspring, monohybrid cross, one trait, flower color, P generation, parental generation, purebred purple, PP, purebred white, pp, F₁ generation, first filial, all purple, Pp, dominant, recessive, F₁ self-pollinated, Pp × Pp, F₂ generation, second filial, 3/4 purple, 1/4 white, phenotypic ratio 3:1, genotypic ratio 1:2:1, PP, Pp, pp, law of segregation, alleles separate, gamete formation, each gamete receives one allele, dihybrid cross, two traits, seed color, yellow dominant Y, green recessive y, seed shape, round dominant R, wrinkled recessive r, P generation, yellow round YYRR, green wrinkled yyrr, F₁ generation, all yellow round YyRr, F₁ self-pollinated, YyRr × YyRr, F₂ generation, 9 yellow round, 3 yellow wrinkled, 3 green round, 1 green wrinkled, 9:3:3:1 ratio, law of independent assortment, alleles of different genes assort independently, gamete formation, genes on different chromosomes, test cross, determine genotype, homozygous PP, heterozygous Pp, cross with homozygous recessive pp, white offspring appear, purple parent heterozygous, Mendel\'s success factors, clear trait alternatives, careful records, large sample sizes, one or two traits at a time, mathematics to analyze results', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(182, '28. BIOLOGY OF HEREDITY', 'Mendel\'s Laws', 'Mendel proposed three laws of inheritance based on his experiments. (1) Law of Segregation (First Law): (a) Statement: During gamete formation, the two alleles for each trait separate (segregate) so that each gamete receives only one allele. At fertilization, the offspring receives one allele from each parent, restoring the pair. (b) Evidence: Monohybrid cross results (3:1 ratio in F₂). Heterozygous parents (Pp) produce gametes with P or p in equal proportions. Random fusion gives PP, Pp, pP, pp - 3 purple : 1 white. (c) Cytological basis: During meiosis I, homologous chromosomes separate, carrying different alleles to different daughter cells. (2) Law of Independent Assortment (Second Law): (a) Statement: Alleles of different genes assort independently during gamete formation, provided the genes are on different chromosomes (or far apart on same chromosome). (b) Evidence: Dihybrid cross results (9:3:3:1 ratio in F₂). Heterozygous parents (YyRr) produce four types of gametes (YR, Yr, yR, yr) in equal proportions. Random fusion produces 16 combinations giving 9:3:3:1 ratio. (c) Cytological basis: During meiosis I, homologous chromosome pairs line up independently at metaphase plate, so different chromosome combinations go to different gametes. (3) Law of Dominance (Third Law): (a) Statement: In a heterozygote, one allele (dominant) may mask the expression of the other (recessive). (b) Evidence: F₁ generation all show dominant trait (purple flowers), not intermediate. (c) Exceptions: Incomplete dominance (snapdragon flower color - red × white gives pink F₁) and codominance (human ABO blood group - IA and IB both expressed) show that dominance is not universal. (4) Exceptions to Mendel\'s laws: (a) Linked genes: Genes on same chromosome do not assort independently; tend to be inherited together. (b) Sex linkage: Genes on sex chromosomes show different inheritance patterns. (c) Multiple alleles: More than two alleles exist for some genes (ABO blood group). (d) Polygenic inheritance: Many genes contribute to one trait (height, skin color). (e) Epistasis: One gene masks expression of another. (f) Environmental effects: Phenotype influenced by environment.', 'Mendel\'s laws, law of segregation, first law, gamete formation, two alleles separate, segregate, each gamete receives one allele, fertilization, offspring receives one allele from each parent, restores pair, evidence, monohybrid cross, 3:1 ratio, F₂, heterozygous parents, Pp, gametes with P or p, equal proportions, random fusion, PP, Pp, pP, pp, 3 purple, 1 white, cytological basis, meiosis I, homologous chromosomes separate, different alleles, different daughter cells, law of independent assortment, second law, alleles of different genes assort independently, gamete formation, genes on different chromosomes, far apart on same chromosome, evidence, dihybrid cross, 9:3:3:1 ratio, F₂, heterozygous parents, YyRr, four types of gametes, YR, Yr, yR, yr, equal proportions, random fusion, 16 combinations, cytological basis, meiosis I, homologous chromosome pairs, line up independently, metaphase plate, different chromosome combinations, different gametes, law of dominance, third law, heterozygote, one allele dominant, masks expression, other recessive, evidence, F₁ generation, all dominant trait, purple flowers, not intermediate, exceptions, incomplete dominance, snapdragon flower color, red × white, pink F₁, codominance, human ABO blood group, IA and IB both expressed, not universal, exceptions to Mendel\'s laws, linked genes, genes on same chromosome, do not assort independently, inherited together, sex linkage, genes on sex chromosomes, different inheritance patterns, multiple alleles, more than two alleles, ABO blood group, polygenic inheritance, many genes contribute to one trait, height, skin color, epistasis, one gene masks expression of another, environmental effects, phenotype influenced by environment', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(183, '28. BIOLOGY OF HEREDITY', 'Chromosome Structure', 'Chromosomes are thread-like structures in the nucleus that carry genetic information. (1) Chemical composition: (a) DNA (deoxyribonucleic acid): Double helix molecule carrying genetic code. (b) Histone proteins: Positively charged proteins (H1, H2A, H2B, H3, H4) that DNA wraps around. (c) Non-histone proteins: Involved in chromosome structure, gene regulation. (2) Levels of organization: (a) DNA double helix (2 nm diameter). (b) Nucleosomes (11 nm): DNA (~146 bp) wrapped around histone octamer (two each of H2A, H2B, H3, H4) like beads on a string. (c) 30 nm chromatin fiber: Nucleosomes coiled into solenoid structure stabilized by H1 histone. (d) Loops: 30 nm fiber forms loops attached to scaffold proteins. (e) Chromatid: Loops further condensed. (f) Metaphase chromosome: Most condensed form, visible under light microscope. Consists of two sister chromatids joined at centromere. (3) Parts of a chromosome: (a) Chromatids: Each chromosome consists of two identical sister chromatids after DNA replication. (b) Centromere (primary constriction): Region where chromatids are joined; site of kinetochore formation (spindle attachment). Position determines chromosome shape: metacentric (centromere middle), submetacentric (off-center), acrocentric (near end), telocentric (at end). (c) Telomeres: Ends of chromosomes; repetitive DNA sequences (TTAGGG in humans) that protect chromosome from deterioration, prevent fusion with other chromosomes. Shorten with each cell division (aging). (d) Chromosome arms: p arm (short), q arm (long). (4) Types of chromatin: (a) Euchromatin: Less condensed, transcriptionally active (gene-rich). (b) Heterochromatin: Highly condensed, transcriptionally inactive (gene-poor). Constitutive heterochromatin (always condensed, centromeres, telomeres); facultative heterochromatin (condensed in some cells, e.g., X-inactivation). (5) Chromosome number: (a) Humans: 46 chromosomes (23 pairs) - 22 pairs autosomes + 1 pair sex chromosomes (XX female, XY male). (b) Diploid (2n): Two sets of chromosomes (somatic cells). (c) Haploid (n): One set (gametes). (6) Karyotype: Organized display of chromosomes by size, centromere position, banding pattern. Used to detect chromosomal abnormalities.', 'chromosome structure, thread-like structures, nucleus, genetic information, chemical composition, DNA, deoxyribonucleic acid, double helix, genetic code, histone proteins, positively charged proteins, H1, H2A, H2B, H3, H4, DNA wraps around, non-histone proteins, chromosome structure, gene regulation, levels of organization, DNA double helix, 2 nm diameter, nucleosomes, 11 nm, 146 bp DNA, wrapped around histone octamer, beads on a string, 30 nm chromatin fiber, nucleosomes coiled, solenoid structure, stabilized by H1 histone, loops, 30 nm fiber, loops attached, scaffold proteins, chromatid, loops condensed, metaphase chromosome, most condensed form, visible under light microscope, sister chromatids, joined at centromere, parts of chromosome, chromatids, identical sister chromatids, DNA replication, centromere, primary constriction, chromatids joined, kinetochore formation, spindle attachment, centromere position, chromosome shape, metacentric, centromere middle, submetacentric, off-center, acrocentric, near end, telocentric, at end, telomeres, ends of chromosomes, repetitive DNA sequences, TTAGGG in humans, protect chromosome, deterioration, prevent fusion, shorten with cell division, aging, chromosome arms, p arm, short, q arm, long, types of chromatin, euchromatin, less condensed, transcriptionally active, gene-rich, heterochromatin, highly condensed, transcriptionally inactive, gene-poor, constitutive heterochromatin, always condensed, centromeres, telomeres, facultative heterochromatin, condensed in some cells, X-inactivation, chromosome number, humans, 46 chromosomes, 23 pairs, autosomes, sex chromosomes, XX female, XY male, diploid, 2n, two sets of chromosomes, somatic cells, haploid, n, one set, gametes, karyotype, organized display, chromosomes by size, centromere position, banding pattern, detect chromosomal abnormalities', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(184, '28. BIOLOGY OF HEREDITY', 'Transmission of Hereditary Characters', 'Hereditary characters are passed from parents to offspring through genes located on chromosomes. The process involves meiosis, gamete formation, and fertilization. (1) Role of chromosomes: Chromosomes carry genes (units of heredity). Each chromosome contains many genes arranged linearly. Humans have 23 pairs of chromosomes (46 total) - one set from mother, one from father. (2) Meiosis: Specialized cell division that reduces chromosome number by half, producing haploid gametes. (a) Meiosis I: Homologous chromosomes separate. Crossing over in prophase I exchanges genetic material between homologous chromosomes, creating new combinations of alleles. Independent assortment in metaphase I randomly distributes maternal and paternal chromosomes to daughter cells. (b) Meiosis II: Sister chromatids separate, producing four haploid cells (gametes). (c) Result: Each gamete receives one complete set of chromosomes (haploid, n), but with a unique combination of maternal and paternal alleles due to crossing over and independent assortment. (3) Gamete formation: (a) Spermatogenesis (males): Continuous production of four functional sperm from each primary spermatocyte. (b) Oogenesis (females): Production of one large egg and three small polar bodies (to conserve cytoplasm). (4) Fertilization: Fusion of haploid sperm and egg restores diploid number (2n) in zygote. (a) Random fertilization: Which sperm fertilizes which egg is random, further increasing genetic variation. (b) Zygote contains new combination of alleles from both parents. (5) Sources of genetic variation: (a) Crossing over (meiosis I). (b) Independent assortment of chromosomes (meiosis I). (c) Random fertilization. (6) Segregation of alleles: During gamete formation, the two alleles for each gene separate (segregate) so each gamete gets one allele. Restored at fertilization. (7) Independent assortment: Alleles of different genes on different chromosomes assort independently, producing various combinations in gametes. (8) Chromosome theory of inheritance (Sutton and Boveri, 1902): Genes are located on chromosomes, and the behavior of chromosomes during meiosis explains Mendel\'s laws.', 'hereditary characters transmission, parents to offspring, genes on chromosomes, meiosis, gamete formation, fertilization, role of chromosomes, chromosomes carry genes, units of heredity, genes arranged linearly, humans 23 pairs, 46 chromosomes, one set from mother, one set from father, meiosis, specialized cell division, reduces chromosome number, haploid gametes, meiosis I, homologous chromosomes separate, crossing over, prophase I, exchanges genetic material, homologous chromosomes, new allele combinations, independent assortment, metaphase I, randomly distributes maternal chromosomes, paternal chromosomes, daughter cells, meiosis II, sister chromatids separate, four haploid cells, gametes, each gamete one complete set, haploid n, unique combination, maternal alleles, paternal alleles, crossing over, independent assortment, gamete formation, spermatogenesis, males, continuous production, four functional sperm, primary spermatocyte, oogenesis, females, one large egg, three small polar bodies, conserve cytoplasm, fertilization, fusion haploid sperm, haploid egg, restores diploid number, 2n zygote, random fertilization, which sperm fertilizes which egg, random, increases genetic variation, zygote contains new allele combination, both parents, sources of genetic variation, crossing over meiosis I, independent assortment chromosomes meiosis I, random fertilization, segregation of alleles, gamete formation, two alleles for each gene separate, segregate, each gamete gets one allele, restored at fertilization, independent assortment, alleles of different genes, different chromosomes, assort independently, various combinations in gametes, chromosome theory of inheritance, Sutton and Boveri, 1902, genes located on chromosomes, behavior of chromosomes during meiosis, explains Mendel\'s laws', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(185, '28. BIOLOGY OF HEREDITY', 'Sex Determination', 'Sex determination is the biological system that determines the development of sexual characteristics in an organism. In humans and many other species, it is controlled by sex chromosomes. (1) Sex chromosomes: (a) Humans have 23 pairs of chromosomes: 22 pairs of autosomes (same in males and females) and 1 pair of sex chromosomes (X and Y). (b) Females: XX (two X chromosomes). (c) Males: XY (one X and one Y chromosome). (2) Mechanism of sex determination: (a) Females produce eggs containing X chromosome (all eggs carry X). (b) Males produce two types of sperm: half carry X chromosome, half carry Y chromosome. (c) At fertilization: X egg + X sperm → XX female (50% probability). X egg + Y sperm → XY male (50% probability). (d) Sex ratio approximately 1:1. (3) Role of Y chromosome: The SRY gene (Sex-determining Region Y) on the Y chromosome triggers male development. It produces testis-determining factor, which causes undifferentiated gonads to develop into testes. In absence of SRY (XX), ovaries develop. (4) Other sex determination systems: (a) Birds: ZW system - females ZW, males ZZ (opposite of mammals). (b) Grasshoppers: XO system - females XX, males XO (one X only). (c) Bees: Haplodiploid system - females develop from fertilized eggs (diploid), males from unfertilized eggs (haploid). (d) Some reptiles: Temperature-dependent sex determination (temperature during egg incubation determines sex). (5) Sex-linked traits: Genes on sex chromosomes show different inheritance patterns (next section). (6) Sex chromosome abnormalities: (a) Turner syndrome (XO): Female, short stature, infertile. (b) Klinefelter syndrome (XXY): Male, tall, infertile, some female characteristics. (c) XXX syndrome: Female, often normal. (d) XYY syndrome: Male, often normal, slightly taller.', 'sex determination, biological system, sexual characteristics, humans, sex chromosomes, 23 pairs chromosomes, 22 pairs autosomes, same in males and females, 1 pair sex chromosomes, X chromosome, Y chromosome, females XX, two X chromosomes, males XY, one X, one Y, mechanism, females produce eggs, X chromosome, all eggs carry X, males produce two types sperm, half carry X, half carry Y, fertilization, X egg + X sperm, XX female, 50% probability, X egg + Y sperm, XY male, 50% probability, sex ratio, 1:1, role of Y chromosome, SRY gene, Sex-determining Region Y, Y chromosome, triggers male development, testis-determining factor, undifferentiated gonads, develop into testes, absence of SRY, XX, ovaries develop, other sex determination systems, birds, ZW system, females ZW, males ZZ, opposite of mammals, grasshoppers, XO system, females XX, males XO, one X only, bees, haplodiploid system, females from fertilized eggs, diploid, males from unfertilized eggs, haploid, reptiles, temperature-dependent sex determination, temperature during egg incubation, determines sex, sex-linked traits, genes on sex chromosomes, different inheritance patterns, sex chromosome abnormalities, Turner syndrome, XO, female, short stature, infertile, Klinefelter syndrome, XXY, male, tall, infertile, some female characteristics, XXX syndrome, female, often normal, XYY syndrome, male, often normal, slightly taller', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(186, '28. BIOLOGY OF HEREDITY', 'Sex-linked Characters', 'Sex-linked characters are traits controlled by genes located on sex chromosomes (usually X chromosome). They show different inheritance patterns in males and females. (1) X-linked traits: Genes on X chromosome. (a) Males (XY) have only one X chromosome, so they express all X-linked alleles (whether dominant or recessive). (b) Females (XX) have two X chromosomes, so they can be homozygous or heterozygous. Recessive traits expressed only when both X chromosomes carry the recessive allele. (c) Inheritance pattern: Affected males pass the gene to all daughters (who become carriers) but not to sons (who receive Y from father). Carrier females pass the gene to half sons (affected) and half daughters (carriers). (2) Examples of X-linked disorders: (a) Hemophilia A: Deficiency of clotting factor VIII. Causes excessive bleeding from minor injuries. Affected males, carrier females. Historical example: Queen Victoria was carrier, passed to European royal families. (b) Color blindness (red-green): Inability to distinguish red and green. Most common form: deficiency of red or green cone photopigments. Affects ~8% males, <1% females. (c) Duchenne muscular dystrophy: Progressive muscle wasting due to deficiency of dystrophin protein. Affected males usually die by early adulthood. (d) Fragile X syndrome: Intellectual disability, characteristic facial features. (3) Y-linked traits: Genes on Y chromosome (holandric genes). Only males affected, passed from father to all sons. Examples: SRY gene (sex determination), some genes involved in spermatogenesis. (4) Sex-influenced traits: Autosomal genes expressed differently in males and females due to hormonal differences. Example: Pattern baldness - dominant in males, recessive in females. (5) Sex-limited traits: Autosomal genes expressed only in one sex. Examples: Beard growth in males, milk production in females. (6) X-inactivation (Lyonization): In female mammals, one X chromosome in each cell is randomly inactivated (forms Barr body). Ensures equal expression of X-linked genes in males and females. Explains mosaic patterns in heterozygous females (e.g., calico cats - coat color gene on X).', 'sex-linked characters, sex-linked traits, genes on sex chromosomes, X chromosome, Y chromosome, different inheritance patterns, males and females, X-linked traits, genes on X chromosome, males XY, only one X, express all X-linked alleles, dominant, recessive, females XX, two X, homozygous, heterozygous, recessive traits expressed only when both X carry recessive, inheritance pattern, affected males, pass gene to all daughters, become carriers, not to sons, sons receive Y from father, carrier females, pass gene to half sons, affected, half daughters, carriers, X-linked disorders, hemophilia A, deficiency clotting factor VIII, excessive bleeding, minor injuries, affected males, carrier females, Queen Victoria, carrier, passed to European royal families, color blindness, red-green, inability distinguish red and green, deficiency red cone photopigments, deficiency green cone photopigments, affects ~8% males, <1% females, Duchenne muscular dystrophy, progressive muscle wasting, deficiency dystrophin protein, affected males die early adulthood, Fragile X syndrome, intellectual disability, characteristic facial features, Y-linked traits, holandric genes, genes on Y chromosome, only males affected, passed from father to all sons, SRY gene, sex determination, spermatogenesis genes, sex-influenced traits, autosomal genes, expressed differently, males and females, hormonal differences, pattern baldness, dominant in males, recessive in females, sex-limited traits, autosomal genes, expressed only in one sex, beard growth males, milk production females, X-inactivation, Lyonization, female mammals, one X chromosome in each cell, randomly inactivated, Barr body, ensures equal expression X-linked genes, males and females, mosaic patterns, heterozygous females, calico cats, coat color gene on X', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(187, '28. BIOLOGY OF HEREDITY', 'Applications in Agriculture', 'Genetic principles are extensively applied in agriculture to improve crops and livestock. (1) Selective breeding (artificial selection): (a) Choosing individuals with desirable traits as parents for next generation. (b) Examples: Higher yield, disease resistance, improved quality, faster growth, better adaptation. (c) Methods: Inbreeding (mating closely related individuals to fix traits - can lead to inbreeding depression). Outbreeding (mating unrelated individuals - increases hybrid vigor). (2) Hybrid vigor (heterosis): Cross between two inbred lines produces offspring superior to both parents. (a) F₁ hybrids show increased yield, growth rate, fertility, disease resistance. (b) Examples: Hybrid maize (corn) revolutionized agriculture; hybrid chickens for meat and eggs. (c) Mechanism: Dominance (masking deleterious recessives), overdominance (heterozygote advantage). (3) New crop varieties: (a) Disease resistance: Breeding rust-resistant wheat, blight-resistant potatoes, virus-resistant tomatoes. (b) Higher yield: High-yielding varieties (HYV) of rice, wheat (Green Revolution). (c) Improved nutritional content: Golden Rice (genetically modified to produce beta-carotene, vitamin A precursor). (d) Stress tolerance: Drought-tolerant maize, salt-tolerant rice. (e) Quality traits: Better taste, longer shelf life, improved processing qualities. (4) New livestock breeds: (a) Meat production: Broiler chickens (fast growth, high meat yield), beef cattle (Hereford, Angus). (b) Milk production: Holstein-Friesian cows (high milk yield). (c) Wool production: Merino sheep (fine wool). (d) Disease resistance: Trypanotolerant cattle breeds in Africa (N\'Dama). (5) Genetic engineering/modern biotechnology: (a) Transgenic crops: Bt cotton, Bt corn (produce insecticidal protein from Bacillus thuringiensis, reducing pesticide use). (b) Herbicide-resistant crops: Roundup Ready soybeans (resistant to glyphosate). (c) Gene editing (CRISPR): Potential for precise trait improvement. (6) Marker-assisted selection: Using DNA markers to select desirable traits, speeding up breeding programs. (7) Advantages: (a) Increased food production. (b) Reduced pesticide use. (c) Improved nutritional quality. (d) Adaptation to changing climate. (8) Disadvantages/concerns: (a) Reduced genetic diversity (vulnerability to diseases). (b) Ethical concerns about genetic modification. (c) Gene flow to wild relatives. (d) Corporate control of seeds.', 'agriculture applications, genetic principles, improve crops, improve livestock, selective breeding, artificial selection, choosing individuals, desirable traits, parents for next generation, higher yield, disease resistance, improved quality, faster growth, better adaptation, inbreeding, mating closely related individuals, fix traits, inbreeding depression, outbreeding, mating unrelated individuals, increases hybrid vigor, hybrid vigor, heterosis, cross between inbred lines, offspring superior to both parents, F₁ hybrids, increased yield, growth rate, fertility, disease resistance, hybrid maize, corn, revolutionized agriculture, hybrid chickens, meat, eggs, mechanism, dominance, masking deleterious recessives, overdominance, heterozygote advantage, new crop varieties, disease resistance, rust-resistant wheat, blight-resistant potatoes, virus-resistant tomatoes, higher yield, high-yielding varieties, HYV, rice, wheat, Green Revolution, improved nutritional content, Golden Rice, genetically modified, beta-carotene, vitamin A precursor, stress tolerance, drought-tolerant maize, salt-tolerant rice, quality traits, better taste, longer shelf life, improved processing qualities, new livestock breeds, meat production, broiler chickens, fast growth, high meat yield, beef cattle, Hereford, Angus, milk production, Holstein-Friesian cows, high milk yield, wool production, Merino sheep, fine wool, disease resistance, trypanotolerant cattle, N\'Dama, Africa, genetic engineering, modern biotechnology, transgenic crops, Bt cotton, Bt corn, insecticidal protein, Bacillus thuringiensis, reducing pesticide use, herbicide-resistant crops, Roundup Ready soybeans, resistant to glyphosate, gene editing, CRISPR, precise trait improvement, marker-assisted selection, DNA markers, select desirable traits, speed up breeding, advantages, increased food production, reduced pesticide use, improved nutritional quality, adaptation to changing climate, disadvantages, reduced genetic diversity, vulnerability to diseases, ethical concerns, genetic modification, gene flow to wild relatives, corporate control of seeds', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(188, '28. BIOLOGY OF HEREDITY', 'Applications in Medicine', 'Genetic principles are increasingly applied in medicine for diagnosis, treatment, and prevention of diseases. (1) Genetic counseling: (a) Assessing risk of inherited disorders for couples planning children. (b) Family history analysis, carrier testing, discussing options. (c) Conditions: Sickle cell anemia, cystic fibrosis, hemophilia, Huntington\'s disease, Tay-Sachs disease. (2) Sickle cell anemia: (a) Autosomal recessive disorder caused by mutation in β-globin gene (glutamate → valine at position 6). (b) Homozygous (HbSS) have sickle-shaped red blood cells, causing pain, anemia, organ damage. (c) Heterozygous (HbAS) have sickle cell trait - usually mild symptoms, but protected against malaria (balanced polymorphism in malaria regions). (d) Genetic counseling: Identify carriers, prenatal diagnosis. (3) Cystic fibrosis: (a) Autosomal recessive disorder affecting CFTR gene (chloride channel). (b) Thick mucus in lungs, pancreas, causing breathing difficulty, infections, digestive problems. (c) Common in Caucasian populations. (d) Carrier screening available. (4) Huntington\'s disease: (a) Autosomal dominant disorder (one copy of mutant gene causes disease). (b) Progressive neurodegeneration, movement disorders, cognitive decline. (c) Symptoms appear in mid-adult life. (d) Genetic testing can predict disease before symptoms. (5) Rhesus factor incompatibility: (a) Rh- mother carrying Rh+ fetus can produce antibodies against fetal Rh antigen. (b) First pregnancy usually unaffected; subsequent pregnancies at risk for hemolytic disease of newborn (HDNB). (c) Prevention: Rh immunoglobulin (RhoGAM) given to Rh- mother during and after pregnancy prevents antibody formation. (6) Prenatal testing: (a) Amniocentesis:取样 amniotic fluid at 15-18 weeks, analyze fetal cells for chromosomal abnormalities. (b) Chorionic villus sampling (CVS):取样 placental tissue at 10-12 weeks, earlier diagnosis. (c) Ultrasound: Detect structural abnormalities. (d) Non-invasive prenatal testing (NIPT):分析 fetal DNA in maternal blood. (7) Newborn screening: Testing for metabolic disorders (PKU, hypothyroidism) to allow early treatment. (8) Gene therapy: (a) Introducing normal genes to correct genetic disorders. (b) Approaches: Viral vectors, CRISPR gene editing. (c) Successes: Some forms of inherited blindness, severe combined immunodeficiency (SCID). (d) Challenges: Delivery, immune response, off-target effects. (9) Pharmacogenomics: Tailoring drug treatments based on individual\'s genetic makeup. (a) Example: Testing for CYP2C9 and VKORC1 variants to determine warfarin (blood thinner) dosage. (b) Avoiding adverse drug reactions, improving efficacy. (10) Cancer genetics: (a) Inherited mutations in BRCA1, BRCA2 genes increase risk of breast, ovarian cancer. (b) Genetic testing identifies high-risk individuals for increased surveillance or preventive surgery.', 'medicine applications, genetic principles, diagnosis, treatment, disease prevention, genetic counseling, assessing risk, inherited disorders, couples planning children, family history analysis, carrier testing, discussing options, sickle cell anemia, cystic fibrosis, hemophilia, Huntington\'s disease, Tay-Sachs disease, sickle cell anemia, autosomal recessive, β-globin gene mutation, glutamate to valine, position 6, homozygous HbSS, sickle-shaped red blood cells, pain, anemia, organ damage, heterozygous HbAS, sickle cell trait, mild symptoms, protected against malaria, balanced polymorphism, malaria regions, genetic counseling, identify carriers, prenatal diagnosis, cystic fibrosis, autosomal recessive, CFTR gene, chloride channel, thick mucus, lungs, pancreas, breathing difficulty, infections, digestive problems, Caucasian populations, carrier screening, Huntington\'s disease, autosomal dominant, one copy causes disease, progressive neurodegeneration, movement disorders, cognitive decline, symptoms mid-adult life, genetic testing, predict disease before symptoms, Rhesus factor incompatibility, Rh- mother, Rh+ fetus, antibodies against fetal Rh antigen, first pregnancy unaffected, subsequent pregnancies risk, hemolytic disease of newborn, HDNB, prevention, Rh immunoglobulin, RhoGAM, given to Rh- mother, during pregnancy, after pregnancy, prevents antibody formation, prenatal testing, amniocentesis, amniotic fluid, 15-18 weeks, analyze fetal cells, chromosomal abnormalities, chorionic villus sampling, CVS, placental tissue, 10-12 weeks, earlier diagnosis, ultrasound, detect structural abnormalities, non-invasive prenatal testing, NIPT, fetal DNA in maternal blood, newborn screening, metabolic disorders, PKU, hypothyroidism, early treatment, gene therapy, introducing normal genes, correct genetic disorders, viral vectors, CRISPR gene editing, successes, inherited blindness, severe combined immunodeficiency, SCID, challenges, delivery, immune response, off-target effects, pharmacogenomics, tailoring drug treatments, individual genetic makeup, CYP2C9 variants, VKORC1 variants, warfarin dosage, blood thinner, avoiding adverse drug reactions, improving efficacy, cancer genetics, inherited mutations, BRCA1, BRCA2 genes, increased risk, breast cancer, ovarian cancer, genetic testing, identify high-risk individuals, increased surveillance, preventive surgery', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(189, '29. ADAPTATION FOR SURVIVAL', 'Behavioural Adaptations in Social Insects - Termites', 'Termites are social insects living in large colonies with complex division of labor and sophisticated behaviors. (1) Castes: (a) Queen: Primary reproductive female. Lays thousands of eggs per day. Can live many years (up to 50). Enlarged abdomen (physogastric) for egg production. (b) King: Reproductive male. Mates with queen throughout life. (c) Workers: Sterile, most numerous caste. Perform all colony maintenance: build and repair nest, forage for food (wood, cellulose), care for young (feed, groom), feed queen and king. Lack wings, have soft bodies. (d) Soldiers: Sterile caste specialized for defense. Have large heads with powerful mandibles (some species) or chemical defense (spray noxious substances). Cannot feed themselves, rely on workers. (2) Division of labor: Each caste performs specific tasks, increasing colony efficiency. (3) Communication: (a) Pheromones: Chemical signals for alarm (alert colony), trail marking (lead workers to food source), recognition (distinguish nestmates from intruders), queen recognition. (b) Trophallaxis: Exchange of liquid food and fluids between colony members. Transmits nutrients, pheromones, symbionts (gut microbes for cellulose digestion). (4) Nest building: Termite mounds can be huge (several meters high) with complex architecture (chimneys for ventilation, temperature regulation). Use soil, saliva, feces as building material. (5) Symbiosis with microorganisms: Termites harbor protozoa and bacteria in their gut that digest cellulose (termites cannot digest cellulose themselves). (6) Ecological importance: Decompose wood, recycle nutrients, aerate soil. (7) Economic importance: Can damage wooden structures (pests).', 'termites, social insects, large colonies, division of labor, sophisticated behaviors, castes, queen, primary reproductive female, lays thousands eggs per day, live many years, up to 50, enlarged abdomen, physogastric, egg production, king, reproductive male, mates with queen throughout life, workers, sterile, most numerous caste, colony maintenance, build nest, repair nest, forage for food, wood, cellulose, care for young, feed young, groom young, feed queen, feed king, lack wings, soft bodies, soldiers, sterile caste, specialized for defense, large heads, powerful mandibles, chemical defense, spray noxious substances, cannot feed themselves, rely on workers, division of labor, each caste specific tasks, colony efficiency, communication, pheromones, chemical signals, alarm, alert colony, trail marking, lead workers to food source, recognition, distinguish nestmates from intruders, queen recognition, trophallaxis, exchange liquid food, fluids, colony members, transmits nutrients, transmits pheromones, transmits symbionts, gut microbes, cellulose digestion, nest building, termite mounds, huge, several meters high, complex architecture, chimneys, ventilation, temperature regulation, soil, saliva, feces, building material, symbiosis with microorganisms, protozoa, bacteria, gut, digest cellulose, cannot digest cellulose themselves, ecological importance, decompose wood, recycle nutrients, aerate soil, economic importance, damage wooden structures, pests', '2026-02-27 15:40:30');
INSERT INTO `biology_syllabus_comprehensive` (`id`, `topic`, `sub_topic`, `note`, `keywords`, `created_at`) VALUES
(190, '29. ADAPTATION FOR SURVIVAL', 'Behavioural Adaptations in Social Insects - Bees', 'Honeybees (Apis mellifera) are highly social insects with complex colony organization and sophisticated communication. (1) Castes: (a) Queen: Only fertile female. Lays up to 2000 eggs per day. Produces queen substance (pheromone) that suppresses development of new queens and regulates colony cohesion. (b) Drones: Males. Develop from unfertilized eggs (haploid). Only function is to mate with virgin queen. Die after mating (or expelled from hive in winter). (c) Workers: Sterile females. Perform all colony tasks: cleaning cells, feeding larvae (royal jelly for queen larvae, pollen/honey for workers), building comb (secrete wax), guarding hive, foraging for nectar, pollen, water, propolis. (2) Division of labor: Age-based polyethism - young workers work inside hive, older workers become foragers. (3) Communication: Waggle dance: (a) Performed by forager bees to communicate location of food source (nectar, pollen, water). (b) Figure-eight pattern on vertical comb. Angle of straight run relative to vertical indicates direction relative to sun. Duration of waggle indicates distance (longer waggle = farther). (c) Round dance: Used for nearby food sources (<100 m). (4) Nest building: Bees secrete wax from abdominal glands, mold it into hexagonal cells (honeycomb). Cells used for rearing young and storing honey, pollen. (5) Thermoregulation: Workers fan wings to cool hive; cluster to generate heat in winter. (6) Defense: Stinger (modified ovipositor) with venom. Stinging causes bee\'s death (stinger barbed, tears out). Alarm pheromone recruits other bees to defend. (7) Honey production: Nectar collected, converted to honey by evaporation and enzyme action. Honey stored as food for winter. (8) Pollination: Bees are major pollinators of crops and wild plants. Economic value of pollination far exceeds honey production.', 'honeybees, Apis mellifera, social insects, complex colony organization, sophisticated communication, castes, queen, only fertile female, lays up to 2000 eggs per day, queen substance, pheromone, suppresses development new queens, regulates colony cohesion, drones, males, develop from unfertilized eggs, haploid, only function to mate with virgin queen, die after mating, expelled from hive in winter, workers, sterile females, perform all colony tasks, cleaning cells, feeding larvae, royal jelly for queen larvae, pollen for worker larvae, honey for worker larvae, building comb, secrete wax, guarding hive, foraging, nectar, pollen, water, propolis, division of labor, age-based polyethism, young workers inside hive, older workers become foragers, communication, waggle dance, performed by forager bees, communicate location of food source, nectar, pollen, water, figure-eight pattern, vertical comb, angle of straight run, relative to vertical, indicates direction relative to sun, duration of waggle, indicates distance, longer waggle = farther, round dance, used for nearby food sources, <100 m, nest building, bees secrete wax, abdominal glands, mold into hexagonal cells, honeycomb, cells for rearing young, storing honey, storing pollen, thermoregulation, workers fan wings, cool hive, cluster generate heat, winter, defense, stinger, modified ovipositor, venom, stinging causes bee\'s death, stinger barbed, tears out, alarm pheromone, recruits other bees to defend, honey production, nectar collected, converted to honey, evaporation, enzyme action, honey stored as food for winter, pollination, major pollinators, crops, wild plants, economic value, pollination far exceeds honey production', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(191, '29. ADAPTATION FOR SURVIVAL', 'Other Animal Behaviours', 'Animals exhibit diverse behaviors that enhance survival and reproduction. (1) Basking: (a) Ectotherms (lizards, snakes, turtles) absorb heat from sun to raise body temperature. (b) Increases metabolic rate, digestion speed, activity level. (c) Example: Lizards on rocks in morning. (2) Territorial behavior: (a) Defense of an area against intruders (same species). (b) Functions: Secure food resources, nesting sites, mates. (c) Examples: Birds sing to defend territory; mammals scent-mark (urine, gland secretions); fish defend breeding territories. (d) Agonistic behavior: Ritualized contests (displays, threat postures) often avoid actual fighting. (3) Hibernation: (a) Winter dormancy in endotherms (mammals). (b) Reduced metabolism, body temperature, heart rate, breathing to conserve energy when food scarce. (c) Examples: Bears, ground squirrels, hedgehogs. (d) Preparation: Accumulate fat reserves, find sheltered site (den, burrow). (e) Arousal: Periodic rewarming, then final awakening in spring. (4) Aestivation: (a) Summer dormancy during hot, dry periods. (b) Reduced metabolism to survive drought. (c) Examples: Snails (seal shell opening), lungfish (burrow in mud), desert tortoises. (5) Migration: (a) Seasonal movement between habitats. (b) Reasons: Avoid harsh conditions, access food, breeding grounds. (c) Examples: Birds (Arctic terns fly Arctic to Antarctic and back), wildebeest (Serengeti migration), salmon (swim from ocean to freshwater streams to spawn), butterflies (monarchs migrate to Mexico). (d) Navigation mechanisms: Sun position, stars, Earth\'s magnetic field, landmarks, olfactory cues. (6) Courtship behavior: (a) Displays to attract mates, ensure species recognition, assess fitness. (b) Examples: Bird songs, peacock tail display, bowerbird nest building, firefly flashing. (c) Leads to mate selection and reproduction. (7) Parental care: (a) Investment in offspring after birth/hatching. (b) Increases offspring survival. (c) Examples: Mammals nurse young; birds feed chicks; crocodiles carry hatchlings to water. (8) Communication: (a) Transfer of information between individuals. (b) Modalities: Visual (color changes, displays), auditory (calls, songs), chemical (pheromones), tactile (touch).', 'animal behaviours, enhance survival, enhance reproduction, basking, ectotherms, lizards, snakes, turtles, absorb heat from sun, raise body temperature, increase metabolic rate, digestion speed, activity level, lizards on rocks morning, territorial behavior, defense of area against intruders, same species, secure food resources, secure nesting sites, secure mates, birds sing to defend territory, mammals scent-mark, urine, gland secretions, fish defend breeding territories, agonistic behavior, ritualized contests, displays, threat postures, avoid actual fighting, hibernation, winter dormancy, endotherms, mammals, reduced metabolism, reduced body temperature, reduced heart rate, reduced breathing, conserve energy, food scarce, bears, ground squirrels, hedgehogs, preparation, accumulate fat reserves, find sheltered site, den, burrow, arousal, periodic rewarming, final awakening spring, aestivation, summer dormancy, hot dry periods, reduced metabolism, survive drought, snails seal shell opening, lungfish burrow in mud, desert tortoises, migration, seasonal movement between habitats, avoid harsh conditions, access food, breeding grounds, birds, Arctic terns, Arctic to Antarctic and back, wildebeest, Serengeti migration, salmon, swim from ocean to freshwater streams, spawn, butterflies, monarchs migrate to Mexico, navigation mechanisms, sun position, stars, Earth\'s magnetic field, landmarks, olfactory cues, courtship behavior, displays to attract mates, ensure species recognition, assess fitness, bird songs, peacock tail display, bowerbird nest building, firefly flashing, leads to mate selection, reproduction, parental care, investment in offspring, after birth, after hatching, increases offspring survival, mammals nurse young, birds feed chicks, crocodiles carry hatchlings to water, communication, transfer of information, between individuals, visual communication, color changes, displays, auditory communication, calls, songs, chemical communication, pheromones, tactile communication, touch', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(192, '29. ADAPTATION FOR SURVIVAL', 'Adaptive Coloration', 'Adaptive coloration refers to color patterns that enhance an organism\'s survival and reproductive success. (1) Camouflage (crypsis): (a) Coloration that blends with environment, making organism difficult to detect. (b) Examples: Chameleon changes color to match background (camouflage, communication). Stick insect resembles twig. Peppered moth (Biston betularia) - light form camouflaged on lichen-covered trees, dark form on soot-covered trees (industrial melanism). Arctic animals (polar bear, arctic fox) white in winter for camouflage in snow. (c) Countershading: Darker on top, lighter below (common in fish, mammals) - reduces shadow, makes animal less visible from above and below. (d) Disruptive coloration: Bold patterns (spots, stripes) break up body outline (zebra stripes confuse predators, leopards spotted). (2) Warning coloration (aposematism): (a) Bright, conspicuous colors signal toxicity or unpalatability to predators. (b) Predators learn to avoid these colors after unpleasant experience. (c) Examples: Poison dart frogs (bright yellow, red, blue), monarch butterflies (orange and black), wasps (yellow and black), coral snakes (red, yellow, black rings). (3) Mimicry: (a) Batesian mimicry: Harmless species mimics toxic/model species to gain protection. Example: Hoverfly (harmless) mimics wasp (stinging). Viceroy butterfly mimics monarch (toxic). Predators avoid both. (b) Müllerian mimicry: Two or more toxic species resemble each other, reinforcing predator learning. Example: Many stinging insects (bees, wasps) share yellow-black coloration. (4) Other functions of coloration: (a) Courtship: Bright colors attract mates (peacock, birds-of-paradise). (b) Species recognition: Distinctive patterns help identify same species. (c) Thermoregulation: Dark colors absorb heat (butterflies basking), light colors reflect heat (desert animals). (d) Startle coloration: Hidden bright colors suddenly revealed to startle predator (eyespots on moth wings, startle birds).', 'adaptive coloration, color patterns, enhance survival, enhance reproductive success, camouflage, crypsis, blends with environment, difficult to detect, chameleon changes color, match background, stick insect resembles twig, peppered moth, Biston betularia, light form, camouflaged on lichen-covered trees, dark form, soot-covered trees, industrial melanism, Arctic animals, polar bear, arctic fox, white in winter, camouflage in snow, countershading, darker on top, lighter below, fish, mammals, reduces shadow, less visible from above, less visible from below, disruptive coloration, bold patterns, spots, stripes, break up body outline, zebra stripes confuse predators, leopards spotted, warning coloration, aposematism, bright conspicuous colors, signal toxicity, signal unpalatability, predators, predators learn to avoid, poison dart frogs, bright yellow, red, blue, monarch butterflies, orange and black, wasps, yellow and black, coral snakes, red, yellow, black rings, mimicry, Batesian mimicry, harmless species mimics toxic species, gains protection, hoverfly harmless, mimics wasp stinging, viceroy butterfly, mimics monarch toxic, predators avoid both, Müllerian mimicry, two or more toxic species, resemble each other, reinforce predator learning, stinging insects, bees, wasps, share yellow-black coloration, other functions of coloration, courtship, bright colors attract mates, peacock, birds-of-paradise, species recognition, distinctive patterns, identify same species, thermoregulation, dark colors absorb heat, butterflies basking, light colors reflect heat, desert animals, startle coloration, hidden bright colors, suddenly revealed, startle predator, eyespots on moth wings, startle birds', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(193, '30. EVOLUTION', 'Evidence of Evolution', 'Evolution is the change in heritable characteristics of populations over generations. Multiple lines of evidence support evolution. (1) Paleontology (fossil record): (a) Fossils show progression of life forms over time. (b) Transitional forms: Fossils with characteristics of two groups. Examples: Archaeopteryx (dinosaur-like reptile with feathers - link between reptiles and birds). Tiktaalik (fish with limb-like fins - link between fish and tetrapods). (c) Sequence of fossils: Simple life in older rocks, more complex in younger rocks. (d) Extinction: Many species appear then disappear, showing life changes. (2) Comparative anatomy: (a) Homologous structures: Structures with same embryonic origin but different functions. Indicate common ancestry. Examples: Forelimbs of mammals (human arm, whale flipper, bat wing, cat leg) - same bones (humerus, radius, ulna, carpals, metacarpals, phalanges) modified for different functions. (b) Analogous structures: Structures with different origins but similar functions. Indicate convergent evolution (similar environmental pressures). Examples: Bird wing and insect wing (different embryonic origin, both for flight). (c) Vestigial structures: Structures with no apparent function, remnants of functional structures in ancestors. Examples: Human appendix, whale pelvic bones, snake hind limb remnants. (3) Comparative embryology: (a) Embryos of related species show similar developmental stages. (b) Examples: Vertebrate embryos (fish, amphibian, reptile, bird, mammal) have pharyngeal pouches (gill slits) and tails at early stages. (c) Supports common ancestry. (4) Comparative biochemistry: (a) DNA and protein similarities reflect evolutionary relationships. (b) Cytochrome c (protein in electron transport chain): Differences in amino acid sequence correlate with evolutionary distance (humans and chimpanzees differ by 0 amino acids, humans and yeast differ by many). (c) DNA hybridization: More similar DNA = more closely related. (d) Molecular clocks: Rate of DNA changes used to estimate divergence time. (5) Geographical distribution: (a) Species distribution reflects evolutionary history. (b) Darwin\'s finches: Different beak shapes on different Galapagos islands adapted to different food sources. (c) Marsupials: Mostly in Australia (diverse forms) due to isolation after continental drift. (d) Island species: Often unique, evolved from mainland ancestors. (6) Systematics: Classification reflects evolutionary relationships (phylogenetic trees).', 'evolution evidence, paleontology, fossil record, progression of life forms, transitional forms, Archaeopteryx, dinosaur-like reptile, feathers, link between reptiles and birds, Tiktaalik, fish with limb-like fins, link between fish and tetrapods, sequence of fossils, simple life older rocks, complex life younger rocks, extinction, species appear, species disappear, life changes, comparative anatomy, homologous structures, same embryonic origin, different functions, common ancestry, forelimbs of mammals, human arm, whale flipper, bat wing, cat leg, same bones, humerus, radius, ulna, carpals, metacarpals, phalanges, modified for different functions, analogous structures, different origins, similar functions, convergent evolution, similar environmental pressures, bird wing, insect wing, different embryonic origin, both for flight, vestigial structures, no apparent function, remnants of functional structures in ancestors, human appendix, whale pelvic bones, snake hind limb remnants, comparative embryology, embryos of related species, similar developmental stages, vertebrate embryos, fish, amphibian, reptile, bird, mammal, pharyngeal pouches, gill slits, tails at early stages, supports common ancestry, comparative biochemistry, DNA similarities, protein similarities, reflect evolutionary relationships, cytochrome c, protein in electron transport chain, amino acid sequence differences, correlate with evolutionary distance, humans and chimpanzees differ 0 amino acids, humans and yeast differ many, DNA hybridization, more similar DNA, more closely related, molecular clocks, rate of DNA changes, estimate divergence time, geographical distribution, species distribution, reflects evolutionary history, Darwin\'s finches, different beak shapes, Galapagos islands, adapted to different food sources, marsupials, mostly in Australia, diverse forms, isolation after continental drift, island species, often unique, evolved from mainland ancestors, systematics, classification reflects evolutionary relationships, phylogenetic trees', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(194, '30. EVOLUTION', 'Theories of Evolution', 'Several theories have been proposed to explain the mechanism of evolution. (1) Lamarckism (Jean-Baptiste Lamarck, 1809): (a) Theory of inheritance of acquired characteristics. (b) Principles: Use and disuse - organs used become stronger and develop; organs not used degenerate. Inheritance of acquired characteristics - changes acquired during lifetime passed to offspring. (c) Example: Giraffe\'s neck stretched to reach leaves, longer neck passed to offspring. (d) Disproven: Acquired characteristics (e.g., muscle building) not inherited; only changes in germ cells (sperm, egg) passed on. However, Lamarck recognized that evolution occurs and environment plays role. (2) Darwinism (Charles Darwin, 1859): (a) Theory of evolution by natural selection. Based on observations: (i) Overproduction: More offspring produced than can survive. (ii) Variation: Individuals in population vary in traits. (iii) Competition: Struggle for existence due to limited resources. (b) Inferences: (i) Survival of the fittest: Individuals with favorable variations more likely to survive and reproduce. (ii) Inheritance of favorable traits: Advantageous variations passed to offspring. (iii) Over generations, accumulation of favorable traits leads to evolution, adaptation. (c) Example: Peppered moth - dark form became common in polluted areas because better camouflaged on soot-covered trees, survived better, reproduced more. (d) Evidence: Darwin\'s finches (different beak shapes on different islands). (3) Modern synthesis (Neo-Darwinism): (a) Combines Darwin\'s natural selection with genetics (Mendel), population genetics, mutation theory. (b) Key elements: (i) Genetic variation arises from mutations, recombination (crossing over, independent assortment). (ii) Natural selection acts on phenotypic variation. (iii) Evolution = change in allele frequencies in population over generations. (iv) Speciation occurs when populations become reproductively isolated. (4) Mutation theory (Hugo de Vries): (a) Mutations (sudden changes in genes) provide raw material for evolution. (b) Most mutations harmful, but rare beneficial mutations can be selected. (5) Contributions: (a) Gregor Mendel: Laws of inheritance. (b) Thomas Hunt Morgan: Chromosome theory, mutations in fruit flies. (c) Ronald Fisher, J.B.S. Haldane, Sewall Wright: Population genetics, mathematical models of evolution. (d) Theodosius Dobzhansky: Genetics and the Origin of Species (1937) - integrated genetics and natural selection. (e) Ernst Mayr: Species concept, allopatric speciation.', 'evolution theories, Lamarckism, Jean-Baptiste Lamarck, 1809, inheritance of acquired characteristics, use and disuse, organs used become stronger, organs not used degenerate, acquired characteristics inherited, passed to offspring, giraffe\'s neck stretched, longer neck passed to offspring, disproven, acquired characteristics not inherited, only changes in germ cells, sperm, egg, Lamarck recognized evolution occurs, environment plays role, Darwinism, Charles Darwin, 1859, evolution by natural selection, observations, overproduction, more offspring produced than can survive, variation, individuals in population vary, competition, struggle for existence, limited resources, inferences, survival of the fittest, individuals with favorable variations, more likely survive, more likely reproduce, inheritance of favorable traits, advantageous variations passed to offspring, over generations, accumulation of favorable traits, leads to evolution, adaptation, peppered moth, dark form common in polluted areas, better camouflaged on soot-covered trees, survived better, reproduced more, evidence, Darwin\'s finches, different beak shapes on different islands, modern synthesis, neo-Darwinism, combines Darwin\'s natural selection with genetics, Mendel, population genetics, mutation theory, genetic variation from mutations, recombination, crossing over, independent assortment, natural selection acts on phenotypic variation, evolution equals change in allele frequencies, population over generations, speciation when populations become reproductively isolated, mutation theory, Hugo de Vries, mutations, sudden changes in genes, raw material for evolution, most mutations harmful, rare beneficial mutations selected, contributions, Gregor Mendel, laws of inheritance, Thomas Hunt Morgan, chromosome theory, mutations in fruit flies, Ronald Fisher, J.B.S. Haldane, Sewall Wright, population genetics, mathematical models of evolution, Theodosius Dobzhansky, Genetics and the Origin of Species, 1937, integrated genetics and natural selection, Ernst Mayr, species concept, allopatric speciation', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(195, '30. EVOLUTION', 'Evolutionary Trends', 'Evolution shows certain broad patterns and trends over geological time. (1) Simple to complex organisms: (a) Early life: Prokaryotes (simple, unicellular) appeared ~3.5 billion years ago. (b) Eukaryotes (complex cells with nucleus, organelles) appeared ~2 billion years ago. (c) Multicellular organisms appeared ~600 million years ago. (d) Increasing complexity over time (but not all lineages become complex - many remain simple). (2) Aquatic to terrestrial adaptations: (a) Life originated in water. (b) Colonization of land required adaptations: Lungs (for air breathing), limbs (for support against gravity), waterproof skin (to prevent desiccation), amniotic eggs (reproduction on land). (c) Examples: Fish → amphibians → reptiles → birds/mammals show progressive adaptations for terrestrial life. (3) Increasing specialization: (a) Generalists (broad niche) evolve into specialists (narrow niche) when environment stable. (b) Examples: Teeth differentiation (incisors, canines, premolars, molars for different functions); digestive system complexity; sensory system specialization. (4) Adaptive radiation: (a) Rapid diversification from common ancestor into new ecological niches. (b) Occurs when new opportunities arise (new habitats, extinction of competitors, key innovations). (c) Examples: Darwin\'s finches (14 species with different beak shapes adapted to different food sources on Galapagos). Mammals after dinosaur extinction (diversified into many forms: bats, whales, primates, rodents). Hawaiian honeycreepers (many species from one finch ancestor). Cichlid fish in African lakes (hundreds of species with diverse feeding adaptations). (5) Convergent evolution: (a) Unrelated species evolve similar traits due to similar environmental pressures. (b) Examples: Wings in birds, bats, insects (all for flight but different origins). Streamlined body in fish, dolphins, ichthyosaurs (for swimming). (6) Coevolution: (a) Two species evolve in response to each other. (b) Examples: Predator-prey (cheetah speed, gazelle speed); plant-pollinator (flower shape and pollinator mouthparts); host-parasite. (7) Extinction: (a) Most species that ever lived are extinct. (b) Mass extinctions (e.g., dinosaurs 65 million years ago) create opportunities for surviving groups to diversify.', 'evolutionary trends, simple to complex organisms, early life, prokaryotes, simple, unicellular, 3.5 billion years ago, eukaryotes, complex cells, nucleus, organelles, 2 billion years ago, multicellular organisms, 600 million years ago, increasing complexity over time, not all lineages become complex, many remain simple, aquatic to terrestrial adaptations, life originated in water, colonization of land, adaptations, lungs, air breathing, limbs, support against gravity, waterproof skin, prevent desiccation, amniotic eggs, reproduction on land, fish to amphibians to reptiles to birds to mammals, progressive adaptations for terrestrial life, increasing specialization, generalists, broad niche, evolve into specialists, narrow niche, environment stable, teeth differentiation, incisors, canines, premolars, molars, different functions, digestive system complexity, sensory system specialization, adaptive radiation, rapid diversification, common ancestor, new ecological niches, new opportunities, new habitats, extinction of competitors, key innovations, Darwin\'s finches, 14 species, different beak shapes, adapted to different food sources, Galapagos, mammals after dinosaur extinction, diversified into many forms, bats, whales, primates, rodents, Hawaiian honeycreepers, many species from one finch ancestor, cichlid fish, African lakes, hundreds of species, diverse feeding adaptations, convergent evolution, unrelated species evolve similar traits, similar environmental pressures, wings in birds, wings in bats, wings in insects, all for flight, different origins, streamlined body, fish, dolphins, ichthyosaurs, for swimming, coevolution, two species evolve in response to each other, predator-prey, cheetah speed, gazelle speed, plant-pollinator, flower shape, pollinator mouthparts, host-parasite, extinction, most species that ever lived are extinct, mass extinctions, dinosaurs 65 million years ago, create opportunities, surviving groups diversify', '2026-02-27 15:40:30'),
(196, '30. EVOLUTION', 'Role of Mutation', 'Mutations are changes in DNA sequence that provide the raw material for evolution. (1) Definition: Sudden, heritable change in genetic material. (2) Types of mutations: (a) Gene mutations (point mutations): Changes in single nucleotides. Substitution (one base replaced by another), insertion (extra base added), deletion (base removed). (b) Chromosomal mutations: Changes in chromosome structure or number. Deletion (chromosome segment lost), duplication (segment repeated), inversion (segment reversed), translocation (segment moved to another chromosome). (c) Genome mutations: Change in chromosome number. Aneuploidy (extra or missing chromosome - e.g., Down syndrome, trisomy 21). Polyploidy (extra sets of chromosomes - common in plants). (3) Causes of mutations: (a) Spontaneous: Errors during DNA replication (about 1 in 10⁹ nucleotides). (b) Induced: Mutagens - radiation (UV, X-rays, gamma rays), chemicals (certain dyes, nitrous acid, benzene), viruses. (4) Effects of mutations: (a) Most mutations are neutral (no effect on fitness) - occur in non-coding DNA or silent mutations (no amino acid change). (b) Some are harmful: Cause genetic disorders (sickle cell anemia, cystic fibrosis, cancer). (c) Rare beneficial mutations: Provide advantage in certain environments. (5) Role in evolution: (a) Source of new genetic variation (new alleles). (b) Without mutation, all individuals would be genetically identical (except recombination). (c) Beneficial mutations can be spread by natural selection. (d) Examples: Sickle cell mutation (harmful in homozygotes, but beneficial in heterozygotes in malaria regions). Antibiotic resistance in bacteria (mutations confer resistance, selected for by antibiotic use). Industrial melanism in peppered moth (mutation for dark color spread in polluted areas). (6) Mutation rate: Generally low, but populations have many individuals, so new mutations arise constantly. (7) Recombination (crossing over, independent assortment) also generates variation, but only reshuffles existing alleles, doesn\'t create new ones.', 'mutation, changes in DNA sequence, raw material for evolution, sudden heritable change, genetic material, gene mutations, point mutations, changes in single nucleotides, substitution, one base replaced by another, insertion, extra base added, deletion, base removed, chromosomal mutations, changes in chromosome structure, chromosome number, deletion, chromosome segment lost, duplication, segment repeated, inversion, segment reversed, translocation, segment moved to another chromosome, genome mutations, change in chromosome number, aneuploidy, extra chromosome, missing chromosome, Down syndrome, trisomy 21, polyploidy, extra sets of chromosomes, common in plants, causes of mutations, spontaneous mutations, errors during DNA replication, 1 in 10⁹ nucleotides, induced mutations, mutagens, radiation, UV, X-rays, gamma rays, chemicals, dyes, nitrous acid, benzene, viruses, effects of mutations, most mutations neutral, no effect on fitness, non-coding DNA, silent mutations, no amino acid change, some harmful, cause genetic disorders, sickle cell anemia, cystic fibrosis, cancer, rare beneficial mutations, provide advantage, certain environments, role in evolution, source of new genetic variation, new alleles, without mutation, all individuals genetically identical, except recombination, beneficial mutations spread by natural selection, sickle cell mutation, harmful in homozygotes, beneficial in heterozygotes, malaria regions, antibiotic resistance in bacteria, mutations confer resistance, selected by antibiotic use, industrial melanism in peppered moth, mutation for dark color spread in polluted areas, mutation rate, generally low, populations have many individuals, new mutations arise constantly, recombination, crossing over, independent assortment, also generates variation, but only reshuffles existing alleles, doesn\'t create new ones', '2026-02-27 15:40:30');
