(1407, 14, 'SCHEME OF EXAMINATION', 'General', 'There will be two papers - Paper 1 and Paper 2, both of which will constitute a composite paper to be taken at one sitting.', 'examination scheme, papers, Paper 1, Paper 2, composite paper, examination structure'),
(1408, 14, 'SCHEME OF EXAMINATION', 'Paper 1', 'Will comprise fifty multiple choice questions, all of which should be answered in 1 hour for 25 marks.', 'Paper 1, multiple choice, objective questions, 50 questions, 1 hour, 25 marks'),
(1409, 14, 'SCHEME OF EXAMINATION', 'Paper 2', 'Will be made up of two sections, Sections A and B and will last 2½ hours.', 'Paper 2, Section A, Section B, 2½ hours'),
(1410, 14, 'SCHEME OF EXAMINATION', 'Paper 2 - Section A', 'Will contain four essay questions on Theory of Financial Accounting. Candidates will be required to answer two out of the four questions for 15 marks each.', 'Section A, essay questions, theory of accounting, 4 questions, answer two, 15 marks each'),
(1411, 14, 'SCHEME OF EXAMINATION', 'Paper 2 - Section B', 'Will contain five essay questions on Financial Accounting Practice. Candidates will be required to answer three out of the questions for 15 marks each.', 'Section B, essay questions, accounting practice, 5 questions, answer three, 15 marks each'),
(1412, 14, '1. INTRODUCTION TO FINANCIAL ACCOUNTING', '1.1 History, nature and functions of Accounting', 'Historical development, nature and roles of accounting in business. Evolution from simple record-keeping to complex financial reporting.', 'accounting history, accounting evolution, nature of accounting, functions of accounting, record-keeping, financial reporting, stewardship, decision-making'),
(1413, 14, '1. INTRODUCTION TO FINANCIAL ACCOUNTING', '1.2 Users of Accounting information', 'Internal and external users of accounting information. Management, owners, investors, creditors, government, employees, public.', 'accounting users, internal users, management, employees, external users, investors, shareholders, creditors, lenders, government, tax authorities, customers, public, stakeholders'),
(1414, 14, '1. INTRODUCTION TO FINANCIAL ACCOUNTING', '1.3 Stages in the Accounting process', 'Steps involved in the accounting cycle. Identifying, recording, classifying, summarizing, analyzing, interpreting, communicating.', 'accounting process, accounting cycle, stages, identifying transactions, recording, journalizing, classifying, posting, summarizing, trial balance, financial statements, analyzing, interpreting, communicating'),
(1415, 14, '1. INTRODUCTION TO FINANCIAL ACCOUNTING', '1.4 Characteristics of Accounting information', 'Qualitative characteristics of useful accounting information. Relevance, reliability, comparability, understandability, timeliness, faithful representation.', 'accounting characteristics, qualitative characteristics, relevance, reliability, comparability, understandability, timeliness, faithful representation, materiality, consistency, prudence'),
(1416, 14, '2. THE ACCOUNTING EQUATION AND DOUBLE ENTRY PRINCIPLES', '2.1 Accounting Equation', 'Assets = Capital + Liabilities - the foundation of double entry. Relationship between assets, liabilities and owner\'s equity.', 'accounting equation, assets = capital + liabilities, assets = liabilities + equity, balance sheet equation, fundamental accounting equation, double entry foundation'),
(1417, 14, '2. THE ACCOUNTING EQUATION AND DOUBLE ENTRY PRINCIPLES', '2.2 Purpose and functions of source documents', 'Source documents: invoices, receipts, credit notes, etc. and their uses. Evidence of transactions.', 'source documents, invoices, sales invoice, purchase invoice, receipts, credit notes, debit notes, payment vouchers, bank statements, cheques, evidence, transaction proof'),
(1418, 14, '2. THE ACCOUNTING EQUATION AND DOUBLE ENTRY PRINCIPLES', '2.3 Subsidiary books', 'Books of original entry: sales journal, purchases journal, returns journals, etc. Recording transactions before ledger.', 'subsidiary books, books of original entry, journals, sales journal, sales day book, purchases journal, purchases day book, returns inwards journal, returns outwards journal, cash book, petty cash book, general journal'),
(1419, 14, '2. THE ACCOUNTING EQUATION AND DOUBLE ENTRY PRINCIPLES', '2.4 The ledger: classification of Accounts', 'Classification of accounts into personal, real and nominal. Ledger accounts, posting from journals.', 'ledger, ledger accounts, classification of accounts, personal accounts, real accounts, nominal accounts, posting, T-accounts, general ledger'),
(1420, 14, '2. THE ACCOUNTING EQUATION AND DOUBLE ENTRY PRINCIPLES', '2.5 Cash Book', 'analytical cash book, including petty cash book. Recording cash transactions.', 'cash book, analytical cash book, two-column cash book, three-column cash book, petty cash book, imprest system, cash transactions, bank transactions, discounts'),
(1421, 14, '2. THE ACCOUNTING EQUATION AND DOUBLE ENTRY PRINCIPLES', '2.6 Preparation of Trial Balance', 'Listing of all ledger balances to test arithmetical accuracy. Debit and credit totals must agree.', 'trial balance, preparation, ledger balances, debit balances, credit balances, arithmetical accuracy, total debits = total credits, suspense account'),
(1422, 14, '2. THE ACCOUNTING EQUATION AND DOUBLE ENTRY PRINCIPLES', '2.7 Bank Reconciliation Statements', 'Reconciling cash book balance with bank statement balance. Causes of differences: unpresented cheques, uncredited lodgments, bank charges, direct debits.', 'bank reconciliation, bank reconciliation statement, cash book balance, bank statement balance, unpresented cheques, uncredited lodgments, outstanding cheques, bank charges, direct debits, standing orders, discrepancies'),
(1423, 14, '2. THE ACCOUNTING EQUATION AND DOUBLE ENTRY PRINCIPLES', '2.8 Correction of errors and Suspense Account', 'Types of errors and use of suspense account to correct discrepancies. Errors not affecting trial balance, errors affecting trial balance.', 'error correction, suspense account, types of errors, errors of omission, errors of commission, errors of principle, compensating errors, complete reversal of entries, trial balance errors, journal entries for correction'),
(1424, 14, '3. ACCOUNTING CONCEPTS', '3.1 Meaning', 'Definition and explanation of accounting concepts. Fundamental assumptions underlying financial statements.', 'accounting concepts meaning, definition, fundamental assumptions, accounting principles, theoretical framework'),
(1425, 14, '3. ACCOUNTING CONCEPTS', '3.2 Types', 'Going concern, accruals, consistency, prudence, materiality, business entity, realization, duality, money measurement, historical cost, periodicity.', 'accounting concepts types, going concern concept, accruals concept, matching concept, consistency concept, prudence concept, conservatism, materiality concept, business entity concept, separate entity, realization concept, duality concept, double entry, money measurement concept, historical cost concept, periodicity concept, accounting period'),
(1426, 14, '3. ACCOUNTING CONCEPTS', '3.3 Significance', 'Importance of accounting concepts in preparing financial statements. Standardization, comparability, reliability.', 'accounting concepts significance, importance, financial statements preparation, standardization, comparability, reliability, consistency, true and fair view'),
