(1447, 14, '7. CONTROL ACCOUNTS AND SELF-BALANCING LEDGERS', '7.5 Reconciliation of Control Accounts', 'Reconciling control accounts with subsidiary ledgers. Ensuring agreement between totals and individual accounts.', 'control account reconciliation, subsidiary ledger agreement, schedule of debtors, schedule of creditors, list of balances, discrepancies, error correction'),
(1448, 14, '8. SINGLE ENTRY AND INCOMPLETE RECORDS', '8.1 Meaning and limitations', 'Definition and drawbacks of single entry system. Incomplete records, no double entry, limited information.', 'single entry meaning, incomplete records, drawbacks, limitations, no double entry, partial records, lack of completeness, no trial balance, difficulty in ascertaining profit'),
(1449, 14, '8. SINGLE ENTRY AND INCOMPLETE RECORDS', '8.2 Computation of profit or loss from opening and closing balance sheets', 'Determining profit from changes in capital. Profit = closing capital - opening capital + drawings - additional capital.', 'profit computation, opening capital, closing capital, statement of affairs, change in capital, drawings, additional capital, net profit, net loss'),
(1450, 14, '8. SINGLE ENTRY AND INCOMPLETE RECORDS', '8.3 Conversion of single entry to double entry', 'Converting incomplete records to double entry system. Reconstructing accounts, completing records.', 'conversion to double entry, single to double entry, reconstructing accounts, completing records, finding missing figures, deriving ledger accounts'),
(1451, 14, '8. SINGLE ENTRY AND INCOMPLETE RECORDS', '8.4 Preparation of final accounts from a set of incomplete records', 'Preparing final accounts from incomplete information. Using available data to prepare trading, profit and loss, balance sheet.', 'final accounts from incomplete records, trading account preparation, profit and loss account, balance sheet, reconstructing sales, reconstructing purchases, missing information techniques'),
(1452, 14, '8. SINGLE ENTRY AND INCOMPLETE RECORDS', '8.5 Mark up and Margin', 'Understanding and calculating mark-up and margin. Mark-up = profit/cost × 100%. Margin = profit/selling price × 100%.', 'mark up, margin, profit on cost, profit on sales, gross profit percentage, cost plus pricing, selling price calculation'),
