(1522, 4, '1. CLASSIFICATION', 'Differences Between Plants and Animals', 'Plants and animals have fundamental differences in nutrition, structure, and behavior. Plants are autotrophic (produce their own food through photosynthesis), have cell walls made of cellulose, and exhibit unlimited growth. Animals are heterotrophic (consume other organisms for food), lack cell walls, and have limited growth.', 'plants vs animals, autotrophic, heterotrophic, cell wall, cellulose, unlimited growth, limited growth, photosynthesis, locomotion'),
(1523, 4, '1. CLASSIFICATION', 'Kingdom Animalia', 'Kingdom Animalia consists of multicellular, eukaryotic organisms that are heterotrophic (obtain food by consuming other organisms). Most animals are motile at some life stage and have specialized tissues. Animals are classified into invertebrates (no backbone) and vertebrates (have backbone).', 'Animalia, multicellular, heterotrophic, ingestion, motile, symmetry, invertebrate, vertebrate, tissues, organs'),
(1524, 4, '1. CLASSIFICATION', 'Kingdom Fungi', 'Kingdom Fungi includes eukaryotic, heterotrophic organisms that absorb nutrients from dead or decaying organic matter. They have cell walls made of chitin. Examples include mushrooms, molds, and yeasts. Fungi reproduce through spores and can be unicellular (yeast) or multicellular (mushrooms).', 'Fungi, eukaryotes, heterotrophic, chitin, hyphae, septate, coenocytic, mycelium, spores, mushroom, yeast, mold, decomposition'),
(1525, 4, '1. CLASSIFICATION', 'Kingdom Monera', 'Kingdom Monera comprises all prokaryotic organisms (lacking a true nucleus and membrane-bound organelles). This kingdom includes bacteria and cyanobacteria (blue-green algae). They are unicellular and reproduce asexually through binary fission. Some are autotrophic (photosynthetic or chemosynthetic), others are heterotrophic.', 'Monera, prokaryotes, bacteria, cyanobacteria, blue-green algae, unicellular, nucleoid, peptidoglycan, binary fission, autotrophic, heterotrophic, pathogenic, beneficial'),
(1526, 4, '1. CLASSIFICATION', 'Kingdom Plantae', 'Kingdom Plantae consists of multicellular, eukaryotic organisms that are autotrophic (produce their own food through photosynthesis). They have cell walls made of cellulose and contain chlorophyll. Plants are classified into non-vascular (bryophytes) and vascular (pteridophytes, gymnosperms, angiosperms).', 'Plantae, multicellular, autotrophic, cellulose, chlorophyll a, chlorophyll b, photosynthesis, vascular, non-vascular, bryophyta, pteridophyta, gymnosperm, angiosperm, monocot, dicot'),
(1527, 4, '1. CLASSIFICATION', 'Kingdom Protista', 'Kingdom Protista is a diverse group of mostly unicellular eukaryotic organisms that do not fit into other kingdoms. They include protozoa (animal-like), algae (plant-like), and slime molds (fungus-like). Some are autotrophic, some heterotrophic, and some are mixotrophic.', 'Protista, protocista, eukaryotes, unicellular, paraphyletic, Rhizopoda, Amoeba, pseudopodia, cytoplasmic streaming, phagocytosis, Zoomastigina, Trypanosoma, sleeping sickness, tsetse fly, Leishmania, Ciliophora, Paramecium, cilia, Apicomplexa, Plasmodium, malaria, algae, Chlorophyta, Chlamydomonas, Volvox, Spirogyra, Euglena, mixotrophic, endosymbiosis'),
(1528, 4, '1. CLASSIFICATION', 'Living and Non-living Things', 'Living organisms are distinguished by seven fundamental life processes collectively remembered as MRS GREN: Movement (change in position or place), Respiration (chemical reactions releasing energy from food), Sensitivity (detecting and responding to stimuli), Growth (permanent increase in size and complexity), Reproduction (producing offspring), Excretion (removing metabolic wastes), and Nutrition (acquiring and processing food). Non-living things do not exhibit these integrated processes.', 'MRS GREN, movement, respiration, sensitivity, growth, reproduction, excretion, nutrition, viruses, acellular, obligate parasites, evolution, metabolism, stimuli, homeostasis, prions, viroids'),
(1529, 4, '2. ORGANIZATION OF LIFE', 'Levels of Organization - Cell Level', 'The cell is the basic structural and functional unit of all living organisms. Unicellular organisms like Amoeba, Euglena, and Paramecium carry out all life processes within a single cell. These organisms demonstrate how a single cell can perform all functions necessary for life including nutrition, respiration, excretion, and reproduction.', 'cell level, unicellular, Amoeba proteus, Euglena viridis, Paramecium caudatum, basic unit of life, cell theory, independent organism'),
(1530, 4, '2. ORGANIZATION OF LIFE', 'Levels of Organization - Tissue Level', 'A tissue is a group of similar cells that work together to perform a specific function. In animals, examples include epithelial tissue, connective tissue, muscle tissue, and nervous tissue. In plants, tissues include meristematic tissue, parenchyma, collenchyma, sclerenchyma, xylem, and phloem.', 'tissue level, specialized cells, epithelial tissue, connective tissue, muscle tissue, nervous tissue, meristem, parenchyma, collenchyma, sclerenchyma, xylem, phloem'),
(1531, 4, '2. ORGANIZATION OF LIFE', 'Levels of Organization - Organ Level', 'An organ is a structure made up of different tissues working together to perform a specific function. Examples in animals include the heart, liver, kidney, and stomach. In plants, storage organs include bulbs (onion), rhizomes (ginger), and corms (cocoyam).', 'organ level, bulb, onion, garlic, scale leaves, stem, rhizome, ginger, turmeric, corm, heart, liver, kidney, stomach, specialized function'),
(1532, 4, '2. ORGANIZATION OF LIFE', 'Levels of Organization - System Level', 'A system consists of multiple organs working together to perform major body functions. Examples include the digestive system (mouth, esophagus, stomach, intestines), respiratory system (lungs, trachea), circulatory system (heart, blood vessels), and excretory system (kidneys, bladder).', 'system level, organ system, digestive system, mouth, esophagus, stomach, small intestine, large intestine, respiratory system, lungs, trachea, circulatory system, heart, blood vessels, excretory system, kidneys, bladder'),
(1533, 4, '2. ORGANIZATION OF LIFE', 'Volume/Surface Area Ratio', 'The volume-to-surface area ratio (SA:V ratio) is critical for efficient exchange of materials. As organisms increase in size, their volume increases faster than surface area. This limits the size of single cells and simple organisms. Larger organisms need specialized transport systems because diffusion alone is insufficient for nutrient and waste exchange.', 'SA:V ratio, volume/surface area ratio, diffusion limit, metabolic demand, waste removal, nutrient uptake, cell size limitation, transport systems'),
(1534, 4, '3. FORMS OF CELL EXISTENCE', 'Single and Free-living Cells', 'Single-celled organisms (unicellular) carry out all life processes within one cell. Examples include Amoeba (moves by pseudopodia, feeds by phagocytosis), Euglena (has chloroplasts for photosynthesis, flagellum for movement), Paramecium (covered with cilia for movement and feeding), and Chlamydomonas (green alga with two flagella).', 'single cells, free-living, unicellular, Amoeba proteus, pseudopodia, cytoplasmic streaming, phagocytosis, Euglena viridis, flagellum, eyespot, Paramecium caudatum, cilia, oral groove, contractile vacuole, Chlamydomonas, chloroplast, pyrenoid'),
(1535, 4, '3. FORMS OF CELL EXISTENCE', 'Colonial Organization', 'Colonial organisms are groups of similar cells that live together but can survive independently. Each cell performs all life functions, but they benefit from living in a colony. Volvox is a colonial green alga where cells are arranged in a hollow sphere, with some cells specialized for reproduction.', 'colonial organization, Volvox, colonial alga, hollow sphere, somatic cells, reproductive cells, independence, cooperation, evolutionary intermediate'),
(1536, 4, '3. FORMS OF CELL EXISTENCE', 'Filamentous Organization', 'Filamentous organisms consist of cells arranged in long chains or threads. Spirogyra is a filamentous green alga where cylindrical cells are joined end-to-end in unbranched filaments. The filaments show some specialization but each cell can still perform basic functions independently.', 'filamentous organization, Spirogyra, unbranched filaments, cylindrical cells, end-to-end arrangement, spiral chloroplasts, conjugation, cell specialization'),
(1537, 4, '4. CELL STRUCTURE AND FUNCTIONS', 'Cell Wall', 'The cell wall is a rigid structure found outside the cell membrane in plant cells, bacteria, fungi, and some protists. It provides structural support, protection, and determines cell shape. Plant cell walls are made of cellulose, hemicellulose, and pectin. Bacterial cell walls contain peptidoglycan. Fungal cell walls contain chitin.', 'cell wall, cellulose, microfibrils, hemicellulose, pectin, primary wall, secondary wall, middle lamella, plasmodesmata, turgor pressure, structural support, protection'),
(1538, 4, '4. CELL STRUCTURE AND FUNCTIONS', 'Cell Membrane (Plasma Membrane)', 'The cell membrane is a selectively permeable barrier that surrounds the cell. It controls the movement of substances into and out of the cell. The fluid mosaic model describes the membrane as a phospholipid bilayer with embedded proteins. Functions include transport, cell signaling, and cell adhesion.', 'cell membrane, plasma membrane, phospholipid bilayer, fluid mosaic model, Singer Nicolson, selectively permeable, diffusion, osmosis, active transport, membrane proteins, receptors, channels, pumps'),
(1539, 4, '4. CELL STRUCTURE AND FUNCTIONS', 'Nucleus', 'The nucleus is the control center of the cell, containing the cell\'s genetic material (DNA). It is enclosed by a double membrane called the nuclear envelope, which has nuclear pores for material exchange. The nucleus contains chromatin (DNA and proteins) and the nucleolus (site of ribosome synthesis).', 'nucleus, nuclear envelope, double membrane, nuclear pores, chromatin, DNA, histones, nucleolus, rRNA synthesis, ribosome assembly, genetic material, control center'),
(1540, 4, '4. CELL STRUCTURE AND FUNCTIONS', 'Cytoplasm', 'Cytoplasm is the jelly-like substance filling the cell between the cell membrane and nucleus. It consists of cytosol (fluid) and organelles. The cytoplasm is the site of many metabolic reactions, including glycolysis and protein synthesis. It contains dissolved ions, nutrients, and enzymes.', 'cytoplasm, cytosol, organelles, cytoskeleton, ions, metabolic reactions, glycolysis, protein synthesis, nutrient storage, waste products, cytoplasmic streaming, cyclosis'),
(1541, 4, '4. CELL STRUCTURE AND FUNCTIONS', 'Mitochondria', 'Mitochondria are the powerhouses of the cell, responsible for aerobic respiration and ATP production. They have a double membrane: the outer membrane is smooth, while the inner membrane is folded into cristae to increase surface area. The matrix contains enzymes for the Krebs cycle. Mitochondria contain their own DNA and reproduce independently.', 'mitochondria, powerhouse of cell, aerobic respiration, ATP synthesis, double membrane, outer membrane, inner membrane, cristae, matrix, Krebs cycle, electron transport chain, own DNA, endosymbiotic theory'),
(1542, 4, '4. CELL STRUCTURE AND FUNCTIONS', 'Chloroplasts', 'Chloroplasts are organelles found in plant cells and algae that carry out photosynthesis. They have a double membrane and contain thylakoids (stacked into grana) where light reactions occur, and stroma where the Calvin cycle occurs. Chloroplasts contain chlorophyll, the green pigment that captures light energy.', 'chloroplasts, photosynthesis, plastids, chromoplasts, leucoplasts, amyloplasts, double membrane, thylakoid, grana, stroma, chlorophyll, light reactions, Calvin cycle, own DNA, endosymbiotic theory'),
(1543, 4, '4. CELL STRUCTURE AND FUNCTIONS', 'Endoplasmic Reticulum (ER)', 'The endoplasmic reticulum is a network of membranes involved in protein and lipid synthesis. Rough ER has ribosomes attached and is involved in protein synthesis and modification. Smooth ER lacks ribosomes and is involved in lipid synthesis, detoxification, and calcium storage.', 'endoplasmic reticulum, ER, rough ER, RER, smooth ER, SER, cisternae, ribosomes, protein synthesis, protein folding, glycosylation, lipid synthesis, detoxification, calcium storage'),
(1544, 4, '4. CELL STRUCTURE AND FUNCTIONS', 'Ribosomes', 'Ribosomes are small organelles responsible for protein synthesis (translation). They are composed of rRNA and proteins. Ribosomes can be free in the cytoplasm (synthesizing proteins for use within the cell) or attached to rough ER (synthesizing proteins for secretion or membrane insertion).', 'ribosomes, rRNA, ribosomal proteins, translation, protein synthesis, free ribosomes, bound ribosomes, polyribosomes, large subunit, small subunit, peptide bond formation'),
(1545, 4, '4. CELL STRUCTURE AND FUNCTIONS', 'Golgi Apparatus (Golgi Body)', 'The Golgi apparatus modifies, sorts, and packages proteins for transport to their final destinations. It consists of stacked flattened sacs called cisternae. Proteins from the ER enter the cis face, are modified (e.g., glycosylation), and exit from the trans face in vesicles.', 'Golgi apparatus, Golgi body, cisternae, cis-Golgi network, medial-Golgi, trans-Golgi network, vesicle transport, protein modification, glycosylation, sorting, packaging, secretion'),
(1546, 4, '4. CELL STRUCTURE AND FUNCTIONS', 'Lysosomes', 'Lysosomes are membrane-bound organelles containing hydrolytic enzymes that break down waste materials, cellular debris, and foreign pathogens. They function in intracellular digestion, autophagy (breaking down old organelles), and apoptosis (programmed cell death).', 'lysosomes, hydrolytic enzymes, acid hydrolases, acidic pH, intracellular digestion, phagocytosis, autophagy, apoptosis, suicidal bags, waste removal, pathogen destruction'),
(1547, 4, '4. CELL STRUCTURE AND FUNCTIONS', 'Centrosomes', 'The centrosome is the microtubule-organizing center (MTOC) in animal cells. It contains a pair of centrioles arranged perpendicular to each other. Centrosomes play a key role in cell division by organizing the mitotic spindle that separates chromosomes.', 'centrosome, MTOC, microtubule-organizing center, animal cells, centrioles, pericentriolar material, PCM, spindle formation, cell division, chromosome separation'),
(1548, 4, '4. CELL STRUCTURE AND FUNCTIONS', 'Chromosomes', 'Chromosomes are thread-like structures in the nucleus that carry genetic information. They are made of DNA wrapped around histone proteins to form nucleosomes. Chromosomes become visible during cell division when they condense. Each species has a characteristic number of chromosomes.', 'chromosomes, DNA, histones, nucleosomes, genes, heredity, sister chromatids, centromere, telomere, chromatin, chromosome condensation, karyotype'),
(1549, 4, '4. CELL STRUCTURE AND FUNCTIONS', 'Similarities and Differences: Plant vs Animal Cells', 'Plant and animal cells share many organelles (nucleus, mitochondria, ER, Golgi, ribosomes) but have key differences. Plant cells have a cell wall, chloroplasts, and large central vacuole. Animal cells have centrioles and lysosomes, and store glycogen instead of starch.', 'plant cell, animal cell, similarities, differences, cell wall, cellulose, chloroplast, photosynthesis, central vacuole, tonoplast, centriole, lysosome, glycogen, starch, plasmodesmata, gap junctions'),
(1550, 4, '5. CELL AND ITS ENVIRONMENT', 'Diffusion', 'Diffusion is the net movement of molecules from an area of higher concentration to an area of lower concentration, driven by the kinetic energy of molecules. It is a passive process (no energy required) and continues until equilibrium is reached. Diffusion is important for gas exchange (O2 and CO2) and movement of small molecules.', 'diffusion, passive transport, concentration gradient, net movement, kinetic energy, equilibrium, simple diffusion, facilitated diffusion, gas exchange, oxygen, carbon dioxide, small molecules'),
(1551, 4, '5. CELL AND ITS ENVIRONMENT', 'Osmosis', 'Osmosis is the net movement of water molecules across a selectively permeable membrane from an area of higher water potential (lower solute concentration) to an area of lower water potential (higher solute concentration). It is a passive process important for maintaining cell turgor and water balance.', 'osmosis, water movement, selectively permeable membrane, water potential, Ψ, solute concentration, hypotonic, hypertonic, isotonic, turgor pressure, plasmolysis, hemolysis'),
(1552, 4, '5. CELL AND ITS ENVIRONMENT', 'Active Transport', 'Active transport is the movement of molecules across a cell membrane against their concentration gradient (from low to high concentration). This process requires energy (ATP) and specific carrier proteins. Examples include the sodium-potassium pump (Na+/K+ ATPase) and uptake of glucose in the intestines.', 'active transport, concentration gradient, energy, ATP, carrier proteins, pumps, sodium-potassium pump, Na+/K+ ATPase, endocytosis, phagocytosis, pinocytosis, exocytosis'),
(1553, 4, '6. PROPERTIES OF LIVING CELL', 'Cellular Respiration - Aerobic', 'Aerobic respiration is the process by which cells break down glucose in the presence of oxygen to produce ATP (energy), carbon dioxide, and water. The equation is C6H12O6 + 6O2 → 6CO2 + 6H2O + ATP. It occurs in three stages: glycolysis (cytoplasm), Krebs cycle (mitochondrial matrix), and electron transport chain (inner mitochondrial membrane).', 'aerobic respiration, glucose oxidation, oxygen, CO2, H2O, ATP, glycolysis, Krebs cycle, electron transport chain, mitochondria, energy production'),
(1554, 4, '6. PROPERTIES OF LIVING CELL', 'Cellular Respiration - Anaerobic', 'Anaerobic respiration (fermentation) occurs in the absence of oxygen. It produces less ATP than aerobic respiration. In animals, lactic acid fermentation produces lactate. In yeast and some bacteria, alcoholic fermentation produces ethanol and CO2. Fermentation is important in food production (bread, beer, yogurt).', 'anaerobic respiration, fermentation, absence of oxygen, ATP yield, lactic acid fermentation, lactate, alcoholic fermentation, ethanol, CO2, yeast, bacteria, food production, bread, beer, yogurt'),
(1555, 4, '6. PROPERTIES OF LIVING CELL', 'Nutrition - Autotrophic (Photosynthesis)', 'Autotrophic organisms produce their own organic compounds from inorganic substances. Photosynthetic autotrophs (plants, algae, cyanobacteria) use light energy to convert CO2 and H2O into glucose and O2. Chemoautotrophs use energy from inorganic chemical reactions (e.g., nitrifying bacteria).', 'autotrophic nutrition, photosynthesis, photoautotrophs, chemoautotrophs, light energy, chemical energy, carbon fixation, Calvin cycle, chlorophyll, chloroplasts'),
(1556, 4, '6. PROPERTIES OF LIVING CELL', 'Nutrition in Amoeba and Paramecium', 'Amoeba and Paramecium are heterotrophic protozoa that feed on smaller organisms. Amoeba uses phagocytosis: it extends pseudopodia to engulf prey, forming a food vacuole. Paramecium uses cilia to sweep food into its oral groove, forming food vacuoles. Digestive enzymes break down food; undigested waste is expelled.', 'Amoeba, Paramecium, heterotrophic, holozoic nutrition, phagocytosis, chemotaxis, pseudopodia, food vacuole, lysosome, digestive enzymes, egestion, anal pore, oral groove, cilia'),
(1557, 4, '6. PROPERTIES OF LIVING CELL', 'Nutrition in Euglena and Chlamydomonas', 'Euglena and Chlamydomonas are mixotrophic (both autotrophic and heterotrophic). They photosynthesize when light is available but can absorb organic nutrients in darkness. Euglena has chloroplasts for photosynthesis and a flagellum for movement. Chlamydomonas is a green alga with two flagella and a cup-shaped chloroplast.', 'Euglena, Chlamydomonas, mixotrophic, mixotroph, photoautotrophic, heterotrophic, chloroplasts, flagella, eyespot, light detection, pyrenoid, starch storage'),
(1558, 4, '6. PROPERTIES OF LIVING CELL', 'Reproduction - Asexual', 'Asexual reproduction involves a single parent and produces genetically identical offspring (clones). Methods include binary fission (bacteria, Amoeba, Paramecium), budding (yeast, Hydra), fragmentation (Spirogyra, starfish), spore formation (fungi, ferns), and vegetative propagation (plants).', 'asexual reproduction, single parent, clones, identical offspring, binary fission, budding, fragmentation, spore formation, vegetative propagation, stolons, rhizomes, tubers, bulbs'),
(1559, 4, '6. PROPERTIES OF LIVING CELL', 'Reproduction - Sexual', 'Sexual reproduction involves the fusion of male and female gametes (fertilization) to produce genetically diverse offspring. Gametes are produced by meiosis. Sexual reproduction generates variation, which is important for adaptation and evolution. Methods include conjugation (Paramecium, Spirogyra) and gamete fusion (animals, plants).', 'sexual reproduction, gametes fusion, two parents, genetic diversity, offspring variation, fertilization, zygote, meiosis, conjugation, Spirogyra, Paramecium, adaptation, evolution');
INSERT INTO `materials` (`id`, `subject_id`, `topic`, `sub_topic`, `note`, `keywords`) VALUES
(1560, 4, '7. TISSUES AND SUPPORTING SYSTEMS', 'Supporting Tissues in Plants', 'Plants have specialized supporting tissues that provide strength and rigidity. Collenchyma provides flexible support in young stems and leaves. Sclerenchyma (fibers and sclereids) provides rigid support and has thick lignin walls. Xylem provides both support and water transport. Turgid parenchyma also provides support when cells are filled with water.', 'plant supporting tissues, mechanical strength, flexibility, resilience, collenchyma, living cells, uneven cell walls, sclerenchyma, fibers, sclereids, lignin, xylem, turgid parenchyma'),
(1561, 4, '7. TISSUES AND SUPPORTING SYSTEMS', 'Types of Skeleton', 'Animals have three types of skeletons. Exoskeleton is an external skeleton (arthropods, mollusks) made of chitin or calcium carbonate. Endoskeleton is an internal skeleton (vertebrates) made of bone and cartilage. Hydrostatic skeleton uses fluid pressure (cnidarians, annelids).', 'exoskeleton, external skeleton, arthropods, insects, crustaceans, arachnids, chitin, mollusks, calcium carbonate, endoskeleton, internal skeleton, vertebrates, bone, cartilage, hydrostatic skeleton, fluid pressure, cnidarians, annelids'),
(1562, 4, '7. TISSUES AND SUPPORTING SYSTEMS', 'Functions of Skeleton in Animals', 'The skeleton performs several essential functions: support (maintains body shape and posture), protection (protects vital organs like brain, heart, lungs), movement (provides attachment for muscles and levers for movement), mineral storage (stores calcium and phosphorus), and blood cell production (bone marrow produces red and white blood cells).', 'skeleton functions, support, protection, brain protection, skull, heart protection, rib cage, movement, muscle attachment, mineral storage, calcium, phosphorus, blood cell production, bone marrow, hematopoiesis'),
(1563, 4, '8. TRANSPORT SYSTEM', 'Need for Transport - Surface Area/Volume Ratio', 'Transport systems are needed because diffusion alone is insufficient for larger organisms. As size increases, volume increases faster than surface area (SA:V ratio decreases). This limits nutrient uptake and waste removal. Larger organisms require specialized transport systems (circulatory system, vascular tissue) to move materials efficiently.', 'need for transport, SA:V ratio, surface area to volume ratio, diffusion limitation, metabolic demand, waste removal, nutrient uptake, transport systems, circulatory system, vascular tissue'),
(1564, 4, '8. TRANSPORT SYSTEM', 'Transport in Plants - Uptake of Water and Minerals', 'Plants absorb water and mineral salts from the soil through root hairs. Root hairs are epidermal cell extensions that increase surface area for absorption. Water enters by osmosis; minerals enter by active transport. Water and minerals move upward through xylem vessels driven by transpiration pull and root pressure.', 'plant transport, water uptake, mineral uptake, root hairs, epidermal cell extensions, surface area, osmosis, active transport, xylem, transpiration pull, cohesion-tension theory, adhesion, root pressure'),
(1565, 4, '8. TRANSPORT SYSTEM', 'Transpiration', 'Transpiration is the loss of water vapor from plant aerial parts, mainly through stomata. It drives water movement from roots to leaves (transpiration pull), cools the plant, and transports minerals. Types include stomatal (main), cuticular, and lenticular transpiration. Factors affecting transpiration include light, temperature, humidity, and wind.', 'transpiration, water vapor loss, aerial plant parts, stomata, gas exchange, photosynthesis, transpiration pull, cohesion-tension, adhesion, cooling effect, mineral transport, stomatal transpiration, cuticular transpiration, lenticular transpiration'),
(1566, 4, '8. TRANSPORT SYSTEM', 'Translocation - Movement of Organic Materials', 'Translocation is the movement of organic nutrients (sucrose, amino acids, hormones) through the phloem from sources (leaves) to sinks (roots, fruits, growing tips). The pressure flow hypothesis explains translocation: phloem loading creates high osmotic pressure, driving flow to lower pressure areas. The ringing experiment demonstrates phloem function.', 'translocation, organic nutrients transport, phloem, sucrose, amino acids, hormones, source, sink, pressure flow hypothesis, phloem loading, osmotic pressure, ringing experiment, girdling experiment'),
(1567, 4, '8. TRANSPORT SYSTEM', 'Structure of the Heart', 'The mammalian heart is a four-chambered muscular pump. It has two atria (receiving chambers) and two ventricles (pumping chambers). The right side pumps deoxygenated blood to the lungs (pulmonary circuit). The left side pumps oxygenated blood to the body (systemic circuit). Valves prevent backflow: atrioventricular valves (tricuspid, mitral) and semilunar valves (pulmonary, aortic).', 'heart structure, mammalian heart, four-chambered, double circulation, atria, ventricles, right atrium, right ventricle, left atrium, left ventricle, pulmonary circuit, systemic circuit, atrioventricular valves, tricuspid, mitral, semilunar valves, pulmonary valve, aortic valve'),
(1568, 4, '8. TRANSPORT SYSTEM', 'Arteries, Veins, and Capillaries', 'Arteries carry blood away from the heart; they have thick, muscular walls to withstand high pressure. Veins carry blood toward the heart; they have thinner walls and valves to prevent backflow. Capillaries are microscopic vessels with one-cell-thick walls that allow exchange of gases, nutrients, and wastes between blood and tissues.', 'arteries, carry blood away from heart, high pressure, thick walls, muscular, elastic, arterioles, veins, carry blood toward heart, low pressure, thin walls, valves, venules, capillaries, exchange vessels, one-cell thick, gas exchange, nutrient exchange'),
(1569, 4, '8. TRANSPORT SYSTEM', 'Composition of Blood', 'Blood is a specialized connective tissue consisting of plasma (55%) and cellular elements (45%). Plasma is mostly water with dissolved proteins, nutrients, hormones, and waste products. Cellular elements include red blood cells (erythrocytes), white blood cells (leukocytes), and platelets (thrombocytes).', 'blood composition, specialized connective tissue, cellular elements, plasma, 55% plasma, 45% cells, water, plasma proteins, albumin, globulins, fibrinogen, nutrients, hormones, waste products, erythrocytes, red blood cells, leukocytes, white blood cells, thrombocytes, platelets'),
(1570, 4, '8. TRANSPORT SYSTEM', 'Functions of Blood', 'Blood performs multiple essential functions: transport of respiratory gases (O2 to tissues, CO2 to lungs), nutrients (from digestive system), hormones (from endocrine glands), and waste products (to excretory organs). It also regulates pH and body temperature, and defends against infection (white blood cells, antibodies).', 'blood functions, transport, respiratory gases, O2 transport, lungs to tissues, CO2 transport, tissues to lungs, nutrient transport, hormone transport, waste transport, pH regulation, temperature regulation, defense, immune function, white blood cells, antibodies, clotting'),
(1571, 4, '9. RESPIRATORY SYSTEM', 'Characteristics of Respiratory Surfaces', 'Respiratory surfaces have specific characteristics for efficient gas exchange: large surface area (to maximize gas exchange), thin (one cell thick for short diffusion distance), moist (gases dissolve before diffusion), and well-ventilated (maintains concentration gradients). Examples include alveoli (lungs), gills (fish), and tracheae (insects).', 'respiratory surfaces, characteristics, large surface area, thin, one cell thick, short diffusion distance, moist, gas dissolution, well-ventilated, concentration gradient, alveoli, lungs, gills, fish, tracheae, insects'),
(1572, 4, '9. RESPIRATORY SYSTEM', 'Respiratory Organs in Mammals (Rat)', 'Mammals breathe air using lungs. Air enters through nostrils, passes through pharynx, larynx, trachea, bronchi, and bronchioles, ending in alveoli. The diaphragm and intercostal muscles create pressure changes for ventilation. Gas exchange occurs in alveoli by diffusion.', 'mammal respiration, lungs, nostrils, pharynx, larynx, trachea, bronchi, bronchioles, alveoli, diaphragm, intercostal muscles, inhalation, exhalation, gas exchange, diffusion'),
(1573, 4, '10. EXCRETORY SYSTEMS', 'Excretory Organs in Mammals', 'The kidneys are the main excretory organs in mammals. They filter blood to remove nitrogenous wastes (urea) and excess water, salts, and other substances. Urine is produced, stored in the bladder, and eliminated through the urethra. The functional unit of the kidney is the nephron.', 'mammal excretory organs, kidneys, blood filtration, urine, urea, excess salts, excess water, bladder storage, urethra, nephron, glomerulus, Bowman capsule, renal tubule, collecting duct'),
(1574, 4, '10. EXCRETORY SYSTEMS', 'Excretory Products', 'Different animals excrete different nitrogenous wastes depending on their environment. Ammonia (NH3) is highly toxic and water-soluble, excreted by aquatic animals (fish, tadpoles). Urea is less toxic, requires less water, excreted by mammals. Uric acid is non-toxic, paste-like, requires minimal water, excreted by birds, reptiles, and insects.', 'excretory products, nitrogenous waste, ammonia, NH3, toxic, soluble, water needed, aquatic animals, fish, tadpoles, urea, less toxic, mammals, uric acid, non-toxic, paste-like, minimal water, birds, reptiles, insects'),
(1575, 4, '13. HORMONAL COORDINATION', 'Endocrine Glands and Hormones', 'Endocrine glands are ductless glands that secrete hormones directly into the bloodstream. Hormones are chemical messengers that travel to target organs to regulate body functions. Major endocrine glands include pituitary (master gland), thyroid (metabolism), adrenal (stress response), pancreas (blood sugar), and gonads (reproduction).', 'endocrine glands, hormones, pituitary, master gland, growth hormone, GH, TSH, ACTH, thyroid, thyroxine, metabolism, adrenal, adrenaline, cortisol, stress response, pancreas, insulin, glucagon, blood sugar, gonads, ovaries, testes, estrogen, testosterone'),
(1576, 4, '14. NERVOUS COORDINATION', 'Structure of a Neurone', 'A neurone (nerve cell) is the functional unit of the nervous system. It consists of a cell body (soma) containing the nucleus, dendrites (receive signals), and an axon (transmits signals). The axon may be myelinated (faster transmission). Neurones communicate via synapses using neurotransmitters.', 'neurone structure, cell body, soma, nucleus, dendrites, receive impulses, axon, transmit impulses, myelin sheath, Schwann cells, nodes of Ranvier, synapse, neurotransmitters, axon terminal'),
(1577, 4, '14. NERVOUS COORDINATION', 'Reflex Actions', 'Reflex actions are automatic, rapid, involuntary responses to stimuli that protect the body from harm. They involve a reflex arc: receptor → sensory neurone → spinal cord (integration) → motor neurone → effector. Examples include knee jerk, withdrawal from hot objects, blinking, and salivation (conditioned reflex).', 'reflex actions, automatic, rapid, involuntary, protection, reflex arc, receptor, sensory neurone, spinal cord, integration, motor neurone, effector, knee jerk, withdrawal reflex, blinking, conditioned reflex, salivation'),
(1578, 4, '15. SENSE ORGANS', 'Structure of the Eye', 'The eye is the organ of sight. The sclera (white outer layer) protects the eye. The cornea (transparent front) focuses light. The iris controls pupil size. The lens focuses light onto the retina. The retina contains photoreceptors (rods for dim light, cones for color). The optic nerve transmits signals to the brain.', 'eye structure, sclera, cornea, choroid, blood vessels, iris, pupil, ciliary body, lens, suspensory ligaments, retina, rods, cones, fovea, optic disc, optic nerve, vitreous humor, aqueous humor'),
(1579, 4, '15. SENSE ORGANS', 'Function of the Eye', 'The eye functions by refracting (bending) light to focus images on the retina. The cornea does most refraction. The lens adjusts shape (accommodation) to focus near or distant objects. Photoreceptors convert light into electrical signals. The brain interprets these signals as images.', 'eye function, light entry, cornea, pupil, iris regulation, lens focusing, retina, photoreceptors, rods, cones, accommodation, near vision, distant vision, image formation, signal transmission, optic nerve'),
(1580, 4, '15. SENSE ORGANS', 'Eye Defects and Corrections', 'Common eye defects include myopia (nearsightedness), where distant objects are blurry; corrected with concave lenses. Hypermetropia (farsightedness), where near objects are blurry; corrected with convex lenses. Astigmatism (irregular cornea curvature) causes distorted vision; corrected with cylindrical lenses. Presbyopia (age-related loss of accommodation) requires bifocal lenses.', 'eye defects, myopia, nearsightedness, concave lenses, diverging lenses, hypermetropia, farsightedness, convex lenses, converging lenses, astigmatism, irregular cornea, cylindrical lenses, presbyopia, age-related, bifocal lenses'),
(1581, 4, '16. REPRODUCTIVE SYSTEM OF MAMMALS', 'Male Reproductive System', 'The male reproductive system produces sperm and male sex hormones. The testes produce sperm and testosterone. The epididymis stores sperm. The vas deferens transports sperm. The seminal vesicles and prostate gland produce seminal fluid. The urethra carries semen and urine. The penis delivers sperm to the female.', 'male reproductive system, testes, scrotum, temperature regulation, sperm production, spermatogenesis, testosterone, epididymis, sperm storage, vas deferens, seminal vesicle, prostate gland, seminal fluid, semen, urethra, penis'),
(1582, 4, '16. REPRODUCTIVE SYSTEM OF MAMMALS', 'Female Reproductive System', 'The female reproductive system produces eggs (ova), female sex hormones (estrogen, progesterone), and supports fetal development. The ovaries produce eggs and hormones. The fallopian tubes (oviducts) transport eggs; fertilization occurs here. The uterus houses the developing fetus. The cervix connects uterus to vagina. The vagina receives sperm and serves as birth canal.', 'female reproductive system, ovaries, egg production, ova, oogenesis, estrogen, progesterone, fallopian tubes, oviducts, fertilization site, uterus, womb, fetal development, endometrium, cervix, vagina, birth canal'),
(1583, 4, '16. REPRODUCTIVE SYSTEM OF MAMMALS', 'Structure of Gametes', 'Sperm are male gametes with a head (containing nucleus and acrosome), midpiece (mitochondria for energy), and tail (flagellum for movement). Eggs (ova) are female gametes that are large, immobile, and contain nutrient-rich cytoplasm. Both are haploid (half the chromosome number).', 'gamete structure, sperm, head, nucleus, haploid, acrosome, enzymes, egg penetration, midpiece, mitochondria, energy, tail, flagellum, movement, egg, ovum, large, immobile, nutrient-rich cytoplasm'),
(1584, 4, '16. REPRODUCTIVE SYSTEM OF MAMMALS', 'Fertilization and Embryo Development', 'Fertilization is the fusion of sperm and egg to form a zygote. It occurs in the fallopian tube. The zygote undergoes cleavage (cell divisions) to form a morula, then a blastocyst. The blastocyst implants in the uterus. The embryo develops, with organs forming during organogenesis. The fetus grows until birth.', 'fertilization, embryo development, sperm, egg, fallopian tube, acrosome, enzymes, zygote, cleavage, cell division, morula, blastocyst, implantation, uterus, embryo, organogenesis, fetus, gestation'),
(1585, 4, '17. METAMORPHOSIS IN INSECTS', 'Complete Metamorphosis', 'Complete metamorphosis (holometabolism) has four stages: egg, larva, pupa, and adult. The larva (caterpillar, grub, maggot) looks completely different from the adult and has different feeding habits. The pupa is a resting stage where transformation occurs. Examples: butterfly, moth, beetle, fly.', 'complete metamorphosis, holometabolism, egg, larva, caterpillar, grub, maggot, pupa, chrysalis, cocoon, adult, butterfly, moth, beetle, fly, different body forms, different feeding habits'),
(1586, 4, '17. METAMORPHOSIS IN INSECTS', 'Incomplete Metamorphosis', 'Incomplete metamorphosis (hemimetabolism) has three stages: egg, nymph, and adult. The nymph resembles the adult but lacks wings and reproductive organs. It molts several times, growing larger each time. There is no pupal stage. Examples: grasshopper, cockroach, cricket, dragonfly.', 'incomplete metamorphosis, hemimetabolism, egg, nymph, instar, molting, adult, resembles adult, no wings, no reproductive organs, gradual change, grasshopper, cockroach, cricket, dragonfly'),
(1587, 4, '21. PLANT NUTRITION', 'Photosynthesis - Process', 'Photosynthesis is the process by which plants convert light energy into chemical energy (glucose). It occurs in chloroplasts. Light reactions (thylakoids) produce ATP and NADPH. The Calvin cycle (stroma) uses ATP and NADPH to fix CO2 into glucose. The overall equation: 6CO2 + 6H2O + light → C6H12O6 + 6O2.', 'photosynthesis process, light energy, chemical energy, glucose, chloroplasts, light reactions, thylakoids, ATP, NADPH, Calvin cycle, stroma, carbon fixation, RuBisCO, 6CO2, 6H2O, C6H12O6, 6O2'),
(1588, 4, '21. PLANT NUTRITION', 'Mineral Requirements - Macro-nutrients', 'Macro-nutrients are elements required by plants in large amounts. Carbon, hydrogen, and oxygen come from air and water. Nitrogen (proteins, nucleic acids), phosphorus (ATP, DNA, RNA), potassium (enzyme activation, osmosis), magnesium (chlorophyll), calcium (cell walls), and sulfur (proteins) come from soil.', 'macro-nutrients, carbon, hydrogen, oxygen, nitrogen, proteins, nucleic acids, phosphorus, ATP, DNA, RNA, potassium, enzyme activation, osmosis, magnesium, chlorophyll, calcium, cell walls, sulfur, proteins, soil nutrients'),
(1589, 4, '21. PLANT NUTRITION', 'Mineral Requirements - Micro-nutrients', 'Micro-nutrients (trace elements) are required in small amounts but are essential for plant health. Iron (chlorophyll synthesis, electron transport), manganese (water splitting in photosynthesis), zinc (enzyme activation), copper (electron transport), boron (cell wall formation), and molybdenum (nitrogen fixation). Deficiency causes specific disorders.', 'micro-nutrients, iron, chlorophyll synthesis, electron transport, manganese, water splitting, photosynthesis, zinc, enzyme activation, copper, boron, cell wall formation, molybdenum, nitrogen fixation, trace elements, deficiency symptoms'),
(1590, 4, '22. ANIMAL NUTRITION', 'Food Substances - Classes', 'Food provides energy, building materials, and regulatory molecules. Carbohydrates (sugars, starch) are the main energy source. Proteins (amino acids) are for growth and repair. Lipids (fats, oils) are energy storage and insulation. Vitamins and minerals are regulatory. Water is essential for all life processes.', 'food classes, carbohydrates, sugars, glucose, fructose, starch, energy source, proteins, amino acids, growth, repair, enzymes, lipids, fats, oils, energy storage, insulation, vitamins, minerals, water, hydration'),
(1591, 4, '23. BASIC ECOLOGICAL CONCEPTS', 'Abiotic Components', 'Abiotic components are non-living factors that affect ecosystems. Physical factors include temperature, light, humidity, rainfall, wind, and soil type. Chemical factors include pH, oxygen concentration, salinity, and mineral availability. These factors determine which organisms can survive in a habitat.', 'abiotic components, non-living factors, physical factors, temperature, light, humidity, rainfall, wind, soil type, chemical factors, pH, oxygen concentration, salinity, minerals, habitat suitability'),
(1592, 4, '23. BASIC ECOLOGICAL CONCEPTS', 'Biotic Components', 'Biotic components are living factors in an ecosystem. Producers (autotrophs) make their own food through photosynthesis (plants, algae). Consumers (heterotrophs) eat other organisms: primary consumers (herbivores), secondary consumers (carnivores), tertiary consumers (top predators). Decomposers (bacteria, fungi) break down dead organic matter.', 'biotic components, trophic relationships, producers, autotrophs, photoautotrophs, chemoautotrophs, consumers, heterotrophs, primary consumers, herbivores, secondary consumers, carnivores, tertiary consumers, top predators, decomposers, bacteria, fungi, detritivores'),
(1593, 4, '23. BASIC ECOLOGICAL CONCEPTS', 'Food Chains and Food Webs', 'A food chain is a linear sequence of organisms showing who eats whom (e.g., grass → grasshopper → frog → snake → hawk). A food web is a network of interconnected food chains, showing all feeding relationships in a community. Food webs are more realistic than food chains.', 'food chains, food webs, feeding relationships, energy flow, linear sequence, grass, grasshopper, frog, snake, hawk, interconnected chains, community feeding, realistic representation'),
(1594, 4, '24. ECOLOGICAL MANAGEMENT', 'Ecological Succession', 'Ecological succession is the gradual, directional change in species composition of a community over time. Primary succession starts on bare rock (no soil). Secondary succession starts on existing soil (after disturbance). The final, stable community is called the climax community.', 'ecological succession, gradual change, directional change, species composition, community development, primary succession, bare rock, no soil, secondary succession, existing soil, disturbance, climax community, stable community'),
(1595, 4, '24. ECOLOGICAL MANAGEMENT', 'Pollution - Air', 'Air pollution is contamination of the atmosphere by harmful substances. Major air pollutants include carbon monoxide (vehicle exhaust), sulfur dioxide (burning fossil fuels), nitrogen oxides (combustion), particulate matter (dust, smoke), and ozone (smog). Effects include respiratory diseases, acid rain, and climate change.', 'air pollution, atmosphere, pollutants, carbon monoxide, vehicle exhaust, incomplete combustion, sulfur dioxide, SO2, burning fossil fuels, acid rain, nitrogen oxides, NOx, combustion, particulate matter, dust, smoke, ozone, smog, respiratory diseases, climate change, greenhouse effect'),
(1596, 4, '24. ECOLOGICAL MANAGEMENT', 'Pollution - Water and Soil', 'Water and soil pollution contaminate essential resources. Sources include industrial discharge (chemicals, heavy metals), agricultural runoff (fertilizers, pesticides), sewage (pathogens, organic waste), oil spills, and plastic waste. Effects include eutrophication (algal blooms), habitat destruction, and health hazards.', 'water pollution, soil pollution, contamination, water bodies, rivers, lakes, oceans, groundwater, industrial discharge, chemicals, heavy metals, agricultural runoff, fertilizers, pesticides, sewage, pathogens, organic waste, oil spills, plastic waste, eutrophication, algal blooms, habitat destruction, health hazards'),
(1597, 4, '28. BIOLOGY OF HEREDITY', 'Genetic Terminologies', 'Genetics is the study of heredity and variation. A gene is a segment of DNA that codes for a protein. Genotype is the genetic makeup of an organism. Phenotype is the observable characteristics. Alleles are different versions of a gene. Dominant alleles mask recessive alleles. Homozygous has two identical alleles; heterozygous has two different alleles.', 'genetics, heredity, variation, gene, segment of DNA, codes for protein, genotype, genetic makeup, phenotype, observable characteristics, alleles, gene versions, dominant allele, recessive allele, homozygous, identical alleles, heterozygous, different alleles'),
(1598, 4, '28. BIOLOGY OF HEREDITY', 'Mendel\'s Laws', 'Mendel\'s experiments with pea plants established the laws of inheritance. The Law of Segregation states that allele pairs separate during gamete formation, so each gamete gets one allele. The Law of Independent Assortment states that genes for different traits are inherited independently of each other.', 'Mendel\'s laws, law of segregation, first law, gamete formation, allele pairs separate, one allele per gamete, law of independent assortment, second law, different traits, independent inheritance, pea plants, Pisum sativum'),
(1599, 4, '28. BIOLOGY OF HEREDITY', 'Sex Determination', 'Sex determination is the biological system that determines sexual characteristics. In humans, sex is determined by X and Y chromosomes. Females are XX, males are XY. The father determines the sex of the baby: X-bearing sperm produces female (XX), Y-bearing sperm produces male (XY).', 'sex determination, biological system, sexual characteristics, humans, sex chromosomes, X chromosome, Y chromosome, female XX, male XY, father determines sex, X-bearing sperm, female offspring, Y-bearing sperm, male offspring'),
(1600, 4, '28. BIOLOGY OF HEREDITY', 'Sex-linked Characters', 'Sex-linked traits are controlled by genes on sex chromosomes (usually X chromosome). They are more common in males because males have only one X chromosome. Examples include hemophilia (blood clotting disorder), red-green color blindness, and Duchenne muscular dystrophy. Females can be carriers without showing symptoms.', 'sex-linked characters, sex-linked traits, X chromosome genes, Y chromosome genes, more common in males, one X chromosome, hemophilia, blood clotting disorder, color blindness, red-green color blindness, Duchenne muscular dystrophy, female carriers, heterozygous, asymptomatic'),
(1601, 4, '30. EVOLUTION', 'Theories of Evolution', 'Evolution is change in species over time. Lamarckism proposed inheritance of acquired characteristics (use and disuse). Darwin\'s theory of natural selection proposed that individuals with favorable traits are more likely to survive and reproduce, passing those traits to offspring. Mutation provides the raw material for evolution.', 'evolution theories, Lamarckism, Jean-Baptiste Lamarck, inheritance of acquired characteristics, use and disuse, Darwinism, Charles Darwin, natural selection, survival of the fittest, favorable traits, reproduction, offspring, mutation, raw material, evolution'),
(1609, 1, 'INTRODUCTION TO CHEMISTRY', 'The Scope and History of Chemistry', 'Chemistry, often called the \"central science,\" bridges physics and biology, explaining the composition, structure, properties, and transformations of matter. Its origins trace to ancient civilizations: Egyptians practiced metallurgy, embalming, and glassmaking (4000 BCE); Greeks proposed elemental theory; Chinese and Indian alchemists sought elixirs and transmutation. Robert Boyle (1661) defined elements as irreducible substances. Antoine Lavoisier (1780s) revolutionized chemistry: identified oxygen, explained combustion, established law of conservation of mass. John Dalton (1803) proposed atomic theory. Dmitri Mendeleev (1869) created periodic table.', 'chemistry history, alchemy, Boyle, Lavoisier, Dalton, Mendeleev, atomic theory, periodic table, conservation of mass, vitalism, urea synthesis, elements, compounds'),
(1610, 1, 'INTRODUCTION TO CHEMISTRY', 'Measurement in Chemistry', 'Chemistry requires precise measurement of physical properties. The International System of Units (SI) provides standardized measurement framework. Base quantities: length (metre, m), mass (kilogram, kg), time (second, s), temperature (kelvin, K), amount of substance (mole, mol), electric current (ampere, A). Derived quantities: volume, density, pressure, energy, concentration. Mass measurement uses analytical balances. Volume measurement uses graduated cylinders, pipettes, burettes, volumetric flasks. Significant figures convey precision.', 'measurement, SI units, base quantities, derived quantities, metre, kilogram, second, kelvin, mole, ampere, volume, density, pressure, concentration, analytical balance, precision, accuracy, calibration, pipette, burette, volumetric flask, significant figures'),
(1611, 1, 'INTRODUCTION TO CHEMISTRY', 'Scientific Methods', 'The scientific method is a systematic approach to investigating phenomena. Steps: Observation, Hypothesis (testable and falsifiable), Prediction, Experiment (with independent and dependent variables), Data analysis, Conclusion. A theory is a well-substantiated explanation integrating many observations. A scientific law is a concise statement of observed relationship, often mathematical. Peer review ensures quality. Scientific writing follows IMRaD format.', 'scientific method, observation, hypothesis, falsifiability, prediction, experiment, independent variable, dependent variable, control group, data analysis, theory, scientific law, peer review, IMRaD, scientific ethics'),
(1612, 1, 'STRUCTURE OF THE ATOM', 'Early Atomic Theories', 'Ancient Greek philosophers Leucippus and Democritus proposed \"atomos\" (indivisible particles). Aristotle rejected atomism, favoring four elements (earth, air, fire, water). John Dalton (1803-1808) proposed first scientific atomic theory: all matter composed of atoms; atoms of same element identical; compounds form from fixed ratios; chemical reactions rearrange atoms. Explained laws: Conservation of mass, Definite proportions, Multiple proportions.', 'ancient atomism, Democritus, Leucippus, atomos, Aristotle, John Dalton, atomic theory, law of conservation of mass, law of definite proportions, law of multiple proportions, atomic weights'),
(1613, 1, 'STRUCTURE OF THE ATOM', 'Discovery of Subatomic Particles', 'J.J. Thomsons cathode ray experiments (1897) discovered electrons. Measured e/m ratio. Proposed \"plum pudding\" model. Robert Millikans oil drop experiment (1909) measured electron charge (1.602 × 10⁻¹⁹ C). Ernest Rutherford discovered nucleus via gold foil experiment (1909). Rutherford also discovered proton (1919). James Chadwick discovered neutron (1932). Radioactivity discovered by Henri Becquerel (1896).', 'subatomic particles, electron, J.J. Thomson, cathode rays, Millikan oil drop experiment, electron charge, proton, neutron, James Chadwick, Ernest Rutherford, gold foil experiment, nucleus, radioactivity, Henri Becquerel, Marie Curie'),
(1614, 1, 'STRUCTURE OF THE ATOM', 'Rutherfords Nuclear Model', 'Ernest Rutherfords gold foil experiment (1909) revolutionized atomic theory. Most alpha particles passed through gold foil; some were deflected; about 1 in 8000 backscattered. Conclusions: Atom mostly empty space; tiny, dense, positively charged nucleus; electrons orbit nucleus. Calculated nuclear size ≈10⁻¹⁴ m, atomic size ≈10⁻¹⁰ m. Nuclear density enormous ≈10¹⁷ kg/m³.', 'Rutherford model, gold foil experiment, alpha particle scattering, nuclear atom, nucleus discovery, mostly empty space, nuclear size, atomic size, nuclear density, planetary model'),
(1615, 1, 'STRUCTURE OF THE ATOM', 'Bohr Model', 'Niels Bohr (1913) combined Rutherfords model with quantum theory. Postulates: electrons in allowed orbits (stationary states); angular momentum quantized (mvr = nħ); electrons dont radiate in stationary states; photon emitted/absorbed when jumping between orbits. Energy levels: E_n = -R_H/n². Explained hydrogen spectrum: Lyman (UV), Balmer (visible), Paschen (infrared).', 'Bohr model, Niels Bohr, quantum theory, stationary states, quantized angular momentum, principal quantum number, photon emission, photon absorption, energy level, ground state, excited state, ionization energy, Rydberg constant, hydrogen spectrum'),
(1616, 1, 'STRUCTURE OF THE ATOM', 'Quantum Mechanical Model', 'Modern quantum mechanics describes electrons by wave functions (probability distributions). Developed by Heisenberg, Schrödinger, Dirac. Schrödinger equation: Hψ = Eψ. |ψ|² gives probability density (electron cloud). Orbitals characterized by quantum numbers: n, ℓ, m_ℓ, m_s. s orbital (spherical), p orbital (dumbbell), d orbital (cloverleaf). Pauli exclusion principle: no two electrons have identical quantum numbers.', 'quantum mechanics, wave function, Schrödinger equation, probability density, electron cloud, orbital, quantum numbers, s orbital, p orbital, d orbital, Pauli exclusion principle, Hunds rule, Aufbau principle, electron configuration, Heisenberg uncertainty principle'),
(1617, 1, 'PERIODIC CHEMISTRY', 'Development of the Periodic Table', 'Dmitri Mendeleev (1869) arranged 63 known elements by atomic weight and valence. Left gaps for undiscovered elements, predicted their properties with accuracy. Henry Moseley (1913) used X-ray spectroscopy to determine atomic numbers, establishing that atomic number is fundamental organizing principle. Modern periodic table: 118 elements, 7 periods, 18 groups. Periodic law: properties are periodic functions of atomic number.', 'periodic table, Mendeleev, Moseley, atomic number, periodic law, periods, groups, s-block, p-block, d-block, f-block, transition metals, alkali metals, halogens, noble gases'),
(1618, 1, 'PERIODIC CHEMISTRY', 'Periodic Trends', 'Periodic properties show systematic variation. Atomic radius decreases across period, increases down group. Ionization energy increases across period, decreases down group. Electronegativity increases across period, decreases down group. Electron affinity generally increases across period. Metallic character decreases across period, increases down group. These trends governed by effective nuclear charge and shielding.', 'periodic trends, atomic radius, ionization energy, electronegativity, electron affinity, metallic character, effective nuclear charge, shielding, period trend, group trend'),
(1619, 1, 'PERIODIC CHEMISTRY', 'Group 1: Alkali Metals', 'Group 1 (Li, Na, K, Rb, Cs, Fr) are alkali metals with ns¹ valence. Soft, low density, low melting points. Highly reactive: react with water to form MOH + H₂; with oxygen: Li forms Li₂O, Na forms Na₂O₂, K,Rb,Cs form MO₂ (superoxides); with halogens form ionic halides. Flame tests: Li crimson, Na yellow, K lilac. Uses: batteries, alloys, medications, fertilizers.', 'alkali metals, lithium, sodium, potassium, rubidium, cesium, francium, ns¹ valence, reaction with water, metal hydroxide, sodium hydroxide, potassium hydroxide, flame tests, lithium batteries, sodium vapor lamps, potassium fertilizer'),
(1620, 1, 'PERIODIC CHEMISTRY', 'Group 17: Halogens', 'Group 17 (F, Cl, Br, I, At) are halogens with ns²np⁵ valence. Diatomic molecules (X₂). Highly reactive oxidizing agents. Reactivity decreases down group. Displacement reactions: more reactive halogen displaces less reactive. Hydrogen halides: HF (weak acid), HCl, HBr, HI (strong acids). Uses: fluoridation, water disinfection, PVC, Teflon, antiseptics, photographic film.', 'halogens, fluorine, chlorine, bromine, iodine, astatine, ns²np⁵ valence, diatomic molecules, oxidizing agents, displacement reactions, hydrogen halides, HF, HCl, HBr, HI, fluoridation, water disinfection, PVC, Teflon'),
(1621, 1, 'PERIODIC CHEMISTRY', 'Transition Metals (First Series)', 'First transition series (Sc to Zn) where 3d orbitals fill. Characteristic properties: variable oxidation states, colored compounds, complex formation, catalytic activity, paramagnetism. Examples: Fe (hemoglobin), Co (vitamin B₁₂), Cu (enzymes), Zn (catalase). Alloys: steel, stainless steel, brass, bronze. Industrial uses: construction, electronics, batteries, magnets, catalysts.', 'transition metals, first transition series, scandium, titanium, vanadium, chromium, manganese, iron, cobalt, nickel, copper, zinc, d-block, variable oxidation states, colored compounds, complex formation, catalysis, alloys, steel, brass, bronze'),
(1622, 1, 'CHEMICAL BONDS', 'Introduction to Chemical Bonding', 'Chemical bonding is attractive forces holding atoms together. Octet rule: atoms tend to gain, lose, or share electrons to achieve 8 valence electrons. Lewis structures show bonding pairs (lines) and lone pairs (dots). Electronegativity difference (ΔEN) predicts bond type: ΔEN > 1.7 ionic, 0.4-1.7 polar covalent, <0.4 nonpolar covalent.', 'chemical bonding, octet rule, Lewis structure, valence electrons, bonding pairs, lone pairs, electronegativity difference, ionic bond, covalent bond, polar covalent, nonpolar covalent'),
(1623, 1, 'CHEMICAL BONDS', 'Ionic Bonding', 'Ionic bonding involves complete electron transfer from metal to nonmetal. Metals lose electrons to become cations; nonmetals gain electrons to become anions. Electrostatic attraction forms ionic lattice. Properties: high melting points, hard but brittle, soluble in water, conduct electricity when molten or dissolved. Lattice energy depends on ion charges and sizes.', 'ionic bonding, electron transfer, cation, anion, electrostatic attraction, lattice energy, ionic lattice, high melting point, solubility in water, electrical conductivity, electrolytes'),
(1624, 1, 'CHEMICAL BONDS', 'Covalent Bonding', 'Covalent bonding involves sharing electron pairs between nonmetals. Single bond: one shared pair (σ bond); double bond: two pairs (σ + π); triple bond: three pairs (σ + 2π). Bond length decreases and bond strength increases with bond order. Polar covalent bonds have partial charges due to electronegativity difference. Network covalent solids: diamond, graphite, SiO₂.', 'covalent bonding, electron sharing, single bond, double bond, triple bond, σ bond, π bond, bond order, bond length, bond energy, polar covalent bond, dipole moment, network covalent, diamond, graphite, silicon dioxide'),
(1625, 1, 'CHEMICAL BONDS', 'Metallic Bonding', 'Metallic bonding involves delocalized valence electrons in a \"sea of electrons\" around metal cations. Explains properties: electrical conductivity, thermal conductivity, malleability, ductility, metallic luster, high melting points. Band theory: valence and conduction bands; band gap determines conductor/semiconductor/insulator behavior.', 'metallic bonding, sea of electrons, delocalized electrons, metal cations, electrical conductivity, thermal conductivity, malleability, ductility, metallic luster, band theory, valence band, conduction band, band gap, conductor, semiconductor, insulator'),
(1626, 1, 'CHEMICAL BONDS', 'Intermolecular Forces', 'Intermolecular forces (IMFs) are attractions between molecules. Types: London dispersion forces (all molecules, weakest), dipole-dipole forces (polar molecules), hydrogen bonding (strong, with H bonded to N,O,F). Hydrogen bonding explains waters high boiling point, surface tension, and ice density. IMFs determine boiling points, melting points, solubility.', 'intermolecular forces, IMF, van der Waals forces, London dispersion forces, dipole-dipole forces, hydrogen bonding, boiling point, melting point, solubility, surface tension, viscosity, water properties'),
(1627, 1, 'STOICHIOMETRY', 'The Mole Concept', 'The mole is SI unit for amount of substance. One mole contains 6.022 × 10²³ entities (Avogadros constant). Molar mass is mass of one mole (g mol⁻¹). Mass-mole-number relationships: mass = moles × molar mass; moles = mass / molar mass; number of entities = moles × N_A. Molar volume of gas at STP ≈ 22.4 L mol⁻¹.', 'mole, amount of substance, Avogadro constant, N_A, 6.022 × 10²³, molar mass, mass-mole relationship, number of entities, molar volume, STP, ideal gas law, PV = nRT'),
(1628, 1, 'STOICHIOMETRY', 'Chemical Equations and Stoichiometry', 'Chemical equations represent reactions with reactants → products. Balancing ensures conservation of mass. Mole ratios from coefficients allow stoichiometric calculations. Limiting reactant is completely consumed first, determines theoretical yield. Percent yield = (actual yield / theoretical yield) × 100%. Types: combustion, synthesis, decomposition, displacement, double displacement.', 'chemical equations, balanced equation, reactants, products, coefficients, mole ratios, limiting reactant, theoretical yield, actual yield, percent yield, combustion, synthesis, decomposition, displacement'),
(1629, 1, 'STOICHIOMETRY', 'Solutions and Concentration', 'Solutions are homogeneous mixtures. Solvent (larger amount) and solute(s). Concentration measures amount of solute. Expressions: molarity (mol L⁻¹), molality (mol kg⁻¹), mole fraction, percent by mass, ppm. Dilution: c₁V₁ = c₂V₂. Primary standards are highly pure, stable substances used to prepare standard solutions. Titration determines unknown concentration.', 'solutions, solvent, solute, concentration, molarity, molality, mole fraction, percent by mass, ppm, dilution, c₁V₁ = c₂V₂, primary standard, secondary standard, standardization, titration, equivalence point, end point, indicator'),
(1630, 1, 'ACIDS, BASES AND SALTS', 'Acid-Base Theories', 'Arrhenius: acids produce H⁺ in water; bases produce OH⁻. Brønsted-Lowry: acid donates H⁺; base accepts H⁺. Conjugate acid-base pairs. Lewis: acid accepts electron pair; base donates electron pair. pH = -log[H⁺]. Strong acids/bases completely dissociate; weak acids/bases partially dissociate. Neutralization: H⁺ + OH⁻ → H₂O.', 'acids, bases, Arrhenius theory, Brønsted-Lowry theory, Lewis theory, proton donor, proton acceptor, electron pair acceptor, electron pair donor, conjugate acid-base pair, pH, strong acid, weak acid, strong base, weak base, neutralization'),
(1631, 1, 'ACIDS, BASES AND SALTS', 'pH and Buffers', 'pH scale: 0-14 (acidic <7, neutral =7, basic >7). Buffer solutions resist pH change, contain weak acid and conjugate base (or weak base and conjugate acid). Henderson-Hasselbalch equation: pH = pK_a + log([A⁻]/[HA]). Buffers important in biological systems (blood pH 7.4), pharmaceuticals, and chemical reactions.', 'pH scale, acidic, neutral, basic, buffer solution, weak acid, conjugate base, Henderson-Hasselbalch equation, pK_a, blood buffer, bicarbonate buffer, phosphate buffer'),
(1632, 1, 'ACIDS, BASES AND SALTS', 'Salts and Hydrolysis', 'Salts are ionic compounds from acid-base neutralization. Types: normal, acidic, basic, double, complex. Salt hydrolysis: salts of strong acid + strong base give neutral solution; strong acid + weak base give acidic; weak acid + strong base give basic. Solubility rules predict precipitation. Common ion effect decreases solubility.', 'salts, neutralization, salt types, normal salt, acidic salt, basic salt, double salt, complex salt, hydrolysis, solubility rules, precipitation, common ion effect, K_sp, solubility product'),
(1633, 1, 'REDOX REACTIONS', 'Oxidation-Reduction', 'Redox reactions involve electron transfer. Oxidation: loss of electrons (increase in oxidation number). Reduction: gain of electrons (decrease in oxidation number). OIL RIG: Oxidation Is Loss, Reduction Is Gain. Oxidizing agent accepts electrons (reduced). Reducing agent donates electrons (oxidized). Half-reactions show electron transfer.', 'redox, oxidation, reduction, electron transfer, OIL RIG, oxidizing agent, oxidant, reducing agent, reductant, half-reaction, oxidation number, oxidation state'),
(1634, 1, 'REDOX REACTIONS', 'Balancing Redox Equations', 'Two methods: half-reaction method and oxidation number method. For acidic conditions: balance atoms other than H,O; add H₂O to balance O; add H⁺ to balance H; add e⁻ to balance charge. For basic conditions: balance as in acid, then add OH⁻ to both sides to neutralize H⁺. Multiply half-reactions to equalize electrons.', 'balancing redox equations, half-reaction method, oxidation number method, acidic conditions, basic conditions, balancing with H₂O, balancing with H⁺, balancing with OH⁻, electron balance'),
(1635, 1, 'REDOX REACTIONS', 'Electrochemical Cells', 'Galvanic cells convert chemical energy to electricity. Standard hydrogen electrode (SHE) is reference (E° = 0.00 V). Cell potential E°_cell = E°_cathode - E°_anode. ΔG° = -nFE°_cell. Nernst equation: E = E° - (RT/nF) ln Q. Batteries: primary (non-rechargeable), secondary (rechargeable), fuel cells. Corrosion is electrochemical process.', 'electrochemical cells, galvanic cell, voltaic cell, standard hydrogen electrode, SHE, cell potential, E°_cell, ΔG° = -nFE°_cell, Faraday constant, Nernst equation, batteries, primary cell, secondary cell, fuel cell, corrosion, rusting'),
(1636, 1, 'ORGANIC CHEMISTRY', 'Introduction to Organic Chemistry', 'Organic chemistry studies carbon compounds. Carbon properties: tetravalent, catenation (forms chains), multiple bonds. Vitalism disproved by Wöhler (urea synthesis). Homologous series: same functional group, gradation in properties. Functional groups determine reactivity. Isomerism: structural (different connectivity) and stereoisomerism (different spatial arrangement).', 'organic chemistry, carbon compounds, tetravalency, catenation, vitalism, Wöhler, urea synthesis, homologous series, functional groups, isomerism, structural isomerism, stereoisomerism'),
(1637, 1, 'ORGANIC CHEMISTRY', 'Alkanes', 'Alkanes: saturated hydrocarbons, CₙH₂ₙ₊₂, sp³ hybridized. Sources: natural gas, petroleum. Nonpolar, insoluble in water. Combustion: complete to CO₂ + H₂O. Halogenation (Cl₂, Br₂, UV): free radical substitution. Cracking breaks larger alkanes into smaller ones. Uses: fuels (gasoline, diesel), solvents, lubricants, waxes.', 'alkanes, saturated hydrocarbons, paraffins, methane, ethane, propane, butane, combustion, halogenation, free radical substitution, cracking, fuels, natural gas, petroleum, LPG, gasoline, diesel'),
(1638, 1, 'ORGANIC CHEMISTRY', 'Alkenes', 'Alkenes: unsaturated hydrocarbons, C=C double bond, CₙH₂ₙ, sp² hybridized. Electrophilic addition reactions: hydrogenation (H₂, catalyst), halogenation (Br₂ decolorizes—test), hydrohalogenation (Markovnikovs rule), hydration (H₂O, acid). Polymerization forms polyethene, polypropene, PVC, polystyrene. Uses: plastics, chemical feedstocks.', 'alkenes, unsaturated hydrocarbons, double bond, electrophilic addition, hydrogenation, halogenation, bromine water test, hydrohalogenation, Markovnikovs rule, hydration, polymerization, polyethylene, polypropylene, PVC, polystyrene'),
(1639, 1, 'ORGANIC CHEMISTRY', 'Alkynes', 'Alkynes: unsaturated hydrocarbons, C≡C triple bond, CₙH₂ₙ₋₂, sp hybridized. Acidity of terminal alkynes (pK_a ≈ 25). Addition reactions: hydrogenation (Lindlar catalyst gives cis-alkene; Na/NH₃ gives trans-alkene). Hydration (HgSO₄/H₂SO₄) gives carbonyl compounds. Acetylide formation test for terminal alkynes. Uses: oxyacetylene welding, chemical synthesis.', 'alkynes, triple bond, acetylene, ethyne, terminal alkyne, internal alkyne, acidity, acetylide ion, hydrogenation, Lindlar catalyst, hydration, oxyacetylene welding, polymerization'),
(1640, 1, 'ORGANIC CHEMISTRY', 'Benzene and Aromatic Compounds', 'Benzene: C₆H₆, planar, hexagonal, delocalized π system, resonance energy ~150 kJ/mol. Aromaticity: 4n+2 π electrons (Hückels rule). Electrophilic aromatic substitution: nitration, sulfonation, halogenation, Friedel-Crafts alkylation/acylation. Substituent effects: activating (EDG, ortho/para) and deactivating (EWG, meta).', 'benzene, aromatic compounds, delocalization, resonance energy, aromaticity, Hückels rule, electrophilic aromatic substitution, nitration, sulfonation, halogenation, Friedel-Crafts, activating groups, deactivating groups, ortho/para directors, meta directors'),
(1641, 1, 'ORGANIC CHEMISTRY', 'Alcohols and Phenols', 'Alcohols: R-OH, classified as primary, secondary, tertiary. Hydrogen bonding gives higher boiling points. Reactions: oxidation (primary→aldehyde→acid; secondary→ketone), dehydration (→alkene), esterification. Phenols: Ar-OH, more acidic than alcohols (pK_a ~10), electrophilic aromatic substitution. Uses: methanol (fuel), ethanol (beverages), phenol (disinfectant, plastics).', 'alcohols, phenols, hydroxyl group, primary alcohol, secondary alcohol, tertiary alcohol, oxidation, dehydration, esterification, acidity of phenols, methanol, ethanol, phenol, Bakelite'),
(1642, 1, 'ORGANIC CHEMISTRY', 'Aldehydes and Ketones', 'Aldehydes (R-CHO) and ketones (R-CO-R) contain carbonyl group (C=O). Nucleophilic addition reactions: HCN (cyanohydrins), Grignard reagents (alcohols), alcohols (hemiacetals, acetals). Oxidation: aldehydes easily oxidized (Tollens test, Fehlings test); ketones resist oxidation. Tests: 2,4-DNP (orange precipitate), iodoform test (methyl ketones).', 'aldehydes, ketones, carbonyl group, nucleophilic addition, cyanohydrin, Grignard reaction, hemiacetal, acetal, Tollens test, Fehlings test, 2,4-DNP test, iodoform test, formaldehyde, acetaldehyde, acetone, benzaldehyde'),
(1643, 1, 'ORGANIC CHEMISTRY', 'Carboxylic Acids and Derivatives', 'Carboxylic acids: R-COOH, acidic (pK_a ~4-5), form hydrogen-bonded dimers. Reactions: with bases (salt formation), with alcohols (esterification), reduction (LiAlH₄ to primary alcohol), decarboxylation. Derivatives: acyl chlorides, esters, amides, anhydrides. Soaps are fatty acid salts. Uses: acetic acid (vinegar), benzoic acid (preservative), nylon (polyamide).', 'carboxylic acids, carboxyl group, acidity, esterification, Fischer esterification, reduction, decarboxylation, acyl chlorides, esters, amides, anhydrides, soaps, fatty acids, acetic acid, benzoic acid, nylon'),
(1644, 1, 'CHEMICAL KINETICS', 'Reaction Rates', 'Reaction rate = change in concentration per time. Factors affecting rate: nature of reactants, concentration, temperature (Arrhenius equation), catalysts, surface area, light. Rate law: rate = k[A]^m[B]^n (order of reaction). Half-life: t½ = ln2/k for first-order. Collision theory: effective collisions require sufficient energy (E_a) and proper orientation.', 'reaction rate, kinetics, rate law, rate constant, order of reaction, half-life, Arrhenius equation, activation energy, collision theory, effective collision, catalyst, enzyme'),
(1645, 1, 'CHEMICAL KINETICS', 'Chemical Equilibrium', 'At equilibrium: forward rate = reverse rate, macroscopic properties constant. Equilibrium constant K = [products]/[reactants]. Le Chateliers principle: system shifts to counteract disturbance. Changes in concentration, pressure, temperature shift equilibrium. Catalysts do not affect position, only speed attainment. ICE tables solve equilibrium problems.', 'chemical equilibrium, dynamic equilibrium, equilibrium constant, K_c, K_p, Le Chateliers principle, concentration change, pressure change, temperature change, catalyst, ICE table');
INSERT INTO `materials` (`id`, `subject_id`, `topic`, `sub_topic`, `note`, `keywords`) VALUES
(1646, 1, 'ENVIRONMENTAL CHEMISTRY', 'Pollution', 'Pollution: introduction of harmful substances into environment. Air pollutants: CO, SO₂, NOₓ, PM, VOCs, O₃, lead. Effects: acid rain, smog, respiratory disease, climate change. Water pollutants: sewage, industrial waste, agricultural runoff, oil spills, plastics. Control: scrubbers, catalytic converters, wastewater treatment, regulations (Clean Air Act, Clean Water Act).', 'pollution, air pollution, water pollution, soil pollution, CO, SO₂, NOₓ, particulate matter, VOCs, acid rain, smog, greenhouse gases, climate change, sewage, industrial waste, agricultural runoff, oil spills, plastics, scrubbers, catalytic converters, wastewater treatment'),
(1647, 1, 'ENVIRONMENTAL CHEMISTRY', 'Green Chemistry', 'Green chemistry principles: prevention, atom economy, less hazardous synthesis, safer chemicals, safer solvents, energy efficiency, renewable feedstocks, reduce derivatives, catalysis, design for degradation, real-time analysis, inherently safer chemistry. Sustainable development meets present needs without compromising future generations.', 'green chemistry, sustainability, atom economy, renewable feedstocks, catalysis, biodegradable, safer solvents, energy efficiency, prevention, waste minimization, sustainable development'),
(1648, 2, 'Concepts of Matter', 'Particle Nature of Matter', 'The concept that matter is composed of discrete particles rather than being continuous has evolved over centuries. Modern understanding confirms that all matter consists of atoms and molecules in constant motion. The particle nature is experimentally demonstrated through Brownian motion, first observed by botanist Robert Brown in 1827 while examining pollen grains suspended in water under a microscope. He noted that the pollen grains executed a continuous, random, zigzag movement. Albert Einstein in 1905 theoretically explained that this motion results from invisible water molecules constantly and randomly bombarding the larger visible pollen grains. This provided compelling evidence for the existence of molecules and their kinetic activity. Further evidence comes from diffusion phenomena, osmosis, and modern microscopy techniques that can now image individual atoms.', 'particle nature, matter, atoms, molecules, Brownian motion, Robert Brown, Albert Einstein, kinetic theory, diffusion, osmosis, random motion, molecular collisions, scanning tunneling microscope'),
(1649, 2, 'Concepts of Matter', 'Kinetic Theory of Matter', 'The Kinetic Theory of Matter provides a comprehensive framework explaining the behavior of matter based on the motion of its constituent particles. This theory rests on several fundamental postulates: all matter consists of tiny particles in continuous, random motion; the particles are separated by distances that vary with the state of matter; attractive forces exist between particles that decrease rapidly with increasing separation; collisions between particles and with container walls are perfectly elastic; the average kinetic energy of particles is directly proportional to the absolute temperature. In solids, particles vibrate about fixed positions; in liquids, particles can slide past one another; in gases, particles are far apart moving at high speeds. The theory explains gas pressure, temperature, phase transitions, and the behavior of matter under different conditions.', 'kinetic theory, molecular motion, particles, elastic collisions, kinetic energy, temperature, absolute zero, solids, liquids, gases, phase transitions, melting, vaporization, sublimation, gas pressure, molecular speeds, Maxwell-Boltzmann distribution'),
(1650, 2, 'Concepts of Matter', 'States of Matter', 'The three primary states of matter—solid, liquid, and gas—represent different levels of molecular organization and energy. Solids have definite shape and volume due to strong intermolecular forces holding particles in fixed positions. Crystalline solids have atoms arranged in regular repeating lattices; amorphous solids lack long-range order. Liquids have definite volume but take the shape of their container; particles can slide past one another while remaining close. Gases have neither definite shape nor volume, expanding to fill any container. Gas particles are far apart with negligible intermolecular forces. Plasma, the fourth state, consists of ionized gas with free electrons and positive ions. Bose-Einstein condensates, produced near absolute zero, represent a fifth state where atoms coalesce into the same quantum state.', 'states of matter, solid, liquid, gas, plasma, Bose-Einstein condensate, crystalline solid, amorphous solid, crystal lattice, viscosity, surface tension, capillary action, ideal gas, real gas, van der Waals forces, phase diagram, triple point, critical point, phase transitions, melting point, boiling point'),
(1651, 2, 'Concepts of Matter', 'Cohesion, Adhesion and Capillarity', 'Cohesion refers to intermolecular attractive forces between like molecules. In water, cohesion results from hydrogen bonding, giving water high surface tension. Adhesion describes attractive forces between unlike molecules, such as water adhering to glass. The competition between cohesion and adhesion determines wetting behavior. Capillarity (capillary action) is the ability of liquids to flow in narrow spaces, arising from the combination of cohesion, adhesion, and surface tension. The height of capillary rise is given by h = 2γ cosθ/(ρgr), where γ is surface tension, θ contact angle, ρ liquid density, g gravity, and r tube radius. Capillarity explains water transport in plants, absorbent materials, and many natural phenomena.', 'cohesion, cohesive forces, adhesion, adhesive forces, hydrogen bonding, surface tension, wetting, contact angle, hydrophilic, hydrophobic, capillarity, capillary action, capillary rise, Jurins law, meniscus, plant water transport, xylem, transpiration pull, absorbent materials, paper towel, wicking'),
(1652, 2, 'Measurements', 'Fundamental Quantities and SI Units', 'The International System of Units (SI) provides a coherent framework for scientific measurement. The seven base quantities are: length (metre, m), mass (kilogram, kg), time (second, s), electric current (ampere, A), thermodynamic temperature (kelvin, K), amount of substance (mole, mol), and luminous intensity (candela, cd). These are now defined by fixing fundamental constants: the speed of light c, Planck constant h, elementary charge e, Boltzmann constant k, Avogadro constant N_A, and luminous efficacy. This 2019 redefinition replaced physical artifacts with invariant constants of nature, ensuring long-term stability and universal accessibility.', 'fundamental quantities, base quantities, SI units, metre, kilogram, second, ampere, kelvin, mole, candela, speed of light, Planck constant, elementary charge, Boltzmann constant, Avogadro constant, measurement standards, metrology, unit definitions'),
(1653, 2, 'Measurements', 'Derived Quantities and Units', 'Derived quantities are combinations of fundamental quantities through multiplication and division. Area (m²), volume (m³), density (kg/m³), speed (m/s), acceleration (m/s²), force (newton, N = kg·m/s²), pressure (pascal, Pa = N/m²), energy (joule, J = N·m), power (watt, W = J/s), frequency (hertz, Hz = s⁻¹), electric charge (coulomb, C = A·s), potential difference (volt, V = J/C), resistance (ohm, Ω = V/A), capacitance (farad, F = C/V), inductance (henry, H = V·s/A), and magnetic flux density (tesla, T = N/(A·m)). Dimensional analysis uses symbols [L], [M], [T], [I], [Θ], [N], [J] to check equation consistency.', 'derived quantities, derived units, area, volume, density, speed, acceleration, force, newton, pressure, pascal, energy, joule, power, watt, frequency, hertz, electric charge, coulomb, potential difference, volt, resistance, ohm, capacitance, farad, inductance, henry, magnetic field, tesla, dimensional analysis, dimensions, [L], [M], [T], [I]'),
(1654, 2, 'Measurements', 'Measurement Techniques and Instruments', 'Precise measurement uses various instruments. Length: metre rule (mm precision), vernier calipers (0.1 mm), micrometer screw gauge (0.01 mm). Mass: beam balance, electronic balance (0.1 mg to 0.01 g precision). Time: mechanical clocks, quartz clocks (32,768 Hz crystal), atomic clocks (cesium, rubidium). Electrical measurements: analog multimeters (moving-coil galvanometer), digital multimeters, oscilloscopes. Measurement uncertainty includes systematic errors (consistent bias from calibration, technique) and random errors (statistical fluctuations). Accuracy indicates closeness to true value; precision indicates reproducibility. Significant figures convey measurement reliability.', 'measurement techniques, length measurement, metre rule, vernier calipers, micrometer screw gauge, mass measurement, beam balance, electronic balance, time measurement, quartz clock, atomic clock, cesium clock, electrical measurement, multimeter, oscilloscope, measurement uncertainty, systematic error, random error, accuracy, precision, significant figures, calibration'),
(1655, 2, 'Kinematics', 'Position and Coordinate Systems', 'Position describes location of a point in space relative to a reference frame. One-dimensional motion uses coordinate x. Two-dimensional Cartesian coordinates (x, y) use perpendicular axes. Polar coordinates (r, θ) describe position by radial distance r from origin and angle θ from reference direction. Three-dimensional coordinates (x, y, z) add depth. Position vectors combine magnitude and direction: r = x i + y j + z k. Choosing the right coordinate system simplifies problem-solving. GPS determines position through trilateration from satellite signals.', 'position, coordinate systems, Cartesian coordinates, rectangular coordinates, x-axis, y-axis, z-axis, origin, polar coordinates, r, θ, position vector, unit vectors, i, j, k, coordinate transformation, GPS, Global Positioning System, trilateration, surveying, theodolite'),
(1656, 2, 'Kinematics', 'Distance and Displacement', 'Distance is the total path length traveled, a scalar quantity. Displacement is the straight-line distance between initial and final positions with direction, a vector quantity. In one dimension, displacement Δx = x₂ - x₁. In two or three dimensions, displacement vector Δr = (Δx, Δy, Δz) with magnitude √(Δx² + Δy² + Δz²). For a round trip returning to start, total distance > 0 but displacement = 0. Displacement is fundamental because physical laws often depend on displacement rather than path length.', 'distance, scalar quantity, displacement, vector quantity, path length, straight-line distance, magnitude, direction, one-dimensional displacement, Δx, two-dimensional displacement, Δx, Δy, displacement magnitude, position change, round trip, zero displacement, path independence'),
(1657, 2, 'Kinematics', 'Speed and Velocity', 'Speed is rate of change of distance, a scalar. Average speed = total distance / total time. Instantaneous speed = ds/dt. SI unit metres per second (m/s). Velocity is rate of change of displacement, a vector. Average velocity = displacement / time. Instantaneous velocity = dr/dt. Velocity includes direction; speed is magnitude of velocity. Uniform velocity means constant speed in constant direction. Velocity-time graphs show velocity vs time; slope gives acceleration; area gives displacement.', 'speed, scalar, average speed, instantaneous speed, ds/dt, m/s, km/h, velocity, vector, average velocity, displacement/time, instantaneous velocity, dr/dt, uniform velocity, non-uniform velocity, velocity-time graph, slope, acceleration, area under curve, displacement from velocity, speed vs velocity distinction'),
(1658, 2, 'Kinematics', 'Acceleration', 'Acceleration is rate of change of velocity, a vector. Average acceleration = Δv/Δt. Instantaneous acceleration = dv/dt. SI unit metres per second squared (m/s²). Deceleration (retardation) is acceleration opposite to velocity direction, causing speed decrease. Uniform acceleration means constant rate of velocity change (e.g., free fall near Earth). Non-uniform acceleration varies with time. Acceleration appears in Newtons second law: F = ma. Centripetal acceleration in circular motion: a_c = v²/r directed toward center.', 'acceleration, rate of change of velocity, vector, average acceleration, instantaneous acceleration, dv/dt, m/s², deceleration, retardation, uniform acceleration, constant acceleration, non-uniform acceleration, Newtons second law, F = ma, centripetal acceleration, a_c = v²/r, circular motion, free fall, acceleration due to gravity, g, 9.8 m/s²'),
(1659, 2, 'Kinematics', 'Equations of Motion', 'For uniformly accelerated rectilinear motion, five equations relate displacement (s), initial velocity (u), final velocity (v), acceleration (a), and time (t): v = u + at; s = ut + ½at²; s = (u+v)t/2; v² = u² + 2as; s_nth = u + a(n - ½). These apply to motion under constant acceleration. For free fall, a = g (downward). Problem-solving: identify knowns, choose appropriate equation, apply sign convention consistently. These equations are essential for predicting motion in countless practical situations.', 'equations of motion, SUVAT equations, uniformly accelerated motion, constant acceleration, v = u + at, s = ut + ½at², s = (u+v)t/2, v² = u² + 2as, displacement during nth second, initial velocity, u, final velocity, v, acceleration, a, time, t, displacement, s, free fall, acceleration due to gravity, sign convention, stopping distance, braking distance, reaction distance'),
(1660, 2, 'Vectors', 'Scalar and Vector Quantities', 'Scalars have only magnitude (mass, distance, speed, time, temperature, energy). Vectors have both magnitude and direction (displacement, velocity, acceleration, force, weight, momentum). Vectors represented graphically as arrows; length proportional to magnitude, arrowhead indicates direction. Unit vectors (î, ĵ, k̂) have magnitude 1 and indicate direction. Vector components: A = A_x î + A_y ĵ + A_z k̂. Vector addition: R = A + B; component method: R_x = A_x + B_x, R_y = A_y + B_y. Vector subtraction: A - B = A + (-B). Dot product: A·B = |A||B| cos θ (scalar). Cross product: A × B = |A||B| sin θ n̂ (vector perpendicular to both).', 'scalars, vectors, magnitude, direction, scalar examples, mass, distance, speed, time, temperature, vector examples, displacement, velocity, acceleration, force, weight, momentum, vector representation, arrow, unit vectors, î, ĵ, k̂, vector components, vector addition, tip-to-tail method, parallelogram method, component addition, vector subtraction, dot product, cross product, scalar product, vector product'),
(1661, 2, 'Vectors', 'Vector Addition and Resolution', 'Vector addition combines vectors to find resultant. Graphical methods: tip-to-tail (place vectors head to tail, resultant from first tail to last head) and parallelogram (diagonal of parallelogram). Analytical method: resolve vectors into components, add components, recompose resultant. Force board experiments demonstrate equilibrium. Vector resolution splits vector into perpendicular components: F_x = F cos θ, F_y = F sin θ. Applications include navigation (boat crossing river, aircraft wind correction), force analysis, and relative velocity problems. Triangle of forces: three forces in equilibrium can be represented by triangle sides.', 'vector addition, tip-to-tail, head-to-tail, parallelogram method, resultant, analytical addition, component addition, force board, equilibrium, vector resolution, decomposition, components, F_x = F cos θ, F_y = F sin θ, navigation, boat crossing river, aircraft wind correction, relative velocity, triangle of forces, force polygon, concurrent forces, inclined plane, projectile motion, initial velocity components'),
(1662, 2, 'Dynamics', 'Newtons First Law (Inertia)', 'Newtons first law (law of inertia): Every body continues in its state of rest or uniform motion in a straight line unless compelled to change that state by forces impressed upon it. This establishes inertia as fundamental property of matter. Inertial mass measures resistance to acceleration. Inertia explains why seatbelts are needed, why tablecloth can be pulled from under dishes, and why passengers lurch forward when car stops. The law defines inertial reference frames where it holds. Earth approximates inertial frame but not exactly (rotation, orbit).', 'Newtons first law, law of inertia, inertial reference frame, constant velocity, rest, uniform motion, straight line, impressed force, inertia of rest, inertia of motion, tablecloth trick, car start passenger jerk, car stop passenger lurch, seatbelt, airbag, inertial mass, resistance to acceleration, Galileo, thought experiment, inclined plane, Foucault pendulum, Earth rotation'),
(1663, 2, 'Dynamics', 'Newtons Second Law (F = ma)', 'Newtons second law: rate of change of momentum is proportional to net force and occurs in direction of force. For constant mass: ΣF = ma. This vector equation quantifies force-mass-acceleration relationship. Unit: newton (N) = kg·m/s². Component equations: ΣF_x = ma_x, ΣF_y = ma_y, ΣF_z = ma_z. Impulse-momentum theorem: ∫F dt = Δp. Applications: calculating forces from accelerations, determining motion from known forces, analyzing collisions, vehicle dynamics, projectile motion. Weight is special case: W = mg. Terminal velocity when drag balances weight.', 'Newtons second law, ΣF = ma, net force, acceleration, momentum, p = mv, rate of change of momentum, newton, N, component equations, impulse-momentum theorem, impulse, ∫F dt, Δp, weight, W = mg, free fall, terminal velocity, drag force, air resistance, applications, force calculation, motion prediction, collisions, vehicle dynamics, projectile motion'),
(1664, 2, 'Dynamics', 'Newtons Third Law (Action-Reaction)', 'Newtons third law: To every action there is an equal and opposite reaction. Forces always occur in pairs: equal magnitude, opposite direction, same type, acting on different objects. Action-reaction pairs never cancel because they act on different bodies. Examples: walking (foot pushes ground backward, ground pushes foot forward), swimming (hands push water backward, water pushes hands forward), rocket propulsion (exhaust gases downward, gases push rocket upward), recoil of gun, book on table (weight down, normal force up). Internal forces cancel in pairs; only external forces affect center of mass motion.', 'Newtons third law, action-reaction, equal and opposite forces, force pairs, different objects, same type forces, walking, swimming, rocket propulsion, recoil, gun recoil, book on table, normal force, weight, internal forces, external forces, center of mass, horse and cart paradox, jet engine, propeller, helicopter rotor, collision forces'),
(1665, 2, 'Energy', 'Forms of Energy', 'Energy is the capacity to do work or produce change. Forms include: mechanical (potential and kinetic), thermal (internal energy), chemical (molecular bonds), electrical (charge separation), light (radiant/electromagnetic), sound (mechanical waves), nuclear (atomic nuclei), magnetic (magnetic fields). Energy can transform between forms: photosynthesis converts light to chemical; combustion converts chemical to thermal; generator converts mechanical to electrical; motor converts electrical to mechanical. Renewable energy sources: solar, wind, hydro, tidal, geothermal, biomass. Non-renewable: fossil fuels (coal, oil, gas), nuclear (uranium).', 'energy forms, mechanical energy, potential energy, kinetic energy, thermal energy, heat, chemical energy, electrical energy, light energy, radiant energy, sound energy, nuclear energy, magnetic energy, energy transformation, photosynthesis, combustion, generator, motor, renewable energy, solar, wind, hydro, tidal, geothermal, biomass, non-renewable energy, fossil fuels, coal, oil, natural gas, nuclear, uranium'),
(1666, 2, 'Energy', 'Conservation of Energy', 'The principle of conservation of energy states that energy cannot be created or destroyed; it can only be transformed from one form to another or transferred between objects. Total energy of an isolated system remains constant. This law emerges from time-translation symmetry (Noethers theorem) and has no known exceptions. Examples: falling object (potential to kinetic), pendulum (energy oscillates between potential and kinetic), friction (mechanical to heat), photosynthesis (light to chemical). Perpetual motion machines are impossible because they violate conservation. Efficiency = (useful output / total input) × 100%.', 'conservation of energy, energy cannot be created or destroyed, energy transformation, energy transfer, isolated system, total energy constant, Noethers theorem, time-translation symmetry, falling object, mgh = ½mv², pendulum, friction, heat, photosynthesis, perpetual motion machine, impossible, efficiency, useful output, total input, first law of thermodynamics, energy accounting'),
(1667, 2, 'Work and Energy', 'Work', 'Work in physics is done when a force causes displacement. Mathematically: W = F·s = F s cos θ, where θ is angle between force and displacement. SI unit: joule (J) = N·m. Positive work (θ < 90°) adds energy; negative work (θ > 90°) removes energy; zero work (θ = 90°) when force perpendicular to displacement (e.g., centripetal force, normal force). Work against gravity: W = mgh. Spring work: W = ½kx². Work-energy theorem: net work = change in kinetic energy (W_net = ΔKE = ½mv_f² - ½mv_i²).', 'work, physics definition, W = F·s = F s cos θ, joule, J, positive work, negative work, zero work, perpendicular force, centripetal force, normal force, work against gravity, W = mgh, spring work, W = ½kx², work-energy theorem, W_net = ΔKE, kinetic energy change, force-displacement graph, area under curve'),
(1668, 2, 'Work and Energy', 'Power', 'Power is the rate at which work is done or energy is transferred. Average power = work/time = ΔW/Δt. Instantaneous power = dW/dt. For constant force and velocity: P = F·v = F v cos θ. SI unit: watt (W) = J/s. Horsepower (hp) ≈ 746 W. Electrical power: P = VI = I²R = V²/R. Energy = power × time; kilowatt-hour (kWh) common for electricity billing. Power efficiency = useful power output / total power input. Power determines performance: more power means faster acceleration, higher top speed.', 'power, rate of doing work, rate of energy transfer, average power, ΔW/Δt, instantaneous power, dW/dt, P = F·v, watt, W, J/s, horsepower, hp, electrical power, P = VI, P = I²R, P = V²/R, energy = power × time, kilowatt-hour, kWh, power efficiency, performance, acceleration, top speed, engine power, motor power'),
(1669, 2, 'Heat', 'Temperature Measurement', 'Temperature measures average kinetic energy of particles. Celsius scale: 0°C ice point, 100°C steam point. Fahrenheit scale: 32°F freezing, 212°F boiling. Kelvin scale: absolute scale with 0 K at absolute zero (-273.15°C). Thermometers use thermometric properties: liquid expansion (mercury, alcohol), electrical resistance (resistance thermometer, thermistor), thermoelectric EMF (thermocouple), radiation (pyrometer). Clinical thermometer (35-42°C) has constriction for reading after removal. Maximum-minimum thermometer records daily extremes. Constant-volume gas thermometer is primary standard.', 'temperature, average kinetic energy, Celsius scale, Anders Celsius, ice point, steam point, Fahrenheit scale, Daniel Fahrenheit, Kelvin scale, Lord Kelvin, absolute zero, 0 K, -273.15°C, thermometers, thermometric properties, liquid-in-glass thermometer, mercury thermometer, alcohol thermometer, clinical thermometer, maximum-minimum thermometer, Sixs thermometer, resistance thermometer, thermistor, thermocouple, pyrometer, gas thermometer'),
(1670, 2, 'Heat', 'Effects of Heat on Matter', 'Heat produces various effects: temperature rise (increased kinetic energy), phase change (melting, vaporization, sublimation at constant temperature), thermal expansion (increase in size), change of electrical resistance (positive coefficient for metals, negative for semiconductors), color change (red heat, white heat), thermionic emission (electron emission from hot surface), and chemical changes (reaction rates increase). Anomalous expansion of water: maximum density at 4°C, ice less dense. This explains why ponds freeze from top down, allowing aquatic life survival. Bimetallic strips use differential expansion for thermostats.', 'effects of heat, temperature rise, kinetic energy increase, phase change, melting, vaporization, sublimation, freezing, condensation, deposition, latent heat, thermal expansion, linear expansion, ΔL = α L₀ ΔT, area expansion, volume expansion, anomalous expansion of water, maximum density at 4°C, ice density, bimetallic strip, thermostat, electrical resistance change, positive temperature coefficient, negative temperature coefficient, color change, red heat, white heat, thermionic emission, chemical changes, reaction rates'),
(1671, 2, 'Waves', 'Wave Production and Propagation', 'Waves transfer energy without transferring matter. Mechanical waves require a medium; electromagnetic waves do not. Wave parameters: frequency f (Hz), period T = 1/f, wavelength λ (m), amplitude A (m), wave speed v = fλ. Transverse waves: particle vibration perpendicular to propagation (waves on string, EM waves). Longitudinal waves: particle vibration parallel to propagation (sound waves). Wave equation: y = A sin(ωt ± kx + φ), where ω = 2πf angular frequency, k = 2π/λ wave number, φ phase constant. Energy transported ∝ A².', 'waves, disturbance, energy transfer, no matter transfer, mechanical waves, electromagnetic waves, frequency, f, hertz, Hz, period, T, wavelength, λ, amplitude, A, wave speed, v = fλ, transverse waves, longitudinal waves, wave equation, y = A sin(ωt ± kx + φ), angular frequency, ω, wave number, k, phase constant, φ, energy ∝ A², ripple tank, wave demonstration'),
(1672, 2, 'Waves', 'Wave Properties', 'All waves exhibit reflection (bouncing off boundaries, angle of incidence = angle of reflection), refraction (bending when speed changes, Snells law n₁ sin θ₁ = n₂ sin θ₂), diffraction (spreading around obstacles, significant when λ ≈ obstacle size), interference (superposition of waves from coherent sources), and superposition (resultant displacement = sum of individual displacements). Constructive interference when waves in phase (path difference = nλ); destructive when out of phase (path difference = (n+½)λ). Standing waves from opposite traveling waves produce nodes (zero displacement) and antinodes (maximum displacement).', 'wave properties, reflection, angle of incidence, angle of reflection, refraction, Snells law, n₁ sin θ₁ = n₂ sin θ₂, refractive index, diffraction, obstacle, aperture, interference, coherent sources, superposition, constructive interference, in phase, path difference = nλ, destructive interference, out of phase, path difference = (n+½)λ, standing waves, stationary waves, nodes, antinodes, Youngs double-slit experiment, beats, thin film interference, ripple tank'),
(1673, 2, 'Light', 'Nature of Light', 'Light is electromagnetic radiation visible to human eye (wavelength 400-700 nm). Travels at c = 3 × 10⁸ m/s in vacuum. Exhibits wave-particle duality: wave nature demonstrated by interference and diffraction; particle nature (photons) by photoelectric effect and Compton scattering. Visible spectrum: violet (400 nm) to red (700 nm). White light is combination of all colors. Luminous objects emit light; non-luminous objects reflect light. Rectilinear propagation: light travels in straight lines in uniform medium, explaining shadows, eclipses, and pinhole camera operation.', 'light, electromagnetic radiation, visible light, wavelength, 400-700 nm, speed of light, c = 3 × 10⁸ m/s, wave-particle duality, photon, photoelectric effect, Compton scattering, visible spectrum, violet, red, white light, luminous objects, non-luminous objects, rectilinear propagation, shadows, umbra, penumbra, eclipses, solar eclipse, lunar eclipse, pinhole camera'),
(1674, 2, 'Light', 'Reflection of Light', 'Reflection: light bounces off surfaces. Law of reflection: angle of incidence equals angle of reflection. Regular (specular) reflection from smooth surfaces (mirrors) produces clear images. Diffuse reflection from rough surfaces scatters light. Plane mirror: virtual, upright, same size, laterally inverted image; image distance equals object distance. Curved mirrors: concave (converging) and convex (diverging). Mirror formula: 1/u + 1/v = 1/f, where f = R/2. Magnification m = v/u. Concave mirrors: real/virtual images depending on object position. Convex mirrors: always virtual, upright, diminished, wide field of view.', 'reflection of light, law of reflection, angle of incidence, angle of reflection, regular reflection, specular reflection, mirror, diffuse reflection, plane mirror, virtual image, upright, same size, laterally inverted, image distance, object distance, curved mirrors, concave mirror, converging, convex mirror, diverging, mirror formula, 1/u + 1/v = 1/f, focal length, f = R/2, magnification, m = v/u, real image, virtual image, diminished, enlarged, field of view'),
(1675, 2, 'Light', 'Refraction of Light', 'Refraction: bending of light when passing between media with different speeds. Snells law: n₁ sin θ₁ = n₂ sin θ₂. Refractive index n = c/v. Light bends toward normal entering denser medium, away from normal entering less dense medium. Dispersion: refractive index varies with wavelength (shorter wavelengths bend more) causing white light to spread into spectrum. Total internal reflection occurs when light tries to go from denser to less dense medium at angle > critical angle C, where sin C = n₂/n₁. Applications: optical fibers (light guided by TIR), diamonds (brilliance), mirages (atmospheric refraction).', 'refraction of light, bending of light, Snells law, n₁ sin θ₁ = n₂ sin θ₂, refractive index, n = c/v, denser medium, less dense medium, bend toward normal, bend away from normal, dispersion, wavelength dependence, spectrum, prism, total internal reflection, TIR, critical angle, sin C = n₂/n₁, optical fiber, fiber optics, diamond brilliance, mirage, apparent depth, real depth, atmospheric refraction'),
(1676, 2, 'Sound', 'Nature of Sound', 'Sound is a mechanical longitudinal wave requiring a medium. Cannot travel in vacuum. Speed in air at 20°C ≈ 343 m/s, increases with temperature (0.6 m/s per °C), faster in liquids (~1500 m/s), fastest in solids (~5000 m/s). Human hearing range: 20 Hz to 20 kHz. Infrasound (<20 Hz): elephants, earthquakes. Ultrasound (>20 kHz): medical imaging, bats, dolphins. Pitch determined by frequency, loudness by amplitude (intensity, measured in decibels dB). Timbre (quality) determined by harmonic content (overtones).', 'sound, mechanical wave, longitudinal wave, requires medium, vacuum, no sound, speed of sound, 343 m/s at 20°C, temperature dependence, 0.6 m/s per °C, speed in water, 1500 m/s, speed in steel, 5000 m/s, human hearing range, 20 Hz to 20 kHz, infrasound, ultrasound, pitch, frequency, loudness, amplitude, intensity, decibel, dB, timbre, quality, harmonic content, overtones, fundamental frequency'),
(1677, 2, 'Sound', 'Sound Properties and Behavior', 'Sound exhibits reflection (echo, reverberation), refraction (bending due to temperature gradients, wind), diffraction (bending around obstacles, more pronounced for low frequencies), and interference (beats from slightly different frequencies). Doppler effect: apparent frequency change due to relative motion: f = f(v ± v_o)/(v ∓ v_s). Applications: radar speed measurement, medical ultrasound Doppler, astronomy (redshift/blueshift). Resonance: when driving frequency matches natural frequency, amplitude大增. Standing waves in air columns: open pipe (all harmonics, f₀ = v/2L), closed pipe (odd harmonics only, f₀ = v/4L).', 'sound properties, reflection, echo, reverberation, refraction, temperature gradient, wind, diffraction, interference, beats, beat frequency, |f₁ - f₂|, Doppler effect, f = f(v ± v_o)/(v ∓ v_s), radar speed measurement, ultrasound Doppler, redshift, blueshift, resonance, natural frequency, driving frequency, standing waves, air column, open pipe, f₀ = v/2L, closed pipe, f₀ = v/4L, harmonics, overtones, organ pipe, musical instrument'),
(1678, 2, 'Electricity', 'Electrostatics', 'Electrostatics deals with charges at rest. Like charges repel, unlike attract. Charge is quantized: e = 1.602 × 10⁻¹⁹ C. Coulombs law: F = k q₁q₂/r², with k = 9 × 10⁹ N·m²/C². Electric field E = F/q = kQ/r², direction radially outward from positive charge. Electric potential V = kQ/r, volt (V). Capacitance C = Q/V, farad (F). Parallel plate capacitor: C = ε₀A/d. Energy stored: U = ½CV². Dielectrics increase capacitance: C = κε₀A/d. Conductors in electrostatics: field inside zero, charge on surface, field perpendicular to surface.', 'electrostatics, charges at rest, like repel, unlike attract, charge quantization, elementary charge, e = 1.602 × 10⁻¹⁹ C, Coulombs law, F = k q₁q₂/r², k = 9 × 10⁹ N·m²/C², electric field, E = F/q = kQ/r², electric potential, V = kQ/r, volt, V, capacitance, C = Q/V, farad, F, parallel plate capacitor, C = ε₀A/d, energy stored, U = ½CV², dielectric, dielectric constant, κ, C = κε₀A/d, conductor, zero internal field, surface charge'),
(1679, 2, 'Electricity', 'Current Electricity', 'Electric current I = ΔQ/Δt, ampere (A). Ohms law: V = IR, resistance R (Ω). Resistivity ρ = RA/L, conductivity σ = 1/ρ. Resistors in series: R_total = R₁ + R₂ + ...; in parallel: 1/R_total = 1/R₁ + 1/R₂ + ... Electrical power: P = VI = I²R = V²/R, watt (W). Energy: W = VIt = I²Rt. Electromotive force (emf) E: energy per charge from source. Internal resistance r causes terminal voltage V = E - Ir. Cells in series: voltages add; in parallel: same voltage, current capacity adds. Wheatstone bridge measures unknown resistance. Potentiometer measures emf accurately.', 'current electricity, electric current, I = ΔQ/Δt, ampere, A, Ohms law, V = IR, resistance, R, ohm, Ω, resistivity, ρ = RA/L, conductivity, σ = 1/ρ, resistors in series, R_total = R₁ + R₂ + ..., resistors in parallel, 1/R_total = 1/R₁ + 1/R₂ + ..., electrical power, P = VI = I²R = V²/R, watt, W, energy, W = VIt, electromotive force, emf, E, internal resistance, r, terminal voltage, V = E - Ir, cells in series, cells in parallel, Wheatstone bridge, potentiometer'),
(1680, 2, 'Magnetism', 'Magnetic Fields', 'Magnetic fields arise from moving charges and intrinsic magnetic moments. Magnetic flux density B (tesla, T). Field lines form closed loops (no monopoles). Force on current-carrying conductor: F = BIL sin θ. Direction: Flemings left-hand rule. Force between parallel conductors: F/L = μ₀I₁I₂/(2πd), μ₀ = 4π × 10⁻⁷ T·m/A. Magnetic field of long straight wire: B = μ₀I/(2πr). Solenoid: B = μ₀nI (n turns per unit length). Earths magnetic field: behaves as dipole, origin from dynamo effect in outer core. Declination and inclination vary with location.', 'magnetic field, magnetic flux density, B, tesla, T, field lines, closed loops, no monopoles, force on conductor, F = BIL sin θ, Flemings left-hand rule, force between parallel conductors, F/L = μ₀I₁I₂/(2πd), permeability of free space, μ₀ = 4π × 10⁻⁷ T·m/A, long straight wire, B = μ₀I/(2πr), solenoid, B = μ₀nI, Earths magnetic field, dynamo effect, outer core, declination, inclination, compass, magnetic storm, aurora'),
(1681, 2, 'Electromagnetism', 'Electromagnetic Induction', 'Electromagnetic induction: production of emf by changing magnetic flux. Faradays law: E = -N dΦ/dt, where Φ = BA cos θ. Lenzs law: induced current opposes change producing it. Motional emf: E = B L v. AC generator: coil rotating in magnetic field produces sinusoidal emf: E = NBAω sin ωt. Transformer: V_p/V_s = N_p/N_s, I_p/I_s = N_s/N_p. Step-up increases voltage, step-down decreases. Eddy currents: induced currents in bulk metal, cause heating and damping, reduced by laminating cores. Self-inductance: emf = -L dI/dt, L henry (H). Energy in inductor: U = ½LI².', 'electromagnetic induction, Faradays law, E = -N dΦ/dt, magnetic flux, Φ = BA cos θ, Lenzs law, induced current opposes change, motional emf, E = B L v, AC generator, alternator, E = NBAω sin ωt, transformer, V_p/V_s = N_p/N_s, I_p/I_s = N_s/N_p, step-up transformer, step-down transformer, eddy currents, laminations, self-inductance, L, henry, H, emf = -L dI/dt, energy in inductor, U = ½LI², mutual inductance, M'),
(1682, 2, 'Atomic Physics', 'Atomic Models', 'Atomic models evolved: Dalton (indivisible atoms), Thomson (plum pudding), Rutherford (nuclear model from gold foil experiment), Bohr (quantized orbits, explained hydrogen spectrum), quantum mechanical (Schrödinger, wave functions, orbitals). Bohr model: electrons in quantized orbits, angular momentum = nħ, energy levels E_n = -13.6/n² eV, transitions ΔE = hf. Quantum numbers: n (principal), l (azimuthal), m_l (magnetic), m_s (spin). Pauli exclusion principle: no two electrons same quantum numbers. Electron configuration follows Aufbau principle and Hunds rule.', 'atomic models, Dalton, Thomson, plum pudding, Rutherford, gold foil experiment, nuclear model, Bohr model, quantized orbits, energy levels, E_n = -13.6/n² eV, hydrogen spectrum, Balmer series, Lyman series, Paschen series, quantum mechanical model, Schrödinger equation, wave function, ψ, orbital, quantum numbers, principal quantum number, n, azimuthal quantum number, l, magnetic quantum number, m_l, spin quantum number, m_s, Pauli exclusion principle, Aufbau principle, Hunds rule, electron configuration'),
(1683, 2, 'Atomic Physics', 'Photoelectric Effect', 'Photoelectric effect: emission of electrons from metal surface when light shines. Observations: threshold frequency, instantaneous emission, kinetic energy increases with frequency (not intensity), intensity affects number of electrons. Einsteins explanation: light consists of photons with energy E = hf. Photoelectric equation: hf = φ + KE_max, where φ work function. Stopping potential V_s: eV_s = hf - φ. Applications: photoelectric cells (light meters, automatic doors), solar cells (photovoltaic), photomultiplier tubes (sensitive light detection), image sensors (CCD, CMOS).', 'photoelectric effect, photoemission, electrons, metal surface, threshold frequency, f₀, instantaneous emission, kinetic energy vs frequency, intensity affects number, Einstein, photons, E = hf, Planck constant, h = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ J·s, photoelectric equation, hf = φ + KE_max, work function, φ, stopping potential, V_s, eV_s = hf - φ, applications, photoelectric cell, light meter, automatic door, solar cell, photovoltaic, photomultiplier tube, PMT, image sensor, CCD, CMOS'),
(1684, 2, 'Nuclear Physics', 'Radioactivity', 'Radioactivity: spontaneous disintegration of unstable nuclei. Alpha decay (α): emits helium nucleus (⁴₂He), reduces Z by 2, A by 4. Beta decay (β⁻): neutron → proton + e⁻ + ν̄ₑ; β⁺: proton → neutron + e⁺ + νₑ. Gamma decay (γ): emits high-energy photon, no change in Z or A. Decay law: N = N₀e^{-λt}, half-life T_{½} = ln2/λ. Activity A = λN, becquerel (Bq) = 1 decay/s. Applications: radioactive dating (carbon-14, uranium-lead), medical tracers (technetium-99m), radiotherapy (cobalt-60), smoke detectors (americium-241). Detection: Geiger-Müller counter, scintillation counter.', 'radioactivity, radioactive decay, alpha decay, α, helium nucleus, ⁴₂He, beta decay, β⁻, β⁺, electron, positron, antineutrino, neutrino, gamma decay, γ, gamma ray, decay law, N = N₀e^{-λt}, decay constant, λ, half-life, T_{½} = ln2/λ, activity, A = λN, becquerel, Bq, curie, Ci, applications, radioactive dating, carbon-14 dating, uranium-lead dating, medical tracers, technetium-99m, radiotherapy, cobalt-60, smoke detector, americium-241, detection, Geiger-Müller counter, scintillation counter, GM tube'),
(1685, 2, 'Nuclear Physics', 'Nuclear Reactions and Energy', 'Nuclear fission: heavy nucleus splits into lighter nuclei, releasing neutrons and energy (∼200 MeV per fission). Induced by neutron absorption. Chain reaction requires critical mass. Nuclear reactors: controlled chain reaction for power. Components: fuel (enriched uranium), moderator (graphite, water), control rods (boron, cadmium), coolant. Reactor types: PWR, BWR, CANDU. Nuclear fusion: light nuclei combine to form heavier nucleus, releasing energy. Occurs in stars (proton-proton chain, CNO cycle). D-T reaction: D + T → He + n + 17.6 MeV. Fusion research: magnetic confinement (tokamak), inertial confinement (lasers). Binding energy per nucleon peaks at iron-56.', 'nuclear fission, heavy nucleus splitting, uranium-235, plutonium-239, neutron absorption, fission fragments, chain reaction, critical mass, nuclear reactor, fuel, enriched uranium, moderator, graphite, heavy water, control rods, boron, cadmium, coolant, PWR, BWR, CANDU, nuclear fusion, light nuclei combining, stellar fusion, proton-proton chain, CNO cycle, D-T reaction, deuterium, tritium, 17.6 MeV, magnetic confinement, tokamak, ITER, inertial confinement, laser fusion, binding energy per nucleon, iron-56, mass-energy equivalence, E = Δmc²');
